declare
  payload jsonb := $hc1_payload${"program":"winter-5787","workbook":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","bucket":"official-learning-materials","chunks":[{"program_id":"winter-5787","chunk_code":"HC1-A1","source_type":"notes","siman":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","workbook_title":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","section_key":"A","section_title":"Introduction to the Mitzvah of Challah","chunk_title":"Zman Overview and Mitzvos HaTeluyos Ba’Aretz","chunk_summary":"Introduces Hafrashas Challah and Terumos U’maasros as mitzvos connected to Eretz Yisrael, but with practical relevance everywhere.","content_markdown":"### A. *Introduction to the Mitzvah of Challah*\n\nBez”H, this *zman* we will learn about *mitzvos* that pertain to *Eretz Yisrael*, specifically the *mitzvos* of *hafrashas challah* and *terumos u’maasros.* While these *mitzvos* are rooted in the *kedusha* of the Land and are thus called *mitzvos hateluyos ba’aretz*, we will see that *hafrashas challah* applies even in *chutz la’aretz*, and there are numerous practical situations in which *terumos u’maasros* may also apply even outside of *Eretz Yisrael*. The importance of these *mitzvos* can be seen from Chazal.  The **גמרא סוטה** (יד.) explains that Moshe Rabbeinu longed to enter *Eretz* *Yisrael* so that he would have the opportunity to fulfill the *mitzvos* that are dependent upon the Land*.* Today, we are blessed with millions of Jews living in and visiting Eretz Yisrael, and we all hope and believe the coming of *Mashiach* is imminent. Therefore, these *halachos* are especially relevant, whether you live in *Eretz Yisrael* or not. \n\nWe will spend the majority of the *zman* learning the *halachos* of *hafrashas challah,* a *mitzvah* that applies throughout the world and about which the Midrash in **בראשית רבה** פ״א-א teaches that the world was created in its merit. In this introductory section, we will discuss whether the obligation of *hafrashas challah* is biblical or rabbinic.","content_document":{"version":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"heading","paragraph":2,"xmlIndex":2,"text":"Introduction to the Mitzvah of Challah","spans":[{"text":"Introduction to the Mitzvah of Challah","italic":true,"color":"#2F5496"}],"marker":"A.","level":0,"indent":56.0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0,"rightIndent":43.2,"hanging":18.0,"tabStops":[{"position":150.45,"alignment":"left","leader":"none"},{"position":234.0,"alignment":"center","leader":"none"},{"position":279.0,"alignment":"left","leader":"none"}]},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":3,"xmlIndex":4,"text":"Bez”H, this zman we will learn about mitzvos that pertain to Eretz Yisrael, specifically the mitzvos of hafrashas challah and terumos u’maasros. While these mitzvos are rooted in the kedusha of the Land and are thus called mitzvos hateluyos ba’aretz, we will see that hafrashas challah applies even in chutz la’aretz, and there are numerous practical situations in which terumos u’maasros may also apply even outside of Eretz Yisrael. The importance of these mitzvos can be seen from Chazal.  The גמרא סוטה (יד.) explains that Moshe Rabbeinu longed to enter Eretz Yisrael so that he would have the opportunity to fulfill the mitzvos that are dependent upon the Land. Today, we are blessed with millions of Jews living in and visiting Eretz Yisrael, and we all hope and believe the coming of Mashiach is imminent. Therefore, these halachos are especially relevant, whether you live in Eretz Yisrael or not. ","spans":[{"text":"Bez”H, this "},{"text":"zman ","italic":true},{"text":"we will learn about "},{"text":"mitzvos ","italic":true},{"text":"that pertain to "},{"text":"Eretz Yisrael","italic":true},{"text":", specifically the "},{"text":"mitzvos ","italic":true},{"text":"of "},{"text":"hafrashas challah ","italic":true},{"text":"and "},{"text":"terumos u’maasros. ","italic":true},{"text":"While these "},{"text":"mitzvos","italic":true},{"text":" are rooted in the "},{"text":"kedusha","italic":true},{"text":" of the Land and are thus called "},{"text":"mitzvos hateluyos ba’aretz","italic":true},{"text":", we will see that "},{"text":"hafrashas challah","italic":true},{"text":" applies even in "},{"text":"chutz la’aretz","italic":true},{"text":", and there are numerous practical situations in which "},{"text":"terumos u’maasros","italic":true},{"text":" may also apply even outside of "},{"text":"Eretz Yisrael","italic":true},{"text":". The importance of these "},{"text":"mitzvos ","italic":true},{"text":"can be seen from Chazal. "},{"text":" ","italic":true},{"text":"The "},{"text":"גמרא סוטה ","bold":true},{"text":"(יד.) explains that Moshe Rabbeinu longed to enter "},{"text":"Eretz","italic":true},{"text":" "},{"text":"Yisrael","italic":true},{"text":" so that he would have the opportunity to fulfill the "},{"text":"mitzvos ","italic":true},{"text":"that are dependent upon the Land"},{"text":". ","italic":true},{"text":"Today, we are blessed with millions of Jews living in and visiting Eretz Yisrael, and we all hope and believe the coming of "},{"text":"Mashiach ","italic":true},{"text":"is imminent. Therefore,"},{"text":" ","italic":true},{"text":"these "},{"text":"halachos ","italic":true},{"text":"are especially relevant, whether you live in "},{"text":"Eretz Yisrael","italic":true},{"text":" or not. "}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":36.0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":4,"xmlIndex":5,"text":"We will spend the majority of the zman learning the halachos of hafrashas challah, a mitzvah that applies throughout the world and about which the Midrash in בראשית רבה פ״א-א teaches that the world was created in its merit. In this introductory section, we will discuss whether the obligation of hafrashas challah is biblical or rabbinic.","spans":[{"text":"We will spend the majority of the "},{"text":"zman ","italic":true},{"text":"learning the "},{"text":"halachos ","italic":true},{"text":"of "},{"text":"hafrashas challah, ","italic":true},{"text":"a "},{"text":"mitzvah ","italic":true},{"text":"that applies throughout the world and about which the Midrash in "},{"text":"בראשית רבה","bold":true},{"text":" פ״א-א teaches that the world was created in its merit. In this introductory section, we will discuss whether the obligation of "},{"text":"hafrashas challah ","italic":true},{"text":"is biblical or rabbinic."}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":36.0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}],"footnotes":[]},"sort_order":1,"official_shiur_number":1,"estimated_minutes":4,"difficulty":"core","tags":["challah-part-1","original-difficulty:core"],"source_file_name":"Notes for Hafrashas Challah (part 1).docx","source_start_page":null,"source_end_page":null,"is_selectable":true},{"program_id":"winter-5787","chunk_code":"HC1-A2","source_type":"notes","siman":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","workbook_title":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","section_key":"A","section_title":"Introduction to the Mitzvah of Challah","chunk_title":"Torah Source and Tevel Status","chunk_summary":"The Torah obligation to give the first portion of dough to a kohen; challah is called terumah and the dough is tevel until separated.","content_markdown":"In **במדבר** (טו:יז-כא) (1), the Torah teaches that when the Jewish people enter the Land of Israel, anyone who bakes is obligated to give the first portion of the dough to a *kohen.* The Torah refers to *hafrashas challah* as “*terumah,”* and we will learn below that according to some customs, the word “*terumah*” is included in the *bracha* of *hafrashas challah.*[1] Thus, when one prepares a batch of dough (of an amount which will be discussed below), the dough has the status of טבל (similar to produce from which *teruma and ma’aasros* were not yet taken). Only after the *challah* is separated does the dough become permissible.[2] \n\nSource Notes\n\n[1]:  Furthermore, when a *kohen* eats the *challah,* he would recite the *bracha* of ״אשר קדשנו בקדושתו של אהרון וצונו לאכול תרומה״; see *Rama* שכב-ה.\n\n[2]:  See רמב״ם ביכורים פ״ח-ה, who *passkins* that one who eats *tevel* of *challah* is חייב מיתה.","content_document":{"version":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":5,"xmlIndex":6,"text":"In במדבר (טו:יז-כא) (1), the Torah teaches that when the Jewish people enter the Land of Israel, anyone who bakes is obligated to give the first portion of the dough to a kohen. The Torah refers to hafrashas challah as “terumah,” and we will learn below that according to some customs, the word “terumah” is included in the bracha of hafrashas challah.[1] Thus, when one prepares a batch of dough (of an amount which will be discussed below), the dough has the status of טבל (similar to produce from which teruma and ma’aasros were not yet taken). Only after the challah is separated does the dough become permissible.[2] ","spans":[{"text":"In "},{"text":"במדבר ","bold":true},{"text":"(טו:יז-כא) (1), the Torah teaches that when the Jewish people enter the Land of Israel, anyone who bakes is obligated to give the first portion of the dough to a "},{"text":"kohen. ","italic":true},{"text":"The Torah refers to "},{"text":"hafrashas challah ","italic":true},{"text":"as “"},{"text":"terumah,” ","italic":true},{"text":"and we will learn below that according to some customs, the word “"},{"text":"terumah","italic":true},{"text":"” is included in the "},{"text":"bracha ","italic":true},{"text":"of "},{"text":"hafrashas challah.","italic":true},{"text":"[1]","footnote":1},{"text":" ","italic":true},{"text":"Thus, when one prepares a batch of dough (of an amount which will be discussed below), the dough has the status of טבל (similar to produce from which "},{"text":"teruma and ma’aasros ","italic":true},{"text":"were not yet taken). Only after the "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"is separated does the dough become permissible."},{"text":"[2]","footnote":2},{"text":" "}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":36.0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[1,2],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}],"footnotes":[{"id":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":0,"xmlIndex":0,"text":" Furthermore, when a kohen eats the challah, he would recite the bracha of ״אשר קדשנו בקדושתו של אהרון וצונו לאכול תרומה״; see Rama שכב-ה.","spans":[{"text":" Furthermore, when a "},{"text":"kohen ","italic":true},{"text":"eats the "},{"text":"challah, ","italic":true},{"text":"he would recite the "},{"text":"bracha ","italic":true},{"text":"of ״אשר קדשנו בקדושתו של אהרון וצונו לאכול תרומה״; see "},{"text":"Rama ","italic":true},{"text":"שכב-ה."}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}]},{"id":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":0,"xmlIndex":0,"text":" See רמב״ם ביכורים פ״ח-ה, who passkins that one who eats tevel of challah is חייב מיתה.","spans":[{"text":" See רמב״ם ביכורים פ״ח-ה, who "},{"text":"passkins ","italic":true},{"text":"that one who eats "},{"text":"tevel","italic":true},{"text":" of "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"is חייב מיתה."}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}]}]},"sort_order":2,"official_shiur_number":1,"estimated_minutes":4,"difficulty":"core","tags":["challah-part-1","original-difficulty:core","source:1"],"source_file_name":"Notes for Hafrashas Challah (part 1).docx","source_start_page":null,"source_end_page":null,"is_selectable":true},{"program_id":"winter-5787","chunk_code":"HC1-A3","source_type":"notes","siman":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","workbook_title":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","section_key":"A","section_title":"Introduction to the Mitzvah of Challah","chunk_title":"“B’voachem” — When the Mitzvah Begins","chunk_summary":"Rashi/Sifrei: unlike other mitzvos hateluyos ba’aretz, challah began immediately when Klal Yisrael entered Eretz Yisrael.","content_markdown":" **Rashi** quotes the ספרי, which teaches that regarding all other *mitzvos* that apply in *Eretz Yisrael,* the Torah uses the language of ״כי תבוא״ or ״כי תבאו״, which teaches us that the *mitzvah* only begins after the Land is conquered and settled, a process that took Yehoshua fourteen after crossing the Jordan River. In contrast, regarding *challah,* the Torah uses the expression ״בבואכם״ – “when you enter,” teaching that the *mitzvah* took effect as soon as the Jewish people entered the land, even before it was fully conquered and settled. ","content_document":{"version":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":6,"xmlIndex":7,"text":" Rashi quotes the ספרי, which teaches that regarding all other mitzvos that apply in Eretz Yisrael, the Torah uses the language of ״כי תבוא״ or ״כי תבאו״, which teaches us that the mitzvah only begins after the Land is conquered and settled, a process that took Yehoshua fourteen after crossing the Jordan River. In contrast, regarding challah, the Torah uses the expression ״בבואכם״ – “when you enter,” teaching that the mitzvah took effect as soon as the Jewish people entered the land, even before it was fully conquered and settled. ","spans":[{"text":" ","italic":true},{"text":"Rashi ","bold":true},{"text":"quotes the ספרי, which teaches that regarding all other "},{"text":"mitzvos ","italic":true},{"text":"that apply in "},{"text":"Eretz Yisrael, ","italic":true},{"text":"the Torah uses the language of ״כי תבוא״ or ״כי תבאו״, which teaches us that the "},{"text":"mitzvah ","italic":true},{"text":"only begins after the Land is conquered and settled, a process that took Yehoshua fourteen after crossing the Jordan River. In contrast, regarding "},{"text":"challah, ","italic":true},{"text":"the Torah uses the expression ״בבואכם״ – “when you enter,” teaching that the "},{"text":"mitzvah ","italic":true},{"text":"took effect as soon as the Jewish people entered the land, even before it was fully conquered and settled. "}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":36.0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}],"footnotes":[]},"sort_order":3,"official_shiur_number":1,"estimated_minutes":3,"difficulty":"core","tags":["challah-part-1","original-difficulty:core"],"source_file_name":"Notes for Hafrashas Challah (part 1).docx","source_start_page":null,"source_end_page":null,"is_selectable":true},{"program_id":"winter-5787","chunk_code":"HC1-A4","source_type":"notes","siman":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","workbook_title":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","section_key":"A","section_title":"Introduction to the Mitzvah of Challah","chunk_title":"Gemara Kesubos: Biblical or Rabbinic Today","chunk_summary":"Machlokes in Gemara; Rambam and S”A that today even in Eretz Yisrael is rabbinic unless the Jewish people are living there; chu”l enacted so challah is not forgotten.","content_markdown":"The **גמרא כתובות** (כה.) (2) quotes a dispute whether the *mitzvah* of *hafrashas challah* today is biblical or rabbinic. The students of Rav maintain that even the opinions who hold that *terumah* today is only rabbinic agree that the *mitzvah* of *challah* is biblical because, as mentioned above, it took effect the moment the Jewish people entered *Eretz Yisrael* and was not dependent upon conquering the Land. רב הונא argues that even according to the opinions that *terumah* today is biblical, the *mitzvah* of *hafrashas challah* is only rabbinic. Since the Torah writes ״בבואכם״ – implying that **everyone** needs to enter the land, the *mitzvah* of *challah* is rabbinic when most Jews no longer live there. This was true already in the time of Ezra, when although many Jews returned to *Eretz Yisrael,* the **majority** of Jews stayed behind (see Rashi). \n\nThe **רמב״ם** (ספר המצות קלג) lists the *mitzvah* of *hafrashas challah* as the 133rd *mitzvah* in his *minyan ha’mitzvos*. In **הלכות** **ביכורים** (פ״ה-ה) (3), the רמב״ם *passkins* like רב הונא that the biblical obligation of *hafrashas challah* only applies in *Eretz Yisrael* when **all****[3]** of the Jews are living there. He quotes רב הונא’s statement in the above Gemara that even in the time of Ezra, the *mitzvah* of *hafrashas challah* was only rabbinic since the majority of Jews remained behind in Bavel. The רמב״ם adds in הלכה ז׳ that Chazal enacted a *mitzvah* of *hafrashas challah* in *chutz la’aretz* in order to ensure that the *mitzvah* of *challah* should not be forgotten (see בכורות כז.).\n\nThe S”A in the beginning of **סימן שכב-א** *passkins* that there is a **biblical** *mitzvah* to separate a portion of your dough and give it to a *kohen.* In **סעיף ב׳**, he *passkins* like the above רמב״ם that today, even in *Eretz* *Yisrael* the *mitzvah* is only **rabbinic** because the biblical *mitzvah* applies only when **all** Jews are living in Israel. In **סעיף ג׳** he adds that there is a rabbinic *mitzvah* of *hafrashas challah* in *chutz la’aretz* in order that the mitzvah should not be forgotten.[4]\n\nSource Notes\n\n[3]:  We will discuss below if this means all Jews or the majority of Jews.\n\n[4]:  Why did Chazal not enact a similar rabbinic *mitzvah* of *terumah* on fruits and vegetables in *chutz la’aretz* in order that it should not be forgotten? The רמב״ן (הוספות בסוף יבמות) explains that *terumah* only applies to farmers, so we are not worried it would be forgotten, but since everyone prepares bread at home, if there was no *mitzvah* of *hafrashas challah,* it might be forgotten. The יראים (קמח) explains that since flour that is brought from *chutz la’aretz* and made into dough in *Eretz Yisrael* is obligated in *challah,* Chazal required being *mafrish challah* on all doughs.","content_document":{"version":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":7,"xmlIndex":8,"text":"The גמרא כתובות (כה.) (2) quotes a dispute whether the mitzvah of hafrashas challah today is biblical or rabbinic. The students of Rav maintain that even the opinions who hold that terumah today is only rabbinic agree that the mitzvah of challah is biblical because, as mentioned above, it took effect the moment the Jewish people entered Eretz Yisrael and was not dependent upon conquering the Land. רב הונא argues that even according to the opinions that terumah today is biblical, the mitzvah of hafrashas challah is only rabbinic. Since the Torah writes ״בבואכם״ – implying that everyone needs to enter the land, the mitzvah of challah is rabbinic when most Jews no longer live there. This was true already in the time of Ezra, when although many Jews returned to Eretz Yisrael, the majority of Jews stayed behind (see Rashi). ","spans":[{"text":"The "},{"text":"גמרא כתובות ","bold":true},{"text":"(כה.) (2) quotes a dispute whether the "},{"text":"mitzvah ","italic":true},{"text":"of "},{"text":"hafrashas challah ","italic":true},{"text":"today is biblical or rabbinic. The students of Rav maintain that even the opinions who hold that "},{"text":"terumah ","italic":true},{"text":"today"},{"text":" ","italic":true},{"text":"is only rabbinic agree that the "},{"text":"mitzvah ","italic":true},{"text":"of "},{"text":"challah","italic":true},{"text":" is biblical because, as mentioned above, it took effect the moment the Jewish people entered "},{"text":"Eretz Yisrael ","italic":true},{"text":"and was not dependent upon conquering the Land. רב הונא argues that even according to the opinions that "},{"text":"terumah ","italic":true},{"text":"today is biblical, the "},{"text":"mitzvah ","italic":true},{"text":"of "},{"text":"hafrashas challah ","italic":true},{"text":"is only rabbinic. Since the Torah writes ״בבואכם״ – implying that "},{"text":"everyone","bold":true},{"text":" needs to enter the land, the "},{"text":"mitzvah ","italic":true},{"text":"of "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"is rabbinic when most Jews no longer live there. This was true already in the time of Ezra, when although many Jews returned to "},{"text":"Eretz Yisrael, ","italic":true},{"text":"the "},{"text":"majority","bold":true},{"text":" of Jews stayed behind (see Rashi). "}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":36.0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":8,"xmlIndex":9,"text":"The רמב״ם (ספר המצות קלג) lists the mitzvah of hafrashas challah as the 133rd mitzvah in his minyan ha’mitzvos. In הלכות ביכורים (פ״ה-ה) (3), the רמב״ם passkins like רב הונא that the biblical obligation of hafrashas challah only applies in Eretz Yisrael when all[3] of the Jews are living there. He quotes רב הונא’s statement in the above Gemara that even in the time of Ezra, the mitzvah of hafrashas challah was only rabbinic since the majority of Jews remained behind in Bavel. The רמב״ם adds in הלכה ז׳ that Chazal enacted a mitzvah of hafrashas challah in chutz la’aretz in order to ensure that the mitzvah of challah should not be forgotten (see בכורות כז.).","spans":[{"text":"The "},{"text":"רמב״ם ","bold":true},{"text":"(ספר המצות קלג) lists the "},{"text":"mitzvah ","italic":true},{"text":"of "},{"text":"hafrashas challah","italic":true},{"text":" as the 133rd "},{"text":"mitzvah ","italic":true},{"text":"in his "},{"text":"minyan ha’mitzvos","italic":true},{"text":". In "},{"text":"הלכות","bold":true},{"text":" "},{"text":"ביכורים","bold":true},{"text":" (פ״ה-ה) (3), the רמב״ם"},{"text":" passkins ","italic":true},{"text":"like רב הונא that the biblical obligation of "},{"text":"hafrashas challah ","italic":true},{"text":"only applies in "},{"text":"Eretz Yisrael ","italic":true},{"text":"when "},{"text":"all","bold":true},{"text":"[3]","bold":true,"footnote":3},{"text":" of the Jews are living there. He quotes רב הונא’s statement in the above Gemara that even in the time of Ezra, the "},{"text":"mitzvah ","italic":true},{"text":"of "},{"text":"hafrashas challah ","italic":true},{"text":"was only rabbinic since the majority of Jews remained behind in Bavel. The רמב״ם adds in הלכה ז׳ that Chazal enacted a "},{"text":"mitzvah ","italic":true},{"text":"of "},{"text":"hafrashas challah ","italic":true},{"text":"in "},{"text":"chutz la’aretz","italic":true},{"text":" in order to ensure that the "},{"text":"mitzvah ","italic":true},{"text":"of "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"should not be forgotten (see בכורות כז.)."}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":36.0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[3],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":9,"xmlIndex":10,"text":"The S”A in the beginning of סימן שכב-א passkins that there is a biblical mitzvah to separate a portion of your dough and give it to a kohen. In סעיף ב׳, he passkins like the above רמב״ם that today, even in Eretz Yisrael the mitzvah is only rabbinic because the biblical mitzvah applies only when all Jews are living in Israel. In סעיף ג׳ he adds that there is a rabbinic mitzvah of hafrashas challah in chutz la’aretz in order that the mitzvah should not be forgotten.[4]","spans":[{"text":"The S”A in the beginning of "},{"text":"סימן שכב-א","bold":true},{"text":" "},{"text":"passkins ","italic":true},{"text":"that there is a "},{"text":"biblical","bold":true},{"text":" "},{"text":"mitzvah ","italic":true},{"text":"to separate a portion of your dough and give it to a "},{"text":"kohen. ","italic":true},{"text":"In "},{"text":"סעיף ב׳","bold":true},{"text":","},{"text":" ","bold":true},{"text":"he "},{"text":"passkins ","italic":true},{"text":"like the above רמב״ם that today, even in "},{"text":"Eretz","italic":true},{"text":" "},{"text":"Yisrael","italic":true},{"text":" the "},{"text":"mitzvah ","italic":true},{"text":"is only "},{"text":"rabbinic","bold":true},{"text":" because the biblical "},{"text":"mitzvah","italic":true},{"text":" applies only when "},{"text":"all","bold":true},{"text":" Jews are living in Israel. In "},{"text":"סעיף ג׳","bold":true},{"text":" he adds that there is a rabbinic "},{"text":"mitzvah ","italic":true},{"text":"of "},{"text":"hafrashas challah","italic":true},{"text":" in "},{"text":"chutz la’aretz","italic":true},{"text":" in order that the mitzvah should not be forgotten."},{"text":"[4]","footnote":4}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":36.0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[4],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}],"footnotes":[{"id":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":0,"xmlIndex":0,"text":" We will discuss below if this means all Jews or the majority of Jews.","spans":[{"text":" We will discuss below if this means all Jews or the majority of Jews."}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}]},{"id":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":0,"xmlIndex":0,"text":" Why did Chazal not enact a similar rabbinic mitzvah of terumah on fruits and vegetables in chutz la’aretz in order that it should not be forgotten? The רמב״ן (הוספות בסוף יבמות) explains that terumah only applies to farmers, so we are not worried it would be forgotten, but since everyone prepares bread at home, if there was no mitzvah of hafrashas challah, it might be forgotten. The יראים (קמח) explains that since flour that is brought from chutz la’aretz and made into dough in Eretz Yisrael is obligated in challah, Chazal required being mafrish challah on all doughs.","spans":[{"text":" Why did Chazal not enact a similar rabbinic "},{"text":"mitzvah ","italic":true},{"text":"of "},{"text":"terumah ","italic":true},{"text":"on fruits and vegetables in "},{"text":"chutz la’aretz ","italic":true},{"text":"in order that it should not be forgotten? The רמב״ן (הוספות בסוף יבמות) explains that "},{"text":"terumah ","italic":true},{"text":"only applies to farmers, so we are not worried it would be forgotten, but since everyone prepares bread at home, if there was no "},{"text":"mitzvah ","italic":true},{"text":"of "},{"text":"hafrashas challah, ","italic":true},{"text":"it might be forgotten. The יראים (קמח) explains that since flour that is brought from "},{"text":"chutz la’aretz ","italic":true},{"text":"and made into dough in "},{"text":"Eretz Yisrael ","italic":true},{"text":"is obligated in "},{"text":"challah, ","italic":true},{"text":"Chazal required being "},{"text":"mafrish challah ","italic":true},{"text":"on all doughs."}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}]}]},"sort_order":4,"official_shiur_number":1,"estimated_minutes":6,"difficulty":"core","tags":["challah-part-1","original-difficulty:core","source:2","source:3"],"source_file_name":"Notes for Hafrashas Challah (part 1).docx","source_start_page":null,"source_end_page":null,"is_selectable":true},{"program_id":"winter-5787","chunk_code":"HC1-B1","source_type":"notes","siman":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","workbook_title":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","section_key":"B","section_title":"Status of Hafrashas Challah Today","chunk_title":"Current Demographics and the Question Today","chunk_summary":"Raises the possibility that the majority of Jews may live in Eretz Yisrael and asks whether that changes challah, shemittah, and terumah.","content_markdown":"### B. *Status of Hafrashas Challah Today*\n\nGiven what we have learned so far in the previous section, the next question that must be asked is what is the *halachic* status of the *mitzvah* of *challah* today in *Eretz Yisrael*? As mentioned above, Rashi to the גמרא כתובות (2) implies that when the majority of Jews reside in *Eretz Yisrael*, the *mitzvah* of *hafrashas challah* is biblical, which would have practical implications as well, such as requiring us to rule stringently about any *safek* that arises. As of 2026, there are about 7.3 million Jews living in Israel and 8.5 million Jews living outside of Israel.[5] From the 8.5 million living outside of Israel, 37% in the US are Reform, many of whom were converted or born to a mother converted through the Reform movement, which would render them not *halachically* Jewish. Therefore, it stands to reason that even today, the majority of Jews in the world already reside in Israel. Even if these figures are not precise, in the next few years, it nevertheless appears likely that a majority of Jews will be living in Israel for the first time since the first Beis Ha’Mikdash.\n\nFurthermore, if we are to assume that the majority of Jews today are living in Israel (or if this will happen in the next few years), we must consider whether this affects the *halachic* status of *shemittah* and *terumah* as well as *challah*. One of the major leniencies relied upon by the *Rabbanut* in Israel during the *shemittah* year to permit the land to be worked is the ״היתר מכירה״ – selling the land to non-Jews. Bez”H, in a future SCP-*zman* we will discuss the details of this *kula* and whether it is a reliable one, but those who rely on the *kula* do so in part because *shemittah* today is rabbinic. If, however, by the next *shemittah* year, the majority of Jews would reside in Israel, would that abolish the validity of the *heter mechirah*? \n\nWe will begin by discussing whether the *mitzvah* of *shemittah* becomes biblical when the majority of Jews reside in Israel. We will then examine the status of *challah* and *terumah* today.\n\nSource Notes\n\n[5]:  Based on Israel’s Central Bureau of Statistics (CBS). Thus, 46% of Jews currently live in Israel.","content_document":{"version":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"heading","paragraph":10,"xmlIndex":11,"text":"Status of Hafrashas Challah Today","spans":[{"text":"Status of Hafrashas Challah Today","italic":true,"color":"#2F5496"}],"marker":"B.","level":0,"indent":56.0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0,"rightIndent":43.2,"hanging":18.0,"tabStops":[{"position":150.45,"alignment":"left","leader":"none"},{"position":234.0,"alignment":"center","leader":"none"},{"position":279.0,"alignment":"left","leader":"none"}]},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":11,"xmlIndex":13,"text":"Given what we have learned so far in the previous section, the next question that must be asked is what is the halachic status of the mitzvah of challah today in Eretz Yisrael? As mentioned above, Rashi to the גמרא כתובות (2) implies that when the majority of Jews reside in Eretz Yisrael, the mitzvah of hafrashas challah is biblical, which would have practical implications as well, such as requiring us to rule stringently about any safek that arises. As of 2026, there are about 7.3 million Jews living in Israel and 8.5 million Jews living outside of Israel.[5] From the 8.5 million living outside of Israel, 37% in the US are Reform, many of whom were converted or born to a mother converted through the Reform movement, which would render them not halachically Jewish. Therefore, it stands to reason that even today, the majority of Jews in the world already reside in Israel. Even if these figures are not precise, in the next few years, it nevertheless appears likely that a majority of Jews will be living in Israel for the first time since the first Beis Ha’Mikdash.","spans":[{"text":"Given what we have learned so far in the previous section, the next question that must be asked is what is the "},{"text":"halachic ","italic":true},{"text":"status of the "},{"text":"mitzvah","italic":true},{"text":" of "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"today in "},{"text":"Eretz Yisrael","italic":true},{"text":"? As mentioned above, Rashi to the גמרא כתובות (2) implies that when the majority of Jews reside in "},{"text":"Eretz Yisrael","italic":true},{"text":", the "},{"text":"mitzvah ","italic":true},{"text":"of "},{"text":"hafrashas challah ","italic":true},{"text":"is biblical, which would have practical implications as well, such as requiring us to rule stringently about any "},{"text":"safek ","italic":true},{"text":"that arises. As of 2026, there are about 7.3 million Jews living in Israel and 8.5 million Jews living outside of Israel."},{"text":"[5]","footnote":5},{"text":" From the 8.5 million living outside of Israel, 37% in the US are Reform, many of whom were converted or born to a mother converted through the Reform movement, which would render them not "},{"text":"halachically","italic":true},{"text":" Jewish. Therefore, it stands to reason that even today, the majority of Jews in the world already reside in Israel. Even if these figures are not precise, in the next few years, it nevertheless appears likely that a majority of Jews will be living in Israel for the first time since the first Beis Ha’Mikdash."}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":36.0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[5],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":12,"xmlIndex":14,"text":"Furthermore, if we are to assume that the majority of Jews today are living in Israel (or if this will happen in the next few years), we must consider whether this affects the halachic status of shemittah and terumah as well as challah. One of the major leniencies relied upon by the Rabbanut in Israel during the shemittah year to permit the land to be worked is the ״היתר מכירה״ – selling the land to non-Jews. Bez”H, in a future SCP-zman we will discuss the details of this kula and whether it is a reliable one, but those who rely on the kula do so in part because shemittah today is rabbinic. If, however, by the next shemittah year, the majority of Jews would reside in Israel, would that abolish the validity of the heter mechirah? ","spans":[{"text":"Furthermore, if we are to assume that the majority of Jews today are living in Israel (or if this will happen in the next few years), we must consider whether this affects the "},{"text":"halachic","italic":true},{"text":" status of "},{"text":"shemittah ","italic":true},{"text":"and "},{"text":"terumah ","italic":true},{"text":"as well as "},{"text":"challah","italic":true},{"text":". One of the major leniencies relied upon by the "},{"text":"Rabbanut ","italic":true},{"text":"in Israel during the "},{"text":"shemittah ","italic":true},{"text":"year to permit the land to be worked is the ״היתר מכירה״ – selling the land to non-Jews. Bez”H, in a future SCP-"},{"text":"zman ","italic":true},{"text":"we will discuss the details of this "},{"text":"kula ","italic":true},{"text":"and whether it is a reliable one, but those who rely on the "},{"text":"kula ","italic":true},{"text":"do so in part because "},{"text":"shemittah ","italic":true},{"text":"today is rabbinic. If, however, by the next "},{"text":"shemittah ","italic":true},{"text":"year, the majority of Jews would reside in Israel, would that abolish the validity of the "},{"text":"heter mechirah","italic":true},{"text":"? "}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":36.0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":13,"xmlIndex":15,"text":"We will begin by discussing whether the mitzvah of shemittah becomes biblical when the majority of Jews reside in Israel. We will then examine the status of challah and terumah today.","spans":[{"text":"We will begin by discussing whether the "},{"text":"mitzvah ","italic":true},{"text":"of "},{"text":"shemittah ","italic":true},{"text":"becomes biblical when the majority of Jews reside in Israel. We will then examine the status of "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"and "},{"text":"terumah ","italic":true},{"text":"today."}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":36.0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}],"footnotes":[{"id":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":0,"xmlIndex":0,"text":" Based on Israel’s Central Bureau of Statistics (CBS). Thus, 46% of Jews currently live in Israel.","spans":[{"text":" Based on Israel’s Central Bureau of Statistics (CBS). Thus, 46% of Jews currently live in Israel."}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}]}]},"sort_order":5,"official_shiur_number":1,"estimated_minutes":5,"difficulty":"practical","tags":["challah-part-1","original-difficulty:practical","source:2"],"source_file_name":"Notes for Hafrashas Challah (part 1).docx","source_start_page":null,"source_end_page":null,"is_selectable":true},{"program_id":"winter-5787","chunk_code":"HC1-B2","source_type":"notes","siman":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","workbook_title":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","section_key":"B","section_title":"Status of Hafrashas Challah Today","chunk_title":"Shemittah Today and Yovel","chunk_summary":"Rambam: shemittah is biblical only when yovel applies, which requires Jews settled by shevet; therefore likely rabbinic until Mashiach.","content_markdown":"<u>I.</u> *<u>Shemittah</u>* <u>today</u>\n\nRegarding *shemittah,* the **רמב״ם** (הלכות שמיטה ויובל פ״י-ח) (4) *passkins* that *shemittah* is biblically mandated only when *yovel* (the 50th year, in which real estate that was sold returns to its  original owner and Jewish slaves are freed) is also in effect. However, *yovel* applies only when all (or at least the majority) of the Jews reside in their ancestral portion of the Land according to their *shevet*. Today, when most Jews do not know what *shevet* they belong to and are certainly not settled in their designated plot of land, *yovel* does not apply. Consequently, *shemittah* remains rabbinic in nature. Therefore, even if all the Jews were to return and live in *Eretz Yisrael*, *shemittah* would remain rabbinic until the coming of *Mashiach*, who will identify each tribe and restore every *shevet* to its designated portion of the Land.[6]\n\nSource Notes\n\n[6]:  However, the ציץ אליעזר (ח״י-א, כ) *paskens* like the *Rishonim* who disagree with the רמב״ם and maintain that dividing *Eretz Yisrael* according to the *shevatim* is required only for the reinstatement of *Yovel.* Regarding *shemittah,* however, once the majority of the Jewish people are living in *Eretz Yisrael,* the *mitzvah* of *shemittah* becomes biblical.","content_document":{"version":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":14,"xmlIndex":16,"text":"I. Shemittah today","spans":[{"text":"I. ","underline":true},{"text":"Shemittah ","italic":true,"underline":true},{"text":"today","underline":true}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":15,"xmlIndex":17,"text":"Regarding shemittah, the רמב״ם (הלכות שמיטה ויובל פ״י-ח) (4) passkins that shemittah is biblically mandated only when yovel (the 50th year, in which real estate that was sold returns to its  original owner and Jewish slaves are freed) is also in effect. However, yovel applies only when all (or at least the majority) of the Jews reside in their ancestral portion of the Land according to their shevet. Today, when most Jews do not know what shevet they belong to and are certainly not settled in their designated plot of land, yovel does not apply. Consequently, shemittah remains rabbinic in nature. Therefore, even if all the Jews were to return and live in Eretz Yisrael, shemittah would remain rabbinic until the coming of Mashiach, who will identify each tribe and restore every shevet to its designated portion of the Land.[6]","spans":[{"text":"Regarding "},{"text":"shemittah, ","italic":true},{"text":"the "},{"text":"רמב״ם","bold":true},{"text":" (הלכות שמיטה ויובל פ״י-ח) (4) "},{"text":"passkins ","italic":true},{"text":"that "},{"text":"shemittah ","italic":true},{"text":"is biblically mandated only when "},{"text":"yovel ","italic":true},{"text":"(the 50th year, in which real estate that was sold returns to its  original owner and Jewish slaves are freed) is also in effect. However, "},{"text":"yovel ","italic":true},{"text":"applies only when all (or at least the majority) of the Jews reside in their ancestral portion of the Land according to their "},{"text":"shevet","italic":true},{"text":". Today, when most Jews do not know what "},{"text":"shevet ","italic":true},{"text":"they belong to and are certainly not settled in their designated plot of land, "},{"text":"yovel ","italic":true},{"text":"does not apply. Consequently, "},{"text":"shemittah ","italic":true},{"text":"remains rabbinic in nature. Therefore, even if all the Jews were to return and live in "},{"text":"Eretz Yisrael","italic":true},{"text":", "},{"text":"shemittah","italic":true},{"text":" would remain rabbinic until the coming of "},{"text":"Mashiach","italic":true},{"text":", who will identify each tribe and restore every "},{"text":"shevet","italic":true},{"text":" to its designated portion of the Land."},{"text":"[6]","footnote":6}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":36.0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[6],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}],"footnotes":[{"id":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":0,"xmlIndex":0,"text":" However, the ציץ אליעזר (ח״י-א, כ) paskens like the Rishonim who disagree with the רמב״ם and maintain that dividing Eretz Yisrael according to the shevatim is required only for the reinstatement of Yovel. Regarding shemittah, however, once the majority of the Jewish people are living in Eretz Yisrael, the mitzvah of shemittah becomes biblical.","spans":[{"text":" However, the ציץ אליעזר (ח״י-א, כ) "},{"text":"paskens","italic":true},{"text":" like the "},{"text":"Rishonim","italic":true},{"text":" who disagree with the רמב״ם and maintain that dividing "},{"text":"Eretz Yisrael","italic":true},{"text":" according to the "},{"text":"shevatim","italic":true},{"text":" is required only for the reinstatement of "},{"text":"Yovel.","italic":true},{"text":" Regarding "},{"text":"shemittah,","italic":true},{"text":" however, once the majority of the Jewish people are living in "},{"text":"Eretz Yisrael,","italic":true},{"text":" the "},{"text":"mitzvah","italic":true},{"text":" of "},{"text":"shemittah","italic":true},{"text":" becomes biblical."}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}]}]},"sort_order":6,"official_shiur_number":1,"estimated_minutes":5,"difficulty":"advanced","tags":["challah-part-1","original-difficulty:advanced","source:4"],"source_file_name":"Notes for Hafrashas Challah (part 1).docx","source_start_page":null,"source_end_page":null,"is_selectable":true},{"program_id":"winter-5787","chunk_code":"HC1-B3","source_type":"notes","siman":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","workbook_title":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","section_key":"B","section_title":"Status of Hafrashas Challah Today","chunk_title":"Challah Today — Majority of Jews in Eretz Yisrael","chunk_summary":"Ritva and Be’er Basadeh say majority suffices; Chazon Ish suggests entry may need Hashem’s direction through king or Navi.","content_markdown":"<u>II.</u> *<u>Challah</u>* <u>today</u>\n\nRegarding *challah,* there is a dispute whether the *mitzvah* becomes biblical when the majority of Jews return to *Eretz Yisrael.* As opposed to *Shemittah, challah* is not connected to *yovel.* Rather, it became obligatory immediately after the Jewish nation entered *Eretz Yisrael,* even before the Land had been conquered and settled. Therefore, it would seem that the moment the majority of Jews enter Israel, even if the land isn’t officially conquered, the *mitzvah* should once again become biblical. In fact, the **ריטב״א** (כתובות כה. ד״ה בבואכם) (5) *passkins* that as soon as the majority of Jews reside in Israel, the *mitzvah* of *challah* in *Eretz Yisrael* becomes biblical again.\n\nFurthermore, even though the רמב״ם and S”A write that “all” Jews must return to the Land for the *mitzvah* to regain its biblical status, the **באר בשדה** (פ״א הע׳ ג) (6) maintains that the רמב״ם would agree that even a majority suffices. He explains that when עם ישראל initially conquered the land in the times of יהושע, not all of the Jews entered Israel, since the *shevatim* of Gad, Reuven, and half of Menashe stayed on the other side of the Jordan river. Granted, the men entered the land to fight, but that was only the fighting men ages 20-60, and even in that age group some stayed behind to protect the women and children who did not enter. Thus, even when the *mitzvah* of *challah* initially took effect, only the majority of Jews were in Israel, and at that moment in time, everyone agrees the *mitzvah* was biblical, so too, in our time, the *mitzvah* will become biblical the moment the majority of Jews reside in Israel. \n\nHowever, the **חזון איש** (שביעית כא-ה) (7) argues that even if the majority of Jews come to Israel, *challah* may nevertheless remain rabbinic, since the biblical obligation only goes into effect when two conditions are met: 1) the Jewish people enter the land under direction of a King or Navi, similar to when we entered the land of Israel the first time with יהושע. The **חלת לחם** (ס״א שירי ברכה-ה) suggests[7] that since with regard to *challah* the Torah commands ״אשר **אני** מביא אתכם שמה״ – “Where **I** am bringing you there,” that implies that the biblical *mitzvah* only goes into effect when the Jewish people enter with Hashem’s explicit direction (i.e., via a Navi or King). 2) The majority of Jews reside in the land. Today, only the second condition may be fulfilled, so the *mitzvah* would remain rabbinic. The חזון איש does entertain the possibility that the *mitzvah* may become biblical even without a king or Navi, but he ultimately appears to reject that.\n\nTo summarize, it seems from the ריטב״א and the באר בשדה that the *mitzvah* of *hafrashas challah* becomes biblical as soon as the majority of Jews reside in *Eretz Yisrael* (which may already have occurred). The חזון איש, however, suggests that the *mitzvah* might remain rabbinic until we enter the Land under the direction of a King or Navi.\n\nSource Notes\n\n[7]:  Written by Rav Simcha Yoel Rubinstein (passed away in 1863), who was a Rav and *dayan* in Boberka, Ukraine. ","content_document":{"version":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":16,"xmlIndex":18,"text":"II. Challah today","spans":[{"text":"II. ","underline":true},{"text":"Challah ","italic":true,"underline":true},{"text":"today","underline":true}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":17,"xmlIndex":19,"text":"Regarding challah, there is a dispute whether the mitzvah becomes biblical when the majority of Jews return to Eretz Yisrael. As opposed to Shemittah, challah is not connected to yovel. Rather, it became obligatory immediately after the Jewish nation entered Eretz Yisrael, even before the Land had been conquered and settled. Therefore, it would seem that the moment the majority of Jews enter Israel, even if the land isn’t officially conquered, the mitzvah should once again become biblical. In fact, the ריטב״א (כתובות כה. ד״ה בבואכם) (5) passkins that as soon as the majority of Jews reside in Israel, the mitzvah of challah in Eretz Yisrael becomes biblical again.","spans":[{"text":"Regarding "},{"text":"challah, ","italic":true},{"text":"there is a dispute whether the "},{"text":"mitzvah ","italic":true},{"text":"becomes biblical when the majority of Jews return to "},{"text":"Eretz Yisrael. ","italic":true},{"text":"As opposed to "},{"text":"Shemittah, challah","italic":true},{"text":" is not connected to "},{"text":"yovel. ","italic":true},{"text":"Rather, it became obligatory immediately after the Jewish nation entered "},{"text":"Eretz Yisrael, ","italic":true},{"text":"even before the Land had been conquered and settled. Therefore, it would seem that the moment the majority of Jews enter Israel, even if the land isn’t officially conquered, the "},{"text":"mitzvah ","italic":true},{"text":"should once again become biblical. In fact, the "},{"text":"ריטב״א ","bold":true},{"text":"(כתובות כה. ד״ה בבואכם) (5) "},{"text":"passkins ","italic":true},{"text":"that as soon as the majority of Jews reside in Israel, the "},{"text":"mitzvah ","italic":true},{"text":"of "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"in "},{"text":"Eretz Yisrael ","italic":true},{"text":"becomes biblical again."}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":36.0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":18,"xmlIndex":20,"text":"Furthermore, even though the רמב״ם and S”A write that “all” Jews must return to the Land for the mitzvah to regain its biblical status, the באר בשדה (פ״א הע׳ ג) (6) maintains that the רמב״ם would agree that even a majority suffices. He explains that when עם ישראל initially conquered the land in the times of יהושע, not all of the Jews entered Israel, since the shevatim of Gad, Reuven, and half of Menashe stayed on the other side of the Jordan river. Granted, the men entered the land to fight, but that was only the fighting men ages 20-60, and even in that age group some stayed behind to protect the women and children who did not enter. Thus, even when the mitzvah of challah initially took effect, only the majority of Jews were in Israel, and at that moment in time, everyone agrees the mitzvah was biblical, so too, in our time, the mitzvah will become biblical the moment the majority of Jews reside in Israel. ","spans":[{"text":"Furthermore, even though the רמב״ם and S”A write that “all” Jews must return to the Land for the "},{"text":"mitzvah ","italic":true},{"text":"to regain its biblical status, the "},{"text":"באר בשדה ","bold":true},{"text":"(פ״א הע׳ ג) (6) maintains that the רמב״ם would agree that even a majority suffices. He explains that when עם ישראל initially conquered the land in the times of יהושע, not all of the Jews entered Israel, since the "},{"text":"shevatim ","italic":true},{"text":"of Gad, Reuven, and half of Menashe stayed on the other side of the Jordan river. Granted, the men entered the land to fight, but that was only the fighting men ages 20-60, and even in that age group some stayed behind to protect the women and children who did not enter. Thus, even when the "},{"text":"mitzvah ","italic":true},{"text":"of "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"initially took effect, only the majority of Jews were in Israel, and at that moment in time, everyone agrees the "},{"text":"mitzvah ","italic":true},{"text":"was biblical, so too, in our time, the "},{"text":"mitzvah ","italic":true},{"text":"will become biblical the moment the majority of Jews reside in Israel. "}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":36.0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":19,"xmlIndex":21,"text":"However, the חזון איש (שביעית כא-ה) (7) argues that even if the majority of Jews come to Israel, challah may nevertheless remain rabbinic, since the biblical obligation only goes into effect when two conditions are met: 1) the Jewish people enter the land under direction of a King or Navi, similar to when we entered the land of Israel the first time with יהושע. The חלת לחם (ס״א שירי ברכה-ה) suggests[7] that since with regard to challah the Torah commands ״אשר אני מביא אתכם שמה״ – “Where I am bringing you there,” that implies that the biblical mitzvah only goes into effect when the Jewish people enter with Hashem’s explicit direction (i.e., via a Navi or King). 2) The majority of Jews reside in the land. Today, only the second condition may be fulfilled, so the mitzvah would remain rabbinic. The חזון איש does entertain the possibility that the mitzvah may become biblical even without a king or Navi, but he ultimately appears to reject that.","spans":[{"text":"However, the "},{"text":"חזון איש ","bold":true},{"text":"(שביעית כא-ה) (7) argues that even if the majority of Jews come to Israel, "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"may nevertheless remain rabbinic, since the biblical obligation only goes into effect when two conditions are met: 1) the Jewish people enter the land under direction of a King or Navi, similar to when we entered the land of Israel the first time with יהושע. The "},{"text":"חלת לחם","bold":true},{"text":" (ס״א שירי ברכה-ה) suggests"},{"text":"[7]","footnote":7},{"text":" that since with regard to "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"the Torah commands ״אשר "},{"text":"אני","bold":true},{"text":" מביא אתכם שמה״ – “Where "},{"text":"I ","bold":true},{"text":"am bringing you there,” that implies that the biblical "},{"text":"mitzvah ","italic":true},{"text":"only goes into effect when the Jewish people enter with Hashem’s explicit direction (i.e., via a Navi or King). 2) The majority of Jews reside in the land. Today, only the second condition may be fulfilled, so the "},{"text":"mitzvah ","italic":true},{"text":"would remain rabbinic. The חזון איש does entertain the possibility that the "},{"text":"mitzvah","italic":true},{"text":" may become biblical even without a king or Navi, but he ultimately appears to reject that."}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":36.0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[7],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":20,"xmlIndex":22,"text":"To summarize, it seems from the ריטב״א and the באר בשדה that the mitzvah of hafrashas challah becomes biblical as soon as the majority of Jews reside in Eretz Yisrael (which may already have occurred). The חזון איש, however, suggests that the mitzvah might remain rabbinic until we enter the Land under the direction of a King or Navi.","spans":[{"text":"To summarize, it seems from the ריטב״א and the באר בשדה that the "},{"text":"mitzvah ","italic":true},{"text":"of "},{"text":"hafrashas challah ","italic":true},{"text":"becomes biblical as soon as the majority of Jews reside in "},{"text":"Eretz Yisrael ","italic":true},{"text":"(which may already have occurred). The חזון איש, however, suggests that the "},{"text":"mitzvah ","italic":true},{"text":"might remain rabbinic until we enter the Land under the direction of a King or Navi."}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":36.0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}],"footnotes":[{"id":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":0,"xmlIndex":0,"text":" Written by Rav Simcha Yoel Rubinstein (passed away in 1863), who was a Rav and dayan in Boberka, Ukraine. ","spans":[{"text":" Written by Rav Simcha Yoel Rubinstein (passed away in 1863), who was a Rav and "},{"text":"dayan ","italic":true},{"text":"in Boberka, Ukraine. "}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}]}]},"sort_order":7,"official_shiur_number":1,"estimated_minutes":8,"difficulty":"core","tags":["challah-part-1","original-difficulty:core","source:5","source:6","source:7"],"source_file_name":"Notes for Hafrashas Challah (part 1).docx","source_start_page":null,"source_end_page":null,"is_selectable":true},{"program_id":"winter-5787","chunk_code":"HC1-B4","source_type":"notes","siman":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","workbook_title":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","section_key":"B","section_title":"Status of Hafrashas Challah Today","chunk_title":"Terumah Today","chunk_summary":"Rambam vs Raavad; Chazon Ish distinguishes terumah from challah; Rav Chaim Kanievsky raises issue of the ten lost tribes.","content_markdown":"<u>III.</u> *<u>Terumah</u>* <u>today</u>\n\nRegarding *terumah,* the **רמב״ם** (תרומות פ״א-כו) (8) equates its status with that of *challah*, that separating *terumah* will only be biblical once the majority of Jews are living in the Land. The **ראב״ד** disagrees, maintaining that *terumah* is not equated to *challah* and the *mitzvah* today is biblical even if the majority of Jews are not living in the land. The S”A in **סימן שלא-ב** *passkins* like the רמב״ם. Although the *Rama* quotes the ראב״ד, he agrees *lema’aseh* with the *pssak* of the S”A that *terumah* is only rabbinic today. Based on what we learned regarding *challah,* one would think that according to the ריטב״א and באר בשדה, the *mitzvah* of *terumah* would also become biblical once the majority of Jews reside in the Land, whereas the חזון איש would argue that it would remain rabbinic. In truth, however, the **חזון איש** (שביעית כא-ה) (9) differentiates between *challah* and *terumah,* suggesting that the רמב״ם agrees that if the majority of Jews return to *Eretz* *Yisrael*, the *mitzvah* of *terumah* becomes biblical. \n\nThe חזון איש explains that although the condition of entering the Land under the direction of a king or *navi* may be needed regarding *challah,* this is not the case with regard to *terumah*. Nevertheless, two conditions are still necessary for *terumah* to become biblical: 1) The Land is sanctified, 2) the majority of Jews live in the Land. When Ezra returned to *Eretz Yisrael* before the second *Beis Hamikdash* was built, he sanctified the Land a second time, so the first condition has already been fulfilled.[8] Therefore, as soon as the majority of Jews live in *Eretz Yisrael*, *terumah* becomes biblical, even if *Mashiach* has not yet arrived. \n\nWhat emerges from here is that as soon as the majority of Jews live in Israel, a milestone that may have already been reached or will likely be reached in the near future, all will agree that the *mitzvah* of *terumah* becomes biblical. This would significantly increase the severity of the *mitzvah* and the importance of ensuring that it is performed properly. Therefore, when purchasing fruits or vegetables grown in *Eretz Yisrael,* one should make sure that the store has a reliable *hechsher,* or know how to properly be *mafrish terumos u’maasros* oneself.\n\nNevertheless, it is still possible that *terumah* will remain rabbinic for the foreseeable future. **Rav Chaim Kanievsky** in דרך אמונה (תרומות פ״א-כו בה״ל ד״ה וכן) (10) suggests that since the ten tribes are still lost, the majority of the Jews may still not reside in Israel. Furthermore, we may add that even without considering the lost tribes, accurately determining the world’s Jewish population is extremely difficult because if a Jewish woman becomes unaffiliated, her daughters and all future female descendants remain Jewish, but they are unaccounted for whenever a census is conducted. Similarly, in the world-wide census, many are counted as Jewish, even though their father alone is Jewish, and they are *halachically* defined as non-Jews.\n\nSource Notes\n\n[8]:  There is a dispute in the גמרא יבמות (פב:) whether this sanctification was nullified with the destruction of the second *Beis Ha’Mikdash* (קדושה שניה קידשה לשעתה או לעתיד לבוא), and the רמב״ם *passkins* like the opinion that the sanctification of the land of Israel by Ezra was eternal.","content_document":{"version":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":21,"xmlIndex":23,"text":"III. Terumah today","spans":[{"text":"III. ","underline":true},{"text":"Terumah ","italic":true,"underline":true},{"text":"today","underline":true}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":22,"xmlIndex":24,"text":"Regarding terumah, the רמב״ם (תרומות פ״א-כו) (8) equates its status with that of challah, that separating terumah will only be biblical once the majority of Jews are living in the Land. The ראב״ד disagrees, maintaining that terumah is not equated to challah and the mitzvah today is biblical even if the majority of Jews are not living in the land. The S”A in סימן שלא-ב passkins like the רמב״ם. Although the Rama quotes the ראב״ד, he agrees lema’aseh with the pssak of the S”A that terumah is only rabbinic today. Based on what we learned regarding challah, one would think that according to the ריטב״א and באר בשדה, the mitzvah of terumah would also become biblical once the majority of Jews reside in the Land, whereas the חזון איש would argue that it would remain rabbinic. In truth, however, the חזון איש (שביעית כא-ה) (9) differentiates between challah and terumah, suggesting that the רמב״ם agrees that if the majority of Jews return to Eretz Yisrael, the mitzvah of terumah becomes biblical. ","spans":[{"text":"Regarding "},{"text":"terumah, ","italic":true},{"text":"the "},{"text":"רמב״ם ","bold":true},{"text":"(תרומות פ״א-כו)"},{"text":" ","bold":true},{"text":"(8) equates its status with that of "},{"text":"challah","italic":true},{"text":", that separating "},{"text":"terumah","italic":true},{"text":" will only be biblical once the majority of Jews are living in the Land. The "},{"text":"ראב״ד","bold":true},{"text":" disagrees, maintaining that "},{"text":"terumah ","italic":true},{"text":"is not equated to "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"and the "},{"text":"mitzvah ","italic":true},{"text":"today is biblical even if the majority of Jews are not living in the land. The S”A in "},{"text":"סימן שלא-ב ","bold":true},{"text":"passkins ","italic":true},{"text":"like the רמב״ם. Although the "},{"text":"Rama ","italic":true},{"text":"quotes the ראב״ד, he agrees "},{"text":"lema’aseh ","italic":true},{"text":"with the "},{"text":"pssak ","italic":true},{"text":"of the S”A that "},{"text":"terumah ","italic":true},{"text":"is only rabbinic today."},{"text":" ","italic":true},{"text":"Based on what we learned regarding "},{"text":"challah, ","italic":true},{"text":"one would think that according to the ריטב״א and באר בשדה, the "},{"text":"mitzvah ","italic":true},{"text":"of "},{"text":"terumah ","italic":true},{"text":"would also become biblical once the majority of Jews reside in the Land, whereas the חזון איש would argue that it would remain rabbinic. In truth, however, the "},{"text":"חזון איש ","bold":true},{"text":"(שביעית כא-ה) (9) differentiates between "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"and "},{"text":"terumah, ","italic":true},{"text":"suggesting that"},{"text":" ","italic":true},{"text":"the רמב״ם agrees that if the majority of Jews return to "},{"text":"Eretz","italic":true},{"text":" "},{"text":"Yisrael","italic":true},{"text":", the "},{"text":"mitzvah ","italic":true},{"text":"of "},{"text":"terumah ","italic":true},{"text":"becomes biblical. "}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":36.0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":23,"xmlIndex":25,"text":"The חזון איש explains that although the condition of entering the Land under the direction of a king or navi may be needed regarding challah, this is not the case with regard to terumah. Nevertheless, two conditions are still necessary for terumah to become biblical: 1) The Land is sanctified, 2) the majority of Jews live in the Land. When Ezra returned to Eretz Yisrael before the second Beis Hamikdash was built, he sanctified the Land a second time, so the first condition has already been fulfilled.[8] Therefore, as soon as the majority of Jews live in Eretz Yisrael, terumah becomes biblical, even if Mashiach has not yet arrived. ","spans":[{"text":"The חזון איש explains that although the condition of entering the Land under the direction of a king or "},{"text":"navi","italic":true},{"text":" may be needed regarding "},{"text":"challah,","italic":true},{"text":" this is not the case with regard to "},{"text":"terumah","italic":true},{"text":". Nevertheless, two conditions are still necessary for "},{"text":"terumah","italic":true},{"text":" to become biblical: 1) The Land is sanctified, 2) the majority of Jews live in the Land. When Ezra returned to "},{"text":"Eretz Yisrael ","italic":true},{"text":"before the second "},{"text":"Beis Hamikdash ","italic":true},{"text":"was built, he sanctified the Land a second time, so the first condition has already been fulfilled."},{"text":"[8]","footnote":8},{"text":" Therefore, as soon as the majority of Jews live in "},{"text":"Eretz Yisrael","italic":true},{"text":", "},{"text":"terumah ","italic":true},{"text":"becomes biblical, even if "},{"text":"Mashiach ","italic":true},{"text":"has not yet arrived. "}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":36.0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[8],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":24,"xmlIndex":26,"text":"What emerges from here is that as soon as the majority of Jews live in Israel, a milestone that may have already been reached or will likely be reached in the near future, all will agree that the mitzvah of terumah becomes biblical. This would significantly increase the severity of the mitzvah and the importance of ensuring that it is performed properly. Therefore, when purchasing fruits or vegetables grown in Eretz Yisrael, one should make sure that the store has a reliable hechsher, or know how to properly be mafrish terumos u’maasros oneself.","spans":[{"text":"What emerges from here is that as soon as the majority of Jews live in Israel, a milestone that may have already been reached or will likely be reached in the near future, all will agree that the "},{"text":"mitzvah ","italic":true},{"text":"of "},{"text":"terumah","italic":true},{"text":" becomes biblical. This would significantly increase the severity of the "},{"text":"mitzvah","italic":true},{"text":" and the importance of ensuring that it is performed properly. Therefore, when purchasing fruits or vegetables grown in "},{"text":"Eretz Yisrael,","italic":true},{"text":" one should make sure that the store has a reliable "},{"text":"hechsher,","italic":true},{"text":" or know how to properly be "},{"text":"mafrish terumos u’maasros","italic":true},{"text":" oneself."}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":36.0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":25,"xmlIndex":27,"text":"Nevertheless, it is still possible that terumah will remain rabbinic for the foreseeable future. Rav Chaim Kanievsky in דרך אמונה (תרומות פ״א-כו בה״ל ד״ה וכן) (10) suggests that since the ten tribes are still lost, the majority of the Jews may still not reside in Israel. Furthermore, we may add that even without considering the lost tribes, accurately determining the world’s Jewish population is extremely difficult because if a Jewish woman becomes unaffiliated, her daughters and all future female descendants remain Jewish, but they are unaccounted for whenever a census is conducted. Similarly, in the world-wide census, many are counted as Jewish, even though their father alone is Jewish, and they are halachically defined as non-Jews.","spans":[{"text":"Nevertheless, it is still possible that "},{"text":"terumah ","italic":true},{"text":"will remain rabbinic for the foreseeable future. "},{"text":"Rav Chaim Kanievsky","bold":true},{"text":" in דרך אמונה (תרומות פ״א-כו בה״ל ד״ה וכן) (10) suggests that since the ten tribes are still lost, the majority of the Jews may still not reside in Israel. Furthermore, we may add that even without considering the lost tribes, accurately determining the world’s Jewish population is extremely difficult because if a Jewish woman becomes unaffiliated, her daughters and all future female descendants remain Jewish, but they are unaccounted for whenever a census is conducted. Similarly, in the world-wide census, many are counted as Jewish, even though their father alone is Jewish, and they are "},{"text":"halachically","italic":true},{"text":" defined as non-Jews."}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":36.0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}],"footnotes":[{"id":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":0,"xmlIndex":0,"text":" There is a dispute in the גמרא יבמות (פב:) whether this sanctification was nullified with the destruction of the second Beis Ha’Mikdash (קדושה שניה קידשה לשעתה או לעתיד לבוא), and the רמב״ם passkins like the opinion that the sanctification of the land of Israel by Ezra was eternal.","spans":[{"text":" There is a dispute in the גמרא יבמות (פב:) whether this sanctification was nullified with the destruction of the second "},{"text":"Beis Ha’Mikdash","italic":true},{"text":" (קדושה שניה קידשה לשעתה או לעתיד לבוא), and the רמב״ם "},{"text":"passkins ","italic":true},{"text":"like the opinion that the sanctification of the land of Israel by Ezra was eternal."}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}]}]},"sort_order":8,"official_shiur_number":1,"estimated_minutes":8,"difficulty":"advanced","tags":["challah-part-1","original-difficulty:advanced","source:8","source:9","source:10"],"source_file_name":"Notes for Hafrashas Challah (part 1).docx","source_start_page":null,"source_end_page":null,"is_selectable":true},{"program_id":"winter-5787","chunk_code":"HC1-B5","source_type":"notes","siman":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","workbook_title":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","section_key":"B","section_title":"Status of Hafrashas Challah Today","chunk_title":"Summary of Status Today","chunk_summary":"Summary bullets: biblical root of challah, current rabbinic status, possible majority-Jews implications, shemittah, terumah, and ten tribes.","content_markdown":"To summarize:\n\n- There is a biblical *mitzvah* to give a portion of one’s dough to a *kohen.*\n\n- The biblical *mitzvah* only applies in Eretz Yisrael, and only when the majority of Jews reside there; otherwise, the obligation is rabbinic.\n\n  - Today, if the majority of Jews are already living in *Eretz Yisrael* (or if this occurs in the near future), some say the *mitzvah* is biblical. But the חזון איש suggests that it may remain rabbinic because perhaps the land needs to be entered by Hashem’s direction, via a king or Navi.\n\n- The *mitzvah* of *hafrashas challah* applies rabbinically in *chutz la’aretz* so that it should not be forgotten*.*\n\n- The biblical obligation of *shemittah* will only go into effect when the land is settled based on the *shevatim* and *yovel* applies once again*.*\n\n- According to many *Rishonim* (the ראב״ד and טור), the *mitzvah* of *teruma* today is biblical, though the S”A *passkins* like the רמב״ם that it is rabbinic.\n\n  - The חזון איש agrees regarding *teruma* that if the majority of Jews return to Israel, the *mitzvah* of *teruma* will again become biblical.\n\n  - R. Chaim Kanievsky suggests that the status of “majority of Jews living in Israel” might be dependent on the return of the 10 lost tribes.","content_document":{"version":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":26,"xmlIndex":28,"text":"To summarize:","spans":[{"text":"To summarize:"}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"list","paragraph":27,"xmlIndex":29,"text":"There is a biblical mitzvah to give a portion of one’s dough to a kohen.","spans":[{"text":"There is a biblical "},{"text":"mitzvah ","italic":true},{"text":"to give a portion of one’s dough to a "},{"text":"kohen.","italic":true}],"marker":"•","level":0,"indent":36.0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0,"hanging":18.0},{"kind":"list","paragraph":28,"xmlIndex":30,"text":"The biblical mitzvah only applies in Eretz Yisrael, and only when the majority of Jews reside there; otherwise, the obligation is rabbinic.","spans":[{"text":"The biblical "},{"text":"mitzvah ","italic":true},{"text":"only applies in Eretz Yisrael, and only when the majority of Jews reside there; otherwise, the obligation is rabbinic."}],"marker":"•","level":0,"indent":36.0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0,"hanging":18.0},{"kind":"list","paragraph":29,"xmlIndex":31,"text":"Today, if the majority of Jews are already living in Eretz Yisrael (or if this occurs in the near future), some say the mitzvah is biblical. But the חזון איש suggests that it may remain rabbinic because perhaps the land needs to be entered by Hashem’s direction, via a king or Navi. ","spans":[{"text":"Today, if the majority of Jews are already living in "},{"text":"Eretz Yisrael","italic":true},{"text":" (or if this occurs in the near future), some say the "},{"text":"mitzvah ","italic":true},{"text":"is biblical. But the חזון איש suggests"},{"text":" ","italic":true},{"text":"that"},{"text":" ","italic":true},{"text":"it may remain rabbinic because perhaps the land needs to be entered by Hashem’s direction, via a king or Navi. "}],"marker":"o","level":1,"indent":72.0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0,"hanging":18.0},{"kind":"list","paragraph":30,"xmlIndex":32,"text":"The mitzvah of hafrashas challah applies rabbinically in chutz la’aretz so that it should not be forgotten.","spans":[{"text":"The "},{"text":"mitzvah ","italic":true},{"text":"of "},{"text":"hafrashas challah ","italic":true},{"text":"applies rabbinically in "},{"text":"chutz la’aretz ","italic":true},{"text":"so that it should not be forgotten"},{"text":".","italic":true}],"marker":"•","level":0,"indent":36.0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0,"hanging":18.0},{"kind":"list","paragraph":31,"xmlIndex":33,"text":"The biblical obligation of shemittah will only go into effect when the land is settled based on the shevatim and yovel applies once again.","spans":[{"text":"The biblical obligation of "},{"text":"shemittah ","italic":true},{"text":"will only go into effect when the land is settled based on the "},{"text":"shevatim ","italic":true},{"text":"and "},{"text":"yovel ","italic":true},{"text":"applies once again"},{"text":".","italic":true}],"marker":"•","level":0,"indent":36.0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0,"hanging":18.0},{"kind":"list","paragraph":32,"xmlIndex":34,"text":"According to many Rishonim (the ראב״ד and טור), the mitzvah of teruma today is biblical, though the S”A passkins like the רמב״ם that it is rabbinic.","spans":[{"text":"According to many "},{"text":"Rishonim ","italic":true},{"text":"(the ראב״ד and טור), the "},{"text":"mitzvah ","italic":true},{"text":"of "},{"text":"teruma ","italic":true},{"text":"today is biblical, though the S”A "},{"text":"passkins ","italic":true},{"text":"like the רמב״ם"},{"text":" ","italic":true},{"text":"that it is rabbinic."}],"marker":"•","level":0,"indent":36.0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0,"hanging":18.0},{"kind":"list","paragraph":33,"xmlIndex":35,"text":"The חזון איש agrees regarding teruma that if the majority of Jews return to Israel, the mitzvah of teruma will again become biblical. ","spans":[{"text":"The חזון איש agrees regarding "},{"text":"teruma","italic":true},{"text":" that if the majority of Jews return to Israel, the "},{"text":"mitzvah ","italic":true},{"text":"of "},{"text":"teruma","italic":true},{"text":" will again become biblical. "}],"marker":"o","level":1,"indent":72.0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0,"hanging":18.0},{"kind":"list","paragraph":34,"xmlIndex":36,"text":"R. Chaim Kanievsky suggests that the status of “majority of Jews living in Israel” might be dependent on the return of the 10 lost tribes.","spans":[{"text":"R. Chaim Kanievsky suggests that the status of “majority of Jews living in Israel” might be dependent on the return of the 10 lost tribes."}],"marker":"o","level":1,"indent":72.0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0,"hanging":18.0}],"footnotes":[]},"sort_order":9,"official_shiur_number":1,"estimated_minutes":4,"difficulty":"core","tags":["challah-part-1","original-difficulty:core"],"source_file_name":"Notes for Hafrashas Challah (part 1).docx","source_start_page":null,"source_end_page":null,"is_selectable":true},{"program_id":"winter-5787","chunk_code":"HC1-C1","source_type":"notes","siman":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","workbook_title":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","section_key":"C","section_title":"Differences Between Challah of Eretz Yisrael and Chutz La’aretz","chunk_title":"Three Differences Between EY and Chu”l Challah","chunk_summary":"Introduces why EY challah is treated more strictly than chu”l and lists three differences: bitul, eating before hafrasha, and min hamukaf.","content_markdown":"### C. *Differences Between Challah of Eretz Yisrael and Chutz La’aretz*\n\nWe learned above that the majority of *posskim* maintain that the *mitzvah* of *hafrashas* *challah*, both in *Eretz Yisrael* and in *chutz la’aretz*, is currently rabbinic in nature. Nevertheless, there are a few situations in which the *posskim* are more *machmir* concerning *challah* in *Eretz Yisrael* than concerning *challah* in *chutz la’aretz.* The reason for this distinction is that the *mitzvah* of *hafrashas challah* in *Eretz Yisrael* is fundamentally biblical in nature, and is only rabbinic today because the majority of Jews do not currently reside in Israel. In contrast, the rabbinic *mitzvah* of *hafrashas challah* in *chutz la’aretz* was only enacted so that it would not be forgotten. We will list three practical distinctions between *challah* in *Eretz Yisrael* and *challah* in *chutz la’aretz* and then apply these principles to some practical situations. \n\n1) The S”A in **סימן שכג** (11) *passkins* that if *challah* separated in *chutz la’aretz* becomes mixed with permitted dough, the mixture is permitted even when the ratio is only 50-50. Furthermore, one may deliberately be *mevatel challah* of *chutz la’aretz.* The *Rama* argues on both points and *passkins* that if *challah* is mixed with other permissible dough, a ratio of 100:1 is required to permit the mixture. If it is mixed with a different type of food (i.e., מין בשאינו מינו), *shishim* is required. Furthermore, one is not allowed to intentionally be *mevatel* a rabbinic prohibition (see *Rama* סימן צט-ו). Thus, according to the S”A, *challah* in *chutz la’aretz* is treated far more leniently than *challah* in *Eretz Yisrael*.\n\n2) We will learn below that *challah* should ideally be separated from the dough before it is baked. However, if one forgot, he may be *mafrish challah* after the bread is baked. The S”A (end of **סימן שכג-א**) *passkins,* based on the **גמרא ביצה** (ט.), that in *chutz la’aretz,* if one baked bread without being *mafrish challah,* he may eat the bread, provided he leaves over a small amount as *challah*. The *Rama* explains that one must leave enough bread so that after separating the *challah,* some bread still remains. In other words, the entire remaining piece may not be designated as *challah*; rather, a piece from it is removed as *challah.* In Eretz Yisrael, by contrast, there is no such provision of eating first and being *mafrish* later.\n\n3) In *chutz la’aretz,* if the dough was divided into multiple loaves and only some are present, one may be *mafrish challah* from the loaves that are present and thereby exempt even the ones not physically present. In *Eretz Yisrael,* however, one should only be *mafrish challah* from bread that is physically present. This concept is known as הפרשה שלא מן המוקף. As we will learn below, if one is *mafrish* שלא מן המוקף in *Eretz Yisrael,* it is still valid after the fact. The *Rama* clarifies that even in *chutz la’aretz,* a *hafrasha* from one loaf of bread to another that is not present is valid only if they originated from the same batch of dough. If they were made from different batches, each of which is independently obligated in *hafrashas challah,* then even in *chutz la’aretz,* one may not be *mafrish* from one to the other if they are in separate locations.","content_document":{"version":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"heading","paragraph":35,"xmlIndex":37,"text":" Differences Between Challah of Eretz Yisrael and Chutz La’aretz","spans":[{"text":" Differences Between Challah of Eretz Yisrael and Chutz La’aretz","italic":true,"color":"#2F5496"}],"marker":"C.","level":0,"indent":56.0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0,"rightIndent":43.2,"hanging":18.0,"tabStops":[{"position":150.45,"alignment":"left","leader":"none"},{"position":234.0,"alignment":"center","leader":"none"},{"position":279.0,"alignment":"left","leader":"none"}]},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":36,"xmlIndex":39,"text":"We learned above that the majority of posskim maintain that the mitzvah of hafrashas challah, both in Eretz Yisrael and in chutz la’aretz, is currently rabbinic in nature. Nevertheless, there are a few situations in which the posskim are more machmir concerning challah in Eretz Yisrael than concerning challah in chutz la’aretz. The reason for this distinction is that the mitzvah of hafrashas challah in Eretz Yisrael is fundamentally biblical in nature, and is only rabbinic today because the majority of Jews do not currently reside in Israel. In contrast, the rabbinic mitzvah of hafrashas challah in chutz la’aretz was only enacted so that it would not be forgotten. We will list three practical distinctions between challah in Eretz Yisrael and challah in chutz la’aretz and then apply these principles to some practical situations. ","spans":[{"text":"We learned above that the majority of "},{"text":"posskim ","italic":true},{"text":"maintain that the "},{"text":"mitzvah","italic":true},{"text":" of "},{"tex