vatel challah of chutz la’aretz. The Rama argues on both points and passkins that if challah is mixed with other permissible dough, a ratio of 100:1 is required to permit the mixture. If it is mixed with a different type of food (i.e., מין בשאינו מינו), shishim is required. Furthermore, one is not allowed to intentionally be mevatel a rabbinic prohibition (see Rama סימן צט-ו). Thus, according to the S”A, challah in chutz la’aretz is treated far more leniently than challah in Eretz Yisrael.","spans":[{"text":"The S”A in "},{"text":"סימן שכג","bold":true},{"text":" (11) "},{"text":"passkins ","italic":true},{"text":"that if "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"separated in "},{"text":"chutz la’aretz ","italic":true},{"text":"becomes mixed with permitted dough, the mixture is permitted even when the ratio is only 50-50. Furthermore, one may deliberately be "},{"text":"mevatel challah ","italic":true},{"text":"of "},{"text":"chutz la’aretz. ","italic":true},{"text":"The "},{"text":"Rama ","italic":true},{"text":"argues on both points and "},{"text":"passkins ","italic":true},{"text":"that if "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"is mixed with other permissible dough, a ratio of 100:1 is required to permit the mixture. If it is mixed with a different type of food (i.e., מין בשאינו מינו), "},{"text":"shishim ","italic":true},{"text":"is required. Furthermore, one is not allowed to intentionally be "},{"text":"mevatel ","italic":true},{"text":"a rabbinic prohibition (see "},{"text":"Rama ","italic":true},{"text":"סימן צט-ו). Thus, according to the S”A, "},{"text":"challah","italic":true},{"text":" in "},{"text":"chutz la’aretz","italic":true},{"text":" is treated far more leniently than "},{"text":"challah","italic":true},{"text":" in "},{"text":"Eretz Yisrael","italic":true},{"text":"."}],"marker":"1)","level":0,"indent":54.0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0,"hanging":18.0},{"kind":"list","paragraph":38,"xmlIndex":41,"text":"We will learn below that challah should ideally be separated from the dough before it is baked. However, if one forgot, he may be mafrish challah after the bread is baked. The S”A (end of סימן שכג-א) passkins, based on the גמרא ביצה (ט.), that in chutz la’aretz, if one baked bread without being mafrish challah, he may eat the bread, provided he leaves over a small amount as challah. The Rama explains that one must leave enough bread so that after separating the challah, some bread still remains. In other words, the entire remaining piece may not be designated as challah; rather, a piece from it is removed as challah. In Eretz Yisrael, by contrast, there is no such provision of eating first and being mafrish later.","spans":[{"text":"We will learn below that "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"should ideally be separated from the dough before it is baked. However, if one forgot, he may be "},{"text":"mafrish challah ","italic":true},{"text":"after the bread is baked. The S”A (end of "},{"text":"סימן שכג-א","bold":true},{"text":")"},{"text":" ","bold":true},{"text":"passkins, ","italic":true},{"text":"based on the "},{"text":"גמרא ביצה ","bold":true},{"text":"(ט.), that in "},{"text":"chutz la’aretz, ","italic":true},{"text":"if one baked bread without being "},{"text":"mafrish challah, ","italic":true},{"text":"he may eat the bread, provided he leaves over a small amount as "},{"text":"challah","italic":true},{"text":". The "},{"text":"Rama ","italic":true},{"text":"explains that one must leave enough bread so that after separating the "},{"text":"challah, ","italic":true},{"text":"some bread still remains. In other words, the entire remaining piece may not be designated as "},{"text":"challah","italic":true},{"text":";"},{"text":" ","italic":true},{"text":"rather, a piece from it is removed as "},{"text":"challah. ","italic":true},{"text":"In Eretz Yisrael, by contrast, there is no such provision of eating first and being "},{"text":"mafrish","italic":true},{"text":" later."}],"marker":"2)","level":0,"indent":54.0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0,"hanging":18.0},{"kind":"list","paragraph":39,"xmlIndex":42,"text":"In chutz la’aretz, if the dough was divided into multiple loaves and only some are present, one may be mafrish challah from the loaves that are present and thereby exempt even the ones not physically present. In Eretz Yisrael, however, one should only be mafrish challah from bread that is physically present. This concept is known as הפרשה שלא מן המוקף. As we will learn below, if one is mafrish שלא מן המוקף in Eretz Yisrael, it is still valid after the fact. The Rama clarifies that even in chutz la’aretz, a hafrasha from one loaf of bread to another that is not present is valid only if they originated from the same batch of dough. If they were made from different batches, each of which is independently obligated in hafrashas challah, then even in chutz la’aretz, one may not be mafrish from one to the other if they are in separate locations. ","spans":[{"text":"In "},{"text":"chutz la’aretz, ","italic":true},{"text":"if the dough was divided into multiple"},{"text":" ","italic":true},{"text":"loaves and only some are present, one may be "},{"text":"mafrish challah","italic":true},{"text":" from the loaves that are present and thereby exempt even the ones not physically present. In "},{"text":"Eretz Yisrael, ","italic":true},{"text":"however, one should only be "},{"text":"mafrish challah ","italic":true},{"text":"from bread that is physically present. This concept is known as הפרשה שלא מן המוקף. As we will learn below, if one is "},{"text":"mafrish ","italic":true},{"text":"שלא מן המוקף in "},{"text":"Eretz Yisrael, ","italic":true},{"text":"it is still valid after the fact. The "},{"text":"Rama ","italic":true},{"text":"clarifies that even in "},{"text":"chutz la’aretz,","italic":true},{"text":" a "},{"text":"hafrasha ","italic":true},{"text":"from one loaf of bread to another that is not present is valid only if they originated from the same batch of dough. If they were made from different batches, each of which is independently obligated in "},{"text":"hafrashas challah, ","italic":true},{"text":"then even in "},{"text":"chutz la’aretz, ","italic":true},{"text":"one may not be "},{"text":"mafrish ","italic":true},{"text":"from one to the other if they are in separate locations. "}],"marker":"3)","level":0,"indent":54.0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0,"hanging":18.0}],"footnotes":[]},"sort_order":10,"official_shiur_number":1,"estimated_minutes":8,"difficulty":"core","tags":["challah-part-1","original-difficulty:core","source:11"],"source_file_name":"Notes for Hafrashas Challah (part 1).docx","source_start_page":null,"source_end_page":null,"is_selectable":true},{"program_id":"winter-5787","chunk_code":"HC1-C2","source_type":"notes","siman":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","workbook_title":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","section_key":"C","section_title":"Differences Between Challah of Eretz Yisrael and Chutz La’aretz","chunk_title":"Forgot Challah for Shabbos","chunk_summary":"If forgotten on Shabbos: in chu”l eat and leave over; in EY do not eat, but bein hashmashos may allow hafrasha for mitzvah need.","content_markdown":"**Practical Applications:**\n\n<u>Forgot to be</u> *<u>mafrish challah</u>* <u>for Shabbos challahs</u>\n\n\tWhat should you do if, on Shabbos night before the meal, your wife tells you that she forgot to be *mafrish challah* from the challahs[9] she baked? The S”A in **או״ח סימן רסא-א** (12) *passkins* that one is not allowed to be *mafrish terumah* on Shabbos, or even during *bein ha’shmashos.* The **משנ״ב** סק״ד explains that *hafrashas challah* is prohibited because of מתקן – making the remaining challah loaf permissible to eat is considered similar to fixing. Based on this, if you live in *chutz la’aretz* and remember that you were not *mafrish challah* during Shabbos*,* you may eat the challah and leave over a small amount that is large enough to be *mafrish* from after Shabbos. However, in *Eretz Yisrael,* you may not eat from the challah on Shabbos and you must wait until after Shabbos to be *mafrish challah.* Instead, you should borrow challah from a neighbor.\n\nThe משנ״ב adds that if you remember during *bein ha’shmashos*[10] that you forgot to be *mafrish,* if you are in *Eretz Yisrael,* you are allowed to be *mafrish* at that time*,* but if you are in *chutz la’aretz,* you may not. He explains that since in *chutz la’aretz* there is a solution available on Shabbos of leaving over a small amount of bread to be *mafrish* from after Shabbos, it is forbidden to be *mafrish* during *bein ha’shmashos*. However, in *Eretz Yisrael* where there is no remedy on Shabbos, you are allowed to be *mafrish* during *bein ha’shmashos* because Chazal did not prohibit a rabbinic prohibition (such as being *mafrish* on Shabbos) during *bein ha’shmashos* when necessary for the sake of a *mitzvah* (such as *lechem mishneh* and *oneg Shabbos*).\n\nSource Notes\n\n[9]:  Throughout these notes, the word *challah* will be italicized when referring to the portion separated during *hafrashas challah*. When referring to the bread baked for Shabbos, it will be written without italics. \n\n[10]:  In *Eretz Yisrael,* this extends until about 13 minutes after *shekiya,* but in other places around the world, it can be up to 20 minutes. ","content_document":{"version":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":40,"xmlIndex":43,"text":"Practical Applications:","spans":[{"text":"Practical Applications:","bold":true}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":41,"xmlIndex":44,"text":"Forgot to be mafrish challah for Shabbos challahs","spans":[{"text":"Forgot to be ","underline":true},{"text":"mafrish challah ","italic":true,"underline":true},{"text":"for Shabbos challahs","underline":true}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":42,"xmlIndex":45,"text":"\tWhat should you do if, on Shabbos night before the meal, your wife tells you that she forgot to be mafrish challah from the challahs[9] she baked? The S”A in או״ח סימן רסא-א (12) passkins that one is not allowed to be mafrish terumah on Shabbos, or even during bein ha’shmashos. The משנ״ב סק״ד explains that hafrashas challah is prohibited because of מתקן – making the remaining challah loaf permissible to eat is considered similar to fixing. Based on this, if you live in chutz la’aretz and remember that you were not mafrish challah during Shabbos, you may eat the challah and leave over a small amount that is large enough to be mafrish from after Shabbos. However, in Eretz Yisrael, you may not eat from the challah on Shabbos and you must wait until after Shabbos to be mafrish challah. Instead, you should borrow challah from a neighbor.","spans":[{"text":"\tWhat should you do if, on Shabbos night before the meal, your wife tells you that she forgot to be "},{"text":"mafrish challah ","italic":true},{"text":"from the challahs"},{"text":"[9]","footnote":9},{"text":" ","italic":true},{"text":"she baked? The S”A in "},{"text":"או״ח סימן רסא-א ","bold":true},{"text":"(12) "},{"text":"passkins ","italic":true},{"text":"that one is not allowed to be "},{"text":"mafrish terumah ","italic":true},{"text":"on Shabbos, or even during "},{"text":"bein ha’shmashos. ","italic":true},{"text":"The "},{"text":"משנ״ב ","bold":true},{"text":"סק״ד explains that "},{"text":"hafrashas challah ","italic":true},{"text":"is prohibited because of מתקן – making the remaining challah loaf permissible to eat is considered similar to fixing. Based on this, if you live in "},{"text":"chutz la’aretz ","italic":true},{"text":"and remember that you were not "},{"text":"mafrish challah ","italic":true},{"text":"during Shabbos"},{"text":", ","italic":true},{"text":"you may eat the challah"},{"text":" ","italic":true},{"text":"and leave over a small amount that is large enough to be "},{"text":"mafrish ","italic":true},{"text":"from after Shabbos. However, in "},{"text":"Eretz Yisrael, ","italic":true},{"text":"you may not eat from the challah on Shabbos and you must wait until after Shabbos to be "},{"text":"mafrish challah. ","italic":true},{"text":"Instead, you should borrow challah from a neighbor."}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":1,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[9],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":43,"xmlIndex":46,"text":"The משנ״ב adds that if you remember during bein ha’shmashos[10] that you forgot to be mafrish, if you are in Eretz Yisrael, you are allowed to be mafrish at that time, but if you are in chutz la’aretz, you may not. He explains that since in chutz la’aretz there is a solution available on Shabbos of leaving over a small amount of bread to be mafrish from after Shabbos, it is forbidden to be mafrish during bein ha’shmashos. However, in Eretz Yisrael where there is no remedy on Shabbos, you are allowed to be mafrish during bein ha’shmashos because Chazal did not prohibit a rabbinic prohibition (such as being mafrish on Shabbos) during bein ha’shmashos when necessary for the sake of a mitzvah (such as lechem mishneh and oneg Shabbos).","spans":[{"text":"The משנ״ב adds that if you remember during "},{"text":"bein ha’shmashos","italic":true},{"text":"[10]","footnote":10},{"text":" ","italic":true},{"text":"that you forgot to be "},{"text":"mafrish, ","italic":true},{"text":"if you are in "},{"text":"Eretz Yisrael, ","italic":true},{"text":"you are allowed to be "},{"text":"mafrish ","italic":true},{"text":"at that time"},{"text":", ","italic":true},{"text":"but if you are in "},{"text":"chutz la’aretz, ","italic":true},{"text":"you may not."},{"text":" ","italic":true},{"text":"He explains that since in "},{"text":"chutz la’aretz ","italic":true},{"text":"there is a solution available on Shabbos of leaving over a small amount of bread"},{"text":" ","italic":true},{"text":"to be "},{"text":"mafrish ","italic":true},{"text":"from after Shabbos, it is forbidden to be "},{"text":"mafrish ","italic":true},{"text":"during "},{"text":"bein ha’shmashos","italic":true},{"text":". However, in "},{"text":"Eretz Yisrael ","italic":true},{"text":"where there is no remedy on Shabbos, you are allowed to be "},{"text":"mafrish ","italic":true},{"text":"during "},{"text":"bein ha’shmashos ","italic":true},{"text":"because Chazal did not prohibit a rabbinic prohibition (such as being "},{"text":"mafrish ","italic":true},{"text":"on Shabbos) during "},{"text":"bein ha’shmashos ","italic":true},{"text":"when necessary for the sake of a "},{"text":"mitzvah ","italic":true},{"text":"(such as "},{"text":"lechem mishneh ","italic":true},{"text":"and "},{"text":"oneg Shabbos","italic":true},{"text":")."}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":36.0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[10],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}],"footnotes":[{"id":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":0,"xmlIndex":0,"text":" Throughout these notes, the word challah will be italicized when referring to the portion separated during hafrashas challah. When referring to the bread baked for Shabbos, it will be written without italics. ","spans":[{"text":" Throughout these notes, the word "},{"text":"challah","italic":true},{"text":" will be italicized when referring to the portion separated during "},{"text":"hafrashas challah","italic":true},{"text":". When referring to the bread baked for Shabbos, it will be written without italics. "}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}]},{"id":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":0,"xmlIndex":0,"text":" In Eretz Yisrael, this extends until about 13 minutes after shekiya, but in other places around the world, it can be up to 20 minutes. ","spans":[{"text":" In "},{"text":"Eretz Yisrael,","italic":true},{"text":" this extends until about 13 minutes after "},{"text":"shekiya, ","italic":true},{"text":"but in other places around the world, it can be up to 20 minutes. "}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}]}]},"sort_order":11,"official_shiur_number":1,"estimated_minutes":7,"difficulty":"practical","tags":["challah-part-1","original-difficulty:practical","source:12"],"source_file_name":"Notes for Hafrashas Challah (part 1).docx","source_start_page":null,"source_end_page":null,"is_selectable":true},{"program_id":"winter-5787","chunk_code":"HC1-C3","source_type":"notes","siman":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","workbook_title":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","section_key":"C","section_title":"Differences Between Challah of Eretz Yisrael and Chutz La’aretz","chunk_title":"Began Eating Before Remembering","chunk_summary":"Weekday case: in chu”l continue and leave over; in EY stop, separate from remaining bread and have in mind for what was already eaten.","content_markdown":"<u>Eating some of the bread without being</u> *<u>mafrish</u>* \n\nWhat should you do if you began eating bread on a weekday and then remembered you had not yet been *mafrish challah*? In *chutz la’aretz,* the solution is simple – you may continue eating as long as you leave over a little bread and then be *mafrish challah* from the amount remaining. \n\nWhat should you do in *Eretz Yisrael,* where you are forbidden from taking another bite? The **חיד״א**, in ברכי יוסף (שכג-ד) (13), *passkins* that you should immediately be *mafrish challah* from the remaining bread and have in mind that the separation should also cover what you already ate. He explains that although the bread that was already eaten is no longer present, and it is generally forbidden to be *mafrish* in *Eretz Yisrael* on food that is not present, *be’dieved,* the *hafrashas challah* is valid, and one should do whatever is possible to rectify the situation. This principle, that a separation made on behalf of food that is not present is nevertheless valid *be’dieved*, is stated explicitly by the S”A in **סימן שלא-כה**. The S”A *paskins* that although one may not be *mafrish terumah* for produce that is not present, if one does so, the separation is valid. The ברכי יוסף extends this principle even to food that has already been consumed. ","content_document":{"version":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":44,"xmlIndex":47,"text":"Eating some of the bread without being mafrish ","spans":[{"text":"Eating some of the bread without being ","underline":true},{"text":"mafrish","italic":true,"underline":true},{"text":" "}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":45,"xmlIndex":48,"text":"What should you do if you began eating bread on a weekday and then remembered you had not yet been mafrish challah? In chutz la’aretz, the solution is simple – you may continue eating as long as you leave over a little bread and then be mafrish challah from the amount remaining. ","spans":[{"text":"What should you do if you began eating bread on a weekday and then remembered you had not yet been "},{"text":"mafrish challah","italic":true},{"text":"?"},{"text":" ","italic":true},{"text":"In "},{"text":"chutz la’aretz, ","italic":true},{"text":"the solution is simple – you may continue eating as long as you leave over a little bread and then be "},{"text":"mafrish challah ","italic":true},{"text":"from the amount remaining. "}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":36.0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":46,"xmlIndex":49,"text":"What should you do in Eretz Yisrael, where you are forbidden from taking another bite? The חיד״א, in ברכי יוסף (שכג-ד) (13), passkins that you should immediately be mafrish challah from the remaining bread and have in mind that the separation should also cover what you already ate. He explains that although the bread that was already eaten is no longer present, and it is generally forbidden to be mafrish in Eretz Yisrael on food that is not present, be’dieved, the hafrashas challah is valid, and one should do whatever is possible to rectify the situation. This principle, that a separation made on behalf of food that is not present is nevertheless valid be’dieved, is stated explicitly by the S”A in סימן שלא-כה. The S”A paskins that although one may not be mafrish terumah for produce that is not present, if one does so, the separation is valid. The ברכי יוסף extends this principle even to food that has already been consumed. ","spans":[{"text":"What should you do in "},{"text":"Eretz Yisrael, ","italic":true},{"text":"where you are forbidden from taking another bite? The "},{"text":"חיד״א","bold":true},{"text":", in ברכי יוסף (שכג-ד) (13), "},{"text":"passkins ","italic":true},{"text":"that you should immediately be "},{"text":"mafrish challah ","italic":true},{"text":"from the remaining bread and have in mind that the separation should also cover what you already ate. He explains that although the bread that was already eaten is no longer present, and it is generally forbidden to be "},{"text":"mafrish ","italic":true},{"text":"in "},{"text":"Eretz Yisrael ","italic":true},{"text":"on food that is not present, "},{"text":"be’dieved, ","italic":true},{"text":"the "},{"text":"hafrashas challah ","italic":true},{"text":"is valid, and one should do whatever is possible to rectify the situation. This principle, that a separation made on behalf of food that is not present is nevertheless valid "},{"text":"be’dieved","italic":true},{"text":", is stated explicitly by the S”A in "},{"text":"סימן שלא-כה","bold":true},{"text":". The S”A "},{"text":"paskins","italic":true},{"text":" that although one may not be "},{"text":"mafrish terumah","italic":true},{"text":" for produce that is not present, if one does so, the separation is valid. The ברכי יוסף extends this principle even to food that has already been consumed. "}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":36.0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}],"footnotes":[]},"sort_order":12,"official_shiur_number":2,"estimated_minutes":5,"difficulty":"practical","tags":["challah-part-1","original-difficulty:practical","source:13"],"source_file_name":"Notes for Hafrashas Challah (part 1).docx","source_start_page":null,"source_end_page":null,"is_selectable":true},{"program_id":"winter-5787","chunk_code":"HC1-C4","source_type":"notes","siman":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","workbook_title":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","section_key":"C","section_title":"Differences Between Challah of Eretz Yisrael and Chutz La’aretz","chunk_title":"Gifting Challah — Core Problem","chunk_summary":"Woman in EY sent challah away and remembered on Shabbos; problem of Shabbos hafrasha and not min hamukaf.","content_markdown":"<u>Gifting</u> *<u>challah</u>* <u>and forgetting to be</u> *<u>mafrish</u>*\n\n\tThere once was a woman in *Eretz Yisrael*[11] who baked challah for Shabbos and sent it to her married children living in a different city*.* On Shabbos night, she remembered that she had forgotten to be *mafrish challah* and wanted to know if she can be *mafrish* on the remaining challah in her home and exempt those sent to her children. In this situation, there are two issues that need to be addressed. First, being *mafrish* on Shabbos is a rabbinic violation of מתקן (fixing), and second, in *EY* one may not be *mafrish* on bread that is not present. Nevertheless, we mentioned in the previous subsection that according to the חיד״א, in a *be’dieved* situation, being *mafrish* from one location to the other still works. The question is: may the mother violate these two rabbinic prohibitions of fixing and being *mafrish* on bread that is not present in order to prevent her children from sinning by eating the challah?\n\nSource Notes\n\n[11]:  If this happens in *chu”l,* one may just leave over some of the challah and after Shabbos be *mafrish* from there on what was eaten and what is sent to others.","content_document":{"version":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":47,"xmlIndex":50,"text":"Gifting challah and forgetting to be mafrish","spans":[{"text":"Gifting ","underline":true},{"text":"challah ","italic":true,"underline":true},{"text":"and forgetting to be ","underline":true},{"text":"mafrish","italic":true,"underline":true}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":48,"xmlIndex":51,"text":"\tThere once was a woman in Eretz Yisrael[11] who baked challah for Shabbos and sent it to her married children living in a different city. On Shabbos night, she remembered that she had forgotten to be mafrish challah and wanted to know if she can be mafrish on the remaining challah in her home and exempt those sent to her children. In this situation, there are two issues that need to be addressed. First, being mafrish on Shabbos is a rabbinic violation of מתקן (fixing), and second, in EY one may not be mafrish on bread that is not present. Nevertheless, we mentioned in the previous subsection that according to the חיד״א, in a be’dieved situation, being mafrish from one location to the other still works. The question is: may the mother violate these two rabbinic prohibitions of fixing and being mafrish on bread that is not present in order to prevent her children from sinning by eating the challah?","spans":[{"text":"\tThere once was a woman in "},{"text":"Eretz Yisrael","italic":true},{"text":"[11]","footnote":11},{"text":" ","italic":true},{"text":"who baked challah for Shabbos and sent it to her married children living in a different city"},{"text":". ","italic":true},{"text":"On Shabbos night, she remembered that she had forgotten to be "},{"text":"mafrish challah ","italic":true},{"text":"and wanted to know if she can be "},{"text":"mafrish ","italic":true},{"text":"on the remaining challah"},{"text":" ","italic":true},{"text":"in her home and exempt those sent to her children. In this situation, there are two issues that need to be addressed. First, being "},{"text":"mafrish ","italic":true},{"text":"on Shabbos is a rabbinic violation of מתקן (fixing), and second, in "},{"text":"EY ","italic":true},{"text":"one may not be "},{"text":"mafrish ","italic":true},{"text":"on bread that is not present. Nevertheless, we mentioned in the previous subsection that according to the חיד״א, in a "},{"text":"be’dieved ","italic":true},{"text":"situation,"},{"text":" ","italic":true},{"text":"being "},{"text":"mafrish ","italic":true},{"text":"from one location to the other still works. The question is: may the mother violate these two rabbinic prohibitions of fixing and being "},{"text":"mafrish ","italic":true},{"text":"on bread that is not present in order to prevent her children from sinning by eating the challah?"}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":1,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[11],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}],"footnotes":[{"id":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":0,"xmlIndex":0,"text":" If this happens in chu”l, one may just leave over some of the challah and after Shabbos be mafrish from there on what was eaten and what is sent to others.","spans":[{"text":" If this happens in "},{"text":"chu”l, ","italic":true},{"text":"one may just leave over some of the challah and after Shabbos be "},{"text":"mafrish ","italic":true},{"text":"from there on what was eaten and what is sent to others."}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}]}]},"sort_order":13,"official_shiur_number":2,"estimated_minutes":5,"difficulty":"practical","tags":["challah-part-1","original-difficulty:practical"],"source_file_name":"Notes for Hafrashas Challah (part 1).docx","source_start_page":null,"source_end_page":null,"is_selectable":true},{"program_id":"winter-5787","chunk_code":"HC1-C5","source_type":"notes","siman":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","workbook_title":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","section_key":"C","section_title":"Differences Between Challah of Eretz Yisrael and Chutz La’aretz","chunk_title":"Gifting Challah — Baruch Omer Leniency","chunk_summary":"Baruch Omer allows lesser issurim to prevent others from eating tevel, especially when sender caused the problem.","content_markdown":"\tThe **ברוך אומר** (יו״ד רכ) (14) quotes the **גמרא עירובין** (לב:), which teaches that a *chaver* (i.e., a *talmid chacham*) is allowed to be *mafrish* on food that is not before him, in order to prevent an *am ha’aretz* from eating *tevel* (food which *terumah* was not separated). The Gemara explains that although being *mafrish* on food that is not before him is forbidden, it is preferable for the *chaver* to violate the less severe prohibition of being *mafrish* on food that is not present, in order to prevent his friend from violating the more severe prohibition of eating *tevel*. \n\n**Tosfos,** in one[12] explanation, writes that this ruling applies only when it is the *chaver’s* fault that the individual will come to eat *tevel* (e.g., the *chaver* sent the *tevel* fruit without being *mafrish*). Based on this explanation of Tosfos, the ברוך אומר *passkins* that since it is the mother’s fault that her children will eat *tevel,* the mother is allowed to violate the lesser prohibition of being *mafrish* on food that is not before her and the lesser prohibition of being *mafrish* on Shabbos in order to prevent her children from transgressing the greater prohibition of eating *tevel*.\n\nHe concludes that since the mother is already being *mafrish* to save the loaves sent to her children, she should also include the remaining loaves she has at home (from which *challah* also was not taken off) in the same *hafrasha,* and she, too, would be allowed to eat them on Shabbos.\n\nSource Notes\n\n[12]:  In the second explanation, he writes that even if it was not the *chaver’s* fault, he is still allowed to be *mafrish.* It seems that this is how the משנ״ב סימן שו-סקנ״ו and  דרך אמונה (תרומות פ״ג-ציון ההלכה סקרפ״ט) *passkin.* ","content_document":{"version":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":49,"xmlIndex":52,"text":"\tThe ברוך אומר (יו״ד רכ) (14) quotes the גמרא עירובין (לב:), which teaches that a chaver (i.e., a talmid chacham) is allowed to be mafrish on food that is not before him, in order to prevent an am ha’aretz from eating tevel (food which terumah was not separated). The Gemara explains that although being mafrish on food that is not before him is forbidden, it is preferable for the chaver to violate the less severe prohibition of being mafrish on food that is not present, in order to prevent his friend from violating the more severe prohibition of eating tevel. ","spans":[{"text":"\tThe "},{"text":"ברוך אומר ","bold":true},{"text":"(יו״ד רכ) (14) quotes the "},{"text":"גמרא עירובין","bold":true},{"text":" (לב:), which teaches that a "},{"text":"chaver ","italic":true},{"text":"(i.e., a "},{"text":"talmid chacham","italic":true},{"text":") is allowed to be "},{"text":"mafrish ","italic":true},{"text":"on food that is not before him, in order to prevent an "},{"text":"am ha’aretz ","italic":true},{"text":"from eating "},{"text":"tevel ","italic":true},{"text":"(food which "},{"text":"terumah ","italic":true},{"text":"was not separated). The Gemara explains that although being "},{"text":"mafrish ","italic":true},{"text":"on food that is not before him is forbidden, it is preferable for the "},{"text":"chaver ","italic":true},{"text":"to violate the less severe prohibition of being "},{"text":"mafrish ","italic":true},{"text":"on food that is not present, in order to prevent his friend from violating the more severe prohibition of eating "},{"text":"tevel","italic":true},{"text":". "}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":1,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":50,"xmlIndex":53,"text":"Tosfos, in one[12] explanation, writes that this ruling applies only when it is the chaver’s fault that the individual will come to eat tevel (e.g., the chaver sent the tevel fruit without being mafrish). Based on this explanation of Tosfos, the ברוך אומר passkins that since it is the mother’s fault that her children will eat tevel, the mother is allowed to violate the lesser prohibition of being mafrish on food that is not before her and the lesser prohibition of being mafrish on Shabbos in order to prevent her children from transgressing the greater prohibition of eating tevel.","spans":[{"text":"Tosfos,","bold":true},{"text":" in one"},{"text":"[12]","footnote":12},{"text":" explanation, writes that this ruling applies only when it is the "},{"text":"chaver’s ","italic":true},{"text":"fault that the individual will come to eat "},{"text":"tevel ","italic":true},{"text":"(e.g., the "},{"text":"chaver ","italic":true},{"text":"sent the "},{"text":"tevel ","italic":true},{"text":"fruit without being "},{"text":"mafrish","italic":true},{"text":"). Based on this explanation of Tosfos, the ברוך אומר "},{"text":"passkins ","italic":true},{"text":"that since it is the mother’s fault that her children will eat "},{"text":"tevel, ","italic":true},{"text":"the mother is allowed to violate the lesser prohibition of being "},{"text":"mafrish ","italic":true},{"text":"on food that is not before her and the lesser prohibition of being "},{"text":"mafrish ","italic":true},{"text":"on Shabbos in order to prevent her children from transgressing the greater prohibition of eating "},{"text":"tevel","italic":true},{"text":"."}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":36.0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[12],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":51,"xmlIndex":54,"text":"He concludes that since the mother is already being mafrish to save the loaves sent to her children, she should also include the remaining loaves she has at home (from which challah also was not taken off) in the same hafrasha, and she, too, would be allowed to eat them on Shabbos.","spans":[{"text":"He concludes that since the mother is already being "},{"text":"mafrish","italic":true},{"text":" to save the loaves sent to her children, she should also include the remaining loaves she has at home (from which "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"also was not taken off) in the same "},{"text":"hafrasha, ","italic":true},{"text":"and she, too, would be allowed to eat them on Shabbos","italic":false},{"text":"."}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":36.0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}],"footnotes":[{"id":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":0,"xmlIndex":0,"text":" In the second explanation, he writes that even if it was not the chaver’s fault, he is still allowed to be mafrish. It seems that this is how the משנ״ב סימן שו-סקנ״ו and  דרך אמונה (תרומות פ״ג-ציון ההלכה סקרפ״ט) passkin. ","spans":[{"text":" In the second explanation, he writes that even if it was not the "},{"text":"chaver’s ","italic":true},{"text":"fault, he is still allowed to be "},{"text":"mafrish. ","italic":true},{"text":"It seems that this is how the משנ״ב סימן שו-סקנ״ו and  דרך אמונה (תרומות פ״ג-ציון ההלכה סקרפ״ט) "},{"text":"passkin. ","italic":true}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}]}]},"sort_order":14,"official_shiur_number":2,"estimated_minutes":7,"difficulty":"advanced","tags":["challah-part-1","original-difficulty:advanced","source:14"],"source_file_name":"Notes for Hafrashas Challah (part 1).docx","source_start_page":null,"source_end_page":null,"is_selectable":true},{"program_id":"winter-5787","chunk_code":"HC1-C6","source_type":"notes","siman":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","workbook_title":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","section_key":"C","section_title":"Differences Between Challah of Eretz Yisrael and Chutz La’aretz","chunk_title":"Gifting Challah — Nesivos Objection and Summary","chunk_summary":"Nesivos may prohibit because recipients are shogeg/ones in derabanan; notes give reasons to still be lenient and summarize views.","content_markdown":"\tHowever, this approach would seem to be against the opinion of the **נתיבות המשפט** (רלד-ג) (15). Let’s explain. The נתיבות *passkins* that although one who unintentionally (*b’shogeg)* transgresses a biblical prohibition must do *teshuva,* one who unintentionally transgresses a rabbinic decree does not even need to do *teshuva.* The **משנת יהושע** (חושן מפשט-סז) (16) explains that regarding a biblical prohibition, the action itself is intrinsically forbidden (איסור חפצא), and even if one transgresses it unintentionally, he is required to do *teshuva*. For example, if one forgot it was Shabbos and lit a match, since the act is intrinsically forbidden, he is obligated to do *teshuva,* even if the violation was *b’shogeg.* In contrast, a rabbinic prohibition is understood as enacted upon the individual (גברא) to listen to *Chazal,* and when one inadvertently (*b’shogeg*) transgresses a rabbinic prohibition, it is not considered as if he did not listen to Chazal. Only intentionally disregarding Chazal is considered going against them, but accidentally not listening to them, is not considered “not listening” to them. \n\nBased on the reasoning of the נתיבות, even if the children unknowingly eat the *tevel* challah, they have done nothing wrong and would not be required to do *teshuva* because they **unintentionally** transgressed a **rabbinic** decree.[13] Therefore, the mother should be forbidden to violate the two prohibitions of being *mafrish* on Shabbos and from one location to other, because she is not saving them from a transgression that must be rectified. \n\nNevertheless, there are several reasons why it still may be worthwhile for the mother to be *mafrish*: 1) Perhaps the majority of Jews in the world already live in *Eretz Yisrael* today, rendering the obligation of *hafrashas challah* a biblical one according to many opinions, as discussed above. 2)  It seems that the majority of *posskim* argue on the נתיבות, such as the **שער הציון** (שלד סקס״ה), who writes that one who unintentionally transgresses a rabbinic prohibition on Shabbos must do *teshuva. Le’maase,* one should ask their Rav regarding what to do on Shabbos, but during the week the majority of *posskim* are lenient and allow being *mafrish* on *challah* that is in a different location if there is no way to inform the recipient; see דרך אמונה (ציה״ל תרומות פ״ג-רפט). \n\nTo summarize, if in EY you forgot to be *mafrish challah* before sending some of the bread elsewhere and only remembered on Shabbos: \n\n- The ברוך אומר allows transgressing the two lesser rabbinic prohibitions of being *mafrish* from one location onto another and being *mafrish* on Shabbos, in order to prevent others from transgressing the greater prohibition of eating *tevel.*\n\n- The נתיבות would seemingly prohibit doing so, because the recipients in the other location do not transgress the rabbinic prohibition (since they did not know about it)*.*\n\n- Some argue on the נתיבות, and some *passkin* that one is allowed to be *mafrish* in this case*.*\n\nSource Notes\n\n[13]:  In this case, where the children had no way of knowing that *challah* was not separated, there is even more reason to be lenient, since their mistake is more comparable to an *ones* than a *shogeg.* ","content_document":{"version":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":52,"xmlIndex":55,"text":"\tHowever, this approach would seem to be against the opinion of the נתיבות המשפט (רלד-ג) (15). Let’s explain. The נתיבות passkins that although one who unintentionally (b’shogeg) transgresses a biblical prohibition must do teshuva, one who unintentionally transgresses a rabbinic decree does not even need to do teshuva. The משנת יהושע (חושן מפשט-סז) (16) explains that regarding a biblical prohibition, the action itself is intrinsically forbidden (איסור חפצא), and even if one transgresses it unintentionally, he is required to do teshuva. For example, if one forgot it was Shabbos and lit a match, since the act is intrinsically forbidden, he is obligated to do teshuva, even if the violation was b’shogeg. In contrast, a rabbinic prohibition is understood as enacted upon the individual (גברא) to listen to Chazal, and when one inadvertently (b’shogeg) transgresses a rabbinic prohibition, it is not considered as if he did not listen to Chazal. Only intentionally disregarding Chazal is considered going against them, but accidentally not listening to them, is not considered “not listening” to them. ","spans":[{"text":"\tHowever, this approach would seem to be against the opinion of the "},{"text":"נתיבות המשפט","bold":true},{"text":" (רלד-ג) (15). Let’s explain. The נתיבות "},{"text":"passkins ","italic":true},{"text":"that although one who unintentionally ("},{"text":"b’shogeg) ","italic":true},{"text":"transgresses a biblical prohibition must do "},{"text":"teshuva,","italic":true},{"text":" one who unintentionally transgresses a rabbinic decree does not even need to do "},{"text":"teshuva.","italic":true},{"text":" The "},{"text":"משנת יהושע ","bold":true},{"text":"(חושן מפשט-סז) (16) explains that regarding a biblical prohibition, the action itself is intrinsically forbidden (איסור חפצא), and even if one transgresses it unintentionally, he is required to do "},{"text":"teshuva","italic":true},{"text":". For example, if one forgot it was Shabbos and lit a match, since the act is intrinsically forbidden, he is obligated to do "},{"text":"teshuva, ","italic":true},{"text":"even if the violation was "},{"text":"b’shogeg.","italic":true},{"text":" In contrast, a rabbinic prohibition is understood as enacted upon the individual (גברא) to listen to "},{"text":"Chazal, ","italic":true},{"text":"and when one inadvertently ("},{"text":"b’shogeg","italic":true},{"text":") transgresses a rabbinic prohibition, it is not considered as if he did not listen to Chazal. Only intentionally disregarding Chazal is considered going against them, but accidentally not listening to them, is not considered “not listening” to them. "}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":1,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":53,"xmlIndex":56,"text":"Based on the reasoning of the נתיבות, even if the children unknowingly eat the tevel challah, they have done nothing wrong and would not be required to do teshuva because they unintentionally transgressed a rabbinic decree.[13] Therefore, the mother should be forbidden to violate the two prohibitions of being mafrish on Shabbos and from one location to other, because she is not saving them from a transgression that must be rectified. ","spans":[{"text":"Based on the reasoning of the נתיבות, even if the children unknowingly eat the "},{"text":"tevel ","italic":true},{"text":"challah, they have done nothing wrong and would not be required to do "},{"text":"teshuva ","italic":true},{"text":"because they "},{"text":"unintentionally","bold":true},{"text":" transgressed a "},{"text":"rabbinic","bold":true},{"text":" decree."},{"text":"[13]","footnote":13},{"text":" Therefore, the mother should be forbidden to violate the two prohibitions of being "},{"text":"mafrish ","italic":true},{"text":"on Shabbos and from one location to other, because she is not saving them from a transgression that must be rectified. "}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":36.0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[13],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":54,"xmlIndex":57,"text":"Nevertheless, there are several reasons why it still may be worthwhile for the mother to be mafrish: 1) Perhaps the majority of Jews in the world already live in Eretz Yisrael today, rendering the obligation of hafrashas challah a biblical one according to many opinions, as discussed above. 2)  It seems that the majority of posskim argue on the נתיבות, such as the שער הציון (שלד סקס״ה), who writes that one who unintentionally transgresses a rabbinic prohibition on Shabbos must do teshuva. Le’maase, one should ask their Rav regarding what to do on Shabbos, but during the week the majority of posskim are lenient and allow being mafrish on challah that is in a different location if there is no way to inform the recipient; see דרך אמונה (ציה״ל תרומות פ״ג-רפט). ","spans":[{"text":"Nevertheless, there are several reasons why it still may be worthwhile for the mother to be "},{"text":"mafrish","italic":true},{"text":": 1) Perhaps the majority of Jews in the world already live in "},{"text":"Eretz Yisrael ","italic":true},{"text":"today, rendering the obligation of "},{"text":"hafrashas challah ","italic":true},{"text":"a biblical one according to many opinions, as discussed above. 2)  It seems that the majority of "},{"text":"posskim ","italic":true},{"text":"argue on the נתיבות, such as the "},{"text":"שער הציון","bold":true},{"text":" (שלד סקס״ה), who writes that one who unintentionally transgresses a rabbinic prohibition on Shabbos must do "},{"text":"teshuva. Le’maase,","italic":true},{"text":" one should ask their Rav regarding what to do on Shabbos, but during the week"},{"text":" ","italic":true},{"text":"the majority of "},{"text":"posskim ","italic":true},{"text":"are lenient and allow being "},{"text":"mafrish ","italic":true},{"text":"on "},{"text":"challah","italic":true},{"text":" that is in a different location if there is no way to inform the recipient; see דרך אמונה (ציה״ל תרומות פ״ג-רפט). "}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":36.0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":55,"xmlIndex":58,"text":"To summarize, if in EY you forgot to be mafrish challah before sending some of the bread elsewhere and only remembered on Shabbos: ","spans":[{"text":"To summarize, if in EY you forgot to be "},{"text":"mafrish challah","italic":true},{"text":" before sending some of the bread"},{"text":" ","italic":true},{"text":"elsewhere and only remembered on Shabbos: "}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":36.0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"list","paragraph":56,"xmlIndex":59,"text":"The ברוך אומר allows transgressing the two lesser rabbinic prohibitions of being mafrish from one location onto another and being mafrish on Shabbos, in order to prevent others from transgressing the greater prohibition of eating tevel. ","spans":[{"text":"The ברוך אומר allows transgressing the two lesser rabbinic prohibitions of being "},{"text":"mafrish ","italic":true},{"text":"from one location onto another and being "},{"text":"mafrish ","italic":true},{"text":"on Shabbos, in order to prevent others from transgressing the greater prohibition of eating "},{"text":"tevel. ","italic":true}],"marker":"•","level":0,"indent":36.0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0,"hanging":18.0},{"kind":"list","paragraph":57,"xmlIndex":60,"text":"The נתיבות would seemingly prohibit doing so, because the recipients in the other location do not transgress the rabbinic prohibition (since they did not know about it).","spans":[{"text":"The נתיבות would seemingly prohibit doing so, because the recipients in the other location do not transgress the rabbinic prohibition (since they did not know about it)"},{"text":".","italic":true}],"marker":"•","level":0,"indent":36.0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0,"hanging":18.0},{"kind":"list","paragraph":58,"xmlIndex":61,"text":" Some argue on the נתיבות, and some passkin that one is allowed to be mafrish in this case.","spans":[{"text":" ","italic":true},{"text":"Some argue on the נתיבות, and some "},{"text":"passkin ","italic":true},{"text":"that one is allowed to be "},{"text":"mafrish ","italic":true},{"text":"in this case"},{"text":".","italic":true}],"marker":"•","level":0,"indent":36.0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0,"hanging":18.0}],"footnotes":[{"id":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":0,"xmlIndex":0,"text":" In this case, where the children had no way of knowing that challah was not separated, there is even more reason to be lenient, since their mistake is more comparable to an ones than a shogeg. ","spans":[{"text":" In this case, where the children had no way of knowing that "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"was not separated, there is even more reason to be lenient, since their mistake is more comparable to an "},{"text":"ones ","italic":true},{"text":"than a "},{"text":"shogeg. ","italic":true}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}]}]},"sort_order":15,"official_shiur_number":2,"estimated_minutes":8,"difficulty":"advanced","tags":["challah-part-1","original-difficulty:advanced","source:15","source:16"],"source_file_name":"Notes for Hafrashas Challah (part 1).docx","source_start_page":null,"source_end_page":null,"is_selectable":true},{"program_id":"winter-5787","chunk_code":"HC1-C7","source_type":"notes","siman":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","workbook_title":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","section_key":"C","section_title":"Differences Between Challah of Eretz Yisrael and Chutz La’aretz","chunk_title":"Matzah — Why Hafrasha Is Often After Baking","chunk_summary":"Matzah often has challah separated after baking due to chametz concerns and small batches; some consumers buy matzah to be mafrish themselves.","content_markdown":"<u>Forgetting to be</u> *<u>mafrish challah</u>* <u>from</u> *<u>matzah</u>*\n\n\tIdeally, the *mitzvah* of *hafrashas challah* should be performed right after the flour and water are mixed and made into dough (we will learn the precise ideal time below). However, with regard to *matzah,* the S”A in או״ח תנז *passkins* that the *hafrashas challah* should be performed after the *matzah* is baked, for two reasons. First, if the *challah* is separated from the batch of dough, the separated *challah* would have to be burned within eighteen minutes before it became *chametz,* and *matzah* bakeries do not want potential *chametz* in the facility during production. Second, the amount of flour used for each batch does not meet the required amount of flour needed for *hafrashas* *challah.*[14] Therefore, after the *matzah* is baked, they are placed all in a container or wagon and *challah* is separated from the entire container. Some *matzah* bakeries sell *matzah* from which *challah* has not yet been separated, allowing the consumer to recite the *bracha* and perform the *mitzvah* of *hafrashas challah.* \n\nSource Notes\n\n[14]:  The S”A does suggest a way of combining the batches of dough together but concludes that it is best to be *mafrish* after baking. ","content_document":{"version":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":59,"xmlIndex":62,"text":"Forgetting to be mafrish challah from matzah","spans":[{"text":"Forgetting to be ","underline":true},{"text":"mafrish challah ","italic":true,"underline":true},{"text":"from ","underline":true},{"text":"matzah","italic":true,"underline":true}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":60,"xmlIndex":63,"text":"\tIdeally, the mitzvah of hafrashas challah should be performed right after the flour and water are mixed and made into dough (we will learn the precise ideal time below). However, with regard to matzah, the S”A in או״ח תנז passkins that the hafrashas challah should be performed after the matzah is baked, for two reasons. First, if the challah is separated from the batch of dough, the separated challah would have to be burned within eighteen minutes before it became chametz, and matzah bakeries do not want potential chametz in the facility during production. Second, the amount of flour used for each batch does not meet the required amount of flour needed for hafrashas challah.[14] Therefore, after the matzah is baked, they are placed all in a container or wagon and challah is separated from the entire container. Some matzah bakeries sell matzah from which challah has not yet been separated, allowing the consumer to recite the bracha and perform the mitzvah of hafrashas challah. ","spans":[{"text":"\tIdeally, the "},{"text":"mitzvah ","italic":true},{"text":"of "},{"text":"hafrashas challah ","italic":true},{"text":"should be performed right after the flour and water are mixed and made into dough (we will learn the precise ideal time below). However, with regard to "},{"text":"matzah,","italic":true},{"text":" the S”A in או״ח תנז "},{"text":"passkins ","italic":true},{"text":"that"},{"text":" ","italic":true},{"text":"the "},{"text":"hafrashas challah ","italic":true},{"text":"should be performed after the "},{"text":"matzah ","italic":true},{"text":"is baked, for two reasons. First, if the "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"is separated from the batch of dough, the separated "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"would have to be burned within eighteen minutes before it became "},{"text":"chametz, ","italic":true},{"text":"and "},{"text":"matzah","italic":true},{"text":" bakeries do not want potential "},{"text":"chametz ","italic":true},{"text":"in the facility during production. Second, the amount of flour used for each batch does not meet the required amount of flour needed for "},{"text":"hafrashas","italic":true},{"text":" "},{"text":"challah.","italic":true},{"text":"[14]","footnote":14},{"text":" ","italic":true},{"text":"Therefore, after the "},{"text":"matzah ","italic":true},{"text":"is baked, they are placed all in a container or wagon and "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"is separated from the entire container. Some "},{"text":"matzah ","italic":true},{"text":"bakeries sell "},{"text":"matzah ","italic":true},{"text":"from"},{"text":" ","italic":true},{"text":"which "},{"text":"challah","italic":true},{"text":" has not yet been separated, allowing the consumer to recite the "},{"text":"bracha ","italic":true},{"text":"and perform the "},{"text":"mitzvah ","italic":true},{"text":"of "},{"text":"hafrashas challah. ","italic":true}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":1,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[14],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}],"footnotes":[{"id":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":0,"xmlIndex":0,"text":" The S”A does suggest a way of combining the batches of dough together but concludes that it is best to be mafrish after baking. ","spans":[{"text":" The S”A does suggest a way of combining the batches of dough together but concludes that it is best to be "},{"text":"mafrish ","italic":true},{"text":"after baking. "}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}]}]},"sort_order":16,"official_shiur_number":2,"estimated_minutes":4,"difficulty":"practical","tags":["challah-part-1","original-difficulty:practical"],"source_file_name":"Notes for Hafrashas Challah (part 1).docx","source_start_page":null,"source_end_page":null,"is_selectable":true},{"program_id":"winter-5787","chunk_code":"HC1-C8","source_type":"notes","siman":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","workbook_title":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","section_key":"C","section_title":"Differences Between Challah of Eretz Yisrael and Chutz La’aretz","chunk_title":"Forgot Matzah in Chu”l on Pesach Night","chunk_summary":"Mishnah Berurah: leave over from each batch/piece as needed and separate after Yom Tov.","content_markdown":"What should you do if you baked *matzos* as part of a group at a *matzah* bakery and forgot to be *mafrish challah* from your *matzah* afterward, and only realized on Pesach night? The **משנ״ב** (תנז סק״י) (17) writes that being *mafrish* on Yom Tov is forbidden just as it is on Shabbos. Therefore, if the obligation to be *mafrish* already took effect before Yom Tov (e.g., the *matzos* were combined in a container, or there was enough dough in each batch separately), then the משנ\"ב writes that in *chu”l,* the *halacha* is as follows. If you know which *matzos* came from each batch (e.g., you bought a batch of whole wheat and a batch of regular flour and can differentiate between them), you should leave over a *matzah* from each batch and then be *mafrish* from it after Yom Tov. If you do not know which *matzah* came from which batch (e.g., you bought two batches of regular flour *matzah* and each batch was obligated in *challah*), you must leave over a small piece from every single *matzah.* After Yom Tov, you combine all of those pieces into a container and be *mafrish* from there. In the **שער הציון** (כא), the Chafetz Chaim explains that since all the leftover pieces were combined into a container, one *hafrasha* suffices for all of them. ","content_document":{"version":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":61,"xmlIndex":64,"text":"What should you do if you baked matzos as part of a group at a matzah bakery and forgot to be mafrish challah from your matzah afterward, and only realized on Pesach night? The משנ״ב (תנז סק״י) (17) writes that being mafrish on Yom Tov is forbidden just as it is on Shabbos. Therefore, if the obligation to be mafrish already took effect before Yom Tov (e.g., the matzos were combined in a container, or there was enough dough in each batch separately), then the משנ\"ב writes that in chu”l, the halacha is as follows. If you know which matzos came from each batch (e.g., you bought a batch of whole wheat and a batch of regular flour and can differentiate between them), you should leave over a matzah from each batch and then be mafrish from it after Yom Tov. If you do not know which matzah came from which batch (e.g., you bought two batches of regular flour matzah and each batch was obligated in challah), you must leave over a small piece from every single matzah. After Yom Tov, you combine all of those pieces into a container and be mafrish from there. In the שער הציון (כא), the Chafetz Chaim explains that since all the leftover pieces were combined into a container, one hafrasha suffices for all of them. ","spans":[{"text":"What should you do if you baked "},{"text":"matzos","italic":true},{"text":" as part of a group at a "},{"text":"matzah ","italic":true},{"text":"bakery and forgot to be "},{"text":"mafrish challah ","italic":true},{"text":"from your "},{"text":"matzah ","italic":true},{"text":"afterward, and only realized on Pesach night? The "},{"text":"משנ״ב ","bold":true},{"text":"(תנז סק״י) (17) writes that being "},{"text":"mafrish ","italic":true},{"text":"on Yom Tov is forbidden just as it is on Shabbos. Therefore, if the obligation to be "},{"text":"mafrish ","italic":true},{"text":"already took effect before Yom Tov (e.g., the "},{"text":"matzos ","italic":true},{"text":"were combined in a container, or there was enough dough in each batch separately), then the משנ\"ב writes that in "},{"text":"chu”l,","italic":true},{"text":" the "},{"text":"halacha ","italic":true},{"text":"is as follows. If you know which "},{"text":"matzos ","italic":true},{"text":"came from each batch (e.g., you bought a batch of whole wheat and a batch of regular flour and can differentiate between them),"},{"text":" ","italic":true},{"text":"you should leave over a "},{"text":"matzah ","italic":true},{"text":"from each batch and then be "},{"text":"mafrish ","italic":true},{"text":"from it after Yom Tov. If you do not know which "},{"text":"matzah ","italic":true},{"text":"came from which batch (e.g., you bought two batches of regular flour "},{"text":"matzah","italic":true},{"text":" and each batch was obligated in "},{"text":"challah","italic":true},{"text":"), you must leave over a small piece from every single "},{"text":"matzah. ","italic":true},{"text":"After Yom Tov, you combine all of those pieces into a container and be "},{"text":"mafrish ","italic":true},{"text":"from there. In the "},{"text":"שער הציון ","bold":true},{"text":"(כא), the Chafetz Chaim explains that since all the leftover pieces were combined into a container, one "},{"text":"hafrasha","italic":true},{"text":" suffices for all of them. "}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":36.0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}],"footnotes":[]},"sort_order":17,"official_shiur_number":2,"estimated_minutes":5,"difficulty":"practical","tags":["challah-part-1","original-difficulty:practical","source:17"],"source_file_name":"Notes for Hafrashas Challah (part 1).docx","source_start_page":null,"source_end_page":null,"is_selectable":true},{"program_id":"winter-5787","chunk_code":"HC1-C9","source_type":"notes","siman":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","workbook_title":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","section_key":"C","section_title":"Differences Between Challah of Eretz Yisrael and Chutz La’aretz","chunk_title":"Forgot Matzah in EY — Three Solutions","chunk_summary":"RSZA permits for biblical matzah if no alternative; child hafrasha option; Rav’s pre-Yom Tov zechus solution and its difficulty.","content_markdown":"The above guidelines apply only in *chutz la’aretz,* where you may leave over some of the *matzah* and be *mafrish* after Yom Tov. What should you do if you are in *Eretz Yisrael*? There are three possible solutions.\n\n\t1) The **שמירת שבת כהלכתה** (פרק יא-טז) (18), citing **Rav Shlomo Zalman Auerbach**, *passkins* that in *Eretz Yisrael,* if there is no other way to get kosher *matzah* (e.g., a Pesach program that only brought *matzah* from which *challah* was not separated)*,* one is allowed to be *mafrish* on Pesach in order to fulfill the biblical *mitzvah* of eating *matzah.* Similarly, on the first night of Sukkos, when there is a biblical obligation to eat a כזית of bread in the *sukkah,* if you forgot to be *mafrish* and there is no other way to obtain bread, you are allowed to be *mafrish*. He adds that when being *mafrish* in *Eretz Yisrael* on Yom Tov, it is best to make the declaration mentally without verbalizing it and without reciting a *bracha* (but the physical separation still needs to be performed)*.* In the footnote (נח), he explains that the above only works in Eretz Yisrael, but in *chutz la’aretz,* one should leave over from each *matzah* or bread. \n\n\t2) The חלת לחם (סימן י-ז) suggests another alternative, but to properly understand it, we first need to learn two important *halachos.*\n\n1. The **באור הלכה** (סימן שמג) (19), quoting the רשב״א (as understood by the ר״ן), *passkins* that a child is allowed to violate an *issur* *derabanan* if it is for his own sake. Therefore, if you forget to bring a *siddur* to *shul* and there is no *eiruv,* the *katan* is allowed to bring one for himself, and you may follow along with him. (The שש״כ (יח-סא) *passkins* in accordance with this באור הלכה.)\n\n2. The **רמב״ם** (תרומות פ״ד-ה) *passkins* that if a *katan* who has reached the age at which his or her oaths are binding, and he/she separates *terumah,* the *terumah* is valid. The S”A in **יו״ד סימן רלג** *passkins* that the vows of a 12-year-old boy or 11-year-old girl who understand to Whom they are making a vow are valid. Accordingly, the S”A in **סימן שלא-לג** *passkins* that the *terumah* taken by such a *katan* is binding.[15]\n\nBased on these two *halachos*, the **חלת לחם** (סימן י-ז) (20) suggests that it is best to give the *matzah* as a gift to the child (a 12-year-old boy or 11-year-old girl) and have them be *mafrish* it for themselves. Since their *hafrasha* is valid, everyone else can benefit from the *matzah* as well*.*\n\n\t3) **RSZA**, in הליכות שלמה (פ״ט-א) (21), suggests that immediately before Pesach begins, the Rav of the community should be *mafrish challah* while having in mind all of those who forgot to do so in his community. In fact, Rav Boyer of Bnei Brak is *mafrish challah* every *erev-*Shabbos 40 minutes before *shekiyah* on behalf of Jews all over the world.[16] This *hafrasha* is effective because it is a *zechus* for those who forgot, and one may act on another person’s behalf when doing so is entirely for that person’s benefit (i.e., the rule of זכין לאדם שלא בפניו). Indeed, RSZA himself used to do this in *Shaarei Chessed.*[17] In the footnote, he explains that the reason it must be done shortly before Yom Tov begins is that if the Rav is *mafrish* earlier in the day and a member of the community is *mafrish* later in the day, the Rav’s *hafrasha* is no longer a *zechus* for this individual, because he prefers to be *yotze* and recite the *bracha* himself*.* Furthermore, the **חקת הפסח** (פכ״ו-סק״יג) goes so far as to claim that if the Rav performs the *hafrasha* in the morning, he could be causing those who are *mafrish* later in the day to recite a *bracha le’vatallah.* \n\nHowever, there is a significant difficulty with RSZA’s suggestion: We learned above that it is forbidden to be *mafrish* from one location to another in *Eretz Yisrael,* and even in *chu”l,* one may not separate from one batch of dough for another. How, then, was he able to be *mafrish* for others? \n\nThe **מנחת יצחק** (ח״ד-נט) (22) explains that this may still be permitted because *bedieved, challah* may be separated even from one batch for another based on what we learned above that a *chaver* is allowed to transgress a lesser prohibition to save another from transgressing a more severe one. Although we learned that Tosfos only allows if the *chaver* is the one who caused the other to violate the prohibition (and here the Rav of the community is not responsible for others forgetting to be *mafrish*), there are two possible solutions for why it is permitted. First, Tosfos in his second answer writes that a *chaver* is allowed to transgress the lesser prohibition even if he is not responsible for his friend consuming *tevel,* and this is how we *passkin lema’ase,* as the **משנ״ב** סימן שו-סקנ״ו writes. Second, even according to Tosfos’ first answer, Tosfos agrees that a *chaver* may still transgress a lesser prohibition on behalf of the public even if he is not responsible for it. Thus, RSZA felt that it is better to transgress the lesser prohibition of being *mafrish* from one location to the other in order to allow people who forgot to be *mafrish* to be able to eat their *matzah.* This is also the *pssak* of **משפטי ארץ** (פ״ה-כא).\n\nSource Notes\n\n[15]:  However, the גר״א (שלא-עח) *passkins* against the S”A that the *hafrasha* of a *katan* is never valid. \n\n[16]:  See: https://www.jdn.co.il/j_world/2221901/\n\n[17]:  In footnote 13 there, RSZA is quoted as saying that almost every year, someone in his community forgot and he was very happy to know (\"ושמחתי\") that he was able to help others.","content_document":{"version":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":62,"xmlIndex":65,"text":"The above guidelines apply only in chutz la’aretz, where you may leave over some of the matzah and be mafrish after Yom Tov. What should you do if you are in Eretz Yisrael? There are three possible solutions.","spans":[{"text":"The above guidelines apply only in "},{"text":"chutz la’aretz, ","italic":true},{"text":"where you may leave over some of the "},{"text":"matzah ","italic":true},{"text":"and be "},{"text":"mafrish","italic":true},{"text":" after Yom Tov. What should you do if you are in "},{"text":"Eretz Yisrael","italic":true},{"text":"? There are three possible solutions."}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":36.0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":63,"xmlIndex":66,"text":"\t1) The שמירת שבת כהלכתה (פרק יא-טז) (18), citing Rav Shlomo Zalman Auerbach, passkins that in Eretz Yisrael, if there is no other way to get kosher matzah (e.g., a Pesach program that only brought matzah from which challah was not separated), one is allowed to be mafrish on Pesach in order to fulfill the biblical mitzvah of eating matzah. Similarly, on the first night of Sukkos, when there is a biblical obligation to eat a כזית of bread in the sukkah, if you forgot to be mafrish and there is no other way to obtain bread, you are allowed to be mafrish. He adds that when being mafrish in Eretz Yisrael on Yom Tov, it is best to make the declaration mentally without verbalizing it and without reciting a bracha (but the physical separation still needs to be performed). In the footnote (נח), he explains that the above only works in Eretz Yisrael, but in chutz la’aretz, one should leave over from each matzah or bread. ","spans":[{"text":"\t1) The "},{"text":"שמירת שבת כהלכתה ","bold":true},{"text":"(פרק יא-טז) (18), citing "},{"text":"Rav Shlomo Zalman Auerbach","bold":true},{"text":","},{"text":" ","bold":true},{"text":"passkins","italic":true},{"text":" that in "},{"text":"Eretz Yisrael, ","italic":true},{"text":"if there is no other way to get kosher "},{"text":"matzah ","italic":true},{"text":"(e.g., a Pesach program that only brought "},{"text":"matzah ","italic":true},{"text":"from which "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"was not separated)"},{"text":", ","italic":true},{"text":"one is allowed to be "},{"text":"mafrish ","italic":true},{"text":"on Pesach in order to fulfill the biblical "},{"text":"mitzvah ","italic":true},{"text":"of eating "},{"text":"matzah. ","italic":true},{"text":"Similarly, on the first night of Sukkos, when there is a biblical obligation to eat a כזית of bread in the "},{"text":"sukkah, ","italic":true},{"text":"if you forgot to be "},{"text":"mafrish ","italic":true},{"text":"and there is no other way to obtain bread, you are allowed to be "},{"text":"mafrish","italic":true},{"text":". He adds that when being "},{"text":"mafrish ","italic":true},{"text":"in "},{"text":"Eretz Yisrael ","italic":true},{"text":"on Yom Tov, it is best to make the declaration mentally without verbalizing it and without reciting a "},{"text":"bracha ","italic":true},{"text":"(but the physical separation still needs to be performed)"},{"text":".","italic":true},{"text":" In the footnote (נח), he explains that the above only works in Eretz Yisrael, but in "},{"text":"chutz la’aretz, ","italic":true},{"text":"one should leave over from each "},{"text":"matzah ","italic":true},{"text":"or bread. "}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":1,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":64,"xmlIndex":67,"text":"\t2) The חלת לחם (סימן י-ז) suggests another alternative, but to properly understand it, we first need to learn two important halachos.","spans":[{"text":"\t2) The חלת לחם (סימן י-ז) suggests another alternative, but to properly understand it, we first need to learn two important "},{"text":"halachos.","italic":true}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":1,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"list","paragraph":65,"xmlIndex":68,"text":"The באור הלכה (סימן שמג) (19), quoting the רשב״א (as understood by the ר״ן), passkins that a child is allowed to violate an issur derabanan if it is for his own sake. Therefore, if you forget to bring a siddur to shul and there is no eiruv, the katan is allowed to bring one for himself, and you may follow along with him. (The שש״כ (יח-סא) passkins in accordance with this באור הלכה.) ","spans":[{"text":"The "},{"text":"באור הלכה ","bold":true},{"text":"(סימן שמג) (19), quoting the רשב״א (as understood by the ר״ן), "},{"text":"passkins ","italic":true},{"text":"that a child is allowed to violate an "},{"text":"issur","italic":true},{"text":" "},{"text":"derabanan ","italic":true},{"text":"if it is for his own sake. Therefore, if you forget to bring a "},{"text":"siddur ","italic":true},{"text":"to "},{"text":"shul ","italic":true},{"text":"and there is no "},{"text":"eiruv, ","italic":true},{"text":"the "},{"text":"katan ","italic":true},{"text":"is allowed to bring one for himself, and you may follow along with him. (The שש״כ (יח-סא) "},{"text":"passkins ","italic":true},{"text":"in accordance with this באור הלכה.) "}],"marker":"1.","level":0,"indent":36.0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0,"hanging":18.0},{"kind":"list","paragraph":66,"xmlIndex":69,"text":"The רמב״ם (תרומות פ״ד-ה) passkins that if a katan who has reached the age at which his or her oaths are binding, and he/she separates terumah, the terumah is valid. The S”A in יו״ד סימן רלג passkins that the vows of a 12-year-old boy or 11-year-old girl who understand to Whom they are making a vow are valid. Accordingly, the S”A in סימן שלא-לג passkins that the terumah taken by such a katan is binding.[15] ","spans":[{"text":"The "},{"text":"רמב״ם","bold":true},{"text":" (תרומות פ״ד-ה) "},{"text":"passkins ","italic":true},{"text":"that if a "},{"text":"katan ","italic":true},{"text":"who has reached the age at which his or her oaths are binding, and he/she separates"},{"text":" terumah, ","italic":true},{"text":"the "},{"text":"terumah ","italic":true},{"text":"is valid. The S”A in "},{"text":"יו״ד סימן רלג","bold":true},{"text":" "},{"text":"passkins ","italic":true},{"text":"that the vows of a 12-year-old boy or 11-year-old girl who understand to Whom they are making a vow are valid. Accordingly, the S”A in "},{"text":"סימן שלא-לג ","bold":true},{"text":"passkins ","italic":true},{"text":"that the "},{"text":"terumah ","italic":true},{"text":"taken by such a "},{"text":"katan ","italic":true},{"text":"is binding."},{"text":"[15]","footnote":15},{"text":" "}],"marker":"2.","level":0,"indent":36.0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[15],"defaultTabWidth":36.0,"hanging":18.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":67,"xmlIndex":70,"text":"Based on these two halachos, the חלת לחם (סימן י-ז) (20) suggests that it is best to give the matzah as a gift to the child (a 12-year-old boy or 11-year-old girl) and have them be mafrish it for themselves. Since their hafrasha is valid, everyone else can benefit from the matzah as well.","spans":[{"text":"Based on these two "},{"text":"halachos","italic":true},{"text":", the "},{"text":"חלת לחם ","bold":true},{"text":"(סימן י-ז) (20) suggests that it is best to give the "},{"text":"matzah ","italic":true},{"text":"as a gift to the child (a 12-year-old boy or 11-year-old girl) and have them be "},{"text":"mafrish ","italic":true},{"text":"it for themselves. Since their "},{"text":"hafrasha ","italic":true},{"text":"is valid, everyone else can benefit from the "},{"text":"matzah ","italic":true},{"text":"as well"},{"text":".","italic":true}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":36.0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":68,"xmlIndex":71,"text":"\t3) RSZA, in הליכות שלמה (פ״ט-א) (21), suggests that immediately before Pesach begins, the Rav of the community should be mafrish challah while having in mind all of those who forgot to do so in his community. In fact, Rav Boyer of Bnei Brak is mafrish challah every erev-Shabbos 40 minutes before shekiyah on behalf of Jews all over the world.[16] This hafrasha is effective because it is a zechus for those who forgot, and one may act on another person’s behalf when doing so is entirely for that person’s benefit (i.e., the rule of זכין לאדם שלא בפניו). Indeed, RSZA himself used to do this in Shaarei Chessed.[17] In the footnote, he explains that the reason it must be done shortly before Yom Tov begins is that if the Rav is mafrish earlier in the day and a member of the community is mafrish later in the day, the Rav’s hafrasha is no longer a zechus for this individual, because he prefers to be yotze and recite the bracha himself. Furthermore, the חקת הפסח (פכ״ו-סק״יג) goes so far as to claim that if the Rav performs the hafrasha in the morning, he could be causing those who are mafrish later in the day to recite a bracha le’vatallah. ","spans":[{"text":"\t3) "},{"text":"RSZA","bold":true},{"text":","},{"text":" ","bold":true},{"text":"in הליכות שלמה (פ״ט-א) (21), suggests"},{"text":" ","italic":true},{"text":"that immediately before Pesach begins, the Rav of the community should be "},{"text":"mafrish challah","italic":true},{"text":" while having in mind all of those who forgot to do so in his community. In fact, Rav Boyer of Bnei Brak is "},{"text":"mafrish challah ","italic":true},{"text":"every "},{"text":"erev-","italic":true},{"text":"Shabbos 40 minutes before "},{"text":"shekiyah ","italic":true},{"text":"on behalf of Jews all over the world."},{"text":"[16]","footnote":16},{"text":" This "},{"text":"hafrasha ","italic":true},{"text":"is effective because it is a "},{"text":"zechus ","italic":true},{"text":"for those who forgot, and one may act on another person’s behalf when doing so is entirely for that person’s benefit (i.e., the rule of זכין לאדם שלא בפניו). Indeed, RSZA himself used to do this in "},{"text":"Shaarei Chessed.","italic":true},{"text":"[17]","footnote":17},{"text":" In the footnote, he explains that the reason it must be done shortly before Yom Tov begins is that if the Rav is "},{"text":"mafrish ","italic":true},{"text":"earlier in the day and a member of the community is "},{"text":"mafrish ","italic":true},{"text":"later in the day, the Rav’s "},{"text":"hafrasha ","italic":true},{"text":"is no longer a "},{"text":"zechus ","italic":true},{"text":"for this individual, because he prefers to be "},{"text":"yotze ","italic":true},{"text":"and recite the "},{"text":"bracha ","italic":true},{"text":"himself"},{"text":". ","italic":true},{"text":"Furthermore, the "},{"text":"חקת הפסח","bold":true},{"text":" (פכ״ו-סק״יג) goes so far as to claim that if the Rav performs the "},{"text":"hafrasha ","italic":true},{"text":"in the morning, he could be causing those who are "},{"text":"mafrish ","italic":true},{"text":"later in the day to recite a "},{"text":"bracha le’vatallah. ","italic":true}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":1,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[16,17],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":69,"xmlIndex":72,"text":"However, there is a significant difficulty with RSZA’s suggestion: We learned above that it is forbidden to be mafrish from one location to another in Eretz Yisrael, and even in chu”l, one may not separate from one batch of dough for another. How, then, was he able to be mafrish for others? ","spans":[{"text":"However, there is a significant difficulty with RSZA’s suggestion: We learned above that it is forbidden to be "},{"text":"mafrish ","italic":true},{"text":"from one location to another in "},{"text":"Eretz Yisrael, ","italic":true},{"text":"and even in "},{"text":"chu”l, ","italic":true},{"text":"one may not separate from one batch of dough for another. How, then, was he able to be "},{"text":"mafrish ","italic":true},{"text":"for others? "}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":36.0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":70,"xmlIndex":73,"text":"The מנחת יצחק (ח״ד-נט) (22) explains that this may still be permitted because bedieved, challah may be separated even from one batch for another based on what we learned above that a chaver is allowed to transgress a lesser prohibition to save another from transgressing a more severe one. Although we learned that Tosfos only allows if the chaver is the one who caused the other to violate the prohibition (and here the Rav of the community is not responsible for others forgetting to be mafrish), there are two possible solutions for why it is permitted. First, Tosfos in his second answer writes that a chaver is allowed to transgress the lesser prohibition even if he is not responsible for his friend consuming tevel, and this is how we passkin lema’ase, as the משנ״ב סימן שו-סקנ״ו writes. Second, even according to Tosfos’ first answer, Tosfos agrees that a chaver may still transgress a lesser prohibition on behalf of the public even if he is not responsible for it. Thus, RSZA felt that it is better to transgress the lesser prohibition of being mafrish from one location to the other in order to allow people who forgot to be mafrish to be able to eat their matzah. This is also the pssak of משפטי ארץ (פ״ה-כא).","spans":[{"text":"The "},{"text":"מנחת יצחק ","bold":true},{"text":"(ח״ד-נט) (22) explains that this may still be permitted because "},{"text":"bedieved, challah ","italic":true},{"text":"may be separated even from one batch for another based on what we learned above that a "},{"text":"chaver ","italic":true},{"text":"is allowed to transgress a lesser prohibition to save another from transgressing a more severe one. Although we learned that Tosfos only allows if the "},{"text":"chaver ","italic":true},{"text":"is the one who caused the other to violate the prohibition (and here the Rav of the community is not responsible for others forgetting to be "},{"text":"mafrish","italic":true},{"text":"), there are two possible solutions for why it is permitted. First, Tosfos in his second answer writes"},{"text":" ","italic":true},{"text":"that a "},{"text":"chaver","italic":true},{"text":" is allowed to transgress the lesser prohibition even if he is not responsible for his friend consuming "},{"text":"tevel,","italic":true},{"text":" and this is how we "},{"text":"passkin lema’ase, ","italic":true},{"text":"as the "},{"text":"משנ״ב","bold":true},{"text":" סימן שו-סקנ״ו writes. Second, even according to Tosfos’ first answer, Tosfos agrees that a "},{"text":"chaver ","italic":true},{"text":"may still transgress a lesser prohibition on behalf of the public even if he is not responsible for it. Thus, RSZA felt that it is better to transgress the lesser prohibition of being "},{"text":"mafrish ","italic":true},{"text":"from one location to the other in order to allow people who forgot to be "},{"text":"mafrish ","italic":true},{"text":"to be able to eat their "},{"text":"matzah. ","italic":true},{"text":"This is also the "},{"text":"pssak ","italic":true},{"text":"of "},{"text":"משפטי ארץ ","bold":true},{"text":"(פ״ה-כא)."}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":36.0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}],"footnotes":[{"id":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":0,"xmlIndex":0,"text":" However, the גר״א (שלא-עח) passkins against the S”A that the hafrasha of a katan is never valid. ","spans":[{"text":" However, the גר״א (שלא-עח) "},{"text":"passkins ","italic":true},{"text":"against the S”A that the "},{"text":"hafrasha ","italic":true},{"text":"of a "},{"text":"katan ","italic":true},{"text":"is never valid. "}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}]},{"id":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":0,"xmlIndex":0,"text":" See: https://www.jdn.co.il/j_world/2221901/","spans":[{"text":" See: https://www.jdn.co.il/j_world/2221901/"}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}]},{"id":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":0,"xmlIndex":0,"text":" In footnote 13 there, RSZA is quoted as saying that almost every year, someone in his community forgot and he was very happy to know (\"ושמחתי\") that he was able to help others.","spans":[{"text":" In footnote 13 there, RSZA is quoted as saying that almost every year, someone in his community forgot and he was very happy to know (\"ושמחתי\") that he was able to help others."}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}]}]},"sort_order":18,"official_shiur_number":2,"estimated_minutes":10,"difficulty":"advanced","tags":["challah-part-1","original-difficulty:advanced","source:18","source:19","source:20","source:21","source:22"],"source_file_name":"Notes for Hafrashas Challah (part 1).docx","source_start_page":null,"source_end_page":null,"is_selectable":true},{"program_id":"winter-5787","chunk_code":"HC1-C10","source_type":"notes","siman":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","workbook_title":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","section_key":"C","section_title":"Differences Between Challah of Eretz Yisrael and Chutz La’aretz","chunk_title":"Sending Matzah Between EY and Chu”l","chunk_summary":"Status follows where dough was made according to Rambam/Kesef Mishneh; Cheshav Ha’Efod/Bach offers more lenient understanding.","content_markdown":"<u>Sending</u> *<u>matzah</u>* <u>to</u> *<u>chutz la’aretz</u>*\n\n\tWe learned above that if one forgot to be *mafrish challah* in *chutz la’aretz,* he may leave over some of the bread/*matzah* used on Shabbos or Yom Tov[18] and then be *mafrish* from what is left, but in *Eretz Yisrael,* it is forbidden. The **רמב״ם** (ביכורים פ״ה-ו) *passkins,* as explained by the כסף משנה and משנה למלך, that the **biblical** obligation of *hafrashas challah* applies only to flour that is made into dough in *Eretz Yisrael,* regardless of where the wheat is from.[19] Conversely, flour made from wheat of *Eretz Yisrael* which is brought to *chutz la’aretz* and made into dough there is only **rabbinically** obligated in *challah,* similar to all *challah* in *chu”l.* Many times, *matzah* bakeries ship their *matzah* from Israel to *chu”l* and vice versa. In light of the רמב\"ם, what should be done if you baked *matzah* with a group of people in a bakery and forgot to be *mafrish challah*?\t\n\n\tIf the *challah* was made in *chu”l* and sent to EY*,* one should leave over from every single *matzah* and then, on *Chol Ha’moed,* place them all in a container and be *mafrish* from the container, as mentioned above in the משנ״ב. However, if the *matzah* was made in *EY*, it would seem that one is not allowed to eat any of it before being *mafrish,* even if the *matzah* is now located in *chu”l,* and one should do what was suggested in the previous subsection. Nevertheless, the **חשב האפוד** (ח״ב-מג) (23), quoting the ב״ח, understands the רמב״ם differently and *passkins* that *challah* that was taken from *EY* to *chu”l* is only rabbinically obligated in *challah.* Therefore, you should eat the *matzah* and leave over a small portion from each piece to be *mafrish* from afterward. However, the **משפטי ארץ** (פ״ט-א) *passkins* like the כסף משנה and משנה למלך’s understanding of the רמב״ם above, and one would not be allowed to be *mafrish.* \n\nSource Notes\n\n[18]:  It should be noted that the S”A in סימן תקו-ג *passkins* that if the batch of dough was made on Yom Tov, you are allowed to be *mafrish.* It is forbidden to be *mafrish* on YT only if the dough was made before YT.\n\n[19]:  As of today, most flour in *Eretz Yisrael* comes from *chutz la’aretz.*","content_document":{"version":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":71,"xmlIndex":74,"text":"Sending matzah to chutz la’aretz","spans":[{"text":"Sending ","underline":true},{"text":"matzah ","italic":true,"underline":true},{"text":"to ","underline":true},{"text":"chutz la’aretz","italic":true,"underline":true}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":72,"xmlIndex":75,"text":"\tWe learned above that if one forgot to be mafrish challah in chutz la’aretz, he may leave over some of the bread/matzah used on Shabbos or Yom Tov[18] and then be mafrish from what is left, but in Eretz Yisrael, it is forbidden. The רמב״ם (ביכורים פ״ה-ו) passkins, as explained by the כסף משנה and משנה למלך, that the biblical obligation of hafrashas challah applies only to flour that is made into dough in Eretz Yisrael, regardless of where the wheat is from.[19] Conversely, flour made from wheat of Eretz Yisrael which is brought to chutz la’aretz and made into dough there is only rabbinically obligated in challah, similar to all challah in chu”l. Many times, matzah bakeries ship their matzah from Israel to chu”l and vice versa. In light of the רמב\"ם, what should be done if you baked matzah with a group of people in a bakery and forgot to be mafrish challah?\t","spans":[{"text":"\tWe learned above that if one forgot to be "},{"text":"mafrish challah ","italic":true},{"text":"in "},{"text":"chutz la’aretz, ","italic":true},{"text":"he may leave over some of the bread/"},{"text":"matzah ","italic":true},{"text":"used on Shabbos or Yom Tov"},{"text":"[18]","footnote":18},{"text":" and then be "},{"text":"mafrish ","italic":true},{"text":"from what is left, but in "},{"text":"Eretz Yisrael, ","italic":true},{"text":"it is forbidden. The "},{"text":"רמב״ם ","bold":true},{"text":"(ביכורים פ״ה-ו) "},{"text":"passkins, ","italic":true},{"text":"as explained by the כסף משנה and משנה למלך, that the "},{"text":"biblical","bold":true},{"text":" obligation of "},{"text":"hafrashas challah ","italic":true},{"text":"applies only to flour that is made into dough in "},{"text":"Eretz Yisrael, ","italic":true},{"text":"regardless of where the wheat is from."},{"text":"[19]","footnote":19},{"text":" Conversely, flour made from wheat of "},{"text":"Eretz Yisrael ","italic":true},{"text":"which is brought to "},{"text":"chutz la’aretz ","italic":true},{"text":"and made into dough there is only "},{"text":"rabbinically","bold":true},{"text":" obligated in "},{"text":"challah, ","italic":true},{"text":"similar to all "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"in "},{"text":"chu”l. ","italic":true},{"text":"Many times, "},{"text":"matzah ","italic":true},{"text":"bakeries ship their "},{"text":"matzah ","italic":true},{"text":"from Israel to "},{"text":"chu”l ","italic":true},{"text":"and vice versa. In light of the רמב\"ם, what should be done if you baked "},{"text":"matzah ","italic":true},{"text":"with a group of people in a bakery and forgot to be "},{"text":"mafrish challah","italic":true},{"text":"?\t"}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":1,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[18,19],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":73,"xmlIndex":76,"text":"\tIf the challah was made in chu”l and sent to EY, one should leave over from every single matzah and then, on Chol Ha’moed, place them all in a container and be mafrish from the container, as mentioned above in the משנ״ב. However, if the matzah was made in EY, it would seem that one is not allowed to eat any of it before being mafrish, even if the matzah is now located in chu”l, and one should do what was suggested in the previous subsection. Nevertheless, the חשב האפוד (ח״ב-מג) (23), quoting the ב״ח, understands the רמב״ם differently and passkins that challah that was taken from EY to chu”l is only rabbinically obligated in challah. Therefore, you should eat the matzah and leave over a small portion from each piece to be mafrish from afterward. However, the משפטי ארץ (פ״ט-א) passkins like the כסף משנה and משנה למלך’s understanding of the רמב״ם above, and one would not be allowed to be mafrish. ","spans":[{"text":"\tIf the "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"was made in "},{"text":"chu”l ","italic":true},{"text":"and sent to EY"},{"text":", ","italic":true},{"text":"one should leave over from every single "},{"text":"matzah ","italic":true},{"text":"and then, on "},{"text":"Chol Ha’moed, ","italic":true},{"text":"place them all in a container and be "},{"text":"mafrish ","italic":true},{"text":"from the container, as mentioned above in the משנ״ב. However, if the "},{"text":"matzah ","italic":true},{"text":"was made in "},{"text":"EY","italic":true},{"text":", it would seem that one is not allowed to eat any of it before being "},{"text":"mafrish, ","italic":true},{"text":"even if the "},{"text":"matzah ","italic":true},{"text":"is now located in "},{"text":"chu”l, ","italic":true},{"text":"and one should do what was suggested in the previous subsection. Nevertheless, the "},{"text":"חשב האפוד ","bold":true},{"text":"(ח״ב-מג) (23), quoting the ב״ח, understands the רמב״ם differently and "},{"text":"passkins ","italic":true},{"text":"that "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"that was taken from "},{"text":"EY","italic":true},{"text":" to "},{"text":"chu”l ","italic":true},{"text":"is only rabbinically obligated in "},{"text":"challah.","italic":true},{"text":" Therefore, you should eat the "},{"text":"matzah","italic":true},{"text":" and leave over a small portion from each piece"},{"text":" ","italic":true},{"text":"to"},{"text":" ","italic":true},{"text":"be"},{"text":" mafrish","italic":true},{"text":" from afterward. However, the "},{"text":"משפטי ארץ ","bold":true},{"text":"(פ״ט-א) "},{"text":"passkins ","italic":true},{"text":"like the כסף משנה and משנה למלך’s understanding of the רמב״ם above, and one would not be allowed to be "},{"text":"mafrish. ","italic":true}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":1,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}],"footnotes":[{"id":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":0,"xmlIndex":0,"text":" It should be noted that the S”A in סימן תקו-ג passkins that if the batch of dough was made on Yom Tov, you are allowed to be mafrish. It is forbidden to be mafrish on YT only if the dough was made before YT.","spans":[{"text":" It should be noted that the S”A in סימן תקו-ג "},{"text":"passkins ","italic":true},{"text":"that if the batch of dough was made on Yom Tov, you are allowed to be "},{"text":"mafrish. ","italic":true},{"text":"It is forbidden to be "},{"text":"mafrish ","italic":true},{"text":"on YT only if the dough was made before YT."}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}]},{"id":19,"blocks":[{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":0,"xmlIndex":0,"text":" As of today, most flour in Eretz Yisrael comes from chutz la’aretz.","spans":[{"text":" As of today, most flour in "},{"text":"Eretz Yisrael ","italic":true},{"text":"comes from "},{"text":"chutz la’aretz.","italic":true}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}]}]},"sort_order":19,"official_shiur_number":2,"estimated_minutes":6,"difficulty":"advanced","tags":["challah-part-1","original-difficulty:advanced","source:23"],"source_file_name":"Notes for Hafrashas Challah (part 1).docx","source_start_page":null,"source_end_page":null,"is_selectable":true},{"program_id":"winter-5787","chunk_code":"HC1-C11","source_type":"notes","siman":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","workbook_title":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","section_key":"C","section_title":"Differences Between Challah of Eretz Yisrael and Chutz La’aretz","chunk_title":"Bracha When Separating After Eating","chunk_summary":"Machlokes whether bracha is recited when separating after Shabbos/Yom Tov in chu”l.","content_markdown":"*<u>Bracha</u>* <u>when</u> *<u>mafrish</u>* <u>after eating</u>\n\n\tWe learned above that if one forgot to be *mafrish challah* before Shabbos or YT in *chu”l,* he may eat and leave over some bread from which to be *mafrish* from after Shabbos or YT. **RSZA**, quoted in שש״כ (פמ״ב-הע׳ נז), *passkins* that one should not recite a *bracha* when being *mafrish* after Shabbos. Since the bread was intentionally set aside on Shabbos for the *hafrasha* after Shabbos, it is viewed as having taken effect retroactively on Shabbos. Therefore, one cannot recite a *bracha* on *Motzaei Shabbos* for a *hafrasha* that is deemed to have already occurred. However, the **ערה״ש** (או״ח שכג-ג) and **Rav Chaim Kanievsky** דרך אמונה, ביכורים פ״ה- סקקכא)) *passkin* that one should recite a *bracha* when being *mafrish* since the Gemara does not say otherwise.","content_document":{"version":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":74,"xmlIndex":77,"text":"Bracha when mafrish after eating","spans":[{"text":"Bracha ","italic":true,"underline":true},{"text":"when ","underline":true},{"text":"mafrish ","italic":true,"underline":true},{"text":"after eating","underline":true}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":75,"xmlIndex":78,"text":"\tWe learned above that if one forgot to be mafrish challah before Shabbos or YT in chu”l, he may eat and leave over some bread from which to be mafrish from after Shabbos or YT. RSZA, quoted in שש״כ (פמ״ב-הע׳ נז), passkins that one should not recite a bracha when being mafrish after Shabbos. Since the bread was intentionally set aside on Shabbos for the hafrasha after Shabbos, it is viewed as having taken effect retroactively on Shabbos. Therefore, one cannot recite a bracha on Motzaei Shabbos for a hafrasha that is deemed to have already occurred. However, the ערה״ש (או״ח שכג-ג) and Rav Chaim Kanievsky דרך אמונה, ביכורים פ״ה- סקקכא)) passkin that one should recite a bracha when being mafrish since the Gemara does not say otherwise.","spans":[{"text":"\tWe learned above that if one forgot to be "},{"text":"mafrish challah ","italic":true},{"text":"before Shabbos or YT in "},{"text":"chu”l, ","italic":true},{"text":"he may eat and leave over some bread from which to be "},{"text":"mafrish ","italic":true},{"text":"from after Shabbos or YT."},{"text":" RSZA","bold":true},{"text":","},{"text":" ","bold":true},{"text":"quoted in שש״כ (פמ״ב-הע׳ נז), "},{"text":"passkins ","italic":true},{"text":"that one should not recite a "},{"text":"bracha ","italic":true},{"text":"when being "},{"text":"mafrish ","italic":true},{"text":"after Shabbos. Since the bread was intentionally set aside on Shabbos for the "},{"text":"hafrasha","italic":true},{"text":" after Shabbos, it is viewed as having taken effect retroactively on Shabbos. Therefore, one cannot recite a "},{"text":"bracha","italic":true},{"text":" on "},{"text":"Motzaei Shabbos","italic":true},{"text":" for a "},{"text":"hafrasha","italic":true},{"text":" that is deemed to have already occurred."},{"text":" ","italic":true},{"text":"However, the "},{"text":"ערה״ש ","bold":true},{"text":"(או״ח שכג-ג) and "},{"text":"Rav Chaim Kanievsky","bold":true},{"text":" דרך אמונה, ביכורים פ״ה- סקקכא)) "},{"text":"passkin ","italic":true},{"text":"that one should recite a "},{"text":"bracha ","italic":true},{"text":"when being "},{"text":"mafrish ","italic":true},{"text":"since the Gemara does not say otherwise."}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":1,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}],"footnotes":[]},"sort_order":20,"official_shiur_number":2,"estimated_minutes":4,"difficulty":"advanced","tags":["challah-part-1","original-difficulty:advanced"],"source_file_name":"Notes for Hafrashas Challah (part 1).docx","source_start_page":null,"source_end_page":null,"is_selectable":true},{"program_id":"winter-5787","chunk_code":"HC1-D1","source_type":"notes","siman":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","workbook_title":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","section_key":"D","section_title":"How Much to be Mafrish?","chunk_title":"How Much to Separate — Intro","chunk_summary":"Introduces 1/24 for household, 1/48 for baker, and that today the practice changed.","content_markdown":"*D.   How Much to be Mafrish?*\n\nAfter learning the basic differences between *hafrashas challah* in EY and *chu”l,* in this section we will discuss how much dough to be *mafrish*. We will learn that initially, in *Eretz Yisrael,* one baking bread at home needed to be *mafrish* 1/24 of the dough, while a baker was required to be *mafrish* only 1/48 of the batch. Today, however, the amount separated has changed, as will be explained below. ","content_document":{"version":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":76,"xmlIndex":79,"text":"D.   How Much to be Mafrish?","spans":[{"text":"D.   How Much to be Mafrish?","italic":true,"color":"#2F5496"}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":38.0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0,"rightIndent":43.2,"tabStops":[{"position":150.45,"alignment":"left","leader":"none"},{"position":234.0,"alignment":"center","leader":"none"},{"position":279.0,"alignment":"left","leader":"none"}]},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":77,"xmlIndex":81,"text":"After learning the basic differences between hafrashas challah in EY and chu”l, in this section we will discuss how much dough to be mafrish. We will learn that initially, in Eretz Yisrael, one baking bread at home needed to be mafrish 1/24 of the dough, while a baker was required to be mafrish only 1/48 of the batch. Today, however, the amount separated has changed, as will be explained below. ","spans":[{"text":"After learning the basic differences between "},{"text":"hafrashas challah ","italic":true},{"text":"in EY and "},{"text":"chu”l, ","italic":true},{"text":"in this section we will discuss how much dough to be "},{"text":"mafrish","italic":true},{"text":". We will learn that initially, in "},{"text":"Eretz Yisrael, ","italic":true},{"text":"one baking bread at home needed to be "},{"text":"mafrish ","italic":true},{"text":"1/24 of the dough, while a baker was required to be "},{"text":"mafrish ","italic":true},{"text":"only 1/48 of the batch. Today, however, the amount separated has changed, as will be explained below. "}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":36.0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}],"footnotes":[]},"sort_order":21,"official_shiur_number":2,"estimated_minutes":3,"difficulty":"core","tags":["challah-part-1","original-difficulty:core"],"source_file_name":"Notes for Hafrashas Challah (part 1).docx","source_start_page":null,"source_end_page":null,"is_selectable":true},{"program_id":"winter-5787","chunk_code":"HC1-D2","source_type":"notes","siman":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","workbook_title":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","section_key":"D","section_title":"How Much to be Mafrish?","chunk_title":"Rambam: One Mitzvah, Any Amount Biblically","chunk_summary":"Rambam: separating and giving are one mitzvah; any amount works biblically, 1/24 or 1/48 is respectable amount.","content_markdown":"In order to understand why 1/24 and 1/48 were originally the required amounts for *hafrasha,* we first need to better understand the structure and framework of the *mitzvah* of *hafrashas challah.* The **רמב״ם** (עשה-קלג) (24) *passkins* that there is one *mitzvah* of separating *challah* from the dough and giving it to the *kohen.* In הלכות ביכורים (פ״ה-א), the **רמב״ם** explains that, biblically speaking, one may fulfill the *mitzvah* by separating and giving even the smallest amount. However, the verse ״תתן לו״ – “you shall give him,” teaches that you should separate an amount that is worthy of giving, which is 1/24 of a household’s batch of dough and 1/48 from a baker’s batch. \n\nThese amounts of 1/24 and 1/48 originate in the **משנה חלה** (פ״ב-ז) (25), which teaches that one who bakes bread at home must separate 1/24 of the dough, even if baking for a large number of guests, while a baker need separate only 1/48 of the dough. The **רמב״ם** in פירוש המשניות explains further that when baking at home, a larger portion of 1/24 is needed in order to fulfill the *passuk* of “giving,” which implies a respectable amount. A baker, in contrast, who prepares much larger batches of dough, gives a smaller percentage of 1/48, which generally results in the same amount of dough as 1/24 of a household batch. The Mishnah adds that if the batch of dough becomes *tamei,* only 1/48 is separated.\n\nIt seems clear from the רמב״ם that since separating and giving constitute one *mitzvah,* and biblically one fulfills the obligation with any amount, his writing that one must give 1/24 or 1/48 to fulfill the *passuk* of “giving a respectable amount” must be only an אסמכתא. Indeed, the **מזרחי** on Rashi (במדבר פט״ו-כא) writes explicitly that the verse is only an אסמכתא.","content_document":{"version":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":78,"xmlIndex":82,"text":"In order to understand why 1/24 and 1/48 were originally the required amounts for hafrasha, we first need to better understand the structure and framework of the mitzvah of hafrashas challah. The רמב״ם (עשה-קלג) (24) passkins that there is one mitzvah of separating challah from the dough and giving it to the kohen. In הלכות ביכורים (פ״ה-א), the רמב״ם explains that, biblically speaking, one may fulfill the mitzvah by separating and giving even the smallest amount. However, the verse ״תתן לו״ – “you shall give him,” teaches that you should separate an amount that is worthy of giving, which is 1/24 of a household’s batch of dough and 1/48 from a baker’s batch. ","spans":[{"text":"In order to understand why 1/24 and 1/48 were originally the required amounts for "},{"text":"hafrasha, ","italic":true},{"text":"we first need to better understand the structure and framework of the "},{"text":"mitzvah ","italic":true},{"text":"of "},{"text":"hafrashas challah. ","italic":true},{"text":"The "},{"text":"רמב״ם","bold":true},{"text":" (עשה-קלג) (24) "},{"text":"passkins ","italic":true},{"text":"that there is one "},{"text":"mitzvah","italic":true},{"text":" of separating "},{"text":"challah","italic":true},{"text":" from the dough and giving it to the "},{"text":"kohen. ","italic":true},{"text":"In הלכות ביכורים (פ״ה-א), the "},{"text":"רמב״ם","bold":true},{"text":" explains"},{"text":" ","italic":true},{"text":"that, biblically speaking, one may fulfill the "},{"text":"mitzvah ","italic":true},{"text":"by separating and giving even the smallest amount. However, the verse ״תתן לו״ – “you shall give him,” teaches that you should separate an amount that is worthy of giving, which is 1/24 of a household’s batch of dough and 1/48 from a baker’s batch. "}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":36.0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":79,"xmlIndex":83,"text":"These amounts of 1/24 and 1/48 originate in the משנה חלה (פ״ב-ז) (25), which teaches that one who bakes bread at home must separate 1/24 of the dough, even if baking for a large number of guests, while a baker need separate only 1/48 of the dough. The רמב״ם in פירוש המשניות explains further that when baking at home, a larger portion of 1/24 is needed in order to fulfill the passuk of “giving,” which implies a respectable amount. A baker, in contrast, who prepares much larger batches of dough, gives a smaller percentage of 1/48, which generally results in the same amount of dough as 1/24 of a household batch. The Mishnah adds that if the batch of dough becomes tamei, only 1/48 is separated.","spans":[{"text":"These amounts of 1/24 and 1/48 originate in the "},{"text":"משנה חלה ","bold":true},{"text":"(פ״ב-ז) (25), which teaches that one who bakes bread at home must separate 1/24 of the dough, even if baking for a large number of guests, while a baker need separate only 1/48 of the dough. The "},{"text":"רמב״ם","bold":true},{"text":" in פירוש המשניות explains further that when baking at home, a larger portion of 1/24 is needed in order to fulfill the "},{"text":"passuk","italic":true},{"text":" of “giving,” which implies a respectable amount. A baker, in contrast, who prepares much larger batches of dough, gives a smaller percentage of 1/48, which generally results in the same amount of dough as 1/24 of a household batch. The Mishnah adds that if the batch of dough becomes "},{"text":"tamei, ","italic":true},{"text":"only 1/48 is separated."}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":36.0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":80,"xmlIndex":84,"text":"It seems clear from the רמב״ם that since separating and giving constitute one mitzvah, and biblically one fulfills the obligation with any amount, his writing that one must give 1/24 or 1/48 to fulfill the passuk of “giving a respectable amount” must be only an אסמכתא. Indeed, the מזרחי on Rashi (במדבר פט״ו-כא) writes explicitly that the verse is only an אסמכתא.","spans":[{"text":"It seems clear from the רמב״ם that since separating and giving constitute one "},{"text":"mitzvah, ","italic":true},{"text":"and biblically one fulfills the obligation with any amount, his writing that one must give 1/24 or 1/48 to fulfill the "},{"text":"passuk","italic":true},{"text":" of “giving a respectable amount” must be only an אסמכתא. Indeed, the "},{"text":"מזרחי ","bold":true},{"text":"on Rashi (במדבר פט״ו-כא) writes explicitly that the verse is only an אסמכתא."}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":36.0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}],"footnotes":[]},"sort_order":22,"official_shiur_number":2,"estimated_minutes":5,"difficulty":"core","tags":["challah-part-1","original-difficulty:core","source:24","source:25"],"source_file_name":"Notes for Hafrashas Challah (part 1).docx","source_start_page":null,"source_end_page":null,"is_selectable":true},{"program_id":"winter-5787","chunk_code":"HC1-D3","source_type":"notes","siman":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","workbook_title":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","section_key":"D","section_title":"How Much to be Mafrish?","chunk_title":"Ramban and Noda B’Yehuda: Separating vs Giving","chunk_summary":"Ramban counts separating and giving as separate mitzvos; Noda B’Yehuda gives framework for biblical amount of giving.","content_markdown":"The **רמב״ן** (השגות-שורש יב) (26) argues on the רמב״ם and *passkins* that *hafrashas challah* consists of two distinct *mitzvos*: 1) Separating dough as *challah*, thereby rendering the remaining dough permissible to eat (and no longer *tevel*). 2) Giving the dough to the *kohen.*\n\nThe **נודע ביהודה** (יו״ד תנינא -רא) (27) writes that even giving 1/24 or 1/48 can constitute part of the biblical obligation. He explains that since there are two parts to the *mitzvah* of *hafrashas challah,* separating and giving, whenever one separates even the smallest amount, he biblically fulfills the *mitzvah* of separating. However, when giving the dough to a *kohen,* the נודע ביהודה suggests that the biblical amount one must separate is always 1/24 of the minimum quantity of flour required for *hafrashas challah* (one-tenth of an *eifah,* which we will learn below is between 1.2-2.2 kg). If one prepares only the minimum amount of dough that requires *hafrashas challah*, then there is a biblical commandment to give an amount that is considered a meaningful gift, i.e., 1/24 of a household batch and 1/48 of a baker’s batch (which is roughly the same amount since the baker bakes more). However, if a household batch is prepared to be double the biblical amount required to be *mafrish*, on a biblical level, he needs to give 1/48 of the batch of dough, while rabbinically, he needs to give 1/24 of the actual batch.[20] \n\nTo summarize:\n\n- The רמב״ם *passkins* that separating and giving *challah* constitute one *mitzvah* that can be fulfilled with any amount, but rabbinically, one should give 1/24 of a household batch of dough and 1/48 of a baker’s batch since that is defined as a respectable portion.\n\n- The רמב״ן argues that there are two *mitzvos* – separating and giving.\n\n- According to theנודע ביהודה, the biblical *mitzvah* of separating is fulfilled by separating any amount, while the biblical *mitzvah* of giving is fulfilled by giving 1/24 from the minimum quantity of dough required for *hafrashas challah.* (If the batch is larger than the minimum quantity, 1/24 is still given, but any amount above 1/24 of the minimum quantity is rabbinic.)\n\nSource Notes\n\n[20]:  Most understand that the נודע ביהודה only makes sense within the רמב״ן who learns that there are two parts to the *mitzvah,* but the שבט הלוי (ח״ב-קעח סק״ב) understands that the נודע ביהודה can even fit within the רמב״ם.","content_document":{"version":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":81,"xmlIndex":85,"text":"The רמב״ן (השגות-שורש יב) (26) argues on the רמב״ם and passkins that hafrashas challah consists of two distinct mitzvos: 1) Separating dough as challah, thereby rendering the remaining dough permissible to eat (and no longer tevel). 2) Giving the dough to the kohen.","spans":[{"text":"The "},{"text":"רמב״ן ","bold":true},{"text":"(השגות-שורש יב)"},{"text":" ","bold":true},{"text":"(26) argues on the רמב״ם and "},{"text":"passkins ","italic":true},{"text":"that "},{"text":"hafrashas challah ","italic":true},{"text":"consists of two distinct "},{"text":"mitzvos","italic":true},{"text":": 1) Separating dough as "},{"text":"challah","italic":true},{"text":", thereby rendering the remaining dough permissible to eat (and no longer "},{"text":"tevel","italic":true},{"text":"). 2) Giving the dough to the "},{"text":"kohen.","italic":true}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":36.0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":82,"xmlIndex":86,"text":"The נודע ביהודה (יו״ד תנינא -רא) (27) writes that even giving 1/24 or 1/48 can constitute part of the biblical obligation. He explains that since there are two parts to the mitzvah of hafrashas challah, separating and giving, whenever one separates even the smallest amount, he biblically fulfills the mitzvah of separating. However, when giving the dough to a kohen, the נודע ביהודה suggests that the biblical amount one must separate is always 1/24 of the minimum quantity of flour required for hafrashas challah (one-tenth of an eifah, which we will learn below is between 1.2-2.2 kg). If one prepares only the minimum amount of dough that requires hafrashas challah, then there is a biblical commandment to give an amount that is considered a meaningful gift, i.e., 1/24 of a household batch and 1/48 of a baker’s batch (which is roughly the same amount since the baker bakes more). However, if a household batch is prepared to be double the biblical amount required to be mafrish, on a biblical level, he needs to give 1/48 of the batch of dough, while rabbinically, he needs to give 1/24 of the actual batch.[20] ","spans":[{"text":"The "},{"text":"נודע ביהודה ","bold":true},{"text":"(יו״ד תנינא -רא) (27) writes that even giving 1/24 or 1/48 can constitute part of the biblical obligation. He explains that since there are two parts to the "},{"text":"mitzvah ","italic":true},{"text":"of "},{"text":"hafrashas challah, ","italic":true},{"text":"separating and giving, whenever one separates even the smallest amount, he biblically fulfills the "},{"text":"mitzvah ","italic":true},{"text":"of separating. However, when giving the dough to a "},{"text":"kohen, ","italic":true},{"text":"the נודע ביהודה suggests that the biblical amount one must separate is always 1/24 of the minimum quantity of flour required for "},{"text":"hafrashas challah ","italic":true},{"text":"(","italic":false},{"text":"one-tenth of an "},{"text":"eifah, ","italic":true},{"text":"which we will learn below is between 1.2-2.2 kg). ","italic":false},{"text":"If one prepares only the minimum amount of dough that requires "},{"text":"hafrashas challah","italic":true},{"text":", then there is a biblical commandment to give an amount that is considered a meaningful gift, i.e., 1/24 of a household batch and 1/48 of a baker’s batch (which is roughly the same amount since the baker bakes more). However, if a household batch is prepared to be double the biblical amount required to be "},{"text":"mafrish","italic":true},{"text":", on a biblical level, he needs to give 1/48 of the batch of dough, while rabbinically, he needs to give 1/24 of the actual batch."},{"text":"[20]","footnote":20},{"text":" "}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":36.0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[20],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":83,"xmlIndex":87,"text":"To summarize:","spans":[{"text":"To summarize:"}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"list","paragraph":84,"xmlIndex":88,"text":"The רמב״ם passkins that separating and giving challah constitute one mitzvah that can be fulfilled with any amount, but rabbinically, one should give 1/24 of a household batch of dough and 1/48 of a baker’s batch since that is defined as a respectable portion. ","spans":[{"text":"The רמב״ם "},{"text":"passkins","italic":true},{"text":" that separating and giving "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"constitute one "},{"text":"mitzvah ","italic":true},{"text":"that can be fulfilled with any amount, but rabbinically, one should give 1/24 of a household batch of dough and 1/48 of a baker’s batch since that is defined as a respectable portion. "}],"marker":"•","level":0,"indent":36.0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0,"hanging":18.0},{"kind":"list","paragraph":85,"xmlIndex":89,"text":"The רמב״ן argues that there are two mitzvos – separating and giving. ","spans":[{"text":"The רמב״ן argues that there are two "},{"text":"mitzvos ","italic":true},{"text":"–"},{"text":" ","italic":true},{"text":"separating and giving. "}],"marker":"•","level":0,"indent":36.0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0,"hanging":18.0},{"kind":"list","paragraph":86,"xmlIndex":90,"text":"According to theנודע ביהודה, the biblical mitzvah of separating is fulfilled by separating any amount, while the biblical mitzvah of giving is fulfilled by giving 1/24 from the minimum quantity of dough required for hafrashas challah. (If the batch is larger than the minimum quantity, 1/24 is still given, but any amount above 1/24 of the minimum quantity is rabbinic.) ","spans":[{"text":"According to theנודע ביהודה, the biblical "},{"text":"mitzvah ","italic":true},{"text":"of separating is fulfilled by separating any amount, while the biblical "},{"text":"mitzvah ","italic":true},{"text":"of giving is fulfilled by giving 1/24 from the minimum quantity of dough required for "},{"text":"hafrashas challah. ","italic":true},{"text":"(If the batch is larger than the minimum quantity, 1/24 is still given, but any amount above 1/24 of the minimum quantity is rabbinic.) "}],"marker":"•","level":0,"indent":36.0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0,"hanging":18.0}],"footnotes":[{"id":20,"blocks":[{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":0,"xmlIndex":0,"text":" Most understand that the נודע ביהודה only makes sense within the רמב״ן who learns that there are two parts to the mitzvah, but the שבט הלוי (ח״ב-קעח סק״ב) understands that the נודע ביהודה can even fit within the רמב״ם.","spans":[{"text":" Most understand that the נודע ביהודה only makes sense within the רמב״ן who learns that there are two parts to the "},{"text":"mitzvah, ","italic":true},{"text":"but the שבט הלוי (ח״ב-קעח סק״ב) understands that the נודע ביהודה can even fit within the רמב״ם."}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}]}]},"sort_order":23,"official_shiur_number":2,"estimated_minutes":6,"difficulty":"advanced","tags":["challah-part-1","original-difficulty:advanced","source:26","source:27"],"source_file_name":"Notes for Hafrashas Challah (part 1).docx","source_start_page":null,"source_end_page":null,"is_selectable":true},{"program_id":"winter-5787","chunk_code":"HC1-D4","source_type":"notes","siman":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","workbook_title":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","section_key":"D","section_title":"How Much to be Mafrish?","chunk_title":"Practical Amount in EY and Chu”l","chunk_summary":"S”A/Rama/minhag: today Ashkenazim and Sephardim separate one challah, any amount, custom is kezayis, and burn it.","content_markdown":" How do we *passkin*? The S”A in **סימן שכב-א** (28) quotes the language of the רמב״ם that there is a biblical *mitzvah* to separate *challah* and give it to a *kohen*, and the biblical requirement is fulfilled by separating even the smallest amount. However, on a rabbinic level, one should give 1/24 of a household batch of dough and 1/48 of a baker’s batch. The S”A adds, based on the **משנה חלה** (פ״ב-ז) quoted above, that if the *challah* becomes *tamei* and will be burned anyway, even a private individual at home  need separate only 1/48. \n\nAll of the above pertains to *hafrashas challah* in *EY,* but in *chu”l,* the *halacha* is somewhat different, as we will see*.* The **משנה חלה** (פ״ד-ח) states that the world is divided into three regions: Parts of *EY* that were sanctified by Ezra, parts of *EY* that were not sanctified by Ezra, and the rest of the world. The Mishna explains that the number of *challos* (i.e., pieces of dough, one or two) separated and the size of each *challah* varies by region. In parts of *EY* that were sanctified by Ezra only one *challah* is separated, and in the other parts of *EY* that were not sanctified by Ezra and in *chu”l,* two *challahs* are separated, one that is burned and another that is given to a *kohen*.[21] \n\nThere is a three-way dispute among the *Rishonim* regarding how to understand this *Mishnah.* For the sake of clarity and brevity, we will only discuss the rulings of the S”A, *Rama*, and the practical *halacha* today, which is agreed upon by both *Ashkenazim* and *Sephardim*. \n\nIn EY, everyone agrees that one *challah* is separated and burned. In *chu”l,* the **S”A** in סעיפים ד-ה (29) *passkins* that you may either separate two, one which is burned and the other which is given to any *kohen,* or you may separate one (the size of 1/48 of the dough)[22] and give it to a *kohen* who is not *tamei* with *tumah* that emanates from his body (i.e., *baal keri, niddah, zav,* or *zava*). The ***Rama*** in סעיף ה argues that both in *EY* and in *chu”l,* only one *challah* is separated. It may be of any size, although the custom is to separate a *kezayis*, and it should be burned*.* \n\nThe **ט״ז** (ה) explains that the custom is to burn the *challah* rather than give it to any *kohen* since we are not certain who is definitely a *kohen* today. The *Rama* adds that if a *kohen* does eat the *challah,* he should first recite a special *bracha.*[23]\n\nTo summarize:\n\n- In <u>EY</u> one *challah* is separated and burned.\n\n  - **S”A** – *challah* should be the size of 1/48 of the dough.\n\n  - ***Rama*** – any amount, although the custom is a *kezayis.*\n\n- In *<u>chu”l</u>*:\n\n  - **S”A** – either separate two *challos*, burn one and give the other to a *kohen,* even if he is *tamei,* or separate one *challah* and give it to a *kohen* who is not *tamei* with a *tumah* that  emanates from his body.\n\n  - ***Rama*** – today, both in EY and in *chu”l,* only one *challah* of any size is separated, but preferably should be the size of a *kezayis,* and it is burned (because we are not certain who is a real *kohen*).\n\nHow do we *passkin lema’ase*? *Ashkenazim passkin* like the *Rama* that any amount of *challah* may be separated, although the custom is to take a *kezayis,* and it should be burned*.* Regarding *Sephardim,* the **בית יוסף** writes that the *minhag* is to be *mafrish* only one challah and burn it, and it seems the בית יוסף is referring to both EY and *chu”l* (like the *Rama*)*.* The **ברכי יוסף** (שכב-ב) (30) writes that although the S”A does not quote it here, in **סימן שלא-יט** he *passkins* that since *terumah* today is burned, it suffices to separate any amount, and the same would apply to *challah.* **Rav Ovadya** in הליכות עולם (ח״ג-עמ׳ מט) also *passkins* that even *Sephardim* should only separate one *challah,* which may be any size, and it should be burned. \n\nSource Notes\n\n[21]:  The ירושלמי (חלה פ״ד-ד) explains the reason for why areas of *EY* not sanctified by Ezra require two *challahs* to be separated. The first *challah* is separated and burned because *Chazal* decreed a *tumah* status on *chu”l* as well as areas of *EY* not sanctified by Ezra (טומאת ארץ העמים). However, since many are not aware of this *tumah* status, they might be led to believe that all separated *challah* needs to be burned. For this reason, we give a second *challah* to the *kohen.* But we also cannot give the *kohen* one *challah* alone because then those who are aware of the *tumah* status will think that *challah* which is *tamei* may be eaten. For this reason, both *challahs* are necessary. In regular *chu”l,* the רמב״ם (ביכורים פ״ה-ח) explains that the first *challah* is burned because *chu”l* is *tamei,* and the second is given to the *kohen* so we do not forget about *challah.* \n\n[22]:  The smallest amount size is used since the *hafrasha* is in *chu”l.*\n\n[23]:  The *Rama* in *hilchos* Pesach in סימן תנז writes that a *kohen* child may eat *matzah* made from the *hafrashas challah* dough*.* The **ש״ך** (שכב-ט) asks that the *Rama* seems to be contradicting himself. Here he *passkins* that the custom is to burn *challah,* yet in *Hilchos* Pesach he *passkins* that one may give it to a *katan?* The ש״ך answers that one must differentiate between Pesach and other times of the year. The **מג״א** (תנז-ט) explains that on *Erev Pesach* (and Pesach) the custom is to give the *challah* to a child *kohen* since there are a lot of *challahs* being separated and if they don’t eat from the *challah*, people might question whether they are *kohanim*. The **ערה״ש** (שכב-כא) proves that the *Rama* agrees to this distinction that there are times when *kohanim* do eat the *challah,* since here he writes the *nussach* of the *bracha* a *kohen* would recite when eating the *challah.* ","content_document":{"version":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":87,"xmlIndex":91,"text":" How do we passkin? The S”A in סימן שכב-א (28) quotes the language of the רמב״ם that there is a biblical mitzvah to separate challah and give it to a kohen, and the biblical requirement is fulfilled by separating even the smallest amount. However, on a rabbinic level, one should give 1/24 of a household batch of dough and 1/48 of a baker’s batch. The S”A adds, based on the משנה חלה (פ״ב-ז) quoted above, that if the challah becomes tamei and will be burned anyway, even a private individual at home  need separate only 1/48. ","spans":[{"text":" How do we "},{"text":"passkin","italic":true},{"text":"? The S”A in "},{"text":"סימן שכב-א","bold":true},{"text":" (28) quotes the language of the רמב״ם that there is a biblical "},{"text":"mitzvah ","italic":true},{"text":"to separate "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"and give it to a "},{"text":"kohen","italic":true},{"text":","},{"text":" ","italic":true},{"text":"and"},{"text":" ","italic":true},{"text":"the biblical requirement is fulfilled by separating even the smallest amount. However, on a rabbinic level, one should give 1/24 of a household batch of dough and 1/48 of a baker’s batch. The S”A adds, based on the "},{"text":"משנה חלה ","bold":true},{"text":"(פ״ב-ז) quoted above, that if the "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"becomes "},{"text":"tamei ","italic":true},{"text":"and will be burned anyway, even a private individual at home  need separate only 1/48. "}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":36.0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":88,"xmlIndex":92,"text":"All of the above pertains to hafrashas challah in EY, but in chu”l, the halacha is somewhat different, as we will see. The משנה חלה (פ״ד-ח) states that the world is divided into three regions: Parts of EY that were sanctified by Ezra, parts of EY that were not sanctified by Ezra, and the rest of the world. The Mishna explains that the number of challos (i.e., pieces of dough, one or two) separated and the size of each challah varies by region. In parts of EY that were sanctified by Ezra only one challah is separated, and in the other parts of EY that were not sanctified by Ezra and in chu”l, two challahs are separated, one that is burned and another that is given to a kohen.[21] ","spans":[{"text":"All of the above pertains to "},{"text":"hafrashas challah ","italic":true},{"text":"in "},{"text":"EY, ","italic":true},{"text":"but in "},{"text":"chu”l, ","italic":true},{"text":"the "},{"text":"halacha ","italic":true},{"text":"is somewhat different, as we will see"},{"text":". ","italic":true},{"text":"The "},{"text":"משנה חלה ","bold":true},{"text":"(פ״ד-ח) states that the world is divided into three regions: Parts of "},{"text":"EY ","italic":true},{"text":"that were sanctified by Ezra, parts of "},{"text":"EY ","italic":true},{"text":"that were not sanctified by Ezra, and the rest of the world. The Mishna explains that the number of "},{"text":"challos ","italic":true},{"text":"(i.e., pieces of dough, one or two) separated and the size of each "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"varies by region. In parts of "},{"text":"EY ","italic":true},{"text":"that were sanctified by Ezra only one "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"is separated, and in the other parts of "},{"text":"EY ","italic":true},{"text":"that were not sanctified by Ezra and in "},{"text":"chu”l, ","italic":true},{"text":"two "},{"text":"challahs ","italic":true},{"text":"are separated, one that is burned and another that is given to a "},{"text":"kohen","italic":true},{"text":"."},{"text":"[21]","footnote":21},{"text":" "}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":36.0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[21],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":89,"xmlIndex":93,"text":"There is a three-way dispute among the Rishonim regarding how to understand this Mishnah. For the sake of clarity and brevity, we will only discuss the rulings of the S”A, Rama, and the practical halacha today, which is agreed upon by both Ashkenazim and Sephardim. ","spans":[{"text":"There is a three-way dispute among the "},{"text":"Rishonim ","italic":true},{"text":"regarding how to understand this "},{"text":"Mishnah. ","italic":true},{"text":"For the sake of clarity and brevity, we will only discuss the rulings of the S”A, "},{"text":"Rama","italic":true},{"text":","},{"text":" ","italic":true},{"text":"and the practical "},{"text":"halacha ","italic":true},{"text":"today,"},{"text":" ","italic":true},{"text":"which is agreed upon by both "},{"text":"Ashkenazim ","italic":true},{"text":"and "},{"text":"Sephardim","italic":true},{"text":". "}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":36.0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":90,"xmlIndex":94,"text":"In EY, everyone agrees that one challah is separated and burned. In chu”l, the S”A in סעיפים ד-ה (29) passkins that you may either separate two, one which is burned and the other which is given to any kohen, or you may separate one (the size of 1/48 of the dough)[22] and give it to a kohen who is not tamei with tumah that emanates from his body (i.e., baal keri, niddah, zav, or zava). The Rama in סעיף ה argues that both in EY and in chu”l, only one challah is separated. It may be of any size, although the custom is to separate a kezayis, and it should be burned. ","spans":[{"text":"In EY, everyone agrees that one "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"is separated and burned. In "},{"text":"chu”l, ","italic":true},{"text":"the "},{"text":"S”A","bold":true},{"text":" in סעיפים ד-ה (29) "},{"text":"passkins ","italic":true},{"text":"that you may either separate two, one which is burned and the other which is given to any "},{"text":"kohen, ","italic":true},{"text":"or you may separate one (the size of 1/48 of the dough)"},{"text":"[22]","footnote":22},{"text":" and give it to a "},{"text":"kohen ","italic":true},{"text":"who is not "},{"text":"tamei ","italic":true},{"text":"with "},{"text":"tumah ","italic":true},{"text":"that emanates from his body (i.e., "},{"text":"baal keri, niddah, zav, ","italic":true},{"text":"or "},{"text":"zava","italic":true},{"text":"). The "},{"text":"Rama","bold":true,"italic":true},{"text":" ","italic":true},{"text":"in סעיף ה argues that both in "},{"text":"EY ","italic":true},{"text":"and in "},{"text":"chu”l, ","italic":true},{"text":"only one "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"is separated. It may be of any size, although the custom is to separate a "},{"text":"kezayis","italic":true},{"text":", and it should be burned"},{"text":". ","italic":true}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":36.0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[22],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":91,"xmlIndex":95,"text":"The ט״ז (ה) explains that the custom is to burn the challah rather than give it to any kohen since we are not certain who is definitely a kohen today. The Rama adds that if a kohen does eat the challah, he should first recite a special bracha.[23]","spans":[{"text":"The "},{"text":"ט״ז ","bold":true},{"text":"(ה) explains that the custom is to burn the "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"rather than give it to any "},{"text":"kohen","italic":true},{"text":" since we are not certain who is definitely a "},{"text":"kohen ","italic":true},{"text":"today. The "},{"text":"Rama ","italic":true},{"text":"adds that if a "},{"text":"kohen ","italic":true},{"text":"does eat the "},{"text":"challah, ","italic":true},{"text":"he should first recite a special "},{"text":"bracha.","italic":true},{"text":"[23]","footnote":23}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":36.0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[23],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":92,"xmlIndex":96,"text":"To summarize:","spans":[{"text":"To summarize:"}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"list","paragraph":93,"xmlIndex":97,"text":"In EY one challah is separated and burned. ","spans":[{"text":"In "},{"text":"EY","underline":true},{"text":" one "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"is separated and burned. "}],"marker":"•","level":0,"indent":36.0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0,"hanging":18.0},{"kind":"list","paragraph":94,"xmlIndex":98,"text":"S”A – challah should be the size of 1/48 of the dough.","spans":[{"text":"S”A ","bold":true},{"text":"– "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"should be the size of 1/48 of the dough."}],"marker":"o","level":1,"indent":72.0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0,"hanging":18.0},{"kind":"list","paragraph":95,"xmlIndex":99,"text":"Rama – any amount, although the custom is a kezayis.","spans":[{"text":"Rama","bold":true,"italic":true},{"text":" ","italic":true},{"text":"– any amount, although the custom is a "},{"text":"kezayis.","italic":true}],"marker":"o","level":1,"indent":72.0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0,"hanging":18.0},{"kind":"list","paragraph":96,"xmlIndex":100,"text":"In chu”l:","spans":[{"text":"In "},{"text":"chu”l","italic":true,"underline":true},{"text":":"}],"marker":"•","level":0,"indent":36.0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0,"hanging":18.0},{"kind":"list","paragraph":97,"xmlIndex":101,"text":"S”A – either separate two challos, burn one and give the other to a kohen, even if he is tamei, or separate one challah and give it to a kohen who is not tamei with a tumah that  emanates from his body.","spans":[{"text":"S”A","bold":true},{"text":" – either separate two"},{"text":" challos","italic":true},{"text":", burn one and give the other to a "},{"text":"kohen, ","italic":true},{"text":"even if he is "},{"text":"tamei, ","italic":true},{"text":"or separate one "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"and give it to a "},{"text":"kohen ","italic":true},{"text":"who is not "},{"text":"tamei ","italic":true},{"text":"with a "},{"text":"tumah ","italic":true},{"text":"that  emanates from his body."}],"marker":"o","level":1,"indent":72.0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0,"hanging":18.0},{"kind":"list","paragraph":98,"xmlIndex":102,"text":"Rama – today, both in EY and in chu”l, only one challah of any size is separated, but preferably should be the size of a kezayis, and it is burned (because we are not certain who is a real kohen).","spans":[{"text":"Rama","bold":true,"italic":true},{"text":" ","italic":true},{"text":"–"},{"text":" ","bold":true},{"text":"today, both in EY and in "},{"text":"chu”l, ","italic":true},{"text":"only one "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"of any size is separated, but preferably should be the size of a "},{"text":"kezayis, ","italic":true},{"text":"and it is burned (because we are not certain who is a real "},{"text":"kohen","italic":true},{"text":")."}],"marker":"o","level":1,"indent":72.0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0,"hanging":18.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":99,"xmlIndex":103,"text":"How do we passkin lema’ase? Ashkenazim passkin like the Rama that any amount of challah may be separated, although the custom is to take a kezayis, and it should be burned. Regarding Sephardim, the בית יוסף writes that the minhag is to be mafrish only one challah and burn it, and it seems the בית יוסף is referring to both EY and chu”l (like the Rama). The ברכי יוסף (שכב-ב) (30) writes that although the S”A does not quote it here, in סימן שלא-יט he passkins that since terumah today is burned, it suffices to separate any amount, and the same would apply to challah. Rav Ovadya in הליכות עולם (ח״ג-עמ׳ מט) also passkins that even Sephardim should only separate one challah, which may be any size, and it should be burned. ","spans":[{"text":"How do we "},{"text":"passkin lema’ase","italic":true},{"text":"? "},{"text":"Ashkenazim passkin ","italic":true},{"text":"like the "},{"text":"Rama ","italic":true},{"text":"that any amount of "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"may be separated, although the custom is to take a"},{"text":" kezayis, ","italic":true},{"text":"and it should be burned"},{"text":". ","italic":true},{"text":"Regarding "},{"text":"Sephardim, ","italic":true},{"text":"the "},{"text":"בית יוסף","bold":true},{"text":" writes that the "},{"text":"minhag ","italic":true},{"text":"is to be "},{"text":"mafrish ","italic":true},{"text":"only one challah and burn it, and it seems the בית יוסף is referring to both EY and "},{"text":"chu”l ","italic":true},{"text":"(like the "},{"text":"Rama","italic":true},{"text":")"},{"text":". ","italic":true},{"text":"The "},{"text":"ברכי יוסף ","bold":true},{"text":"(שכב-ב) (30) writes that although the S”A does not quote it here, in "},{"text":"סימן שלא-יט ","bold":true},{"text":"he "},{"text":"passkins ","italic":true},{"text":"that since "},{"text":"terumah ","italic":true},{"text":"today is burned, it suffices to separate any amount, and the same would apply to "},{"text":"challah. ","italic":true},{"text":"Rav Ovadya ","bold":true},{"text":"in הליכות עולם (ח״ג-עמ׳ מט) also "},{"text":"passkins ","italic":true},{"text":"that even "},{"text":"Sephardim ","italic":true},{"text":"should only separate one "},{"text":"challah, ","italic":true},{"text":"which may be any size,"},{"text":" ","italic":true},{"text":"and it should be burned. "}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":36.0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}],"footnotes":[{"id":21,"blocks":[{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":0,"xmlIndex":0,"text":" The ירושלמי (חלה פ״ד-ד) explains the reason for why areas of EY not sanctified by Ezra require two challahs to be separated. The first challah is separated and burned because Chazal decreed a tumah status on chu”l as well as areas of EY not sanctified by Ezra (טומאת ארץ העמים). However, since many are not aware of this tumah status, they might be led to believe that all separated challah needs to be burned. For this reason, we give a second challah to the kohen. But we also cannot give the kohen one challah alone because then those who are aware of the tumah status will think that challah which is tamei may be eaten. For this reason, both challahs are necessary. In regular chu”l, the רמב״ם (ביכורים פ״ה-ח) explains that the first challah is burned because chu”l is tamei, and the second is given to the kohen so we do not forget about challah. ","spans":[{"text":" The ירושלמי (חלה פ״ד-ד) explains the reason for why areas of "},{"text":"EY","italic":true},{"text":" not sanctified by Ezra require two "},{"text":"challahs ","italic":true},{"text":"to be separated. The first "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"is separated and burned because "},{"text":"Chazal ","italic":true},{"text":"decreed a "},{"text":"tumah ","italic":true},{"text":"status on "},{"text":"chu”l ","italic":true},{"text":"as well as areas of "},{"text":"EY","italic":true},{"text":" not sanctified by Ezra (טומאת ארץ העמים). However, since many are not aware of this "},{"text":"tumah ","italic":true},{"text":"status, they might be led to believe that all separated "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"needs to be burned. For this reason, we give a second "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"to the "},{"text":"kohen. ","italic":true},{"text":"But we also cannot give the "},{"text":"kohen ","italic":true},{"text":"one "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"alone because then those who are aware of the "},{"text":"tumah ","italic":true},{"text":"status will think that "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"which is "},{"text":"tamei ","italic":true},{"text":"may be eaten. For this reason, both "},{"text":"challahs ","italic":true},{"text":"are necessary. In regular "},{"text":"chu”l, ","italic":true},{"text":"the רמב״ם (ביכורים פ״ה-ח) explains that the first "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"is burned because "},{"text":"chu”l ","italic":true},{"text":"is "},{"text":"tamei, ","italic":true},{"text":"and the second is given to the "},{"text":"kohen ","italic":true},{"text":"so we do not forget about "},{"text":"challah. ","italic":true}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}]},{"id":22,"blocks":[{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":0,"xmlIndex":0,"text":" The smallest amount size is used since the hafrasha is in chu”l.","spans":[{"text":" The smallest amount size is used since the "},{"text":"hafrasha ","italic":true},{"text":"is in "},{"text":"chu”l.","italic":true}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}]},{"id":23,"blocks":[{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":0,"xmlIndex":0,"text":" The Rama in hilchos Pesach in סימן תנז writes that a kohen child may eat matzah made from the hafrashas challah dough. The ש״ך (שכב-ט) asks that the Rama seems to be contradicting himself. Here he passkins that the custom is to burn challah, yet in Hilchos Pesach he passkins that one may give it to a katan? The ש״ך answers that one must differentiate between Pesach and other times of the year. The מג״א (תנז-ט) explains that on Erev Pesach (and Pesach) the custom is to give the challah to a child kohen since there are a lot of challahs being separated and if they don’t eat from the challah, people might question whether they are kohanim. The ערה״ש (שכב-כא) proves that the Rama agrees to this distinction that there are times when kohanim do eat the challah, since here he writes the nussach of the bracha a kohen would recite when eating the challah. ","spans":[{"text":" The "},{"text":"Rama ","italic":true},{"text":"in "},{"text":"hilchos ","italic":true},{"text":"Pesach in סימן תנז writes that a "},{"text":"kohen ","italic":true},{"text":"child may eat "},{"text":"matzah ","italic":true},{"text":"made from the "},{"text":"hafrashas challah ","italic":true},{"text":"dough"},{"text":". ","italic":true},{"text":"The "},{"text":"ש״ך ","bold":true},{"text":"(שכב-ט) asks that the "},{"text":"Rama ","italic":true},{"text":"seems to be contradicting himself. Here he "},{"text":"passkins ","italic":true},{"text":"that the custom is to burn "},{"text":"challah, ","italic":true},{"text":"yet in "},{"text":"Hilchos ","italic":true},{"text":"Pesach he "},{"text":"passkins","italic":true},{"text":" that one may give it to a "},{"text":"katan?","italic":true},{"text":" The ש״ך answers that one must differentiate between Pesach and other times of the year. The "},{"text":"מג״א ","bold":true},{"text":"(תנז-ט) explains that on "},{"text":"Erev Pesach ","italic":true},{"text":"(and Pesach)"},{"text":" ","italic":true},{"text":"the custom is to give the "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"to a child "},{"text":"kohen ","italic":true},{"text":"since there are a lot of "},{"text":"challahs ","italic":true},{"text":"being separated and if they don’t eat from the "},{"text":"challah","italic":true},{"text":", people might question whether they are "},{"text":"kohanim","italic":true},{"text":". The "},{"text":"ערה״ש","bold":true},{"text":" (שכב-כא) proves that the "},{"text":"Rama ","italic":true},{"text":"agrees to this distinction that there are times when "},{"text":"kohanim ","italic":true},{"text":"do eat the "},{"text":"challah,","italic":true},{"text":" since here he writes the "},{"text":"nussach ","italic":true},{"text":"of the "},{"text":"bracha ","italic":true},{"text":"a "},{"text":"kohen ","italic":true},{"text":"would recite when eating the "},{"text":"challah. ","italic":true}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}]}]},"sort_order":24,"official_shiur_number":2,"estimated_minutes":8,"difficulty":"core","tags":["challah-part-1","original-difficulty:core","source:28","source:29","source:30"],"source_file_name":"Notes for Hafrashas Challah (part 1).docx","source_start_page":null,"source_end_page":null,"is_selectable":true},{"program_id":"winter-5787","chunk_code":"HC1-E1","source_type":"notes","siman":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","workbook_title":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","section_key":"E","section_title":"What To Do with the Separated Challah?","chunk_title":"Burning the Separated Challah — Source and Concern","chunk_summary":"Gemara/Rishonim/S”A/Rama: challah should be burned; Yisrael may not benefit from its fire; separate fire/oven concern.","content_markdown":"### E. *What To Do with the Separated Challah?*\n\nAfter learning that today both *Sephardim* and *Ashkenazim* agree that approximately a *kezayis* of *challah* should be separated and burned, we will now delve into the *halachos* of burning the *challah.* \n\nThe **גמרא שבת** (כה.) (31) teaches that just like there is a *mitzvah* to burn *kodshim* (e.g., *korbanos*) that became *tamei,* so too, there is a *mitzvah* to burn *terumah* (and *challah*) that have become *tamei.* The Gemara adds that when the *terumah/challah* is being burned, the *kohen* is allowed to benefit from the fire, but a *Yisrael* may not*.* **Rashi** explains that *terumah* is burned either because it is similar to *kodshim*, or because leaving it unburned might lead one to accidentally benefit from it. Most *Rishonim*[24] understand that there is an obligation to specifically burn *tamei terumah* and *challah,* rather than merely disposing of them.\n\nThe S”A in **סעיף ד** and the *Rama* in **סעיף ה** (29) *passkin* that the *challah* should be burned in a separate fire because a non-*kohen* may not benefit from its burning. The *Rama* adds that the custom in his time was to burn the *challah* before using the oven, because if food is baked in the oven while the *challah* is being burned, one benefits from the additional flames and heat created by the burning of the *challah.* The **חלקת בנימין** (שכב סקצ״ז) (32) explains that although placing the *challah* in the oven after it is fully heated will also increase the temperature (and thus cause benefit to be derived from the *challah*), that increase is so minimal that it is not considered benefiting from the *challah.* \n\nSource Notes\n\n[24]:  See Tosfos there ד״ה כך and אגרות משה (יו״ד ח״ג-קכט).","content_document":{"version":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"heading","paragraph":100,"xmlIndex":104,"text":"What To Do with the Separated Challah?","spans":[{"text":"What To Do with the Separated Challah?","italic":true,"color":"#2F5496"}],"marker":"E.","level":0,"indent":56.0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0,"rightIndent":43.2,"hanging":18.0,"tabStops":[{"position":150.45,"alignment":"left","leader":"none"},{"position":234.0,"alignment":"center","leader":"none"},{"position":279.0,"alignment":"left","leader":"none"}]},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":101,"xmlIndex":106,"text":"After learning that today both Sephardim and Ashkenazim agree that approximately a kezayis of challah should be separated and burned, we will now delve into the halachos of burning the challah. ","spans":[{"text":"After learning that today both "},{"text":"Sephardim ","italic":true},{"text":"and "},{"text":"Ashkenazim ","italic":true},{"text":"agree that approximately a "},{"text":"kezayis ","italic":true},{"text":"of "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"should be separated and burned, we will now delve into the "},{"text":"halachos ","italic":true},{"text":"of burning the "},{"text":"challah. ","italic":true}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":36.0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":102,"xmlIndex":107,"text":"The גמרא שבת (כה.) (31) teaches that just like there is a mitzvah to burn kodshim (e.g., korbanos) that became tamei, so too, there is a mitzvah to burn terumah (and challah) that have become tamei. The Gemara adds that when the terumah/challah is being burned, the kohen is allowed to benefit from the fire, but a Yisrael may not. Rashi explains that terumah is burned either because it is similar to kodshim, or because leaving it unburned might lead one to accidentally benefit from it. Most Rishonim[24] understand that there is an obligation to specifically burn tamei terumah and challah, rather than merely disposing of them.","spans":[{"text":"The "},{"text":"גמרא שבת ","bold":true},{"text":"(כה.) (31) teaches that just like there is a "},{"text":"mitzvah ","italic":true},{"text":"to burn "},{"text":"kodshim ","italic":true},{"text":"(e.g., "},{"text":"korbanos","italic":true},{"text":")"},{"text":" ","italic":true},{"text":"that became "},{"text":"tamei, ","italic":true},{"text":"so too, there is a "},{"text":"mitzvah ","italic":true},{"text":"to burn "},{"text":"terumah ","italic":true},{"text":"(and "},{"text":"challah","italic":true},{"text":")"},{"text":" ","italic":true},{"text":"that have become "},{"text":"tamei. ","italic":true},{"text":"The Gemara adds that when the "},{"text":"terumah/challah ","italic":true},{"text":"is being burned, the "},{"text":"kohen ","italic":true},{"text":"is allowed to benefit from the fire, but a "},{"text":"Yisrael ","italic":true},{"text":"may not"},{"text":". ","italic":true},{"text":"Rashi ","bold":true},{"text":"explains that "},{"text":"terumah ","italic":true},{"text":"is burned either because it is similar to "},{"text":"kodshim","italic":true},{"text":","},{"text":" ","italic":true},{"text":"or because leaving it unburned might lead one to accidentally benefit from it. Most "},{"text":"Rishonim","italic":true},{"text":"[24]","footnote":24},{"text":" ","italic":true},{"text":"understand that there is an obligation to specifically burn "},{"text":"tamei terumah ","italic":true},{"text":"and "},{"text":"challah, ","italic":true},{"text":"rather than merely disposing of them."}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":36.0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[24],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":103,"xmlIndex":108,"text":"The S”A in סעיף ד and the Rama in סעיף ה (29) passkin that the challah should be burned in a separate fire because a non-kohen may not benefit from its burning. The Rama adds that the custom in his time was to burn the challah before using the oven, because if food is baked in the oven while the challah is being burned, one benefits from the additional flames and heat created by the burning of the challah. The חלקת בנימין (שכב סקצ״ז) (32) explains that although placing the challah in the oven after it is fully heated will also increase the temperature (and thus cause benefit to be derived from the challah), that increase is so minimal that it is not considered benefiting from the challah. ","spans":[{"text":"The S”A in "},{"text":"סעיף ד ","bold":true},{"text":"and the "},{"text":"Rama ","italic":true},{"text":"in "},{"text":"סעיף ה ","bold":true},{"text":"(29) "},{"text":"passkin ","italic":true},{"text":"that the "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"should be burned in a separate fire because a non-"},{"text":"kohen ","italic":true},{"text":"may not benefit from its burning. The "},{"text":"Rama ","italic":true},{"text":"adds that the custom in his time was to burn the "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"before using the oven, because if food is baked in the oven while the "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"is being burned, one benefits from the additional flames and heat created by the burning of the "},{"text":"challah. ","italic":true},{"text":"The "},{"text":"חלקת בנימין ","bold":true},{"text":"(שכב סקצ״ז) (32) explains that although placing the "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"in the oven after it is fully heated will also increase the temperature (and thus cause benefit to be derived from the "},{"text":"challah","italic":true},{"text":"), that increase is so minimal that it is not considered benefiting from the "},{"text":"challah. ","italic":true}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":36.0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}],"footnotes":[{"id":24,"blocks":[{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":0,"xmlIndex":0,"text":" See Tosfos there ד״ה כך and אגרות משה (יו״ד ח״ג-קכט).","spans":[{"text":" See Tosfos there ד״ה כך and אגרות משה (יו״ד ח״ג-קכט)."}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}]}]},"sort_order":25,"official_shiur_number":3,"estimated_minutes":7,"difficulty":"core","tags":["challah-part-1","original-difficulty:core","source:29","source:31","source:32"],"source_file_name":"Notes for Hafrashas Challah (part 1).docx","source_start_page":null,"source_end_page":null,"is_selectable":true},{"program_id":"winter-5787","chunk_code":"HC1-E2","source_type":"notes","siman":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","workbook_title":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","section_key":"E","section_title":"What To Do with the Separated Challah?","chunk_title":"Burning Challah Today in an Oven","chunk_summary":"In modern ovens, burning may not add meaningful heat, but should be double-wrapped to avoid reicha/zeiah concerns.","content_markdown":"**Practical Applications**\n\n<u>Burning</u> *<u>challah</u>* <u>today</u>\n\n\tWhat is the best way to burn *challah* today? Are you required to burn the *challah* before baking the bread? The **חלקת בנימין** (שכב סקצ״ט) (32) *passkins* that since our gas ovens are already fully heated when baking, the burning *challah* inside does not increase the temperature in the oven. Therefore, baking other food while the *challah* is being burned is not considered benefiting from the burning of the *challah.* Nevertheless, since the *challah* is forbidden to be eaten (and the prohibition is more severe than eating pork[25]), you should make sure to double wrap the *challah* in aluminum foil to avoid any issues of ריחא or זיעא that could forbid the other food in the oven or the oven itself. \n\nSource Notes\n\n[25]:  A Yisrael who eats *terumah* is חייב מיתה, but eating pork is punishable by *malkos,* though here the prohibition is only rabbinic because *challah* today is rabbinic.  ","content_document":{"version":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":104,"xmlIndex":109,"text":"Practical Applications","spans":[{"text":"Practical Applications","bold":true}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":105,"xmlIndex":110,"text":"Burning challah today","spans":[{"text":"Burning ","underline":true},{"text":"challah ","italic":true,"underline":true},{"text":"today","underline":true}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":106,"xmlIndex":111,"text":"\tWhat is the best way to burn challah today? Are you required to burn the challah before baking the bread? The חלקת בנימין (שכב סקצ״ט) (32) passkins that since our gas ovens are already fully heated when baking, the burning challah inside does not increase the temperature in the oven. Therefore, baking other food while the challah is being burned is not considered benefiting from the burning of the challah. Nevertheless, since the challah is forbidden to be eaten (and the prohibition is more severe than eating pork[25]), you should make sure to double wrap the challah in aluminum foil to avoid any issues of ריחא or זיעא that could forbid the other food in the oven or the oven itself. ","spans":[{"text":"\tWhat is the best way to burn "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"today? Are you required to burn the "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"before baking the bread? The "},{"text":"חלקת בנימין ","bold":true},{"text":"(שכב סקצ״ט) (32) "},{"text":"passkins ","italic":true},{"text":"that since our gas ovens are already fully heated when baking, the burning "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"inside"},{"text":" ","italic":true},{"text":"does not increase the temperature in the oven. Therefore, baking other food while the "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"is being burned is not considered benefiting from the burning of the "},{"text":"challah.","italic":true},{"text":" Nevertheless, since the "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"is forbidden to be eaten (and the prohibition is more severe than eating pork"},{"text":"[25]","footnote":25},{"text":"), you should make sure to double wrap the "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"in aluminum foil to avoid any issues of ריחא or זיעא that could forbid the other food in the oven or the oven itself. "}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":1,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[25],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}],"footnotes":[{"id":25,"blocks":[{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":0,"xmlIndex":0,"text":" A Yisrael who eats terumah is חייב מיתה, but eating pork is punishable by malkos, though here the prohibition is only rabbinic because challah today is rabbinic.  ","spans":[{"text":" A Yisrael who eats "},{"text":"terumah ","italic":true},{"text":"is חייב מיתה, but eating pork is punishable by "},{"text":"malkos, ","italic":true},{"text":"though here the prohibition is only rabbinic because "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"today is rabbinic. "},{"text":" ","italic":true}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}]}]},"sort_order":26,"official_shiur_number":3,"estimated_minutes":5,"difficulty":"practical","tags":["challah-part-1","original-difficulty:practical","source:32"],"source_file_name":"Notes for Hafrashas Challah (part 1).docx","source_start_page":null,"source_end_page":null,"is_selectable":true},{"program_id":"winter-5787","chunk_code":"HC1-E3","source_type":"notes","siman":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","workbook_title":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","section_key":"E","section_title":"What To Do with the Separated Challah?","chunk_title":"Ripped Tinfoil in Oven","chunk_summary":"Reviews reicha/zeiah; Rav Moshe/Chayei Halevi permit other food if challah exposed, but rack needs libun; some argue even bread emits steam.","content_markdown":"<u>Ripped tinfoil</u>\n\n\tWhat is the *halacha* if you are burning *challah* in an oven with other food and forgot to cover the *challah* or if the aluminum foil ripped as the *challah* expanded during baking? Is the other food permitted? To answer this question, we will briefly review what we learned in the *halachos* of *basar b’chalav.* Whenever two foods are cooked in an oven, where one food is forbidden and the other is permitted, or one is meat and the other dairy, there are two issues that need to be considered: 1) ריחא – the transfer of smell/aroma from one food to the other, and 2) זיעה – the transfer of steam. \n\nThe S”A in **קח-א** *passkins* that *be’dieved,* we do not take ריחא into consideration. Therefore, if you cooked dry meat with dry cheese in the same oven simultaneously (such that there is no issue of זיעה), both are permitted, even though the smell/aroma mix. However, if the meat or cheese were moist (and many *posskim* assume that most foods are moist), there is an issue of זיעה, because the steam from one food enters the other. Many *posskim* hold that even using the same oven for dairy and then meat (or vice versa) is forbidden because the steam from the dairy food is absorbed into the walls of the oven and is later released when you cook meat. \n\n**Rav Moshe** in אגרו״מ (יו״ד ח״א-מ) writes that there are certain foods that do not emit any steam, such as *challah* and other non-liquid dishes. Based on this, the **חיי הלוי** (ח״ג יו״ד ע״ט-ב) (33) *passkins* that if one accidentally baked *challah* without wrapping it in foil, or if the foil tore during baking, the other foods in the oven are permitted. However, if the oven rack comes into direct contact with the exposed *challah,* it needs to be *kashered* with *libun.* It should be noted that many *acharonim,* such as the **הפרשת חלה כהלכתה** (עמ׳ קה), argue on Rav Moshe and *passkin* that even bread emits steam and all the food in the oven, as well as the oven itself, would be rendered not-kosher, if the foil rips. ","content_document":{"version":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":107,"xmlIndex":112,"text":"Ripped tinfoil","spans":[{"text":"Ripped tinfoil","underline":true}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":108,"xmlIndex":113,"text":"\tWhat is the halacha if you are burning challah in an oven with other food and forgot to cover the challah or if the aluminum foil ripped as the challah expanded during baking? Is the other food permitted? To answer this question, we will briefly review what we learned in the halachos of basar b’chalav. Whenever two foods are cooked in an oven, where one food is forbidden and the other is permitted, or one is meat and the other dairy, there are two issues that need to be considered: 1) ריחא – the transfer of smell/aroma from one food to the other, and 2) זיעה – the transfer of steam. ","spans":[{"text":"\tWhat is the "},{"text":"halacha ","italic":true},{"text":"if you are burning "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"in an oven with other food and forgot to cover the "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"or if the aluminum foil ripped as the "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"expanded during baking? Is the other food permitted? To answer this question, we will briefly review what we learned in the "},{"text":"halachos ","italic":true},{"text":"of "},{"text":"basar b’chalav. ","italic":true},{"text":"Whenever two foods are cooked in an oven, where one food is forbidden and the other is permitted, or one is meat and the other dairy, there are two issues that need to be considered: 1) ריחא – the transfer of smell/aroma from one food to the other, and 2) זיעה – the transfer of steam. "}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":1,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":109,"xmlIndex":114,"text":"The S”A in קח-א passkins that be’dieved, we do not take ריחא into consideration. Therefore, if you cooked dry meat with dry cheese in the same oven simultaneously (such that there is no issue of זיעה), both are permitted, even though the smell/aroma mix. However, if the meat or cheese were moist (and many posskim assume that most foods are moist), there is an issue of זיעה, because the steam from one food enters the other. Many posskim hold that even using the same oven for dairy and then meat (or vice versa) is forbidden because the steam from the dairy food is absorbed into the walls of the oven and is later released when you cook meat. ","spans":[{"text":"The S”A in "},{"text":"קח-א","bold":true},{"text":" "},{"text":"passkins ","italic":true},{"text":"that "},{"text":"be’dieved, ","italic":true},{"text":"we do not take ריחא into consideration. Therefore, if you cooked dry meat with dry cheese in the same oven simultaneously (such that there is no issue of זיעה), both are permitted, even though the smell/aroma mix. However, if the meat or cheese were moist (and many "},{"text":"posskim ","italic":true},{"text":"assume that most foods are moist), there is an issue of זיעה, because the steam from one food enters the other. Many "},{"text":"posskim ","italic":true},{"text":"hold that even using the same oven for dairy and then meat (or vice versa) is forbidden because the steam from the dairy food is absorbed into the walls of the oven and is later released when you cook meat. "}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":36.0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":110,"xmlIndex":115,"text":"Rav Moshe in אגרו״מ (יו״ד ח״א-מ) writes that there are certain foods that do not emit any steam, such as challah and other non-liquid dishes. Based on this, the חיי הלוי (ח״ג יו״ד ע״ט-ב) (33) passkins that if one accidentally baked challah without wrapping it in foil, or if the foil tore during baking, the other foods in the oven are permitted. However, if the oven rack comes into direct contact with the exposed challah, it needs to be kashered with libun. It should be noted that many acharonim, such as the הפרשת חלה כהלכתה (עמ׳ קה), argue on Rav Moshe and passkin that even bread emits steam and all the food in the oven, as well as the oven itself, would be rendered not-kosher, if the foil rips. ","spans":[{"text":"Rav Moshe ","bold":true},{"text":"in אגרו״מ (יו״ד ח״א-מ) writes that there are certain foods that do not emit any steam, such as "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"and other non-liquid dishes. Based on this, the "},{"text":"חיי הלוי ","bold":true},{"text":"(ח״ג יו״ד ע״ט-ב) (33) "},{"text":"passkins ","italic":true},{"text":"that if one accidentally baked "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"without wrapping it in foil, or if the foil tore during baking, the other foods in the oven are permitted. However, if the oven rack comes into direct contact with the exposed "},{"text":"challah, ","italic":true},{"text":"it needs to be "},{"text":"kashered ","italic":true},{"text":"with"},{"text":" libun. ","italic":true},{"text":"It should be noted that many "},{"text":"acharonim, ","italic":true},{"text":"such as the "},{"text":"הפרשת חלה כהלכתה ","bold":true},{"text":"(עמ׳ קה),"},{"text":" ","italic":true},{"text":"argue on Rav Moshe and "},{"text":"passkin ","italic":true},{"text":"that even bread emits steam and all the food in the oven, as well as the oven itself, would be rendered not-kosher, if the foil rips. "}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":36.0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}],"footnotes":[]},"sort_order":27,"official_shiur_number":3,"estimated_minutes":8,"difficulty":"practical","tags":["challah-part-1","original-difficulty:practical","source:33"],"source_file_name":"Notes for Hafrashas Challah (part 1).docx","source_start_page":null,"source_end_page":null,"is_selectable":true},{"program_id":"winter-5787","chunk_code":"HC1-E4","source_type":"notes","siman":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","workbook_title":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","section_key":"E","section_title":"What To Do with the Separated Challah?","chunk_title":"Burning Challah in Biur Chametz","chunk_summary":"Whether others benefit from challah flame in biur chametz; permitted because fire is already sufficient and mitzvos lav lehenos nitnu.","content_markdown":"<u>Burning</u> *<u>chametz</u>*\n\n\tAs mentioned earlier, many people buy *matzah* from which *challah* was not separated and are *mafrish* at home. They then take the *matzah* designated as *challah* and throw it into the fire used for burning *chametz* on *Erev Pesach.* The **חלקת בנימין** (שכב, ביאורים ד״ה ונהגין) (34) asks whether this practice should be prohibited because all those burning their *chametz* are benefiting from the extra flames produced by the burning *challah*.\n\nHe suggests, as mentioned above (32), that if the fire is already very hot, adding a little bit of *matzah* does not constitute a significant increase in the fire, and is therefore not defined as deriving benefit from the fire. Additionally, even if the *matzah* does increase the level of heat significantly, perhaps it is still permitted because of the principle ״מצות לאו להינות נתנו״ – *mitzvos* are not given for (personal) benefit, and the burning of your *chametz* by the heat of the *challah* is not considered הנאה.  ","content_document":{"version":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":111,"xmlIndex":116,"text":"Burning chametz","spans":[{"text":"Burning ","underline":true},{"text":"chametz","italic":true,"underline":true}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":112,"xmlIndex":117,"text":"\tAs mentioned earlier, many people buy matzah from which challah was not separated and are mafrish at home. They then take the matzah designated as challah and throw it into the fire used for burning chametz on Erev Pesach. The חלקת בנימין (שכב, ביאורים ד״ה ונהגין) (34) asks whether this practice should be prohibited because all those burning their chametz are benefiting from the extra flames produced by the burning challah.","spans":[{"text":"\t","italic":true},{"text":"As mentioned earlier, many people buy "},{"text":"matzah ","italic":true},{"text":"from which "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"was not separated and are "},{"text":"mafrish","italic":true},{"text":" at home. They then take the "},{"text":"matzah ","italic":true},{"text":"designated as "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"and throw it into the fire used for burning "},{"text":"chametz ","italic":true},{"text":"on "},{"text":"Erev Pesach. ","italic":true},{"text":"The "},{"text":"חלקת בנימין","bold":true},{"text":" (שכב, ביאורים ד״ה ונהגין) (34) asks whether this practice should be prohibited because all those burning their "},{"text":"chametz ","italic":true},{"text":"are benefiting from the extra flames produced by the burning "},{"text":"challah","italic":true},{"text":"."}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":1,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":113,"xmlIndex":118,"text":"He suggests, as mentioned above (32), that if the fire is already very hot, adding a little bit of matzah does not constitute a significant increase in the fire, and is therefore not defined as deriving benefit from the fire. Additionally, even if the matzah does increase the level of heat significantly, perhaps it is still permitted because of the principle ״מצות לאו להינות נתנו״ – mitzvos are not given for (personal) benefit, and the burning of your chametz by the heat of the challah is not considered הנאה.  ","spans":[{"text":"He suggests, as mentioned above (32), that if the fire is already very hot, adding a little bit of "},{"text":"matzah ","italic":true},{"text":"does not constitute a significant increase in the fire, and is therefore not defined as deriving benefit from the fire. Additionally, even if the "},{"text":"matzah ","italic":true},{"text":"does increase the level of heat significantly, perhaps it is still permitted because of the principle ״מצות לאו להינות נתנו״ – "},{"text":"mitzvos ","italic":true},{"text":"are not given for (personal) benefit, and the burning of your "},{"text":"chametz ","italic":true},{"text":"by the heat of the "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"is not considered הנאה.  "}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":36.0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}],"footnotes":[]},"sort_order":28,"official_shiur_number":3,"estimated_minutes":5,"difficulty":"practical","tags":["challah-part-1","original-difficulty:practical","source:32","source:34"],"source_file_name":"Notes for Hafrashas Challah (part 1).docx","source_start_page":null,"source_end_page":null,"is_selectable":true},{"program_id":"winter-5787","chunk_code":"HC1-E5","source_type":"notes","siman":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","workbook_title":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","section_key":"E","section_title":"What To Do with the Separated Challah?","chunk_title":"Throwing Out Challah When Burning Is Not Possible","chunk_summary":"Ideally burn; Rav Ovadya and others allow less than kezayis wrapped/disposed when no way to burn.","content_markdown":"<u>Disposing of</u> *<u>Challah</u>*\n\n\tBased on the גמרא שבת (31) and the *pssak* of the S”A and *Rama,* it seems clear that separated *challah* should be burned*.* Some, however, wrap the *challah* and throw it out, basing their practice on Rashi in the above Gemara and the **חזון איש** (סוף קונטרס תרו\"מ ודמאי טו-א), who writes that one can dispose of *teruma* in this manner*,* and the same would presumably apply to *challah.* However, the **מנחת יצחק** (ח״ד-יג) argues that the חזון איש was lenient only with regard to *terumah,* but not with *challah* (see there for his reasoning). Thus, it would seem that the *challah* should always be burned. \n\nNevertheless, Rav Ovadya, in **חזון עובדיה** (תרומה ומעשרות עמ׳ רכז) (35), *passkins* that since the *challah* separated can be of any size (even less than a *kezayis*), if there is no way to burn it*,* one should separate less than a *kezayis,* place it in a plastic bag, and dispose of it. This is based on the **מהר״י אסאד**  (יו״ד-שנב), who *passkins* that the obligation to burn *challah* applies only to an amount the size of a *kezayis,* but anything smaller than a *kezayis* does not have to be burned. Therefore, if you do not have a way to burn it, or it is *erev-*Shabbos and the oven/stovetops are being used, you should be *mafrish* less than a *kezayis. Lema’ase,* the מנחת יצחק (above) and **שש״כ** (פמ״ב הע׳ נג) write that if there is no way to burn the *challah,* it may be thrown out. ","content_document":{"version":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":114,"xmlIndex":119,"text":"Disposing of Challah","spans":[{"text":"Disposing of ","underline":true},{"text":"Challah","italic":true,"underline":true}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":115,"xmlIndex":120,"text":"\tBased on the גמרא שבת (31) and the pssak of the S”A and Rama, it seems clear that separated challah should be burned. Some, however, wrap the challah and throw it out, basing their practice on Rashi in the above Gemara and the חזון איש (סוף קונטרס תרו\"מ ודמאי טו-א), who writes that one can dispose of teruma in this manner, and the same would presumably apply to challah. However, the מנחת יצחק (ח״ד-יג) argues that the חזון איש was lenient only with regard to terumah, but not with challah (see there for his reasoning). Thus, it would seem that the challah should always be burned. ","spans":[{"text":"\t","italic":true},{"text":"Based on the גמרא שבת (31) and the "},{"text":"pssak ","italic":true},{"text":"of the S”A and "},{"text":"Rama, ","italic":true},{"text":"it seems clear that separated "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"should be burned"},{"text":".","italic":true},{"text":" Some, however, wrap the "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"and throw it out, basing their practice on Rashi in the above Gemara and the "},{"text":"חזון איש","bold":true},{"text":" (סוף קונטרס תרו\"מ ודמאי טו-א), who writes that one can dispose of "},{"text":"teruma ","italic":true},{"text":"in this manner"},{"text":", ","italic":true},{"text":"and the same would presumably apply to "},{"text":"challah. ","italic":true},{"text":"However, the "},{"text":"מנחת יצחק ","bold":true},{"text":"(ח״ד-יג) argues that the חזון איש was lenient only with regard to "},{"text":"terumah, ","italic":true},{"text":"but not with "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"(see there for his reasoning). Thus, it would seem that the "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"should always be burned. "}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":1,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":116,"xmlIndex":121,"text":"Nevertheless, Rav Ovadya, in חזון עובדיה (תרומה ומעשרות עמ׳ רכז) (35), passkins that since the challah separated can be of any size (even less than a kezayis), if there is no way to burn it, one should separate less than a kezayis, place it in a plastic bag, and dispose of it. This is based on the מהר״י אסאד  (יו״ד-שנב), who passkins that the obligation to burn challah applies only to an amount the size of a kezayis, but anything smaller than a kezayis does not have to be burned. Therefore, if you do not have a way to burn it, or it is erev-Shabbos and the oven/stovetops are being used, you should be mafrish less than a kezayis. Lema’ase, the מנחת יצחק (above) and שש״כ (פמ״ב הע׳ נג) write that if there is no way to burn the challah, it may be thrown out. ","spans":[{"text":"Nevertheless, Rav Ovadya, in "},{"text":"חזון עובדיה ","bold":true},{"text":"(תרומה ומעשרות עמ׳ רכז) (35), "},{"text":"passkins ","italic":true},{"text":"that since the "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"separated can be of any size (even less than a "},{"text":"kezayis","italic":true},{"text":"), if there is no way to burn it"},{"text":", ","italic":true},{"text":"one should separate less than a "},{"text":"kezayis, ","italic":true},{"text":"place it in a plastic bag, and dispose of it. This is based on the "},{"text":"מהר״י אסאד  ","bold":true},{"text":"(יו״ד-שנב), who "},{"text":"passkins ","italic":true},{"text":"that the obligation to burn "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"applies only to an amount the size of a "},{"text":"kezayis, ","italic":true},{"text":"but anything smaller than a "},{"text":"kezayis","italic":true},{"text":" does not have to be burned. Therefore, if you do not have a way to burn it, or it is "},{"text":"erev-","italic":true},{"text":"Shabbos and the oven/stovetops are being used, you should be "},{"text":"mafrish","italic":true},{"text":" less than a "},{"text":"kezayis. Lema’ase, ","italic":true},{"text":"the מנחת יצחק (above) and "},{"text":"שש״כ","bold":true},{"text":" (פמ״ב הע׳ נג) write that if there is no way to burn the "},{"text":"challah, ","italic":true},{"text":"it may be thrown out. "}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":36.0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}],"footnotes":[]},"sort_order":29,"official_shiur_number":3,"estimated_minutes":6,"difficulty":"practical","tags":["challah-part-1","original-difficulty:practical","source:31","source:35"],"source_file_name":"Notes for Hafrashas Challah (part 1).docx","source_start_page":null,"source_end_page":null,"is_selectable":true},{"program_id":"winter-5787","chunk_code":"HC1-E6","source_type":"notes","siman":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","workbook_title":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","section_key":"E","section_title":"What To Do with the Separated Challah?","chunk_title":"Must Challah Be Burned Immediately?","chunk_summary":"Some require immediate burning; others allow saving/freezing in a marked bag; best to burn soon.","content_markdown":"<u>Disposing of</u> *<u>challah</u>* <u>right away</u>\n\n\tAre you obligated to burn the *challah* immediately, or may you hold on to it and burn it at a later time? The **חלה כהלכתה** (סימן א-יב שיח הארץ) (36) *passkins* that the *challah* must be burned immediately in order to avoid the possibility of accidentally benefiting from it. He explains that although the *Rama,* ש״ך and ט״ז permit leaving *terumah* in one’s possession before burning it, *challah* is much more common, and one must burn it immediately.\n\n\tHowever, it seems from the **גר״א** (שלא-סקמ״ב) that there is no issue of leaving *challah* around.[26] The **חלה כהלכתה** (סימן א סק״מ) (37) explains that some have the custom to freeze their *challah* and burn it during *biur chametz,* but in order to avoid accidentally benefiting from it*,* it should be placed in a special bag. Additionally, when burning the *challah* on *erev* Pesach, one should be careful to take the *challah* out of the freezer in advance because if thrown into the fire while frozen, it will likely get burned only on the outside. \n\n*Lema’ase,* it would seem best to burn the *challah* immediately after separating it.\n\nSource Notes\n\n[26]:  The גר״א writes that since ״אין שום תרומה נאכלת״, all types of *terumah* (including *challah*) can be left around.","content_document":{"version":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":117,"xmlIndex":122,"text":"Disposing of challah right away","spans":[{"text":"Disposing of ","underline":true},{"text":"challah","italic":true,"underline":true},{"text":" right away","underline":true}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":118,"xmlIndex":123,"text":"\tAre you obligated to burn the challah immediately, or may you hold on to it and burn it at a later time? The חלה כהלכתה (סימן א-יב שיח הארץ) (36) passkins that the challah must be burned immediately in order to avoid the possibility of accidentally benefiting from it. He explains that although the Rama, ש״ך and ט״ז permit leaving terumah in one’s possession before burning it, challah is much more common, and one must burn it immediately.","spans":[{"text":"\tAre you obligated to burn the "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"immediately, or may you hold on to it and burn it at a later time? The "},{"text":"חלה כהלכתה ","bold":true},{"text":"(סימן א-יב שיח הארץ) (36) "},{"text":"passkins ","italic":true},{"text":"that the "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"must be burned immediately in order to avoid the possibility of accidentally benefiting from it. He explains that although the "},{"text":"Rama, ","italic":true},{"text":"ש״ך and ט״ז permit leaving "},{"text":"terumah","italic":true},{"text":" in one’s possession before burning it, "},{"text":"challah","italic":true},{"text":" is much more common, and one must burn it immediately."}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":1,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":119,"xmlIndex":124,"text":"\tHowever, it seems from the גר״א (שלא-סקמ״ב) that there is no issue of leaving challah around.[26] The חלה כהלכתה (סימן א סק״מ) (37) explains that some have the custom to freeze their challah and burn it during biur chametz, but in order to avoid accidentally benefiting from it, it should be placed in a special bag. Additionally, when burning the challah on erev Pesach, one should be careful to take the challah out of the freezer in advance because if thrown into the fire while frozen, it will likely get burned only on the outside. ","spans":[{"text":"\tHowever, it seems from the "},{"text":"גר״א ","bold":true},{"text":"(שלא-סקמ״ב) that there is no issue of leaving "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"around."},{"text":"[26]","footnote":26},{"text":" The "},{"text":"חלה כהלכתה ","bold":true},{"text":"(סימן א סק״מ) (37) explains that some have the custom to freeze their "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"and burn it during "},{"text":"biur chametz, ","italic":true},{"text":"but in order to avoid accidentally benefiting from it"},{"text":", ","italic":true},{"text":"it should be placed in a special bag. Additionally, when burning the "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"on "},{"text":"erev ","italic":true},{"text":"Pesach, one should be careful to take the "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"out of the freezer"},{"text":" ","italic":true},{"text":"in advance because if thrown into the fire while frozen, it will likely get burned only on the outside. "}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":1,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[26],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":120,"xmlIndex":125,"text":"Lema’ase, it would seem best to burn the challah immediately after separating it.","spans":[{"text":"Lema’ase, ","italic":true},{"text":"it would seem best to burn the "},{"text":"challah","italic":true},{"text":" immediately after separating it."}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":36.0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}],"footnotes":[{"id":26,"blocks":[{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":0,"xmlIndex":0,"text":" The גר״א writes that since ״אין שום תרומה נאכלת״, all types of terumah (including challah) can be left around.","spans":[{"text":" The גר״א writes that since ״אין שום תרומה נאכלת״, all types of "},{"text":"terumah ","italic":true},{"text":"(including "},{"text":"challah","italic":true},{"text":") can be left around."}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}]}]},"sort_order":30,"official_shiur_number":3,"estimated_minutes":4,"difficulty":"practical","tags":["challah-part-1","original-difficulty:practical","source:36","source:37"],"source_file_name":"Notes for Hafrashas Challah (part 1).docx","source_start_page":null,"source_end_page":null,"is_selectable":true},{"program_id":"winter-5787","chunk_code":"HC1-E7","source_type":"notes","siman":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","workbook_title":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","section_key":"E","section_title":"What To Do with the Separated Challah?","chunk_title":"Fork Used in Burning Challah","chunk_summary":"Fork inserted into unburned challah becomes treif and likely needs libun; use tongs on wrapped challah.","content_markdown":"<u>Fork</u>\n\n\tThe **אמרי יעקב** (ארץ ישראל סימן ב, ילקוטים סקמ״ג) (38) writes that when burning *challah* in the oven, you should make sure not to stick a fork into it to determine if it is fully burned because if it is not, the fork would absorb forbidden flavor from the *challah* and become *treif*. He writes that if the fork does become *treif* from inserting into the *challah,* since it absorbed through dry heat inside an oven, *hagallah* would not suffice, and one would need to perform *libun* on the fork. The best way to remove the wrapped *challah* is with a set of tongs. Since the *challah* is double wrapped, you may even use your regular tongs, as flavor does not travel through the two layers of foil (as we learned in a previous SCP *zman* and will be discussed below regarding two pots touching one another).","content_document":{"version":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":121,"xmlIndex":126,"text":"Fork","spans":[{"text":"Fork","underline":true}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":122,"xmlIndex":127,"text":"\tThe אמרי יעקב (ארץ ישראל סימן ב, ילקוטים סקמ״ג) (38) writes that when burning challah in the oven, you should make sure not to stick a fork into it to determine if it is fully burned because if it is not, the fork would absorb forbidden flavor from the challah and become treif. He writes that if the fork does become treif from inserting into the challah, since it absorbed through dry heat inside an oven, hagallah would not suffice, and one would need to perform libun on the fork. The best way to remove the wrapped challah is with a set of tongs. Since the challah is double wrapped, you may even use your regular tongs, as flavor does not travel through the two layers of foil (as we learned in a previous SCP zman and will be discussed below regarding two pots touching one another).","spans":[{"text":"\tThe "},{"text":"אמרי יעקב ","bold":true},{"text":"(ארץ ישראל סימן ב, ילקוטים סקמ״ג) (38) writes that when burning "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"in the oven, you should make sure not to stick a fork into it to determine if it is fully burned because if it is not, the fork would absorb forbidden flavor from the "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"and become "},{"text":"treif","italic":true},{"text":". He writes that if the fork does become "},{"text":"treif ","italic":true},{"text":"from inserting into the "},{"text":"challah, ","italic":true},{"text":"since it absorbed through dry heat inside an oven, "},{"text":"hagallah ","italic":true},{"text":"would not suffice, and one would need to perform "},{"text":"libun ","italic":true},{"text":"on the fork. The best way to remove the wrapped "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"is with a set of tongs. Since the "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"is double wrapped, you may even use your regular tongs, as flavor does not travel through the two layers of foil (as we learned in a previous SCP "},{"text":"zman ","italic":true},{"text":"and will be discussed below regarding two pots touching one another)."}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":1,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}],"footnotes":[]},"sort_order":31,"official_shiur_number":3,"estimated_minutes":4,"difficulty":"practical","tags":["challah-part-1","original-difficulty:practical","source:38"],"source_file_name":"Notes for Hafrashas Challah (part 1).docx","source_start_page":null,"source_end_page":null,"is_selectable":true},{"program_id":"winter-5787","chunk_code":"HC1-E8","source_type":"notes","siman":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","workbook_title":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","section_key":"E","section_title":"What To Do with the Separated Challah?","chunk_title":"Burning Challah on Stovetop","chunk_summary":"Better to place challah on foil; issue whether grates absorb and need libun; Rav Moshe likely lenient when wrapped.","content_markdown":"<u>Burning on the stove top</u>\n\n\tSome have the custom to burn the *challah* on the stovetop. Although, in theory, one may burn the *challah* directly on the grates, it is best to place the *challah* on a piece of aluminum foil for two reasons: 1) It prevents the grates from getting dirty, and 2) the קובץ מבית לוי *passkins* that the grates  become non-kosher. Let’s explain.\n\nThe **קובץ מבית לוי** (ח״א עמ׳ כט) (39) quotes the משנ״ב in *Hilchos Pesach,* who *passkins* that for Pesach, grates do not require *libun* for two reasons: 1) Even if the grates have absorbed *chametz,* the *chametz* flavor cannot transfer to the pot sitting on the grates. The reason is that it is similar to two pots touching one another (on the outside), where the flavor does not travel from one to the other unless there is moisture in between them (or in between the grates and the pot). 2) Any *chametz* flavor absorbed in the grates is immediately burned out by the flames below. The קובץ מבית לוי explains that the second reason of the משנ״ב only applied in the time of the משנ״ב where the grates were directly on the fire, but stovetop grates today are elevated above the fire. Consequently, even if the flames come in contact with the grates, that is only a small part of the grates. Therefore, the קובץ מבית לוי rules that on *Erev* Pesach one should be sure to perform *libun* on his grates. Similarly, regarding *challah,* one should be careful not to burn the *challah* directly on the grates.[27] Rav Elyashiv in אשרי האיש (יו״ד ח״ב עמ׳ קסה) also *passkins* that one should not burn his *challah* on the grates. \n\n**Rav Moshe** in אגרו״מ יו״ד (ח״א-קכד) *passkins* that you do not need separate grates for meat and dairy. Presumably, he would hold that if you wrap the *challah* with foil, you can burn it directly on the grates, just like any other meat and dairy pot that is placed directly on the grates.\n\nSource Notes\n\n[27]:  Based on this, he is also *machmir* to have separate grates for meat and dairy.","content_document":{"version":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":123,"xmlIndex":128,"text":"Burning on the stove top","spans":[{"text":"Burning on the stove top","underline":true}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":124,"xmlIndex":129,"text":"\tSome have the custom to burn the challah on the stovetop. Although, in theory, one may burn the challah directly on the grates, it is best to place the challah on a piece of aluminum foil for two reasons: 1) It prevents the grates from getting dirty, and 2) the קובץ מבית לוי passkins that the grates  become non-kosher. Let’s explain.","spans":[{"text":"\tSome have the custom to burn the "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"on the stovetop. Although, in theory, one may burn the "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"directly on the grates, it is best to place the "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"on a piece of aluminum foil for two reasons: 1) It prevents the grates from getting dirty, and 2) the קובץ מבית לוי "},{"text":"passkins ","italic":true},{"text":"that the grates  become non-kosher. Let’s explain."}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":1,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":125,"xmlIndex":130,"text":"The קובץ מבית לוי (ח״א עמ׳ כט) (39) quotes the משנ״ב in Hilchos Pesach, who passkins that for Pesach, grates do not require libun for two reasons: 1) Even if the grates have absorbed chametz, the chametz flavor cannot transfer to the pot sitting on the grates. The reason is that it is similar to two pots touching one another (on the outside), where the flavor does not travel from one to the other unless there is moisture in between them (or in between the grates and the pot). 2) Any chametz flavor absorbed in the grates is immediately burned out by the flames below. The קובץ מבית לוי explains that the second reason of the משנ״ב only applied in the time of the משנ״ב where the grates were directly on the fire, but stovetop grates today are elevated above the fire. Consequently, even if the flames come in contact with the grates, that is only a small part of the grates. Therefore, the קובץ מבית לוי rules that on Erev Pesach one should be sure to perform libun on his grates. Similarly, regarding challah, one should be careful not to burn the challah directly on the grates.[27] Rav Elyashiv in אשרי האיש (יו״ד ח״ב עמ׳ קסה) also passkins that one should not burn his challah on the grates. ","spans":[{"text":"The "},{"text":"קובץ מבית לוי","bold":true},{"text":" (ח״א עמ׳ כט) (39) quotes the משנ״ב in "},{"text":"Hilchos Pesach, ","italic":true},{"text":"who "},{"text":"passkins ","italic":true},{"text":"that for Pesach, grates do not require "},{"text":"libun ","italic":true},{"text":"for two reasons: 1) Even if the grates have absorbed "},{"text":"chametz, ","italic":true},{"text":"the "},{"text":"chametz ","italic":true},{"text":"flavor cannot transfer to the pot sitting on the grates. The reason is that it is similar to two pots touching one another (on the outside), where the flavor does not travel from one to the other unless there is moisture in between them (or in between the grates and the pot). 2) Any "},{"text":"chametz ","italic":true},{"text":"flavor absorbed in the grates is immediately burned out by the flames below. The קובץ מבית לוי explains that the second reason of the משנ״ב only applied in the time of the משנ״ב where the grates were directly on the fire, but stovetop grates today are elevated above the fire. Consequently, even if the flames come in contact with the grates, that is only a small part of the grates. Therefore, the קובץ מבית לוי rules that on "},{"text":"Erev ","italic":true},{"text":"Pesach one should be sure to perform "},{"text":"libun ","italic":true},{"text":"on his grates. Similarly, regarding "},{"text":"challah, ","italic":true},{"text":"one should be careful not to burn the "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"directly on the grates."},{"text":"[27]","footnote":27},{"text":" Rav Elyashiv in אשרי האיש (יו״ד ח״ב עמ׳ קסה) also "},{"text":"passkins ","italic":true},{"text":"that one should not burn his "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"on the grates. "}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":36.0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[27],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":126,"xmlIndex":131,"text":"Rav Moshe in אגרו״מ יו״ד (ח״א-קכד) passkins that you do not need separate grates for meat and dairy. Presumably, he would hold that if you wrap the challah with foil, you can burn it directly on the grates, just like any other meat and dairy pot that is placed directly on the grates.","spans":[{"text":"Rav Moshe","bold":true},{"text":" in אגרו״מ יו״ד (ח״א-קכד) "},{"text":"passkins ","italic":true},{"text":"that you do not need separate grates for meat and dairy. Presumably, he would hold that if you wrap the "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"with foil, you can burn it directly on the grates, just like any other meat and dairy pot that is placed directly on the grates."}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":36.0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}],"footnotes":[{"id":27,"blocks":[{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":0,"xmlIndex":0,"text":" Based on this, he is also machmir to have separate grates for meat and dairy.","spans":[{"text":" Based on this, he is also "},{"text":"machmir ","italic":true},{"text":"to have separate grates for meat and dairy."}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}]}]},"sort_order":32,"official_shiur_number":3,"estimated_minutes":5,"difficulty":"practical","tags":["challah-part-1","original-difficulty:practical","source:39"],"source_file_name":"Notes for Hafrashas Challah (part 1).docx","source_start_page":null,"source_end_page":null,"is_selectable":true},{"program_id":"winter-5787","chunk_code":"HC1-F1","source_type":"notes","siman":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","workbook_title":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","section_key":"F","section_title":"Amount of Dough Needed to be Mafrish","chunk_title":"Minimum Dough Amount — Introduction","chunk_summary":"Introduces minimum shiur and broader importance of kezayis, beitzah, and revi’is calculations.","content_markdown":"### F. *Amount of Dough Needed to be Mafrish*\n\nAfter learning how much *challah* should be separated from the dough (a *kezayis*)[28] and that it is best to burn the *challah,* in this section we will discuss the minimum amount of dough needed to create the obligation of *hafrashas challah.* While discussing this question*,* we will also encounter one of the most important *halachic* issues that arises on a daily basis: the calculation of the size of a *kezayis* and *beitzah.* As usual, we will begin with the earliest sources and work our way down to the modern-day *posskim.* After doing so, we will be able to better understand why the calculations of a *kezayis* and *beitzah* vary so greatly among the *posskim.* \n\nThis extremely important and eye-opening *limud* will also impact on many other *halachos* that relate to the size of a *kezayis*, *beitzah*, or *revi’is,* such as the amount required to *bentch* or recite a *bracha acharona*, the size of *matzah* and *maror* eaten on *Pesach*, the minimum volume needed for your *kiddush* cup, and much more. By the end of this section, you will finally understand why the *shiurim* of the חזון איש are almost double the *shiurim* of other *posskim,* and why the opinions regarding the amount of flour needed for *hafrashas challah* vary so greatly*.* Let’s begin! \n\nWe will begin by learning which types of flour are obligated in *hafrashas challah*, followed by the minimum amount of flour required. We will then explore how to calculate that amount, examine an apparent contradiction within that calculation, and finally see the reconciliation that forever changed the understanding of *shiurim*.\n\nSource Notes\n\n[28]:  Unless you do not burn the *challah,* in which case we learned that it is best to separate less than a *kezayis.*","content_document":{"version":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"heading","paragraph":127,"xmlIndex":132,"text":" Amount of Dough Needed to be Mafrish","spans":[{"text":" Amount of Dough Needed to be Mafrish","italic":true,"color":"#2F5496"}],"marker":"F.","level":0,"indent":56.0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0,"rightIndent":43.2,"hanging":18.0,"tabStops":[{"position":150.45,"alignment":"left","leader":"none"},{"position":234.0,"alignment":"center","leader":"none"},{"position":279.0,"alignment":"left","leader":"none"}]},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":128,"xmlIndex":134,"text":"After learning how much challah should be separated from the dough (a kezayis)[28] and that it is best to burn the challah, in this section we will discuss the minimum amount of dough needed to create the obligation of hafrashas challah. While discussing this question, we will also encounter one of the most important halachic issues that arises on a daily basis: the calculation of the size of a kezayis and beitzah. As usual, we will begin with the earliest sources and work our way down to the modern-day posskim. After doing so, we will be able to better understand why the calculations of a kezayis and beitzah vary so greatly among the posskim. ","spans":[{"text":"After learning how much "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"should be separated from the dough (a "},{"text":"kezayis","italic":true},{"text":")"},{"text":"[28]","footnote":28},{"text":" and that it is best to burn the "},{"text":"challah, ","italic":true},{"text":"in this section we will discuss the minimum amount of dough needed to create the obligation of "},{"text":"hafrashas challah. ","italic":true},{"text":"While discussing this question"},{"text":", ","italic":true},{"text":"we will also encounter one of the most important "},{"text":"halachic ","italic":true},{"text":"issues that arises on a daily basis: the calculation of the size of a "},{"text":"kezayis ","italic":true},{"text":"and "},{"text":"beitzah. ","italic":true},{"text":"As usual, we will begin with the earliest sources and work our way down to the modern-day "},{"text":"posskim. ","italic":true},{"text":"After doing so, we will be able to better understand why the calculations of a "},{"text":"kezayis ","italic":true},{"text":"and "},{"text":"beitzah ","italic":true},{"text":"vary so greatly among the "},{"text":"posskim. ","italic":true}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":36.0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[28],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":129,"xmlIndex":135,"text":"This extremely important and eye-opening limud will also impact on many other halachos that relate to the size of a kezayis, beitzah, or revi’is, such as the amount required to bentch or recite a bracha acharona, the size of matzah and maror eaten on Pesach, the minimum volume needed for your kiddush cup, and much more. By the end of this section, you will finally understand why the shiurim of the חזון איש are almost double the shiurim of other posskim, and why the opinions regarding the amount of flour needed for hafrashas challah vary so greatly. Let’s begin! ","spans":[{"text":"This extremely important and eye-opening "},{"text":"limud","italic":true},{"text":" will also impact on many other "},{"text":"halachos","italic":true},{"text":" that relate to the size of a "},{"text":"kezayis","italic":true},{"text":", "},{"text":"beitzah","italic":true},{"text":", or "},{"text":"revi’is, ","italic":true},{"text":"such as the amount required to "},{"text":"bentch ","italic":true},{"text":"or recite a ","italic":false},{"text":"bracha acharona","italic":true},{"text":", the size of "},{"text":"matzah","italic":true},{"text":" and "},{"text":"maror","italic":true},{"text":" eaten on "},{"text":"Pesach","italic":true},{"text":", the minimum volume needed for your "},{"text":"kiddush","italic":true},{"text":" cup, and much more."},{"text":" ","italic":true},{"text":"By the end of this section, you will finally understand why the "},{"text":"shiurim ","italic":true},{"text":"of the חזון איש are almost double the "},{"text":"shiurim ","italic":true},{"text":"of other "},{"text":"posskim, ","italic":true},{"text":"and why the opinions regarding the amount of flour needed for "},{"text":"hafrashas challah ","italic":true},{"text":"vary so greatly"},{"text":". ","italic":true},{"text":"Let’s begin!"},{"text":" ","italic":true}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":36.0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":130,"xmlIndex":136,"text":"We will begin by learning which types of flour are obligated in hafrashas challah, followed by the minimum amount of flour required. We will then explore how to calculate that amount, examine an apparent contradiction within that calculation, and finally see the reconciliation that forever changed the understanding of shiurim.","spans":[{"text":"We will begin by learning which types of flour are obligated in "},{"text":"hafrashas challah","italic":true},{"text":", followed by the minimum amount of flour required. We will then explore how to calculate that amount, examine an apparent contradiction within that calculation, and finally see the reconciliation that forever changed the understanding of "},{"text":"shiurim","italic":true},{"text":"."}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":36.0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}],"footnotes":[{"id":28,"blocks":[{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":0,"xmlIndex":0,"text":" Unless you do not burn the challah, in which case we learned that it is best to separate less than a kezayis.","spans":[{"text":" Unless you do not burn the "},{"text":"challah, ","italic":true},{"text":"in which case we learned that it is best to separate less than a "},{"text":"kezayis.","italic":true}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}]}]},"sort_order":33,"official_shiur_number":3,"estimated_minutes":5,"difficulty":"core","tags":["challah-part-1","original-difficulty:core"],"source_file_name":"Notes for Hafrashas Challah (part 1).docx","source_start_page":null,"source_end_page":null,"is_selectable":true},{"program_id":"winter-5787","chunk_code":"HC1-F2","source_type":"notes","siman":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","workbook_title":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","section_key":"F","section_title":"Amount of Dough Needed to be Mafrish","chunk_title":"Five Grains and One-Tenth of an Eifah","chunk_summary":"Only five grains are obligated; minimum amount is an omer, one-tenth of an eifah.","content_markdown":"<u>I. The type and amount</u>\n\nThe **משנה חלה** (פ״א-א) (40) teaches that only dough made from the five species of grain is obligated in *challah.* The five grains are: Wheat (חיטה), barley (שעורה), spelt (כוסמין), oats (שיבולת שועל),[29] and rye (שיפון).  The **Bartenura** explains that the *mitzvah* of *hafrashas challah* is linked to Pesach because both use the word ״לחם״. Just like *matzah* may only be made from the five grains that can rise and become *chametz,* so too, the *mitzvah* of *hafrashas challah* only applies to dough made from the five grains.\n\nNow that we know which types of flour require *hafrasha*, we can learn about the minimum amount needed. The **גמרא עירובין** (פג:) (41) derives from a *passuk* that the obligation to be *mafrish challah* applies only from a batch of dough equal in size to the portion of manna (*man*) in the *midbar* – an *omer,* which is 1/10 of an *eifah.* \n\nSource Notes\n\n[29]:  Most *posskim* assume that שיבולת שועל refers to oats (see, e.g., וזאת הברכה פי\"ב, R. Bodner, *Halochos of Brachos,* p.480). However, it should be noted that some have suggested, based on the *Aruch* (ערך שבל), and the רמב\"ם (פיה\"מ כלאים א:א)*,* that it is not oats, since שבולת שועל is a subspecies of barley according to Chazal (see כלאים א-א), whereas oats are not related to barley. As a result, a number of *posskim,* including שערי ציון cited by Rav Sternbuch (תשובות והנהגות א-שב, though see also ה-קל) and Rav Schachter (in a *shiur* on Yutorah), consider it a *safek* whether oats are actually grain. \n","content_document":{"version":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":131,"xmlIndex":137,"text":"I. The type and amount","spans":[{"text":"I. The type and amount","underline":true}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":132,"xmlIndex":138,"text":"The משנה חלה (פ״א-א) (40) teaches that only dough made from the five species of grain is obligated in challah. The five grains are: Wheat (חיטה), barley (שעורה), spelt (כוסמין), oats (שיבולת שועל),[29] and rye (שיפון).  The Bartenura explains that the mitzvah of hafrashas challah is linked to Pesach because both use the word ״לחם״. Just like matzah may only be made from the five grains that can rise and become chametz, so too, the mitzvah of hafrashas challah only applies to dough made from the five grains.","spans":[{"text":"The "},{"text":"משנה חלה ","bold":true},{"text":"(פ״א-א) (40) teaches that only dough made from the five species of grain is obligated in "},{"text":"challah. ","italic":true},{"text":"The five grains are: Wheat (חיטה), barley (שעורה), spelt (כוסמין), oats (שיבולת שועל),"},{"text":"[29]","footnote":29},{"text":" and rye (שיפון).  The "},{"text":"Bartenura ","bold":true},{"text":"explains that the "},{"text":"mitzvah ","italic":true},{"text":"of "},{"text":"hafrashas challah ","italic":true},{"text":"is linked to Pesach because both use the word ״לחם״. Just like "},{"text":"matzah ","italic":true},{"text":"may only be made from the five grains that can rise and become "},{"text":"chametz, ","italic":true},{"text":"so too, the "},{"text":"mitzvah ","italic":true},{"text":"of "},{"text":"hafrashas challah ","italic":true},{"text":"only applies to dough made from the five grains."}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":36.0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[29],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":133,"xmlIndex":139,"text":"Now that we know which types of flour require hafrasha, we can learn about the minimum amount needed. The גמרא עירובין (פג:) (41) derives from a passuk that the obligation to be mafrish challah applies only from a batch of dough equal in size to the portion of manna (man) in the midbar – an omer, which is 1/10 of an eifah. ","spans":[{"text":"Now that we know which types of flour require "},{"text":"hafrasha","italic":true},{"text":", we can learn about the minimum amount needed. The "},{"text":"גמרא עירובין ","bold":true},{"text":"(פג:) (41) derives from a "},{"text":"passuk","italic":true},{"text":" that the obligation to be "},{"text":"mafrish challah ","italic":true},{"text":"applies only from a batch of dough equal in size to the portion of manna ("},{"text":"man","italic":true},{"text":") in the "},{"text":"midbar","italic":true},{"text":" – an "},{"text":"omer, ","italic":true},{"text":"which is 1/10 of an "},{"text":"eifah. ","italic":true}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":36.0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}],"footnotes":[{"id":29,"blocks":[{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":0,"xmlIndex":0,"text":" Most posskim assume that שיבולת שועל refers to oats (see, e.g., וזאת הברכה פי\"ב, R. Bodner, Halochos of Brachos, p.480). However, it should be noted that some have suggested, based on the Aruch (ערך שבל), and the רמב\"ם (פיה\"מ כלאים א:א), that it is not oats, since שבולת שועל is a subspecies of barley according to Chazal (see כלאים א-א), whereas oats are not related to barley. As a result, a number of posskim, including שערי ציון cited by Rav Sternbuch (תשובות והנהגות א-שב, though see also ה-קל) and Rav Schachter (in a shiur on Yutorah), consider it a safek whether oats are actually grain. ","spans":[{"text":" Most "},{"text":"posskim ","italic":true},{"text":"assume that שיבולת שועל refers to oats (see, e.g., וזאת הברכה פי\"ב, R. Bodner, "},{"text":"Halochos of Brachos, ","italic":true},{"text":"p.480). However, it should be noted that some have suggested, based on the "},{"text":"Aruch ","italic":true},{"text":"(ערך שבל), and the רמב\"ם (פיה\"מ כלאים א:א)"},{"text":", ","italic":true},{"text":"that it is not oats, since שבולת שועל is a subspecies of barley according to Chazal (see כלאים א-א), whereas oats are not related to barley. As a result, a number of "},{"text":"posskim, ","italic":true},{"text":"including שערי ציון cited by Rav Sternbuch (תשובות והנהגות א-שב, though see also ה-קל) and Rav Schachter (in a "},{"text":"shiur ","italic":true},{"text":"on Yutorah), consider it a "},{"text":"safek ","italic":true},{"text":"whether oats are actually grain. "}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":0,"xmlIndex":0,"text":"","spans":[],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}]}]},"sort_order":34,"official_shiur_number":3,"estimated_minutes":5,"difficulty":"core","tags":["challah-part-1","original-difficulty:core","source:40","source:41"],"source_file_name":"Notes for Hafrashas Challah (part 1).docx","source_start_page":null,"source_end_page":null,"is_selectable":true},{"program_id":"winter-5787","chunk_code":"HC1-F3","source_type":"notes","siman":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","workbook_title":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","section_key":"F","section_title":"Amount of Dough Needed to be Mafrish","chunk_title":"Calculating 43.2 Eggs and 311 Cubic Thumbbreadths","chunk_summary":"Measurement chain from eifah to beitzah; 43.2 eggs; revi’is calculation; S”A/Rama accept both calculations.","content_markdown":"<u>II. The calculation</u>\n\nHow much is 1/10 of an *eifah*? There is an important acronym that every learned Jew should know that will help you determine **any** measurement you ever encounter when learning Mishnah, Gemara, or *halacha.* The acronym is **כאסקלב יגודו**— where **כאסקלב**  represents the units of measurement: **כ**ור, **א**יפה, **ס**אה, **ק**ב, **ל**וג, **ב**יצה. The word **יגודו** provides the numerical relationship between each unit, indicating how many of each measure is contained within the next. Thus, a כור contains 10 איפה, an איפה contains 3 סאה, a סאה contains 6 קב, a קב contains 4 לוגין, and a לוג contains 6 ביצים. See below. \n\n![Hebrew measurement mnemonic from paragraphs 136-137](storage://official-learning-materials/sha256/bb24a9def2253bc6b0f08ca2d4a52342f42f70e3e36d1e727585939ef1fd55d8.png)\n\nYou can use the above diagram to calculate the size of any *halachic* measurement. For example, if you want to calculate how many eggs are in a קב, you would multiply 6 x 4 = 24, since there are 6 eggs in a לוג and 4 לוג in a קב. \n\nRegarding *hafrashas challah,* we learned above that the Gemara teaches that the measurement is 1/10 of an *eifah.* If we want to figure out that exact measurement in eggs, we would need to calculate how many eggs are in an *eifah* and take 10% of that. Using the above diagram, we can easily calculate the number of eggs in an *eifah* by multiplying 6 x 4 x 6 x 3 = 432 eggs. 10% of 432 is 43.2, meaning that the minimum amount of dough required for *hafrashas challah* is 43⅕ eggs.\n\nHowever, there is another Gemara that can also shed light on the volume needed for *hafrashas challah.* The **גמרא פסחים** (קט.) (42) teaches that a *revi’is* (¼ of a לוג) is a volume of 2 fingerbreadths by 2 fingerbreadths by 2.7 fingerbreadths, which equals a total volume of **10.8 cubic fingerbreadths** (2 x 2 x 2.7) (see image). Thus, an entire לוג is 43.2 cubic fingerbreadths (10.8 x 4). Using the calculation above, we can calculate that there are 72 לוג in an *eifah* (4 x 6 x 3), which totals a volume of 3110.4 cubic fingerbreadths. Since the minimum amount of dough required for *challah* is 1/10 of an *eifah,* the volume required is 311.04 cubic fingerbreadths.\n\n![Official diagram at paragraph 140](storage://official-learning-materials/sha256/4963316dba30b30d76e22beda0b9cb26bef3b28a19eaae83868dd12459bc3eec.jpg)\n\nThe S”A and *Rama* in **סימן שכד-א** (43) *passkin* like both of the above גמרות. The S”A writes that one is only obligated to be *mafrish challah* from a batch of dough made from the five grains, and only if it contains the volume of 43⅕ eggs. The **ש״ך** adds that an easy way to remember this is the word חלה, which has the *gematria* of 43, and the ה at the end also alludes to the additional ⅕. The S”A also references the טור (או״ח סימן תנ\"ו), who explains that this volume can be measured by using the water-displacement method. A bowl is filled to the top with water and placed inside a bigger empty bowl. One then places 43⅕ eggs into the smaller bowl, and the amount of displaced water that overflows equals the volume of flour needed for *hafrashas challah*. \n\nThe *Rama* adds, based on the second Gemara above, that this volume can also be calculated by constructing a *kli* with a volume of 10 fingerbreadths x 10 fingerbreadths and a height of 3 and ⅑  fingerbreadths (or 3.1111). The volume of such a vessel is 311 cubic fingerbreadths (10 x 10 x 3.111). The *Rama* concludes that the fingerbreadth referred to here is the width of an average thumb (see image above).","content_document":{"version":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":134,"xmlIndex":140,"text":"II. The calculation","spans":[{"text":"II. The calculation","underline":true}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":135,"xmlIndex":141,"text":"How much is 1/10 of an eifah? There is an important acronym that every learned Jew should know that will help you determine any measurement you ever encounter when learning Mishnah, Gemara, or halacha. The acronym is כאסקלב יגודו— where כאסקלב  represents the units of measurement: כור, איפה, סאה, קב, לוג, ביצה. The word יגודו provides the numerical relationship between each unit, indicating how many of each measure is contained within the next. Thus, a כור contains 10 איפה, an איפה contains 3 סאה, a סאה contains 6 קב, a קב contains 4 לוגין, and a לוג contains 6 ביצים. See below. ","spans":[{"text":"How much is 1/10 of an "},{"text":"eifah","italic":true},{"text":"? There is an important acronym that every learned Jew should know that will help you determine "},{"text":"any","bold":true},{"text":" measurement you ever encounter when learning Mishnah, Gemara, or "},{"text":"halacha.","italic":true},{"text":" The acronym is "},{"text":"כאסקלב יגודו","bold":true},{"text":"— where "},{"text":"כאסקלב","bold":true},{"text":"  represents the units of measurement: "},{"text":"כ","bold":true},{"text":"ור, "},{"text":"א","bold":true},{"text":"יפה, "},{"text":"ס","bold":true},{"text":"אה, "},{"text":"ק","bold":true},{"text":"ב, "},{"text":"ל","bold":true},{"text":"וג, "},{"text":"ב","bold":true},{"text":"יצה. The word "},{"text":"יגודו","bold":true},{"text":" provides the numerical relationship between each unit, indicating how many of each measure is contained within the next. Thus, a כור contains 10 איפה, an איפה contains 3 סאה, a סאה contains 6 קב, a קב contains 4 לוגין, and a לוג contains 6 ביצים. See below. "}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":36.0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"image","paragraph":136,"alt":"Hebrew measurement mnemonic from paragraphs 136-137","width":359,"height":126,"displayWidth":359,"storagePath":"sha256/bb24a9def2253bc6b0f08ca2d4a52342f42f70e3e36d1e727585939ef1fd55d8.png"},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":138,"xmlIndex":145,"text":"You can use the above diagram to calculate the size of any halachic measurement. For example, if you want to calculate how many eggs are in a קב, you would multiply 6 x 4 = 24, since there are 6 eggs in a לוג and 4 לוג in a קב. ","spans":[{"text":"You can use the above diagram to calculate the size of any "},{"text":"halachic ","italic":true},{"text":"measurement. For example, if you want to calculate how many eggs are in a קב, you would multiply 6 x 4 = 24, since there are 6 eggs in a לוג and 4 לוג in a קב. "}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":36.0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":139,"xmlIndex":146,"text":"Regarding hafrashas challah, we learned above that the Gemara teaches that the measurement is 1/10 of an eifah. If we want to figure out that exact measurement in eggs, we would need to calculate how many eggs are in an eifah and take 10% of that. Using the above diagram, we can easily calculate the number of eggs in an eifah by multiplying 6 x 4 x 6 x 3 = 432 eggs. 10% of 432 is 43.2, meaning that the minimum amount of dough required for hafrashas challah is 43⅕ eggs.","spans":[{"text":"Regarding "},{"text":"hafrashas challah, ","italic":true},{"text":"we learned above that the Gemara teaches that the measurement is 1/10 of an "},{"text":"eifah. ","italic":true},{"text":"If we want to figure out that exact measurement in eggs, we would need to calculate how many eggs are in an "},{"text":"eifah ","italic":true},{"text":"and take 10% of that. Using the above diagram, we can easily calculate the number of eggs in an "},{"text":"eifah ","italic":true},{"text":"by multiplying 6 x 4 x 6 x 3 = 432 eggs. 10% of 432 is 43.2, meaning that the minimum amount of dough required for "},{"text":"hafrashas challah ","italic":true},{"text":"is 43⅕ eggs."}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":36.0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":140,"xmlIndex":147,"text":"However, there is another Gemara that can also shed light on the volume needed for hafrashas challah. The גמרא פסחים (קט.) (42) teaches that a revi’is (¼ of a לוג) is a volume of 2 fingerbreadths by 2 fingerbreadths by 2.7 fingerbreadths, which equals a total volume of 10.8 cubic fingerbreadths (2 x 2 x 2.7) (see image). Thus, an entire לוג is 43.2 cubic fingerbreadths (10.8 x 4). Using the calculation above, we can calculate that there are 72 לוג in an eifah (4 x 6 x 3), which totals a volume of 3110.4 cubic fingerbreadths. Since the minimum amount of dough required for challah is 1/10 of an eifah, the volume required is 311.04 cubic fingerbreadths.","spans":[{"text":"However, there is another Gemara that can also shed light on the volume needed for "},{"text":"hafrashas challah. ","italic":true},{"text":"The "},{"text":"גמרא פסחים ","bold":true},{"text":"(קט.) (42) teaches that a "},{"text":"revi’is ","italic":true},{"text":"(¼ of a לוג) is a volume of 2 fingerbreadths by 2 fingerbreadths by 2.7 fingerbreadths, which equals a total volume of "},{"text":"10.8 cubic fingerbreadths","bold":true},{"text":" (2 x 2 x 2.7) (see image). Thus, an entire לוג is 43.2 cubic fingerbreadths (10.8 x 4). Using the calculation above, we can calculate that there are 72 לוג in an "},{"text":"eifah ","italic":true},{"text":"(4 x 6 x 3), which totals a volume of 3110.4 cubic fingerbreadths. Since the minimum amount of dough required for "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"is 1/10 of an "},{"text":"eifah, ","italic":true},{"text":"the volume required is 311.04 cubic fingerbreadths."}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":36.0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"image","paragraph":140,"alt":"Official diagram at paragraph 140","width":547,"height":365,"displayWidth":239,"storagePath":"sha256/4963316dba30b30d76e22beda0b9cb26bef3b28a19eaae83868dd12459bc3eec.jpg"},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":141,"xmlIndex":148,"text":"The S”A and Rama in סימן שכד-א (43) passkin like both of the above גמרות. The S”A writes that one is only obligated to be mafrish challah from a batch of dough made from the five grains, and only if it contains the volume of 43⅕ eggs. The ש״ך adds that an easy way to remember this is the word חלה, which has the gematria of 43, and the ה at the end also alludes to the additional ⅕. The S”A also references the טור (או״ח סימן תנ\"ו), who explains that this volume can be measured by using the water-displacement method. A bowl is filled to the top with water and placed inside a bigger empty bowl. One then places 43⅕ eggs into the smaller bowl, and the amount of displaced water that overflows equals the volume of flour needed for hafrashas challah. ","spans":[{"text":"The S”A and "},{"text":"Rama","italic":true},{"text":" in "},{"text":"סימן שכד-א","bold":true},{"text":" (43) "},{"text":"passkin ","italic":true},{"text":"like both of the above גמרות. The S”A writes that one is only obligated to be "},{"text":"mafrish challah ","italic":true},{"text":"from a batch of dough made from the five grains, and only if it contains the volume of 43⅕ eggs. The "},{"text":"ש״ך ","bold":true},{"text":"adds that an easy way to remember this is the word חלה, which has the "},{"text":"gematria ","italic":true},{"text":"of 43, and the ה at the end also alludes to the additional ⅕. The S”A also references the טור (או״ח סימן תנ\"ו), who explains that this volume can be measured by using the water-displacement method. A bowl is filled to the top with water and placed inside a bigger empty bowl. One then places 43⅕ eggs into the smaller bowl, and the amount of displaced water that overflows equals the volume of flour needed for "},{"text":"hafrashas challah","italic":true},{"text":". "}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":36.0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":142,"xmlIndex":149,"text":"The Rama adds, based on the second Gemara above, that this volume can also be calculated by constructing a kli with a volume of 10 fingerbreadths x 10 fingerbreadths and a height of 3 and ⅑  fingerbreadths (or 3.1111). The volume of such a vessel is 311 cubic fingerbreadths (10 x 10 x 3.111). The Rama concludes that the fingerbreadth referred to here is the width of an average thumb (see image above).","spans":[{"text":"The "},{"text":"Rama ","italic":true},{"text":"adds, based on the second Gemara above, that this volume can also be calculated by constructing a "},{"text":"kli ","italic":true},{"text":"with a volume of 10 fingerbreadths x 10 fingerbreadths and a height of 3 and ⅑  fingerbreadths (or 3.1111). The volume of such a vessel is 311 cubic fingerbreadths (10 x 10 x 3.111). The "},{"text":"Rama ","italic":true},{"text":"concludes that the fingerbreadth referred to here is the width of an average thumb (see image above)."}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":36.0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}],"footnotes":[]},"sort_order":35,"official_shiur_number":3,"estimated_minutes":10,"difficulty":"core","tags":["challah-part-1","original-difficulty:core","source:42","source:43"],"source_file_name":"Notes for Hafrashas Challah (part 1).docx","source_start_page":null,"source_end_page":null,"is_selectable":true},{"program_id":"winter-5787","chunk_code":"HC1-F4","source_type":"notes","siman":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","workbook_title":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","section_key":"F","section_title":"Amount of Dough Needed to be Mafrish","chunk_title":"The Contradiction Between Egg and Thumb Measures","chunk_summary":"Tzlach notes that thumb-based measurement is nearly double egg-based measurement.","content_markdown":"<u>III. The contradiction</u>\n\nAt first glance, the two methods of calculation mentioned in the S”A appear to describe the same measurement and should therefore produce the same volume. However, the צל\"ח (Rav Yechezkel Landau, 1713-1793, author of the נודע ביהודה) points out that, in practice, the two methods yield drastically different results. The **צל״ח** (פסחים קטז:) (44) writes that he measured both 43.2 eggs and 311 cubic thumb-widths and discovered that the thumb-widths calculation was almost double that of the egg-based calculation. The צל״ח had to do the actual calculation, but today by using ChatGPT, you can have it do the calculation for you.\n\nWe’ll first calculate the volume of 43.2 eggs and then the volume of 311 cubic thumbs. According to ChatGPT, the average volume of an egg is between 50-55 ml. Using an average volume of about 52 ml per egg, the volume of 43.2 eggs equals approximately 2246.4 ml (52 x 43.2), or about **2.25 liters**.\n\nNow let’s calculate the thumb measurement. The average width of a male thumb is approximately 2.5 cm (also according to ChatGPT). If you make a vessel with the volume of 10 thumb-widths by 10 thumb-widths by 3 and ⅑thumb-widths, the vessel will hold **4.86 liters**, almost double the measurement of the eggs! This is a major discrepancy between the two measurements brought in the Gemara and employed in the *pssak* of the S”A and *Rama*!","content_document":{"version":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":143,"xmlIndex":150,"text":"III. The contradiction","spans":[{"text":"III. The contradiction","underline":true}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":144,"xmlIndex":151,"text":"At first glance, the two methods of calculation mentioned in the S”A appear to describe the same measurement and should therefore produce the same volume. However, the צל\"ח (Rav Yechezkel Landau, 1713-1793, author of the נודע ביהודה) points out that, in practice, the two methods yield drastically different results. The צל״ח (פסחים קטז:) (44) writes that he measured both 43.2 eggs and 311 cubic thumb-widths and discovered that the thumb-widths calculation was almost double that of the egg-based calculation. The צל״ח had to do the actual calculation, but today by using ChatGPT, you can have it do the calculation for you.","spans":[{"text":"At first glance, the two methods of calculation mentioned in the S”A appear to describe the same measurement and should therefore produce the same volume. However, the צל\"ח (Rav Yechezkel Landau, 1713-1793, author of the נודע ביהודה) points out that, in practice, the two methods yield drastically different results. The "},{"text":"צל״ח","bold":true},{"text":" (פסחים קטז:) (44) writes that he measured both 43.2 eggs and 311 cubic thumb-widths and discovered that the thumb-widths calculation was almost double that of the egg-based calculation. The צל״ח had to do the actual calculation, but today by using ChatGPT, you can have it do the calculation for you."}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":36.0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":145,"xmlIndex":152,"text":"We’ll first calculate the volume of 43.2 eggs and then the volume of 311 cubic thumbs. According to ChatGPT, the average volume of an egg is between 50-55 ml. Using an average volume of about 52 ml per egg, the volume of 43.2 eggs equals approximately 2246.4 ml (52 x 43.2), or about 2.25 liters.","spans":[{"text":"We’ll first calculate the volume of 43.2 eggs and then the volume of 311 cubic thumbs. According to ChatGPT, the average volume of an egg is between 50-55 ml. Using an average volume of about 52 ml per egg, the volume of 43.2 eggs equals approximately 2246.4 ml (52 x 43.2), or about "},{"text":"2.25 liters","bold":true},{"text":"."}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":36.0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":146,"xmlIndex":153,"text":"Now let’s calculate the thumb measurement. The average width of a male thumb is approximately 2.5 cm (also according to ChatGPT). If you make a vessel with the volume of 10 thumb-widths by 10 thumb-widths by 3 and ⅑thumb-widths, the vessel will hold 4.86 liters, almost double the measurement of the eggs! This is a major discrepancy between the two measurements brought in the Gemara and employed in the pssak of the S”A and Rama!","spans":[{"text":"Now let’s calculate the thumb measurement. The average width of a male thumb is approximately 2.5 cm (also according to ChatGPT). If you make a vessel with the volume of 10 thumb-widths by 10 thumb-widths by 3 and ⅑thumb-widths, the vessel will hold "},{"text":"4.86 liters","bold":true},{"text":", almost double the measurement of the eggs! This is a major discrepancy between the two measurements brought in the Gemara and employed in the "},{"text":"pssak ","italic":true},{"text":"of the S”A and "},{"text":"Rama","italic":true},{"text":"!"}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":36.0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}],"footnotes":[]},"sort_order":36,"official_shiur_number":3,"estimated_minutes":6,"difficulty":"advanced","tags":["challah-part-1","original-difficulty:advanced","source:44"],"source_file_name":"Notes for Hafrashas Challah (part 1).docx","source_start_page":null,"source_end_page":null,"is_selectable":true},{"program_id":"winter-5787","chunk_code":"HC1-F5","source_type":"notes","siman":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","workbook_title":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","section_key":"F","section_title":"Amount of Dough Needed to be Mafrish","chunk_title":"Tzlach and Chazon Ish Reconciliation","chunk_summary":"Eggs shrank; modern egg equals kezayis; Chazon Ish defends Tzlach and rejects claim he retracted.","content_markdown":"<u>IV. The צל״ח and חזון איש reconciliation</u>\n\nThe **צל״ח** concludes that one of two possibilities must be true: either the size of eggs has shrunk by approximately half or people’s thumbs have approximately doubled in size. Ultimately, he concludes that eggs have shrunk, and today’s eggs are approximately half the size of those used in Chazal’s calculations. Therefore, if one wishes to calculate the minimum size for *hafrashas challah* using today’s eggs, about 86 eggs would be required. The צל״ח adds that since our eggs are half the size of those used by Chazal, and a *kezayis* is half an egg, one of today’s eggs would be equal to one *kezayis.* Consequently, he would calculate the *kezayis* of *matzah* and *maror* to be used on Pesach as equal to the size of a modern-day egg.  \n\nThe **חזון איש** (או״ח לט-יב) (45) *passkins* like the צל״ח and writes that this was also the *pssak* of the **חתם סופר** and many others (including the גר״א quoted in the פת״ש (שכד-א)). The חזון איש also cites  the תשובה מאהבה, a student of the צל״ח, who describes the צל״ח as a very tall and large individual. The תשובה מאהבה suggested to his *rebbe* that maybe his measurements were inaccurate because he used his own thumbs, which were larger than average, and the צל״ח laughed in response, implying that perhaps he rescinded his *pssak*. \n\nThe חזון איש, however, strongly rejects the possibility that the צל״ח retracted his *pssak* for a number of reasons: 1) The צל״ח was certainly aware that Chazal’s measurements were based on the thumb of an average person. 2) The תשובה מאהבה was not present when the actual measurements were being conducted. 3) Had the צל״ח really rescinded his *pssak,* he would have publicized it, since it is an earth-shattering *pssak* that has tremendous ramifications throughout *halacha*. ","content_document":{"version":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":147,"xmlIndex":154,"text":"IV. The צל״ח and חזון איש reconciliation","spans":[{"text":"IV. The צל״ח and חזון איש reconciliation","underline":true}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":148,"xmlIndex":155,"text":"The צל״ח concludes that one of two possibilities must be true: either the size of eggs has shrunk by approximately half or people’s thumbs have approximately doubled in size. Ultimately, he concludes that eggs have shrunk, and today’s eggs are approximately half the size of those used in Chazal’s calculations. Therefore, if one wishes to calculate the minimum size for hafrashas challah using today’s eggs, about 86 eggs would be required. The צל״ח adds that since our eggs are half the size of those used by Chazal, and a kezayis is half an egg, one of today’s eggs would be equal to one kezayis. Consequently, he would calculate the kezayis of matzah and maror to be used on Pesach as equal to the size of a modern-day egg.  ","spans":[{"text":"The "},{"text":"צל״ח ","bold":true},{"text":"concludes that one of two possibilities must be true: either the size of eggs has shrunk by approximately half or people’s thumbs have approximately doubled in size. Ultimately, he concludes that eggs have shrunk, and today’s eggs are approximately "},{"text":"half the size","bold":false},{"text":" of those used in Chazal’s calculations. Therefore, if one wishes to calculate the minimum size for "},{"text":"hafrashas challah ","italic":true},{"text":"using today’s eggs, about 86 eggs would be required. The צל״ח adds that since our eggs are half the size of those used by Chazal, and a "},{"text":"kezayis ","italic":true},{"text":"is half an egg, one of today’s eggs would be equal to one "},{"text":"kezayis. ","italic":true},{"text":"Consequently, he would calculate the "},{"text":"kezayis ","italic":true},{"text":"of "},{"text":"matzah ","italic":true},{"text":"and "},{"text":"maror ","italic":true},{"text":"to be used on Pesach as equal to the size of a modern-day egg.  "}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":36.0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":149,"xmlIndex":156,"text":"The חזון איש (או״ח לט-יב) (45) passkins like the צל״ח and writes that this was also the pssak of the חתם סופר and many others (including the גר״א quoted in the פת״ש (שכד-א)). The חזון איש also cites  the תשובה מאהבה, a student of the צל״ח, who describes the צל״ח as a very tall and large individual. The תשובה מאהבה suggested to his rebbe that maybe his measurements were inaccurate because he used his own thumbs, which were larger than average, and the צל״ח laughed in response, implying that perhaps he rescinded his pssak. ","spans":[{"text":"The "},{"text":"חזון איש ","bold":true},{"text":"(או״ח לט-יב) (45) "},{"text":"passkins ","italic":true},{"text":"like the צל״ח and writes that this was also the "},{"text":"pssak ","italic":true},{"text":"of the "},{"text":"חתם סופר","bold":true},{"text":" and many others (including the גר״א quoted in the פת״ש (שכד-א)). The חזון איש also cites  the תשובה מאהבה, a student of the צל״ח, who describes the צל״ח as a very tall and large individual. The תשובה מאהבה suggested to his "},{"text":"rebbe ","italic":true},{"text":"that maybe his measurements were inaccurate because he used his own thumbs, which were larger than average, and the צל״ח laughed in response, implying that perhaps he rescinded his "},{"text":"pssak","italic":true},{"text":". "}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":36.0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":150,"xmlIndex":157,"text":"The חזון איש, however, strongly rejects the possibility that the צל״ח retracted his pssak for a number of reasons: 1) The צל״ח was certainly aware that Chazal’s measurements were based on the thumb of an average person. 2) The תשובה מאהבה was not present when the actual measurements were being conducted. 3) Had the צל״ח really rescinded his pssak, he would have publicized it, since it is an earth-shattering pssak that has tremendous ramifications throughout halacha. ","spans":[{"text":"The חזון איש, however, strongly rejects the possibility that the צל״ח retracted his "},{"text":"pssak ","italic":true},{"text":"for a number of reasons: 1) The צל״ח was certainly aware that Chazal’s measurements were based on the thumb of an average person. 2) The תשובה מאהבה was not present when the actual measurements were being conducted. 3) Had the צל״ח really rescinded his "},{"text":"pssak, ","italic":true},{"text":"he would have publicized it, since it is an earth-shattering "},{"text":"pssak ","italic":true},{"text":"that has tremendous ramifications throughout "},{"text":"halacha","italic":true},{"text":". "}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":36.0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}],"footnotes":[]},"sort_order":37,"official_shiur_number":3,"estimated_minutes":8,"difficulty":"advanced","tags":["challah-part-1","original-difficulty:advanced","source:45"],"source_file_name":"Notes for Hafrashas Challah (part 1).docx","source_start_page":null,"source_end_page":null,"is_selectable":true},{"program_id":"winter-5787","chunk_code":"HC1-F6","source_type":"notes","siman":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","workbook_title":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","section_key":"F","section_title":"Amount of Dough Needed to be Mafrish","chunk_title":"Rav Chaim Naeh Reconciliation","chunk_summary":"Rav Chaim Naeh argues eggs did not shrink and reconciles by using narrower thumb measurement.","content_markdown":"<u>V. Reconciliation of רב חיים נאה</u>\n\n\tIn תש״ג (1943), רב חיים נאה published a *sefer* called שיעורי תורה where he argues on the צל״ח and maintains that the size of eggs has not decreased since the time of Chazal. In תש״ז (1947), the חזון איש responded by publishing קונטרס השיעורים to defend the position of the צל״ח. Two years later, in תש״ט (1949), the *Steipler* defended his brother-in-law, the חזון איש, in a *sefer* called שיעורין דאורייתא. Later that year, רב חיים נאה defended his opinion by publishing another *sefer* called שיעורי ציון. We could spend months going through the intricacies and back-and-forth arguments of this fundamental and far-reaching dispute, but we will only mention a few main points to help us better understand the various opinions.\n\n\tרב חיים נאה in **שיעורי תורה** (שער ה-א) (46) explains that there is a dispute whether to calculate the width of the thumb from the widest part of the thumb or the narrower section. Therefore, when the egg-based calculation does not match the thumb-width-based calculation, the width should be measured at its narrowest area. רב חיים נאה writes that when calculating the width of the thumb from the narrow section, the two measurements quoted in the Gemara and S”A are consistent. The width of a thumb at its narrowest part is about 2 cm, and 311 cubic thumbs of 2 cm each equals **2.49 liters,** which is much closer to the calculation of the eggs (2.25 liters),[30] and if the volume of the eggs is calculated to be slightly larger, the measurements are nearly the same.\n\n![Official diagram at paragraph 153](storage://official-learning-materials/sha256/fcb5861bdea64e9a80ad556f80a8767929dfb78692bb1b44ae32eb1f6fe8384e.png)\n\nSource Notes\n\n[30]:  Above we reached 2.25 ml because we used an egg with a volume of 52ml, but if we use an egg with a bigger volume, the numbers will equal as will be explained below by רב חיים נאה.","content_document":{"version":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":151,"xmlIndex":158,"text":"V. Reconciliation of רב חיים נאה","spans":[{"text":"V. Reconciliation of רב חיים נאה","underline":true}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":152,"xmlIndex":159,"text":"\tIn תש״ג (1943), רב חיים נאה published a sefer called שיעורי תורה where he argues on the צל״ח and maintains that the size of eggs has not decreased since the time of Chazal. In תש״ז (1947), the חזון איש responded by publishing קונטרס השיעורים to defend the position of the צל״ח. Two years later, in תש״ט (1949), the Steipler defended his brother-in-law, the חזון איש, in a sefer called שיעורין דאורייתא. Later that year, רב חיים נאה defended his opinion by publishing another sefer called שיעורי ציון. We could spend months going through the intricacies and back-and-forth arguments of this fundamental and far-reaching dispute, but we will only mention a few main points to help us better understand the various opinions.","spans":[{"text":"\tIn תש״ג (1943), רב חיים נאה published a "},{"text":"sefer ","italic":true},{"text":"called שיעורי תורה where he argues on the צל״ח and maintains that the size of eggs has not decreased since the time of Chazal. In תש״ז (1947), the חזון איש responded by publishing קונטרס השיעורים to defend the position of the צל״ח. Two years later, in תש״ט (1949), the "},{"text":"Steipler ","italic":true},{"text":"defended his brother-in-law, the חזון איש, in a "},{"text":"sefer ","italic":true},{"text":"called שיעורין דאורייתא. Later that year, רב חיים נאה defended his opinion by publishing another "},{"text":"sefer ","italic":true},{"text":"called שיעורי ציון. We could spend months going through the intricacies and back-and-forth arguments of this fundamental and far-reaching dispute, but we will only mention a few main points to help us better understand the various opinions."}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":1,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":153,"xmlIndex":160,"text":"\tרב חיים נאה in שיעורי תורה (שער ה-א) (46) explains that there is a dispute whether to calculate the width of the thumb from the widest part of the thumb or the narrower section. Therefore, when the egg-based calculation does not match the thumb-width-based calculation, the width should be measured at its narrowest area. רב חיים נאה writes that when calculating the width of the thumb from the narrow section, the two measurements quoted in the Gemara and S”A are consistent. The width of a thumb at its narrowest part is about 2 cm, and 311 cubic thumbs of 2 cm each equals 2.49 liters, which is much closer to the calculation of the eggs (2.25 liters),[30] and if the volume of the eggs is calculated to be slightly larger, the measurements are nearly the same.","spans":[{"text":"\tרב חיים נאה in "},{"text":"שיעורי תורה ","bold":true},{"text":"(שער ה-א) (46) explains that there is a dispute whether to calculate the width of the thumb from the widest part of the thumb or the narrower section. Therefore, when the egg-based calculation does not match the thumb-width-based calculation, the width should be measured at its narrowest area. רב חיים נאה writes that when calculating the width of the thumb from the narrow section, the two measurements quoted in the Gemara and S”A are consistent. The width of a thumb at its narrowest part is about 2 cm, and 311 cubic thumbs of 2 cm each equals "},{"text":"2.49 liters,","bold":true},{"text":" which is much closer to the calculation of the eggs (2.25 liters),"},{"text":"[30]","footnote":30},{"text":" and if the volume of the eggs is calculated to be slightly larger, the measurements are nearly the same."}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":1,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[30],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"image","paragraph":153,"alt":"Official diagram at paragraph 153","width":349,"height":222,"displayWidth":152,"storagePath":"sha256/fcb5861bdea64e9a80ad556f80a8767929dfb78692bb1b44ae32eb1f6fe8384e.png"}],"footnotes":[{"id":30,"blocks":[{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":0,"xmlIndex":0,"text":" Above we reached 2.25 ml because we used an egg with a volume of 52ml, but if we use an egg with a bigger volume, the numbers will equal as will be explained below by רב חיים נאה.","spans":[{"text":" Above we reached 2.25 ml because we used an egg with a volume of 52ml, but if we use an egg with a bigger volume, the numbers will equal as will be explained below by רב חיים נאה."}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}]}]},"sort_order":38,"official_shiur_number":3,"estimated_minutes":6,"difficulty":"advanced","tags":["challah-part-1","original-difficulty:advanced","source:46"],"source_file_name":"Notes for Hafrashas Challah (part 1).docx","source_start_page":null,"source_end_page":null,"is_selectable":true},{"program_id":"winter-5787","chunk_code":"HC1-F7","source_type":"notes","siman":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","workbook_title":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","section_key":"F","section_title":"Amount of Dough Needed to be Mafrish","chunk_title":"How Do We Pasken?","chunk_summary":"Biur Halacha: be machmir for biblical mitzvos with larger shiur, smaller for rabbinic; question of challah’s status today.","content_markdown":"<u>VI. How do we</u> *<u>passkin</u>*<u>?</u>\n\n\tThe **ביאור הלכה** (סימן רעא ד״ה יש רביעית) (47) quotes the צל״ח’s question regarding the large discrepancy between the thumb-based and egg-based measurements and writes that although the צל\"ח asked “a great and difficult question,” there is an equally strong question on his answer from the גמרא יומא (פ.). The Gemara there writes that two cheekfuls of an average-sized man is slightly more than a *revi’is,* and a *revi’is* is about the size of one and a half eggs. Accordingly, today, when the eggs are half the size they used to be, a *revi’is* would be the size of three of our eggs. The חפץ חיים writes that he asked several average-sized individuals if they could fit three eggs in their mouth, and the most any of them were able to fit was two. (Furthermore, we cannot explain this discrepancy that three eggs do not fit in a person’s mouth by suggesting that people themselves have become smaller, because the צל״ח writes explicitly that human body size has remained unchanged since the time of Chazal). \n\n\tThe ביאור הלכה concludes that since there is a dispute regarding how to calculate today’s *shiurim* and we are not fully certain who to follow, one should be stringent for **biblical** ***mitzvos*** to follow the larger measurement of the **צל״ח**, while for **rabbinic** ***mitzvos***, the smaller measurement may be used. For example, when fulfilling the biblical *mitzvah* of eating *matzah* on Pesach night or of eating a *kezayis* of bread on the first night of Sukkos in the *sukkah*, the larger measurement of the צל״ח should be used to calculate the size of the *kezayis,* and it should be the size of today’s *beitzah* (since, according to the S”A, a *kezayis* is half the size of a *beitzah,* and our eggs are half the size of what they used to be). Similarly, when reciting *kiddush* on Friday night (which is, at least on its most basic level, a biblical mitzvah),[31] the size of a *revi’is* should be calculated based on the bigger measurement of the צל״ח. However, the eating of the *afikomen,* which is rabbinic, may be calculated using the smaller measurement of half a *beitzah* of the eggs today. The **משנ״ב** (תפו- ק״א) in *hilchos Pesach* quotes this *pssak* from the ביאור הלכה.\n\n\tWhat emerges from this ruling of the ביאור הלכה is that since the *mitzvah* of *hafrashas challah* today is rabbinic, the amount of flour needed should be calculated based on the volume of 43.2 of today’s eggs. On the other hand, one might argue that since the *mitzvah* of *hafrashas challah* (at least in *Eretz Yisrael*) is an especially stringent rabbinic commandment (as we learned above), and potentially biblical, perhaps the larger *shiur* should be used (similar to the *revi’is* for *kiddush*). \n\nIn the next subsection we will answer two fundamental questions: 1) Regarding the amount of flour needed for *hafrashas challah, lema’ase,* do we follow the צל״ח or רב חיים נאה? 2) Why is it that today everyone uses weight to calculate the required amount of flour when until now, we have only discussed measurement by volume? In the next subsection, we will answer these questions. \n\nSource Notes\n\n[31]:  Although we *passkin* that the obligation to recite *kiddush* on a cup of wine is rabbinic, it seems that the ביאור הלכה is claiming that since the biblical *mitzvah* is being fulfilled when using a cup, the *shiur* of the צל״ח should be used. There is a lengthy debate if one fulfills the biblical obligation of *kiddush* by reciting the *Amidah* in *shul,* and this seems to be a proof that the משנ״ב felt that you do not fulfill your biblical obligation of *kiddush* during *davening* in shul on Shabbos night. ","content_document":{"version":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":154,"xmlIndex":161,"text":"VI. How do we passkin?","spans":[{"text":"VI. How do we ","underline":true},{"text":"passkin","italic":true,"underline":true},{"text":"?","underline":true}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":155,"xmlIndex":162,"text":"\tThe ביאור הלכה (סימן רעא ד״ה יש רביעית) (47) quotes the צל״ח’s question regarding the large discrepancy between the thumb-based and egg-based measurements and writes that although the צל\"ח asked “a great and difficult question,” there is an equally strong question on his answer from the גמרא יומא (פ.). The Gemara there writes that two cheekfuls of an average-sized man is slightly more than a revi’is, and a revi’is is about the size of one and a half eggs. Accordingly, today, when the eggs are half the size they used to be, a revi’is would be the size of three of our eggs. The חפץ חיים writes that he asked several average-sized individuals if they could fit three eggs in their mouth, and the most any of them were able to fit was two. (Furthermore, we cannot explain this discrepancy that three eggs do not fit in a person’s mouth by suggesting that people themselves have become smaller, because the צל״ח writes explicitly that human body size has remained unchanged since the time of Chazal). ","spans":[{"text":"\tThe "},{"text":"ביאור הלכה","bold":true},{"text":" (סימן רעא ד״ה יש רביעית) (47) quotes the צל״ח’s question regarding the large discrepancy between the thumb-based and egg-based measurements and writes that although the צל\"ח asked “a great and difficult question,” there is an equally strong question on his answer from the גמרא יומא (פ.). The Gemara there writes that two cheekfuls of an average-sized man is slightly more than a "},{"text":"revi’is, ","italic":true},{"text":"and a "},{"text":"revi’is ","italic":true},{"text":"is about the size of one and a half eggs. Accordingly, today, when the eggs are half the size they used to be, a "},{"text":"revi’is ","italic":true},{"text":"would be the size of three of our eggs. The חפץ חיים writes that he asked several average-sized individuals if they could fit three eggs in their mouth, and the most any of them were able to fit was two. (Furthermore, we cannot explain this discrepancy that three eggs do not fit in a person’s mouth by suggesting that people themselves have become smaller, because the צל״ח writes explicitly that human body size has remained unchanged since the time of Chazal). "}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":1,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":156,"xmlIndex":163,"text":"\tThe ביאור הלכה concludes that since there is a dispute regarding how to calculate today’s shiurim and we are not fully certain who to follow, one should be stringent for biblical mitzvos to follow the larger measurement of the צל״ח, while for rabbinic mitzvos, the smaller measurement may be used. For example, when fulfilling the biblical mitzvah of eating matzah on Pesach night or of eating a kezayis of bread on the first night of Sukkos in the sukkah, the larger measurement of the צל״ח should be used to calculate the size of the kezayis, and it should be the size of today’s beitzah (since, according to the S”A, a kezayis is half the size of a beitzah, and our eggs are half the size of what they used to be). Similarly, when reciting kiddush on Friday night (which is, at least on its most basic level, a biblical mitzvah),[31] the size of a revi’is should be calculated based on the bigger measurement of the צל״ח. However, the eating of the afikomen, which is rabbinic, may be calculated using the smaller measurement of half a beitzah of the eggs today. The משנ״ב (תפו- ק״א) in hilchos Pesach quotes this pssak from the ביאור הלכה.","spans":[{"text":"\tThe ביאור הלכה concludes that since there is a dispute regarding how to calculate today’s "},{"text":"shiurim","italic":true},{"text":" and we are not fully certain who to follow, one should be stringent for"},{"text":" biblical ","bold":true},{"text":"mitzvos","bold":true,"italic":true},{"text":" to follow the larger measurement of the "},{"text":"צל״ח","bold":true},{"text":", while for "},{"text":"rabbinic ","bold":true},{"text":"mitzvos","bold":true,"italic":true},{"text":", the smaller measurement may be used. For example, when fulfilling the biblical "},{"text":"mitzvah ","italic":true},{"text":"of eating "},{"text":"matzah","italic":true},{"text":" on Pesach night or of eating a "},{"text":"kezayis ","italic":true},{"text":"of bread on the first night of Sukkos in the "},{"text":"sukkah","italic":true},{"text":", the larger measurement of the צל״ח should be used to calculate the size of the "},{"text":"kezayis, ","italic":true},{"text":"and it should be the size of today’s "},{"text":"beitzah ","italic":true},{"text":"(since, according to the S”A, a "},{"text":"kezayis ","italic":true},{"text":"is half the size of a "},{"text":"beitzah, ","italic":true},{"text":"and our eggs"},{"text":" ","italic":true},{"text":"are half the size of what they used to be). Similarly, when reciting "},{"text":"kiddush ","italic":true},{"text":"on Friday night (which is, at least on its most basic level, a biblical mitzvah),"},{"text":"[31]","footnote":31},{"text":" the size of a "},{"text":"revi’is ","italic":true},{"text":"should be calculated based on the bigger measurement of the צל״ח. However, the eating of the "},{"text":"afikomen, ","italic":true},{"text":"which is rabbinic, may be calculated using the smaller measurement of half a "},{"text":"beitzah ","italic":true},{"text":"of the eggs today. The "},{"text":"משנ״ב ","bold":true},{"text":"(תפו- ק״א)"},{"text":" ","bold":true},{"text":"in "},{"text":"hilchos Pesach ","italic":true},{"text":"quotes this "},{"text":"pssak ","italic":true},{"text":"from the ביאור הלכה."}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":1,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[31],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":157,"xmlIndex":164,"text":"\tWhat emerges from this ruling of the ביאור הלכה is that since the mitzvah of hafrashas challah today is rabbinic, the amount of flour needed should be calculated based on the volume of 43.2 of today’s eggs. On the other hand, one might argue that since the mitzvah of hafrashas challah (at least in Eretz Yisrael) is an especially stringent rabbinic commandment (as we learned above), and potentially biblical, perhaps the larger shiur should be used (similar to the revi’is for kiddush). ","spans":[{"text":"\tWhat emerges from this ruling of the ביאור הלכה is that since the "},{"text":"mitzvah ","italic":true},{"text":"of "},{"text":"hafrashas challah ","italic":true},{"text":"today is rabbinic, the amount of flour needed should be calculated based on the volume of 43.2 of today’s eggs. On the other hand, one might argue that since the "},{"text":"mitzvah ","italic":true},{"text":"of "},{"text":"hafrashas challah ","italic":true},{"text":"(at least in "},{"text":"Eretz Yisrael","italic":true},{"text":") is an especially stringent rabbinic commandment (as we learned above), and potentially biblical, perhaps the larger "},{"text":"shiur ","italic":true},{"text":"should be used (similar to the "},{"text":"revi’is ","italic":true},{"text":"for "},{"text":"kiddush","italic":true},{"text":"). "}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":1,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":158,"xmlIndex":165,"text":"In the next subsection we will answer two fundamental questions: 1) Regarding the amount of flour needed for hafrashas challah, lema’ase, do we follow the צל״ח or רב חיים נאה? 2) Why is it that today everyone uses weight to calculate the required amount of flour when until now, we have only discussed measurement by volume? In the next subsection, we will answer these questions. ","spans":[{"text":"In the next subsection we will answer two fundamental questions: 1) Regarding the amount of flour needed for "},{"text":"hafrashas challah, lema’ase, ","italic":true},{"text":"do we"},{"text":" ","italic":true},{"text":"follow the צל״ח or רב חיים נאה? 2) Why is it that today everyone uses weight to calculate the required amount of flour when until now, we have only discussed measurement by volume? In the next subsection, we will answer these questions. "}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":36.0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}],"footnotes":[{"id":31,"blocks":[{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":0,"xmlIndex":0,"text":" Although we passkin that the obligation to recite kiddush on a cup of wine is rabbinic, it seems that the ביאור הלכה is claiming that since the biblical mitzvah is being fulfilled when using a cup, the shiur of the צל״ח should be used. There is a lengthy debate if one fulfills the biblical obligation of kiddush by reciting the Amidah in shul, and this seems to be a proof that the משנ״ב felt that you do not fulfill your biblical obligation of kiddush during davening in shul on Shabbos night. ","spans":[{"text":" Although we "},{"text":"passkin ","italic":true},{"text":"that the obligation to recite "},{"text":"kiddush ","italic":true},{"text":"on a cup of wine is rabbinic, it seems that the ביאור הלכה is claiming that since the biblical "},{"text":"mitzvah ","italic":true},{"text":"is being fulfilled when using a cup, the "},{"text":"shiur ","italic":true},{"text":"of the צל״ח should be used. There is a lengthy debate if one fulfills the biblical obligation of "},{"text":"kiddush ","italic":true},{"text":"by reciting the "},{"text":"Amidah","italic":true},{"text":" in "},{"text":"shul, ","italic":true},{"text":"and this seems to be a proof that the משנ״ב felt that you do not fulfill your biblical obligation of "},{"text":"kiddush ","italic":true},{"text":"during "},{"text":"davening ","italic":true},{"text":"in shul on Shabbos night. "}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}]}]},"sort_order":39,"official_shiur_number":4,"estimated_minutes":7,"difficulty":"core","tags":["challah-part-1","original-difficulty:core","source:47"],"source_file_name":"Notes for Hafrashas Challah (part 1).docx","source_start_page":null,"source_end_page":null,"is_selectable":true},{"program_id":"winter-5787","chunk_code":"HC1-F8","source_type":"notes","siman":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","workbook_title":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","section_key":"F","section_title":"Amount of Dough Needed to be Mafrish","chunk_title":"Why We Measure Flour by Weight and Lema’aseh Amounts","chunk_summary":"Converts volume to weight; 1.2 kg, 1.56 kg, 1.66 kg, 2.25 kg; only flour weight counts; density varies.","content_markdown":"<u>VII. Why do we measure</u> *<u>challah</u>* <u>in weight and how do we</u> *<u>passkin lema’ase</u>*<u>?</u>\n\nIt is clear from the Gemara and *Rishonim* that the amount of flour required for *hafrashas* *challah* (either 43.2 eggs or 311 cubic thumb-widths) is calculated in volume. Nevertheless, we find that the רמב״ם already converts this volume into a weight, and the S”A (43) quotes him as writing that the volume of 43.2 eggs is equivalent to the weight of 520 Egyptian dirhams (silver coins). The **חזון איש** (או״ח יב-ח) explains that it can only be converted into weight if that is established by the *Chachmei Yisrael* and becomes the accepted custom among עם ישראל (which is most likely based on the *pssak* of *Chachmei Yisrael* of the previous generation)*.* The reason the measurement was converted to weight is because it is easier to calculate weight at home than volume. This is done by the *Chachamim* of each generation filling a container with a volume of 43.2 eggs with flour, weighing it, and using that weight as the standard for *hafrashas challah*.\n\n\tTo better understand how the volume is converted into weight, a brief introduction is required. The ratio between volume and weight depends on the density of the substance being measured. For water, the ratio of volume to weight is equal, such that one liter of water weighs 1 kg.[32] However, one liter of water weighs more than one liter of feathers because feathers are much less dense. Similarly, flour also weighs much less than water, with one liter of flour weighing about 0.5 kg. However, this measurement applies only when the flour is loosely poured into the container. If the flour is packed in tightly, more flour can fit in the same container with the same volume. Here the *posskim* write that tightly packed flour weighs approximately two-thirds as much as an equal volume of water. Thus, 1 liter of tightly packed flour weighs approximately .67 kg rather than .5 kg. This distinction is significant because there is a dispute in the *Rishonim* whether the volume of 43.2 eggs necessary for *hafrashas challah* is calculated based on packed flour or loosely poured flour. This obviously affects the amount of flour, and therefore the weight, needed in practice for *hafrashas challah*.\n\n\tBased on the above, the **לבוש מרדכי** (פ״ח-ה) (48) summarizes the calculations of רב חיים נאה and the חזון איש. **רב חיים נאה** calculated the volume of an egg today as being 57.6 cc. Thus, the *shiur* needed for *challah* is 2488 cc (57.6 x 43.2), or **2.48 liters**. If flour weighs approximately half of its volume, the corresponding weight required for *hafrashas challah* would be 1.24 kg, which he rounds up to 1.25 kg. The **שמירת שבת כהלכתה** (פמ״ב-ט) writes that one should estimate even lower and be *mafrish* from flour weighing <u>1.2 kg</u>. (It seems that the שש״כ is concerned that the ratio between the volume and weight of flour is not half, but 48% of the weight of water, and therefore he multiplies .48 x 2488, yielding 1.2). However, רב חיים נאה writes that one should not recite a *bracha* on this amount because according to some *Rishonim,* one should measure the volume with densely packed flour, which yields a much higher weight. Thus, the weight of 2488 cc of packed flour is two-thirds of that, which is 1658 cc, or 1.66 kg. Therefore, רב חיים נאה *passkins* that one should be *mafrish* without a *bracha* when using 1.25 kg of flour and recite a *bracha* when using at least 1.66 kg, at which point all opinions would require a *bracha*. \n\n\tIn contrast, the **חזון איש** assumes that an average egg today measures approximately 50 cc, but eggs are half the size that they were in the time of Chazal, when they measured 100 cc. Therefore, the volume required for *challah* is 4320 cc (43.2 eggs x 100 cc), or 4.32 liters. The חזון איש does not take into consideration the opinion that requires densely packed flour,[33] and he calculates the weight of flour as 52% of its volume, rather than 50%. Thus, the required weight needed to separate *challah* with a *bracha* is **2.25 kg** (.52 x 4.32 = 2.25 kg). However, even the חזון איש agrees that when one has 1.2 kg of flour, he should be *mafrish* without a *bracha,* in consideration of the opinion of רב חיים נאה (according to the understanding of the שש״כ brought above).\n\n\tThere is also a third opinion that must be taken into consideration.[34] We learned above that the S”A gives the measurement of 43.2 eggs and then adds that the רמב״ם converted this volume into the weight of 520 Egyptian dirhams. **רב חיים נאה** in שיעורי תורה (עמ׳ 16) writes that a dirham weighs 3.206 grams, which means that 520 dirhams equals exactly 1.6667 kg (3.206 x 520) – the same weight as his calculation for the volume of 43.2 eggs. Based on this, **Rav Ovadya Yosef** (49) in הליכות עולם (ח״א-עמ׳ רפח) initially writes that one should be *mafrish* with a *bracha* from 1.66 kg of dough. However, he then cites a *talmid chacham* who traveled to Egypt to examine historical dirhams from various periods, and he found that none ever weighed more than 3 grams. Therefore, Rav Ovadya concludes that one should be *mafrish* with a *bracha* from a batch of dough that weighs 1.56 kg (3 x 520). \n\nIt is clear that *Sephardim* who follow Rav Ovadya should be *mafrish* with a *bracha* when using 1.56 kg, but how do *Ashkenazim passkin*? Do they *passkin* like רב חיים נאה or the חזון איש? The custom in Yerushalayim was to always *passkin* like רב חיים נאה (even for biblical commandments). Therefore, one should be *mafrish* with a *bracha* on **1.66 kg** of flour. This is also the *pssak* of the **שש״כ** (מב-ט), **RSZA** in הליכות שלמה (פסח פ״ז-הע׳ 78), **Rav Wosner** in שבט הלוי (חי״א רנג-ג) and the *minhag* in Yerushalayim. Nevertheless, the ***Steipler*** and **Rav Chaim Kanievsky** are *machmir* like the חזון איש that a *bracha* is recited from only from **2.25 kg**. This seems to be the accepted custom in the US*,* where a *bracha* is generally recited only when using 5 pounds (which is approximately equivalent to 2.25 kg) or more. However, it should be emphasized again that according to most opinions, one should be *mafrish challah* without a *bracha* when using 1.2 kg of flour. \n\nTo summarize:\n\n- **1.2 kg (2.65 lbs)** – Separate without a *bracha* according to most opinions\n\n- **1.56 kg (3.44 lbs)** – **Rav Ovadya**: Separate with a *bracha*\n\n- **1.66 kg (3.66 lbs)** – **רב חיים נאה**: Separate with a *bracha*\n\n- **2.25 kg (4.96 lbs) – חזון איש**: Separate with a *bracha*\n\nThere are two last points that are important to stress: 1) The **חתם סופר** (או״ח קכז ד״ה או) and many others *passkin* that the above weight refers only to the flour itself, and not to the total weight after adding water, sugar and eggs. 2) As we will see in the next section, the weight calculations may vary depending on the type of flour being used. \n\nSource Notes\n\n[32]:  We will use kilos when calculating weight since the metric system is the one generally used by *posskim* in this matter*.* Those living in the U.S., where pounds are used, can easily convert the measurements using the formula 1 kg = 2.2 lbs. \n\n[33]:  Rav Elyashiv in הליכות שדה (עמ׳ 134) explains that we do not need to be *machmir* for both the densely packed flour and the larger *shiur* of the חזו״א.\n\n[34]:  There are many more opinions, but in the notes, we have quoted the three primary ones. Other opinions include the בן איש חי (שמיני שנה א) and Rav Mordechai Eliyahu in התורה והארץ (ח״ה) who require *hafrasha* without a *bracha* from 1.66 kg and with a *bracha* from 2.49 kg, and Rav Tuccachinsky in ספר ארץ ישראל (חלה-ב), who writes that one should be *mafrish* from 1.05 kg without a *bracha* and with a *bracha* from 1.57 kg.","content_document":{"version":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":159,"xmlIndex":166,"text":"VII. Why do we measure challah in weight and how do we passkin lema’ase?","spans":[{"text":"VII. Why do we measure ","underline":true},{"text":"challah ","italic":true,"underline":true},{"text":"in weight and how do we ","underline":true},{"text":"passkin lema’ase","italic":true,"underline":true},{"text":"?","underline":true}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":160,"xmlIndex":167,"text":"It is clear from the Gemara and Rishonim that the amount of flour required for hafrashas challah (either 43.2 eggs or 311 cubic thumb-widths) is calculated in volume. Nevertheless, we find that the רמב״ם already converts this volume into a weight, and the S”A (43) quotes him as writing that the volume of 43.2 eggs is equivalent to the weight of 520 Egyptian dirhams (silver coins). The חזון איש (או״ח יב-ח) explains that it can only be converted into weight if that is established by the Chachmei Yisrael and becomes the accepted custom among עם ישראל (which is most likely based on the pssak of Chachmei Yisrael of the previous generation). The reason the measurement was converted to weight is because it is easier to calculate weight at home than volume. This is done by the Chachamim of each generation filling a container with a volume of 43.2 eggs with flour, weighing it, and using that weight as the standard for hafrashas challah.","spans":[{"text":"It is clear from the Gemara and "},{"text":"Rishonim ","italic":true},{"text":"that the amount of flour required for "},{"text":"hafrashas","italic":true},{"text":" "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"(either 43.2 eggs or 311 cubic thumb-widths) is calculated in volume. Nevertheless, we find that the רמב״ם already converts this volume into a weight, and the S”A (43) quotes him as writing that the volume of 43.2 eggs is equivalent to the weight of 520 Egyptian dirhams (silver coins). The "},{"text":"חזון איש ","bold":true},{"text":"(או״ח יב-ח) explains that it can only be converted into weight if that is established by the "},{"text":"Chachmei Yisrael ","italic":true},{"text":"and becomes the accepted custom among עם ישראל (which is most likely based on the "},{"text":"pssak ","italic":true},{"text":"of "},{"text":"Chachmei Yisrael ","italic":true},{"text":"of the previous generation)"},{"text":". ","italic":true},{"text":"The reason the measurement was converted to weight is because it is easier to calculate weight at home than volume. This is done by the "},{"text":"Chachamim ","italic":true},{"text":"of each generation filling a"},{"text":" ","italic":true},{"text":"container with a volume of 43.2 eggs with flour, weighing it, and using that weight as the standard for "},{"text":"hafrashas challah","italic":true},{"text":"."}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":36.0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":161,"xmlIndex":168,"text":"\tTo better understand how the volume is converted into weight, a brief introduction is required. The ratio between volume and weight depends on the density of the substance being measured. For water, the ratio of volume to weight is equal, such that one liter of water weighs 1 kg.[32] However, one liter of water weighs more than one liter of feathers because feathers are much less dense. Similarly, flour also weighs much less than water, with one liter of flour weighing about 0.5 kg. However, this measurement applies only when the flour is loosely poured into the container. If the flour is packed in tightly, more flour can fit in the same container with the same volume. Here the posskim write that tightly packed flour weighs approximately two-thirds as much as an equal volume of water. Thus, 1 liter of tightly packed flour weighs approximately .67 kg rather than .5 kg. This distinction is significant because there is a dispute in the Rishonim whether the volume of 43.2 eggs necessary for hafrashas challah is calculated based on packed flour or loosely poured flour. This obviously affects the amount of flour, and therefore the weight, needed in practice for hafrashas challah.","spans":[{"text":"\tTo better understand how the volume is converted into weight, a brief introduction is required. The ratio between volume and weight depends on the density of the substance being measured. For water, the ratio of volume to weight is equal, such that one liter of water weighs 1 kg."},{"text":"[32]","footnote":32},{"text":" However, one liter of water weighs more than one liter of feathers because feathers are much less dense. Similarly, flour also weighs much less than water, with one liter of flour weighing about 0.5 kg. However, this measurement applies only when the flour is loosely poured into the container. If the flour is packed in tightly, more flour can fit in the same container with the same volume. Here the "},{"text":"posskim ","italic":true},{"text":"write that tightly packed flour weighs approximately two-thirds as much as an equal volume of water. Thus, 1 liter of tightly packed flour weighs approximately .67 kg rather than .5 kg. This distinction is significant because there is a dispute in the "},{"text":"Rishonim ","italic":true},{"text":"whether the volume of 43.2 eggs necessary for "},{"text":"hafrashas challah ","italic":true},{"text":"is calculated based on packed flour or loosely poured flour. This obviously affects the amount of flour, and therefore the weight, needed in practice for "},{"text":"hafrashas challah","italic":true},{"text":"."}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":1,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[32],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":162,"xmlIndex":169,"text":"\tBased on the above, the לבוש מרדכי (פ״ח-ה) (48) summarizes the calculations of רב חיים נאה and the חזון איש. רב חיים נאה calculated the volume of an egg today as being 57.6 cc. Thus, the shiur needed for challah is 2488 cc (57.6 x 43.2), or 2.48 liters. If flour weighs approximately half of its volume, the corresponding weight required for hafrashas challah would be 1.24 kg, which he rounds up to 1.25 kg. The שמירת שבת כהלכתה (פמ״ב-ט) writes that one should estimate even lower and be mafrish from flour weighing 1.2 kg. (It seems that the שש״כ is concerned that the ratio between the volume and weight of flour is not half, but 48% of the weight of water, and therefore he multiplies .48 x 2488, yielding 1.2). However, רב חיים נאה writes that one should not recite a bracha on this amount because according to some Rishonim, one should measure the volume with densely packed flour, which yields a much higher weight. Thus, the weight of 2488 cc of packed flour is two-thirds of that, which is 1658 cc, or 1.66 kg. Therefore, רב חיים נאה passkins that one should be mafrish without a bracha when using 1.25 kg of flour and recite a bracha when using at least 1.66 kg, at which point all opinions would require a bracha. ","spans":[{"text":"\tBased on the above, the "},{"text":"לבוש מרדכי ","bold":true},{"text":"(פ״ח-ה) (48) summarizes the calculations of רב חיים נאה and the חזון איש. "},{"text":"רב חיים נאה","bold":true},{"text":" calculated the volume of an egg today as being 57.6 cc. Thus, the "},{"text":"shiur","italic":true},{"text":" needed for "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"is 2488 cc (57.6 x 43.2), or "},{"text":"2.48 liters","bold":true},{"text":". If flour weighs approximately half of its volume, the corresponding weight required for "},{"text":"hafrashas challah ","italic":true},{"text":"would be 1.24 kg, which he rounds up to 1.25 kg. The "},{"text":"שמירת שבת כהלכתה ","bold":true},{"text":"(פמ״ב-ט) writes that one should estimate even lower and be "},{"text":"mafrish ","italic":true},{"text":"from flour weighing "},{"text":"1.2 kg","underline":true},{"text":". (It seems that the שש״כ is concerned that the ratio between the volume and weight of flour is not half, but 48% of the weight of water, and therefore he multiplies .48 x 2488, yielding 1.2). However, רב חיים נאה writes that one should not recite a "},{"text":"bracha ","italic":true},{"text":"on this amount because according to some "},{"text":"Rishonim, ","italic":true},{"text":"one should measure the volume with densely packed flour, which yields a much higher weight. Thus, the weight of 2488 cc of packed flour is two-thirds of that, which is 1658 cc, or 1.66 kg. Therefore, רב חיים נאה "},{"text":"passkins","italic":true},{"text":" that one should be "},{"text":"mafrish ","italic":true},{"text":"without a "},{"text":"bracha ","italic":true},{"text":"when using 1.25 kg of flour"},{"text":" ","italic":true},{"text":"and recite a "},{"text":"bracha ","italic":true},{"text":"when using at least 1.66 kg, at which point all opinions would require a "},{"text":"bracha","italic":true},{"text":". "}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":1,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":163,"xmlIndex":170,"text":"\tIn contrast, the חזון איש assumes that an average egg today measures approximately 50 cc, but eggs are half the size that they were in the time of Chazal, when they measured 100 cc. Therefore, the volume required for challah is 4320 cc (43.2 eggs x 100 cc), or 4.32 liters. The חזון איש does not take into consideration the opinion that requires densely packed flour,[33] and he calculates the weight of flour as 52% of its volume, rather than 50%. Thus, the required weight needed to separate challah with a bracha is 2.25 kg (.52 x 4.32 = 2.25 kg). However, even the חזון איש agrees that when one has 1.2 kg of flour, he should be mafrish without a bracha, in consideration of the opinion of רב חיים נאה (according to the understanding of the שש״כ brought above).","spans":[{"text":"\tIn contrast, the "},{"text":"חזון איש","bold":true},{"text":" assumes that an average egg today measures approximately 50 cc, but eggs are half the size that they were in the time of Chazal, when they measured 100 cc. Therefore, the volume required for "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"is 4320 cc (43.2 eggs x 100 cc), or 4.32 liters. The חזון איש does not take into consideration the opinion that requires densely packed flour,"},{"text":"[33]","footnote":33},{"text":" and he calculates the weight of flour as 52% of its volume, rather than 50%. Thus, the required weight needed to separate "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"with a "},{"text":"bracha ","italic":true},{"text":"is "},{"text":"2.25 kg ","bold":true},{"text":"(.52 x 4.32 = 2.25 kg). However, even the חזון איש agrees that when one has 1.2 kg of flour, he should be "},{"text":"mafrish ","italic":true},{"text":"without a "},{"text":"bracha, ","italic":true},{"text":"in consideration of the opinion of רב חיים נאה (according to the understanding of the שש״כ brought above)."}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":1,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[33],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":164,"xmlIndex":171,"text":"\tThere is also a third opinion that must be taken into consideration.[34] We learned above that the S”A gives the measurement of 43.2 eggs and then adds that the רמב״ם converted this volume into the weight of 520 Egyptian dirhams. רב חיים נאה in שיעורי תורה (עמ׳ 16) writes that a dirham weighs 3.206 grams, which means that 520 dirhams equals exactly 1.6667 kg (3.206 x 520) – the same weight as his calculation for the volume of 43.2 eggs. Based on this, Rav Ovadya Yosef (49) in הליכות עולם (ח״א-עמ׳ רפח) initially writes that one should be mafrish with a bracha from 1.66 kg of dough. However, he then cites a talmid chacham who traveled to Egypt to examine historical dirhams from various periods, and he found that none ever weighed more than 3 grams. Therefore, Rav Ovadya concludes that one should be mafrish with a bracha from a batch of dough that weighs 1.56 kg (3 x 520). ","spans":[{"text":"\tThere is also a third opinion that must be taken into consideration."},{"text":"[34]","footnote":34},{"text":" We learned above that the S”A gives the measurement of 43.2 eggs and then adds that the רמב״ם converted this volume into the weight of 520 Egyptian dirhams. "},{"text":"רב חיים נאה","bold":true},{"text":" in שיעורי תורה (עמ׳ 16) writes that a dirham weighs 3.206 grams, which means that 520 dirhams equals exactly 1.6667 kg (3.206 x 520) – the same weight as his calculation for the volume of 43.2 eggs. Based on this, "},{"text":"Rav Ovadya Yosef","bold":true},{"text":" (49) in הליכות עולם (ח״א-עמ׳ רפח) initially writes that one should be "},{"text":"mafrish","italic":true},{"text":" with a "},{"text":"bracha ","italic":true},{"text":"from 1.66 kg of dough. However, he then cites a "},{"text":"talmid chacham","italic":true},{"text":" who traveled to Egypt to examine historical dirhams from various periods, and he found that none ever weighed more than 3 grams. Therefore, Rav Ovadya concludes that one should be "},{"text":"mafrish ","italic":true},{"text":"with a "},{"text":"bracha ","italic":true},{"text":"from a batch of dough that weighs 1.56 kg (3 x 520). "}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":1,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[34],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":165,"xmlIndex":172,"text":"It is clear that Sephardim who follow Rav Ovadya should be mafrish with a bracha when using 1.56 kg, but how do Ashkenazim passkin? Do they passkin like רב חיים נאה or the חזון איש? The custom in Yerushalayim was to always passkin like רב חיים נאה (even for biblical commandments). Therefore, one should be mafrish with a bracha on 1.66 kg of flour. This is also the pssak of the שש״כ (מב-ט), RSZA in הליכות שלמה (פסח פ״ז-הע׳ 78), Rav Wosner in שבט הלוי (חי״א רנג-ג) and the minhag in Yerushalayim. Nevertheless, the Steipler and Rav Chaim Kanievsky are machmir like the חזון איש that a bracha is recited from only from 2.25 kg. This seems to be the accepted custom in the US, where a bracha is generally recited only when using 5 pounds (which is approximately equivalent to 2.25 kg) or more. However, it should be emphasized again that according to most opinions, one should be mafrish challah without a bracha when using 1.2 kg of flour. ","spans":[{"text":"It is clear that "},{"text":"Sephardim ","italic":true},{"text":"who follow Rav Ovadya should be "},{"text":"mafrish ","italic":true},{"text":"with a "},{"text":"bracha ","italic":true},{"text":"when using 1.56 kg, but how do "},{"text":"Ashkenazim passkin","italic":true},{"text":"?"},{"text":" ","italic":true},{"text":"Do they "},{"text":"passkin ","italic":true},{"text":"like רב חיים נאה or the חזון איש? The custom in Yerushalayim was to always "},{"text":"passkin ","italic":true},{"text":"like רב חיים נאה (even for biblical commandments). Therefore, one should be "},{"text":"mafrish ","italic":true},{"text":"with a "},{"text":"bracha ","italic":true},{"text":"on "},{"text":"1.66 kg","bold":true},{"text":" of flour. This is also the "},{"text":"pssak ","italic":true},{"text":"of the "},{"text":"שש״כ","bold":true},{"text":" (מב-ט), "},{"text":"RSZA","bold":true},{"text":" in הליכות שלמה (פסח פ״ז-הע׳ 78), "},{"text":"Rav Wosner ","bold":true},{"text":"in שבט הלוי (חי״א רנג-ג) and the "},{"text":"minhag ","italic":true},{"text":"in Yerushalayim. Nevertheless, the "},{"text":"Steipler","bold":true,"italic":true},{"text":" ","italic":true},{"text":"and "},{"text":"Rav Chaim Kanievsky","bold":true},{"text":" are "},{"text":"machmir ","italic":true},{"text":"like the חזון איש that a "},{"text":"bracha ","italic":true},{"text":"is recited from only from "},{"text":"2.25 kg","bold":true},{"text":". This seems to be the accepted custom in the US"},{"text":", ","italic":true},{"text":"where a "},{"text":"bracha ","italic":true},{"text":"is generally recited only when using 5 pounds (which is approximately equivalent to 2.25 kg) or more. However, it should be emphasized again that according to most opinions, "},{"text":"one","bold":false},{"text":" should be "},{"text":"mafrish challah","italic":true},{"text":" without a "},{"text":"bracha","italic":true},{"text":" when using 1.2 kg of flour. "}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":36.0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":166,"xmlIndex":173,"text":"To summarize:","spans":[{"text":"To summarize:"}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"list","paragraph":167,"xmlIndex":174,"text":"1.2 kg (2.65 lbs) – Separate without a bracha according to most opinions","spans":[{"text":"1.2 kg (2.65 lbs) ","bold":true},{"text":"–"},{"text":" ","bold":true},{"text":"Separate without a "},{"text":"bracha ","italic":true},{"text":"according to most opinions"}],"marker":"•","level":0,"indent":72.0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0,"hanging":18.0},{"kind":"list","paragraph":168,"xmlIndex":175,"text":"1.56 kg (3.44 lbs) – Rav Ovadya: Separate with a bracha ","spans":[{"text":"1.56 kg (3.44 lbs)","bold":true},{"text":" – "},{"text":"Rav Ovadya","bold":true},{"text":": Separate with a "},{"text":"bracha ","italic":true}],"marker":"•","level":0,"indent":72.0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0,"hanging":18.0},{"kind":"list","paragraph":169,"xmlIndex":176,"text":"1.66 kg (3.66 lbs) – רב חיים נאה: Separate with a bracha","spans":[{"text":"1.66 kg (3.66 lbs) ","bold":true},{"text":"– "},{"text":"רב חיים נאה","bold":true},{"text":": Separate with a "},{"text":"bracha","italic":true}],"marker":"•","level":0,"indent":72.0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0,"hanging":18.0},{"kind":"list","paragraph":170,"xmlIndex":177,"text":"2.25 kg (4.96 lbs) – חזון איש: Separate with a bracha","spans":[{"text":"2.25 kg (4.96 lbs) – חזון איש","bold":true},{"text":": Separate with a "},{"text":"bracha","italic":true}],"marker":"•","level":0,"indent":72.0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0,"hanging":18.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":171,"xmlIndex":178,"text":"There are two last points that are important to stress: 1) The חתם סופר (או״ח קכז ד״ה או) and many others passkin that the above weight refers only to the flour itself, and not to the total weight after adding water, sugar and eggs. 2) As we will see in the next section, the weight calculations may vary depending on the type of flour being used. ","spans":[{"text":"There are two last points that are important to stress: 1) The "},{"text":"חתם סופר","bold":true},{"text":" (או״ח קכז ד״ה או) and many others "},{"text":"passkin ","italic":true},{"text":"that the above weight refers only to the flour itself, and not to the total weight after adding water, sugar and eggs. 2) As we will see in the next section, the weight calculations may vary depending on the type of flour being used. "}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":36.0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}],"footnotes":[{"id":32,"blocks":[{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":0,"xmlIndex":0,"text":" We will use kilos when calculating weight since the metric system is the one generally used by posskim in this matter. Those living in the U.S., where pounds are used, can easily convert the measurements using the formula 1 kg = 2.2 lbs. ","spans":[{"text":" We will use kilos when calculating weight since the metric system is the one generally used by "},{"text":"posskim ","italic":true},{"text":"in this matter"},{"text":". ","italic":true},{"text":"Those living in the U.S., where pounds are used, can easily convert the measurements using the formula 1 kg = 2.2 lbs. "}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}]},{"id":33,"blocks":[{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":0,"xmlIndex":0,"text":" Rav Elyashiv in הליכות שדה (עמ׳ 134) explains that we do not need to be machmir for both the densely packed flour and the larger shiur of the חזו״א.","spans":[{"text":" Rav Elyashiv in הליכות שדה (עמ׳ 134) explains that we do not need to be "},{"text":"machmir ","italic":true},{"text":"for both the densely packed flour and the larger "},{"text":"shiur ","italic":true},{"text":"of the חזו״א."}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}]},{"id":34,"blocks":[{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":0,"xmlIndex":0,"text":" There are many more opinions, but in the notes, we have quoted the three primary ones. Other opinions include the בן איש חי (שמיני שנה א) and Rav Mordechai Eliyahu in התורה והארץ (ח״ה) who require hafrasha without a bracha from 1.66 kg and with a bracha from 2.49 kg, and Rav Tuccachinsky in ספר ארץ ישראל (חלה-ב), who writes that one should be mafrish from 1.05 kg without a bracha and with a bracha from 1.57 kg.","spans":[{"text":" There are many more opinions, but in the notes, we have quoted the three primary ones. Other opinions include the בן איש חי (שמיני שנה א) and Rav Mordechai Eliyahu in התורה והארץ (ח״ה) who require "},{"text":"hafrasha ","italic":true},{"text":"without a "},{"text":"bracha ","italic":true},{"text":"from 1.66 kg and with a "},{"text":"bracha ","italic":true},{"text":"from 2.49 kg, and Rav Tuccachinsky in ספר ארץ ישראל (חלה-ב), who writes that one should be "},{"text":"mafrish ","italic":true},{"text":"from 1.05 kg without a "},{"text":"bracha ","italic":true},{"text":"and with a "},{"text":"bracha ","italic":true},{"text":"from 1.57 kg."}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}]}]},"sort_order":40,"official_shiur_number":4,"estimated_minutes":12,"difficulty":"core","tags":["challah-part-1","original-difficulty:core","source:43","source:48","source:49"],"source_file_name":"Notes for Hafrashas Challah (part 1).docx","source_start_page":null,"source_end_page":null,"is_selectable":true},{"program_id":"winter-5787","chunk_code":"HC1-G1","source_type":"notes","siman":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","workbook_title":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","section_key":"G","section_title":"Practical Applications","chunk_title":"Small Batch Then Added More Flour","chunk_summary":"If initially separated without bracha and later added to reach bracha threshold, generally separate again without bracha; Chalah K’hilchasa nuance.","content_markdown":"### G. *Practical Applications*\n\nIn this section, we will delve further into the *halachos* that pertain to the amount of flour needed and apply what we learned in the previous section. As a reminder, we learned that if you are using **1.2 kg** of flour, everyone agrees you should be *mafrish* without a *bracha.* In order to recite a *bracha,* we saw three opinions in the *Acharonim*: **1.66** according to רב חיים נאה, **1.56** according to Rav Ovadya, and **2.25** kg according to the חזון איש*.* For the sake of clarity, in the subsections below we will use the measurement of רב חיים נאה, but the same principles apply according to the measurements of Rav Ovadya and the חזון איש. \n\n<u>Made a small batch of dough and added more</u>\n\n\tIf you make a batch of dough using 1.3 kg of flour (an amount that most agree requires *hafrasha* without a *bracha*) and are *mafrish* without a *bracha,* you should be careful not to add enough flour afterward to bring the total amount to 1.66 kg or more – the amount that requires a *bracha* – since this creates a *safek* about whether you need to be *mafrish* again. Let’s explain. If 1.2 kg is really the amount needed for *hafrashas challah,* then there is no problem, because the batch of dough was already obligated and was exempted by your initial *hafrasha,* and the additional flour that was added later (which is less than 1.2 kg of flour) is not enough to require a new *hafrasha.* However, if 1.66 kg is the minimum amount required for *hafrasha*, your original *hafrasha* on 1.3 kg was meaningless. Once  you add enough flour to exceed the 1.66 kg threshold, the dough becomes obligated in *hafrasha* for the first time, and it should ideally be done with a *bracha.* In other words, if you ask the *posskim/Rishonim* who require *hafrasha* on 1.2 kg,[35] they would tell you that you do not need to be *mafrish* at all, but if you ask the *posskim/Rishonim* who hold that you need 1.66 kg,[36] they would tell you that you now have to be *mafrish* with a *bracha.* \n\nIn essence, you are stuck. On the one hand, you cannot recite a *bracha* because perhaps this batch was already obligated and exempted by your original *hafrasha.* On the other hand, you also cannot eat the batch without being *mafrish*, since perhaps it has only now become obligated in *hafrasha* for the first time. It is therefore best to be *mafrish* again without a *bracha* (see קומץ החלה עמ׳ טז)*.*[37]\n\nHowever, the **חלה כהלכתה** (סימן ה, שיח הלכה-י) (50) *passkins* that if the first batch was 1.3 kg and you were *mafrish* without a *bracha* (as required) and then you added another 1.3 kg, you should be *mafrish* again with a *bracha* ממה נפשך (regardless of which side is correct)*.* The logic is as follows: if the real requirement is 1.66, the *hafrasha* you did earlier on 1.3 kg was meaningless, and now that you have more than 1.66 for the first time, you should be *mafrish* with a *bracha.* On the flip side, if the real amount required to be *mafrish* is 1.2 kg, now that you have another 1.3, you should be *mafrish* and recite a *bracha.* Even though you were already *mafrish*, this side would say that since another 1.3 was added now, you should be *mafrish* again and recite a *bracha*. The only reason you did not recite a *bracha* earlier is because maybe the real requirement is 1.66 kg, but now, even that side would require you to recite a *bracha*, because for the first time, there is now more than 1.66. In other words, in this case, if you ask the *posskim/Rishonim* who hold you need 1.2 kg and the *posskim/Rishonim* who hold you need 1.66 kg, both would tell you to recite a *bracha.*\n\nSource Notes\n\n[35]:  Because they use loosely packed flour, as discussed in the previous section.\n\n[36]:  Because they require densely packed flour.\n\n[37]:  The לבוש מרדכי (חלה פ״ח-ט) writes that it is best to avoid adding the 1.3 kg after you were *mafrish,* lest you forget to be *mafrish* again. However, the חלה כהלכתה (סימן ה-ד) *passkins* that you are allowed to add more flour *le’chatchilla*, but you must be *mafrish m’safek* again since you added less than 1.2 kg.","content_document":{"version":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"heading","paragraph":172,"xmlIndex":179,"text":"Practical Applications","spans":[{"text":"Practical Applications","italic":true,"color":"#2F5496"}],"marker":"G.","level":0,"indent":56.0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0,"rightIndent":43.2,"hanging":18.0,"tabStops":[{"position":150.45,"alignment":"left","leader":"none"},{"position":234.0,"alignment":"center","leader":"none"},{"position":279.0,"alignment":"left","leader":"none"}]},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":173,"xmlIndex":181,"text":"In this section, we will delve further into the halachos that pertain to the amount of flour needed and apply what we learned in the previous section. As a reminder, we learned that if you are using 1.2 kg of flour, everyone agrees you should be mafrish without a bracha. In order to recite a bracha, we saw three opinions in the Acharonim: 1.66 according to רב חיים נאה, 1.56 according to Rav Ovadya, and 2.25 kg according to the חזון איש. For the sake of clarity, in the subsections below we will use the measurement of רב חיים נאה, but the same principles apply according to the measurements of Rav Ovadya and the חזון איש. ","spans":[{"text":"In this section, we will delve further into the "},{"text":"halachos ","italic":true},{"text":"that pertain to the amount of flour needed and apply what we learned in the previous section. As a reminder, we learned that if you are using "},{"text":"1.2 kg","bold":true},{"text":" of flour, everyone agrees you should be "},{"text":"mafrish ","italic":true},{"text":"without a "},{"text":"bracha. ","italic":true},{"text":"In order to recite a "},{"text":"bracha, ","italic":true},{"text":"we saw three opinions in the "},{"text":"Acharonim","italic":true},{"text":":"},{"text":" ","italic":true},{"text":"1.66","bold":true},{"text":" according to רב חיים נאה, "},{"text":"1.56","bold":true},{"text":" according to Rav Ovadya, and "},{"text":"2.25","bold":true},{"text":" kg according to the חזון איש"},{"text":". ","italic":true},{"text":"For the sake of clarity, in the subsections below we will use the measurement of רב חיים נאה, but the same principles apply according to the measurements of Rav Ovadya and the חזון איש. "}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":36.0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":174,"xmlIndex":182,"text":"Made a small batch of dough and added more","spans":[{"text":"Made a small batch of dough and added more","underline":true}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":175,"xmlIndex":183,"text":"\tIf you make a batch of dough using 1.3 kg of flour (an amount that most agree requires hafrasha without a bracha) and are mafrish without a bracha, you should be careful not to add enough flour afterward to bring the total amount to 1.66 kg or more – the amount that requires a bracha – since this creates a safek about whether you need to be mafrish again. Let’s explain. If 1.2 kg is really the amount needed for hafrashas challah, then there is no problem, because the batch of dough was already obligated and was exempted by your initial hafrasha, and the additional flour that was added later (which is less than 1.2 kg of flour) is not enough to require a new hafrasha. However, if 1.66 kg is the minimum amount required for hafrasha, your original hafrasha on 1.3 kg was meaningless. Once  you add enough flour to exceed the 1.66 kg threshold, the dough becomes obligated in hafrasha for the first time, and it should ideally be done with a bracha. In other words, if you ask the posskim/Rishonim who require hafrasha on 1.2 kg,[35] they would tell you that you do not need to be mafrish at all, but if you ask the posskim/Rishonim who hold that you need 1.66 kg,[36] they would tell you that you now have to be mafrish with a bracha. ","spans":[{"text":"\tIf you make a batch of dough using 1.3 kg of flour (an amount that most agree requires "},{"text":"hafrasha ","italic":true},{"text":"without a "},{"text":"bracha","italic":true},{"text":") and are "},{"text":"mafrish ","italic":true},{"text":"without a "},{"text":"bracha, ","italic":true},{"text":"you should be careful not to add enough flour afterward to bring the total amount to 1.66 kg or more – the amount that requires a "},{"text":"bracha ","italic":true},{"text":"–"},{"text":" ","italic":true},{"text":"since this creates a "},{"text":"safek ","italic":true},{"text":"about whether you need to be "},{"text":"mafrish ","italic":true},{"text":"again. Let’s explain. If 1.2 kg is really the amount needed for "},{"text":"hafrashas challah, ","italic":true},{"text":"then there is no problem, because the batch of dough was already obligated and was exempted by your initial "},{"text":"hafrasha, ","italic":true},{"text":"and the additional flour that was added later (which is less than 1.2 kg of flour) is not enough to require a new "},{"text":"hafrasha. ","italic":true},{"text":"However, if 1.66 kg is the minimum amount required for "},{"text":"hafrasha","italic":true},{"text":", your original "},{"text":"hafrasha ","italic":true},{"text":"on 1.3 kg was meaningless. Once  you add enough flour to exceed the 1.66 kg threshold, the dough becomes obligated in "},{"text":"hafrasha ","italic":true},{"text":"for the first time, and it should ideally be done with a "},{"text":"bracha. ","italic":true},{"text":"In other words, if you ask the "},{"text":"posskim/Rishonim","italic":true},{"text":" who require "},{"text":"hafrasha ","italic":true},{"text":"on 1.2 kg,"},{"text":"[35]","footnote":35},{"text":" they would tell you that you do not need to be "},{"text":"mafrish ","italic":true},{"text":"at all, but if you ask the "},{"text":"posskim/Rishonim","italic":true},{"text":" who hold that you need 1.66 kg,"},{"text":"[36]","footnote":36},{"text":" they would tell you that you now have to be "},{"text":"mafrish ","italic":true},{"text":"with a "},{"text":"bracha. ","italic":true}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":1,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[35,36],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":176,"xmlIndex":184,"text":"In essence, you are stuck. On the one hand, you cannot recite a bracha because perhaps this batch was already obligated and exempted by your original hafrasha. On the other hand, you also cannot eat the batch without being mafrish, since perhaps it has only now become obligated in hafrasha for the first time. It is therefore best to be mafrish again without a bracha (see קומץ החלה עמ׳ טז).[37]","spans":[{"text":"In essence, you are stuck. On the one hand, you cannot recite a "},{"text":"bracha ","italic":true},{"text":"because perhaps this batch was already obligated and exempted by your original "},{"text":"hafrasha.","italic":true},{"text":" On the other hand, you also cannot eat the batch without being "},{"text":"mafrish","italic":true},{"text":", since perhaps it has only now become obligated in "},{"text":"hafrasha ","italic":true},{"text":"for the first time. It is therefore best to be "},{"text":"mafrish ","italic":true},{"text":"again without a "},{"text":"bracha ","italic":true},{"text":"(see קומץ החלה עמ׳ טז)"},{"text":".","italic":true},{"text":"[37]","footnote":37}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":36.0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[37],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":177,"xmlIndex":185,"text":"However, the חלה כהלכתה (סימן ה, שיח הלכה-י) (50) passkins that if the first batch was 1.3 kg and you were mafrish without a bracha (as required) and then you added another 1.3 kg, you should be mafrish again with a bracha ממה נפשך (regardless of which side is correct). The logic is as follows: if the real requirement is 1.66, the hafrasha you did earlier on 1.3 kg was meaningless, and now that you have more than 1.66 for the first time, you should be mafrish with a bracha. On the flip side, if the real amount required to be mafrish is 1.2 kg, now that you have another 1.3, you should be mafrish and recite a bracha. Even though you were already mafrish, this side would say that since another 1.3 was added now, you should be mafrish again and recite a bracha. The only reason you did not recite a bracha earlier is because maybe the real requirement is 1.66 kg, but now, even that side would require you to recite a bracha, because for the first time, there is now more than 1.66. In other words, in this case, if you ask the posskim/Rishonim who hold you need 1.2 kg and the posskim/Rishonim who hold you need 1.66 kg, both would tell you to recite a bracha.","spans":[{"text":"However, the "},{"text":"חלה כהלכתה ","bold":true},{"text":"(סימן ה, שיח הלכה-י) (50) "},{"text":"passkins ","italic":true},{"text":"that if the first batch was 1.3 kg and you were "},{"text":"mafrish ","italic":true},{"text":"without a "},{"text":"bracha ","italic":true},{"text":"(as required)"},{"text":" ","italic":true},{"text":"and then you added another 1.3 kg, you should be "},{"text":"mafrish ","italic":true},{"text":"again with a "},{"text":"bracha ","italic":true},{"text":"ממה נפשך (regardless of which side is correct)"},{"text":". ","italic":true},{"text":"The logic is as follows: if the real requirement is 1.66, the "},{"text":"hafrasha ","italic":true},{"text":"you did earlier on 1.3 kg was meaningless, and now that you have more than 1.66 for the first time, you should be "},{"text":"mafrish ","italic":true},{"text":"with a "},{"text":"bracha. ","italic":true},{"text":"On the flip side, if the real amount required to be "},{"text":"mafrish ","italic":true},{"text":"is 1.2 kg, now that you have another 1.3, you should be "},{"text":"mafrish ","italic":true},{"text":"and recite a "},{"text":"bracha. ","italic":true},{"text":"Even though you were already "},{"text":"mafrish","italic":true},{"text":", this side would say that since another 1.3 was added now, you should be "},{"text":"mafrish","italic":true},{"text":" again and recite a "},{"text":"bracha","italic":true},{"text":". The only reason you did not recite a "},{"text":"bracha","italic":true},{"text":" earlier is because maybe the real requirement is 1.66 kg, but now, even that side would require you to recite a "},{"text":"bracha","italic":true},{"text":", because for the first time, there is now more than 1.66."},{"text":" ","italic":true},{"text":"In other words, in this case, if you ask the "},{"text":"posskim/Rishonim","italic":true},{"text":" who hold you need 1.2 kg and the "},{"text":"posskim/Rishonim","italic":true},{"text":" who hold you need 1.66 kg, both would tell you to recite a "},{"text":"bracha.","italic":true}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":36.0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}],"footnotes":[{"id":35,"blocks":[{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":0,"xmlIndex":0,"text":" Because they use loosely packed flour, as discussed in the previous section.","spans":[{"text":" Because they use loosely packed flour, as discussed in the previous section."}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}]},{"id":36,"blocks":[{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":0,"xmlIndex":0,"text":" Because they require densely packed flour.","spans":[{"text":" Because they require densely packed flour."}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}]},{"id":37,"blocks":[{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":0,"xmlIndex":0,"text":" The לבוש מרדכי (חלה פ״ח-ט) writes that it is best to avoid adding the 1.3 kg after you were mafrish, lest you forget to be mafrish again. However, the חלה כהלכתה (סימן ה-ד) passkins that you are allowed to add more flour le’chatchilla, but you must be mafrish m’safek again since you added less than 1.2 kg.","spans":[{"text":" The לבוש מרדכי (חלה פ״ח-ט) writes that it is best to avoid adding the 1.3 kg after you were "},{"text":"mafrish, ","italic":true},{"text":"lest you forget to be "},{"text":"mafrish ","italic":true},{"text":"again. However, the חלה כהלכתה (סימן ה-ד) "},{"text":"passkins ","italic":true},{"text":"that you are allowed to add more flour "},{"text":"le’chatchilla","italic":true},{"text":", but you must be "},{"text":"mafrish m’safek ","italic":true},{"text":"again since you added less than 1.2 kg."}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}]}]},"sort_order":41,"official_shiur_number":4,"estimated_minutes":7,"difficulty":"practical","tags":["challah-part-1","original-difficulty:practical","source:50"],"source_file_name":"Notes for Hafrashas Challah (part 1).docx","source_start_page":null,"source_end_page":null,"is_selectable":true},{"program_id":"winter-5787","chunk_code":"HC1-G2","source_type":"notes","siman":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","workbook_title":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","section_key":"G","section_title":"Practical Applications","chunk_title":"Frozen Flour","chunk_summary":"Frozen flour may weigh more due to moisture, so add more to reach true volume.","content_markdown":"<u>Frozen flour</u>\n\nIt is important to stress again that the measurement for *hafrashas challah* is based on volume, and the *rishonim* and later *chachamim* converted it into a measurement of weight. Therefore, the **לבוש מרדכי** (פ״ח-יא) (51) writes that since frozen flour weighs more than room temperature flour because it absorbs moisture when frozen, when using frozen flour, you should add a bit to the weight of 1.66 kg to ensure there is a volume of 43.2 eggs. ","content_document":{"version":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":178,"xmlIndex":186,"text":"Frozen flour","spans":[{"text":"Frozen flour","underline":true}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":179,"xmlIndex":187,"text":"It is important to stress again that the measurement for hafrashas challah is based on volume, and the rishonim and later chachamim converted it into a measurement of weight. Therefore, the לבוש מרדכי (פ״ח-יא) (51) writes that since frozen flour weighs more than room temperature flour because it absorbs moisture when frozen, when using frozen flour, you should add a bit to the weight of 1.66 kg to ensure there is a volume of 43.2 eggs. ","spans":[{"text":"It is important to stress again that the measurement for "},{"text":"hafrashas challah ","italic":true},{"text":"is based on volume, and the "},{"text":"rishonim ","italic":true},{"text":"and later "},{"text":"chachamim ","italic":true},{"text":"converted it into a measurement of weight. Therefore, the "},{"text":"לבוש מרדכי ","bold":true},{"text":"(פ״ח-יא)"},{"text":" ","italic":true},{"text":"(51) writes that since frozen flour weighs more than room temperature flour because it absorbs moisture when frozen, when using frozen flour, you should add a bit to the weight of 1.66 kg to ensure there is a volume of 43.2 eggs. "}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":36.0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}],"footnotes":[]},"sort_order":42,"official_shiur_number":4,"estimated_minutes":4,"difficulty":"practical","tags":["challah-part-1","original-difficulty:practical","source:51"],"source_file_name":"Notes for Hafrashas Challah (part 1).docx","source_start_page":null,"source_end_page":null,"is_selectable":true},{"program_id":"winter-5787","chunk_code":"HC1-G3","source_type":"notes","siman":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","workbook_title":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","section_key":"G","section_title":"Practical Applications","chunk_title":"Whole Wheat Challah","chunk_summary":"Bran/endosperm/germ; if bran removed and added back, it does not count toward 1.66 kg; whole wheat may require ~1.9 kg.","content_markdown":"<u>Whole wheat</u> *<u>challah</u>*\n\n![Official diagram at paragraph 180](storage://official-learning-materials/sha256/aa38145d1f95110887570e1e01e1333a426bde175021cfe45945238507e33be9.jpg)\n\nIn order to better understand this subsection, we first need to familiarize ourselves with the anatomy of the wheat kernel. A kernel is made up of three parts: the bran, the endosperm and the germ. The bran is the outer layer of the kernel, which makes up about 14% of it and is used when making whole wheat flour. The endosperm is the source of white flour and makes up about 83% of the kernel. The germ is the embryo – the sprouting section of the kernel, and makes up about 3% of the kernel. It is usually separated from the flour. Milling is the process of crushing the wheat kernel and separating it into its various parts. See video on the anatomy of a wheat kernel: [<u>bit.ly/SCPkernel</u>](https://bit.ly/SCPkernel) \n\nThe S”A in **סימן שכד סעיף ג** (52) *passkins,* based on the **משנה חלה** (פ״ב-ו), that when calculating the volume of flour needed for *hafrashas challah*, the sourdough starter (שאור), bran (סובין) and wheat middling (מורסן), also known as the small pieces of bran, may be included in the calculation. However, if the bran is removed and later added back to the flour, it may not be included as part of the measurement. \n\nAs shown in the video above, today most industrial mills first separate the white flour from the bran, and when they want to produce and sell whole wheat flour, they add the bran back.[38] This has major ramifications for *hafrashas challah.* If you make a batch of dough using 1.66 kg of whole wheat flour, you are still not allowed to recite a *bracha* when being *mafrish,* because the bran was added afterwards and may not be included in the required amount of flour. In such a situation, you must ensure that you have 1.66 kg of flour without the bran.\n\nBased on the above, the **לבוש מרדכי** (פ״ח-יג) (53) writes that when buying whole wheat flour in which the bran is added back after milling, you need to add at least 15% to the required weight to ensure that you have the required amount of actual flour without the bran. Thus, according to רב חיים נאה, you may only be *mafrish* with a *bracha* if you have 1.916 kg of such whole wheat flour, and according to the חזון איש, only if you have 2.58 kg.[39]  \n\nThe לבוש מרדכי adds that if the bran was not removed during the milling process, as is common in smaller organic mills, then the required weight is actually less than 1.66 kg. Since the measurement for *hafrashas challah* is based on the volume of 43.2 eggs, and bran takes up more volume while weighing less than flour, a volume of 43.2 eggs filled with whole wheat flour will weigh less than the same volume filled with regular flour (1.66 kg). Therefore, when using proper whole wheat flour, in which the bran was never removed, the required weight will actually be less than the amounts listed above. \n\nThe **לבוש מרדכי** writes that each of the 5 grains has a different weight when measured in a volume of 43.2 eggs. The weight also varies depending on whether the grain is whole or regular and whether the bran was removed and then added back to the flour or remained in the flour from the time of milling. The לבוש מרדכי measured and weighed each of the five grains, both whole grain (with bran, both when it was removed and later added back,[40] and when it remained in the flour throughout the milling[41]) and regular (without bran), and presents the findings in a neatly organized chart shown in the Marei Mekomos. \n\nSource Notes\n\n[38]:  For this reason, you will see flour marketed as “80% whole wheat” or “50% whole wheat,” depending on how much bran they put back into the flour.\n\n[39]:  It should be noted that the תשובות והנהגות (ח״ג-עא) *passkins* that since the bran was removed with intent to be added back, it is considered as if it was never removed. According to this, you only need to take into consideration the lighter weight and not the heavier one. Consequently, you may recite a *bracha* with 1.66 kg according to רב חיים נאה (or even from 1.59, since whole wheat flour weighs less than regular flour), even when using whole wheat flour. \n\n[40]:  The heavier weight listed.\n\n[41]:  The lighter weight listed.","content_document":{"version":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":180,"xmlIndex":188,"text":"Whole wheat challah","spans":[{"text":"Whole wheat ","underline":true},{"text":"challah","italic":true,"underline":true}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"image","paragraph":180,"alt":"Official diagram at paragraph 180","width":291,"height":326,"displayWidth":127,"storagePath":"sha256/aa38145d1f95110887570e1e01e1333a426bde175021cfe45945238507e33be9.jpg"},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":181,"xmlIndex":189,"text":"In order to better understand this subsection, we first need to familiarize ourselves with the anatomy of the wheat kernel. A kernel is made up of three parts: the bran, the endosperm and the germ. The bran is the outer layer of the kernel, which makes up about 14% of it and is used when making whole wheat flour. The endosperm is the source of white flour and makes up about 83% of the kernel. The germ is the embryo – the sprouting section of the kernel, and makes up about 3% of the kernel. It is usually separated from the flour. Milling is the process of crushing the wheat kernel and separating it into its various parts. See video on the anatomy of a wheat kernel: bit.ly/SCPkernel ","spans":[{"text":"In order to better understand this subsection, we first need to familiarize ourselves with the anatomy of the wheat kernel. A kernel is made up of three parts: the bran, the endosperm and the germ. The bran is the outer layer of the kernel, which makes up about 14% of it and is used when making whole wheat flour. The endosperm is the source of white flour and makes up about 83% of the kernel. The germ is the embryo – the sprouting section of the kernel, and makes up about 3% of the kernel. It is usually separated from the flour. Milling is the process of crushing the wheat kernel and separating it into its various parts. See video on the anatomy of a wheat kernel: "},{"text":"bit.ly/SCPkernel","underline":true,"color":"#0563C1","href":"https://bit.ly/SCPkernel"},{"text":" "}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":36.0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":182,"xmlIndex":190,"text":"The S”A in סימן שכד סעיף ג (52) passkins, based on the משנה חלה (פ״ב-ו), that when calculating the volume of flour needed for hafrashas challah, the sourdough starter (שאור), bran (סובין) and wheat middling (מורסן), also known as the small pieces of bran, may be included in the calculation. However, if the bran is removed and later added back to the flour, it may not be included as part of the measurement. ","spans":[{"text":"The S”A in "},{"text":"סימן שכד סעיף ג","bold":true},{"text":" (52) "},{"text":"passkins, ","italic":true},{"text":"based on the "},{"text":"משנה חלה","bold":true},{"text":" (פ״ב-ו), that when calculating the volume of flour needed for "},{"text":"hafrashas challah","italic":true},{"text":", the sourdough starter (שאור), bran (סובין) and wheat middling (מורסן), also known as the small pieces of bran, may be included in the calculation. However, if the bran is removed and later added back to the flour, it may not be included as part of the measurement. "}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":36.0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":183,"xmlIndex":191,"text":"As shown in the video above, today most industrial mills first separate the white flour from the bran, and when they want to produce and sell whole wheat flour, they add the bran back.[38] This has major ramifications for hafrashas challah. If you make a batch of dough using 1.66 kg of whole wheat flour, you are still not allowed to recite a bracha when being mafrish, because the bran was added afterwards and may not be included in the required amount of flour. In such a situation, you must ensure that you have 1.66 kg of flour without the bran.","spans":[{"text":"As shown in the video above, today most industrial mills first separate the white flour from the bran, and when they want to produce and sell whole wheat flour, they add the bran back."},{"text":"[38]","footnote":38},{"text":" This has major ramifications for "},{"text":"hafrashas challah. ","italic":true},{"text":"If you make a batch of dough using 1.66 kg of whole wheat flour, you are still not allowed to recite a "},{"text":"bracha ","italic":true},{"text":"when being "},{"text":"mafrish, ","italic":true},{"text":"because the bran was added afterwards and may not be included in the required amount of flour. In such a situation, you must ensure that you have 1.66 kg of flour without the bran."}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":36.0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[38],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":184,"xmlIndex":192,"text":"Based on the above, the לבוש מרדכי (פ״ח-יג) (53) writes that when buying whole wheat flour in which the bran is added back after milling, you need to add at least 15% to the required weight to ensure that you have the required amount of actual flour without the bran. Thus, according to רב חיים נאה, you may only be mafrish with a bracha if you have 1.916 kg of such whole wheat flour, and according to the חזון איש, only if you have 2.58 kg.[39]  ","spans":[{"text":"Based on the above, the "},{"text":"לבוש מרדכי ","bold":true},{"text":"(פ״ח-יג)"},{"text":" ","italic":true},{"text":"(53) writes that when buying whole wheat flour in which the bran is added back after milling, you need to add at least 15% to the required weight to ensure that you have the required amount of actual flour without the bran. Thus, according to רב חיים נאה, you may only be "},{"text":"mafrish ","italic":true},{"text":"with a "},{"text":"bracha ","italic":true},{"text":"if you have 1.916 kg of such whole wheat flour, and according to the חזון איש, only if you have 2.58 kg."},{"text":"[39]","footnote":39},{"text":"  "}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":36.0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[39],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":185,"xmlIndex":193,"text":"The לבוש מרדכי adds that if the bran was not removed during the milling process, as is common in smaller organic mills, then the required weight is actually less than 1.66 kg. Since the measurement for hafrashas challah is based on the volume of 43.2 eggs, and bran takes up more volume while weighing less than flour, a volume of 43.2 eggs filled with whole wheat flour will weigh less than the same volume filled with regular flour (1.66 kg). Therefore, when using proper whole wheat flour, in which the bran was never removed, the required weight will actually be less than the amounts listed above. ","spans":[{"text":"The לבוש מרדכי adds that if the bran was not removed during the milling process, as is common in smaller organic mills, then the required weight is actually less than 1.66 kg. Since the measurement for "},{"text":"hafrashas challah","italic":true},{"text":" is based on the volume of 43.2 eggs, and bran takes up more volume while weighing less than flour, a volume of 43.2 eggs filled with whole wheat flour will weigh less than the same volume filled with regular flour (1.66 kg). Therefore, when using proper whole wheat flour, in which the bran was never removed, the required weight will actually be less than the amounts listed above. "}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":36.0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":186,"xmlIndex":194,"text":"The לבוש מרדכי writes that each of the 5 grains has a different weight when measured in a volume of 43.2 eggs. The weight also varies depending on whether the grain is whole or regular and whether the bran was removed and then added back to the flour or remained in the flour from the time of milling. The לבוש מרדכי measured and weighed each of the five grains, both whole grain (with bran, both when it was removed and later added back,[40] and when it remained in the flour throughout the milling[41]) and regular (without bran), and presents the findings in a neatly organized chart shown in the Marei Mekomos. ","spans":[{"text":"The "},{"text":"לבוש מרדכי ","bold":true},{"text":"writes that each of the 5 grains has a different weight when measured in a volume of 43.2 eggs. The weight also varies depending on whether the grain is whole or regular and whether the bran was removed and then added back to the flour or remained in the flour from the time of milling. The לבוש מרדכי measured and weighed each of the five grains, both whole grain (with bran, both when it was removed and later added back,"},{"text":"[40]","footnote":40},{"text":" and when it remained in the flour throughout the milling"},{"text":"[41]","footnote":41},{"text":") and regular (without bran), and presents the findings in a neatly organized chart shown in the Marei Mekomos. "}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":36.0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[40,41],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}],"footnotes":[{"id":38,"blocks":[{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":0,"xmlIndex":0,"text":" For this reason, you will see flour marketed as “80% whole wheat” or “50% whole wheat,” depending on how much bran they put back into the flour.","spans":[{"text":" For this reason, you will see flour marketed as “80% whole wheat” or “50% whole wheat,” depending on how much bran they put back into the flour."}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}]},{"id":39,"blocks":[{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":0,"xmlIndex":0,"text":" It should be noted that the תשובות והנהגות (ח״ג-עא) passkins that since the bran was removed with intent to be added back, it is considered as if it was never removed. According to this, you only need to take into consideration the lighter weight and not the heavier one. Consequently, you may recite a bracha with 1.66 kg according to רב חיים נאה (or even from 1.59, since whole wheat flour weighs less than regular flour), even when using whole wheat flour. ","spans":[{"text":" It should be noted that the תשובות והנהגות (ח״ג-עא) "},{"text":"passkins ","italic":true},{"text":"that since the bran was removed with intent to be added back, it is considered as if it was never removed. According to this, you only need to take into consideration the lighter weight and not the heavier one. Consequently, you may recite a "},{"text":"bracha ","italic":true},{"text":"with 1.66 kg according to רב חיים נאה (or even from 1.59, since whole wheat flour weighs less than regular flour), even when using whole wheat flour. "}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}]},{"id":40,"blocks":[{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":0,"xmlIndex":0,"text":" The heavier weight listed.","spans":[{"text":" The heavier weight listed."}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}]},{"id":41,"blocks":[{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":0,"xmlIndex":0,"text":" The lighter weight listed.","spans":[{"text":" The lighter weight listed."}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}]}]},"sort_order":43,"official_shiur_number":4,"estimated_minutes":8,"difficulty":"practical","tags":["challah-part-1","original-difficulty:practical","source:52","source:53"],"source_file_name":"Notes for Hafrashas Challah (part 1).docx","source_start_page":null,"source_end_page":null,"is_selectable":true},{"program_id":"winter-5787","chunk_code":"HC1-G4","source_type":"notes","siman":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","workbook_title":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","section_key":"G","section_title":"Practical Applications","chunk_title":"Gluten-Free Wheat Flour","chunk_summary":"Is wheat starch obligated? Amrei Yaakov says separate without bracha; Rav Rubin may require bracha if made by Jews; additives and brachos.","content_markdown":"<u>Gluten-free wheat flour</u>\n\n\tWe learned above in the **משנה חלה** (פ״א-א) (40) that *hafrashas challah* is compared to *matzos* on Pesach. Just as *matzos* may only be made from the five grains that can become *chametz*, so too, *hafrashas challah* is only required from flour made from those same five grains. Gluten is the ingredient that makes the flour rise, and today there are companies that produce wheat flour from which the  gluten has been removed*.* Is there an obligation to be *mafrish challah* from gluten-free wheat flour? On the one hand, gluten-free wheat flour does not fully rise and become *chametz,* but on the other hand, perhaps we look at wheat flour as a species: since ordinary wheat flour rises, even wheat flour from which the gluten has been removed is obligated in *hafrashas challah*. \n\n The **אמרי יעקב** (ארץ ישראל ס״ב-ח ביאורים ד״ה ה׳ מיני דגן) (54) *passkins* that since there might be some remnants of gluten in the flour, one should be *mafrish* without a *bracha.* The **משנת יוסף** (חלק טו-רב) and the **אהל יעקב** (שכד-ז) also *passkin* that because of the *safek,* one should be *mafrish* without a *bracha.* However, Rav Rubin, the author of ארחות שבת, and the OU[42] *passkin* that in certain circumstances one should be *mafrish challah* with a *bracha.* \n\nIn order to better understand this last question, we need to first understand how gluten-free wheat flour is made. In essence, gluten-free wheat flour is wheat starch. It is made by kneading flour and water into a batch of dough and then soaking the dough in water. The water becomes milky white because it absorbs the starch from the dough. The dough is then removed, and the remaining water sits until all the starch settles to the bottom, at which point the water is discarded and the starch is dried. Once dried, it is crushed and appears as white flour. See **video** for the exact process: <u>bit.ly/SCPglutenfreewheat</u>. Is this extract of flour considered “flour” that is obligated in *hafrasha*?\n\nRav Rubin writes that two main questions need to be answered: 1) If gluten is completely removed, do we say that since the flour cannot fully rise it is exempt from *challah,* or do we look at the species of flour and say that since ordinary wheat flour rises, this flour is also obligated in *hafrashas challah*?[43] 2) Even if we look at the species as a whole, perhaps *hafrashas challah* is only required when the entire grain, or at least the endosperm, is used, while  gluten-free flour is essentially just wheat starch (i.e., a derivative of flour). \n\nRegarding the first question, Rav Rubin in **היכלא** (חי״א-עמ׳ רכד) (55) quotes the ר״ן, who *passkins* that we look at the species of wheat flour as a whole. Since ordinary wheat flour rises, even if this specific flour does not, it is still obligated in *hafrashas challah.* Regarding the second question, Rav Rubin writes that according to the רי״ף, bread made with just the bran is considered bread even though it is missing the main ingredient of flour made from the endosperm. Although we do not *passkin* like the רי״ף regarding *matzah* on Pesach, we would still agree that wheat starch is considered flour regarding the obligation of *hafrashas challah,* since it is derived from the endosperm, the main part of the grain*.* \n\nBased on these two points, Rav Rubin *passkins* that one should be *mafrish challah* with a *bracha* on wheat starch. However, there is a caveat: if the wheat starch was made by non-Jews, a *bracha* should not be recited when being *mafrish.* This is based on the S”A in **של-א**, who *passkins* that flour owned and kneaded into dough by a non-Jew is not obligated in *challah.* Therefore, since the starch is produced by first kneading the flour into dough and then soaking it in water, if the initial kneading was done by a non-Jew, it was already exempt from *challah,* and no *hafrasha* is necessary*.* However, one may argue that since the non-Jew is not kneading the dough to make bread, it may not be considered “kneading by a non-Jew.” Therefore, it is best to be *mafrish* without a *bracha.* \n\nWhat emerges from here is that according to Rav Rubin, *hafrashas challah* with a *bracha* should be performed when making bread from wheat starch produced by a Jew, but if the starch was made by a non-Jew, a *bracha* should not be recited. Rav Rubin adds that today many gluten-free wheat flours  contain significant amounts of non-wheat additives, such as rice and corn flour. In order to be obligated in *hafrasha,* the volume of 43.2 eggs of the gluten-free wheat flour itself are necessary, without the other additives.[44] \n\nFurthermore, according to Rav Rubin, if the wheat starch is made into bread, the *bracha* is *hamotzi,* and if it is made into a pastry, the *bracha* is *mezonos.* The S”A in **או״ח סימן רח-ב** *passkins* that the *bracha* on a pastry made with the five grains and other ingredients is *mezonos,* even if the five grains are a minority, as long as the grains are not used as a binding agent. The **וזאת הברכה** (עמ׳ 201) writes that if “wheat starch” is listed after the main ingredient, such as corn flour, the wheat starch is being used as a binding agent, and the *bracha* on the food is *shehakol.* The example he gives is Cocoa Puffs, which has wheat starch listed as the second ingredient, and the *bracha* is *shehakol* because wheat starch is used as a binding agent. (According to the Star-K, when wheat comprises less than 15% of a product, it generally serves only as a binding agent. When it exceeds 25%, it also contributes flavor. Between 15% and 25%, its function is considered a *safek*, and the manufacturer should be contacted to clarify its intended purpose.)\n\nSource Notes\n\n[42]:  <u>bit.ly/SCPwheatstarch</u> \n\n[43]:  When learning *hilchos brachos* we saw a similar dispute regarding hydroponic plants that grow without earth. Is *ha’adama* recited since this species of food grows in the ground, or is *shehakol* recited because this specific vegetable grew in water?\n\n[44]:  If the flour has a good *hechsher,* it is possible that *hafrashas challah* was already performed when it was initially made into a batch of dough. ","content_document":{"version":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":187,"xmlIndex":195,"text":"Gluten-free wheat flour","spans":[{"text":"Gluten-free wheat flour","underline":true}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":188,"xmlIndex":196,"text":"\tWe learned above in the משנה חלה (פ״א-א) (40) that hafrashas challah is compared to matzos on Pesach. Just as matzos may only be made from the five grains that can become chametz, so too, hafrashas challah is only required from flour made from those same five grains. Gluten is the ingredient that makes the flour rise, and today there are companies that produce wheat flour from which the  gluten has been removed. Is there an obligation to be mafrish challah from gluten-free wheat flour? On the one hand, gluten-free wheat flour does not fully rise and become chametz, but on the other hand, perhaps we look at wheat flour as a species: since ordinary wheat flour rises, even wheat flour from which the gluten has been removed is obligated in hafrashas challah. ","spans":[{"text":"\tWe learned above in the "},{"text":"משנה חלה","bold":true},{"text":" (פ״א-א) (40) that "},{"text":"hafrashas challah ","italic":true},{"text":"is compared to "},{"text":"matzos ","italic":true},{"text":"on Pesach. Just as "},{"text":"matzos","italic":true},{"text":" may only be made from the five grains that can become "},{"text":"chametz","italic":true},{"text":", so too, "},{"text":"hafrashas challah","bold":false,"italic":true},{"text":" is only required from flour made from those same five grains."},{"text":" ","italic":true},{"text":"Gluten is the ingredient that makes the flour rise, and today there are companies that produce wheat flour from which the  gluten has been removed"},{"text":". ","italic":true},{"text":"Is there an obligation to be "},{"text":"mafrish challah ","italic":true},{"text":"from gluten-free wheat flour? On the one hand, gluten-free wheat flour does not fully rise and become "},{"text":"chametz, ","italic":true},{"text":"but on the other hand, perhaps we look at wheat flour as a species: since ordinary wheat flour rises, even wheat flour from which the gluten has been removed is obligated in "},{"text":"hafrashas challah","italic":true},{"text":"."},{"text":" ","italic":true}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":1,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":189,"xmlIndex":197,"text":" The אמרי יעקב (ארץ ישראל ס״ב-ח ביאורים ד״ה ה׳ מיני דגן) (54) passkins that since there might be some remnants of gluten in the flour, one should be mafrish without a bracha. The משנת יוסף (חלק טו-רב) and the אהל יעקב (שכד-ז) also passkin that because of the safek, one should be mafrish without a bracha. However, Rav Rubin, the author of ארחות שבת, and the OU[42] passkin that in certain circumstances one should be mafrish challah with a bracha. ","spans":[{"text":" The "},{"text":"אמרי יעקב ","bold":true},{"text":"(ארץ ישראל ס״ב-ח ביאורים ד״ה ה׳ מיני דגן) (54) "},{"text":"passkins ","italic":true},{"text":"that since there might be some remnants of gluten in the flour, one should be "},{"text":"mafrish ","italic":true},{"text":"without a "},{"text":"bracha. ","italic":true},{"text":"The "},{"text":"משנת יוסף","bold":true},{"text":" (חלק טו-רב) and the "},{"text":"אהל יעקב","bold":true},{"text":" (שכד-ז) also "},{"text":"passkin ","italic":true},{"text":"that because of the "},{"text":"safek,","italic":true},{"text":" one should be "},{"text":"mafrish ","italic":true},{"text":"without a "},{"text":"bracha. ","italic":true},{"text":"However, Rav Rubin, the author of ארחות שבת, and the OU"},{"text":"[42]","footnote":42},{"text":" "},{"text":"passkin ","italic":true},{"text":"that in certain circumstances one should be "},{"text":"mafrish challah ","italic":true},{"text":"with a "},{"text":"bracha. ","italic":true}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":36.0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[42],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":190,"xmlIndex":198,"text":"In order to better understand this last question, we need to first understand how gluten-free wheat flour is made. In essence, gluten-free wheat flour is wheat starch. It is made by kneading flour and water into a batch of dough and then soaking the dough in water. The water becomes milky white because it absorbs the starch from the dough. The dough is then removed, and the remaining water sits until all the starch settles to the bottom, at which point the water is discarded and the starch is dried. Once dried, it is crushed and appears as white flour. See video for the exact process: bit.ly/SCPglutenfreewheat. Is this extract of flour considered “flour” that is obligated in hafrasha?","spans":[{"text":"In order to better understand this last question, we need to first understand how gluten-free wheat flour is made. In essence, gluten-free wheat flour is wheat starch. It is made by kneading flour and water into a batch of dough and then soaking the dough in water. The water becomes milky white because it absorbs the starch from the dough. The dough is then removed, and the remaining water sits until all the starch settles to the bottom, at which point the water is discarded and the starch is dried. Once dried, it is crushed and appears as white flour. See "},{"text":"video","bold":true},{"text":" for the exact process: "},{"text":"bit.ly/SCPglutenfreewheat","underline":true,"color":"#0563C1"},{"text":". Is this extract of flour considered “flour” that is obligated in "},{"text":"hafrasha","italic":true},{"text":"?"}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":36.0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":191,"xmlIndex":199,"text":"Rav Rubin writes that two main questions need to be answered: 1) If gluten is completely removed, do we say that since the flour cannot fully rise it is exempt from challah, or do we look at the species of flour and say that since ordinary wheat flour rises, this flour is also obligated in hafrashas challah?[43] 2) Even if we look at the species as a whole, perhaps hafrashas challah is only required when the entire grain, or at least the endosperm, is used, while  gluten-free flour is essentially just wheat starch (i.e., a derivative of flour). ","spans":[{"text":"Rav Rubin writes that two main questions need to be answered: 1) If gluten is completely removed, do we say that since the flour cannot fully rise it is exempt from "},{"text":"challah, ","italic":true},{"text":"or do we look at the species of flour and say that since ordinary wheat flour rises, this flour is also obligated in "},{"text":"hafrashas challah","italic":true},{"text":"?"},{"text":"[43]","footnote":43},{"text":" 2) Even if we look at the species as a whole, perhaps "},{"text":"hafrashas challah","italic":true},{"text":" is only required when the entire grain, or at least the endosperm, is used, while  gluten-free flour is essentially just wheat starch (i.e., a derivative of flour). "}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":36.0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[43],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":192,"xmlIndex":200,"text":"Regarding the first question, Rav Rubin in היכלא (חי״א-עמ׳ רכד) (55) quotes the ר״ן, who passkins that we look at the species of wheat flour as a whole. Since ordinary wheat flour rises, even if this specific flour does not, it is still obligated in hafrashas challah. Regarding the second question, Rav Rubin writes that according to the רי״ף, bread made with just the bran is considered bread even though it is missing the main ingredient of flour made from the endosperm. Although we do not passkin like the רי״ף regarding matzah on Pesach, we would still agree that wheat starch is considered flour regarding the obligation of hafrashas challah, since it is derived from the endosperm, the main part of the grain. ","spans":[{"text":"Regarding the first question, Rav Rubin in "},{"text":"היכלא","bold":true},{"text":" (חי״א-עמ׳ רכד) (55) quotes the ר״ן, who "},{"text":"passkins ","italic":true},{"text":"that we look at the species of wheat flour as a whole. Since ordinary wheat flour rises, even if this specific flour does not, it is still obligated in "},{"text":"hafrashas challah. ","italic":true},{"text":"Regarding the second question, Rav Rubin writes that according to the רי״ף, bread made with just the bran is considered bread even though it is missing the main ingredient of flour made from the endosperm. Although we do not "},{"text":"passkin ","italic":true},{"text":"like the רי״ף regarding "},{"text":"matzah ","italic":true},{"text":"on Pesach, we would still agree that wheat starch is considered flour regarding the obligation of "},{"text":"hafrashas challah, ","italic":true},{"text":"since it is derived from the endosperm, the main part of the grain"},{"text":". ","italic":true}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":36.0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":193,"xmlIndex":201,"text":"Based on these two points, Rav Rubin passkins that one should be mafrish challah with a bracha on wheat starch. However, there is a caveat: if the wheat starch was made by non-Jews, a bracha should not be recited when being mafrish. This is based on the S”A in של-א, who passkins that flour owned and kneaded into dough by a non-Jew is not obligated in challah. Therefore, since the starch is produced by first kneading the flour into dough and then soaking it in water, if the initial kneading was done by a non-Jew, it was already exempt from challah, and no hafrasha is necessary. However, one may argue that since the non-Jew is not kneading the dough to make bread, it may not be considered “kneading by a non-Jew.” Therefore, it is best to be mafrish without a bracha. ","spans":[{"text":"Based on these two points, Rav Rubin "},{"text":"passkins ","italic":true},{"text":"that one should be "},{"text":"mafrish challah ","italic":true},{"text":"with a "},{"text":"bracha ","italic":true},{"text":"on wheat starch. However, there is a caveat: if the wheat starch was made by non-Jews, a "},{"text":"bracha ","italic":true},{"text":"should not be recited when being "},{"text":"mafrish. ","italic":true},{"text":"This is based on the S”A in "},{"text":"של-א","bold":true},{"text":", who "},{"text":"passkins ","italic":true},{"text":"that flour owned and kneaded into dough by a non-Jew is not obligated in "},{"text":"challah. ","italic":true},{"text":"Therefore, since the starch is produced by first kneading the flour into dough and then soaking it in water, if the initial kneading was done by a non-Jew, it was already exempt from "},{"text":"challah, ","italic":true},{"text":"and no "},{"text":"hafrasha ","italic":true},{"text":"is necessary"},{"text":". ","italic":true},{"text":"However, one may argue that since the non-Jew is not kneading the dough to make bread, it may not be considered “kneading by a non-Jew.” Therefore, it is best to be "},{"text":"mafrish ","italic":true},{"text":"without a "},{"text":"bracha. ","italic":true}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":36.0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":194,"xmlIndex":202,"text":"What emerges from here is that according to Rav Rubin, hafrashas challah with a bracha should be performed when making bread from wheat starch produced by a Jew, but if the starch was made by a non-Jew, a bracha should not be recited. Rav Rubin adds that today many gluten-free wheat flours  contain significant amounts of non-wheat additives, such as rice and corn flour. In order to be obligated in hafrasha, the volume of 43.2 eggs of the gluten-free wheat flour itself are necessary, without the other additives.[44] ","spans":[{"text":"What emerges from here is that according to Rav Rubin, "},{"text":"hafrashas challah ","italic":true},{"text":"with a "},{"text":"bracha ","italic":true},{"text":"should be performed when making bread from wheat starch produced by a Jew, but if the starch was made by a non-Jew, a "},{"text":"bracha ","italic":true},{"text":"should not be recited. Rav Rubin adds that today many gluten-free wheat flours  contain significant amounts of non-wheat additives, such as rice and corn flour. In order to be obligated in "},{"text":"hafrasha, ","italic":true},{"text":"the volume of 43.2 eggs of the gluten-free wheat flour itself are necessary, without the other additives."},{"text":"[44]","footnote":44},{"text":" "}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":36.0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[44],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":195,"xmlIndex":203,"text":"Furthermore, according to Rav Rubin, if the wheat starch is made into bread, the bracha is hamotzi, and if it is made into a pastry, the bracha is mezonos. The S”A in או״ח סימן רח-ב passkins that the bracha on a pastry made with the five grains and other ingredients is mezonos, even if the five grains are a minority, as long as the grains are not used as a binding agent. The וזאת הברכה (עמ׳ 201) writes that if “wheat starch” is listed after the main ingredient, such as corn flour, the wheat starch is being used as a binding agent, and the bracha on the food is shehakol. The example he gives is Cocoa Puffs, which has wheat starch listed as the second ingredient, and the bracha is shehakol because wheat starch is used as a binding agent. (According to the Star-K, when wheat comprises less than 15% of a product, it generally serves only as a binding agent. When it exceeds 25%, it also contributes flavor. Between 15% and 25%, its function is considered a safek, and the manufacturer should be contacted to clarify its intended purpose.)","spans":[{"text":"Furthermore, according to Rav Rubin, if the wheat starch is made into bread, the "},{"text":"bracha ","italic":true},{"text":"is "},{"text":"hamotzi, ","italic":true},{"text":"and if it is made into a pastry, the "},{"text":"bracha ","italic":true},{"text":"is "},{"text":"mezonos. ","italic":true},{"text":"The S”A in "},{"text":"או״ח סימן רח-ב ","bold":true},{"text":"passkins ","italic":true},{"text":"that the "},{"text":"bracha ","italic":true},{"text":"on a pastry made with the five grains and other ingredients is "},{"text":"mezonos, ","italic":true},{"text":"even if the five grains are a minority, as long as the grains are not used as a binding agent. The "},{"text":"וזאת הברכה ","bold":true},{"text":"(עמ׳ 201) writes that if “wheat starch” is listed after the main ingredient, such as corn flour, the wheat starch is being used as a binding agent, and the "},{"text":"bracha ","italic":true},{"text":"on the food is "},{"text":"shehakol. ","italic":true},{"text":"The example he gives is Cocoa Puffs, which has wheat starch listed as the second ingredient, and the "},{"text":"bracha ","italic":true},{"text":"is "},{"text":"shehakol ","italic":true},{"text":"because wheat starch is used as a binding agent. (According to the Star-K, when wheat comprises less than 15% of a product, it generally serves only as a binding agent. When it exceeds 25%, it also contributes flavor. Between 15% and 25%, its function is considered a "},{"text":"safek","italic":true},{"text":", and the manufacturer should be contacted to clarify its intended purpose.)"}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":36.0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}],"footnotes":[{"id":42,"blocks":[{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":0,"xmlIndex":0,"text":" bit.ly/SCPwheatstarch ","spans":[{"text":" "},{"text":"bit.ly/SCPwheatstarch","underline":true,"color":"#0563C1"},{"text":" "}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}]},{"id":43,"blocks":[{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":0,"xmlIndex":0,"text":" When learning hilchos brachos we saw a similar dispute regarding hydroponic plants that grow without earth. Is ha’adama recited since this species of food grows in the ground, or is shehakol recited because this specific vegetable grew in water?","spans":[{"text":" When learning "},{"text":"hilchos brachos ","italic":true},{"text":"we saw a similar dispute regarding hydroponic plants that grow without earth. Is "},{"text":"ha’adama ","italic":true},{"text":"recited since this species of food grows in the ground, or is "},{"text":"shehakol ","italic":true},{"text":"recited because this specific vegetable grew in water?"}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}]},{"id":44,"blocks":[{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":0,"xmlIndex":0,"text":" If the flour has a good hechsher, it is possible that hafrashas challah was already performed when it was initially made into a batch of dough. ","spans":[{"text":" If the flour has a good "},{"text":"hechsher, ","italic":true},{"text":"it is possible that "},{"text":"hafrashas challah ","italic":true},{"text":"was already performed when it was initially made into a batch of dough. "}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}]}]},"sort_order":44,"official_shiur_number":4,"estimated_minutes":10,"difficulty":"advanced","tags":["challah-part-1","original-difficulty:advanced","source:40","source:54","source:55"],"source_file_name":"Notes for Hafrashas Challah (part 1).docx","source_start_page":null,"source_end_page":null,"is_selectable":true},{"program_id":"winter-5787","chunk_code":"HC1-G5","source_type":"notes","siman":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","workbook_title":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","section_key":"G","section_title":"Practical Applications","chunk_title":"Volume of Dough Increased","chunk_summary":"Obligation depends on original flour volume, not later rising; do not deliberately make smaller batch to avoid challah.","content_markdown":"<u>Volume of dough increased</u> \n\n\tWe mentioned above that the amount of flour required for *hafrashas challah* is calculated in volume and was only later converted into a measurement of weight. What is the *halacha* if you do not have the volume of 43.2 eggs of flour, but the dough later rises to a volume well beyond that size? Are you required to be *mafrish challah*? \n\n\tThe S”A in **סימן שכד-יג** (56) *passkins* that if the dough rises while baking and exceeds the volume of 43.2 eggs, one is not obligated to be *mafrish challah.* The *Rama* adds that the same applies if the dough rises before it is baked. The **ט״ז** (טז) explains that since the requirement of *hafrashas challah* takes effect when the dough is first made, that is when the amount of flour is calculated.[45] \n\n\tThe S”A adds in **סעיף יד**, based on the ירושלמי (ג-א), that it is forbidden to deliberately make a smaller batch to avoid being *mafrish challah.* However, if you make a smaller batch because you are not hosting any guests or for some other legitimate reason, it is permitted. \n\nSource Notes\n\n[45]:  Bez”H, we will learn later in the *zman* whether the best time to be *mafrish* is before the dough rises or afterward.","content_document":{"version":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":196,"xmlIndex":204,"text":"Volume of dough increased ","spans":[{"text":"Volume of dough increased ","underline":true}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":197,"xmlIndex":205,"text":"\tWe mentioned above that the amount of flour required for hafrashas challah is calculated in volume and was only later converted into a measurement of weight. What is the halacha if you do not have the volume of 43.2 eggs of flour, but the dough later rises to a volume well beyond that size? Are you required to be mafrish challah? ","spans":[{"text":"\tWe mentioned above that the amount of flour required for "},{"text":"hafrashas challah ","italic":true},{"text":"is calculated in volume and was only later converted into a measurement of weight. What is the "},{"text":"halacha ","italic":true},{"text":"if you do not have the volume of 43.2 eggs of flour, but the dough later rises to a volume well beyond that size? Are you required to be "},{"text":"mafrish challah","italic":true},{"text":"? "}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":1,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":198,"xmlIndex":206,"text":"\tThe S”A in סימן שכד-יג (56) passkins that if the dough rises while baking and exceeds the volume of 43.2 eggs, one is not obligated to be mafrish challah. The Rama adds that the same applies if the dough rises before it is baked. The ט״ז (טז) explains that since the requirement of hafrashas challah takes effect when the dough is first made, that is when the amount of flour is calculated.[45] ","spans":[{"text":"\tThe S”A in "},{"text":"סימן שכד-יג","bold":true},{"text":" (56) "},{"text":"passkins ","italic":true},{"text":"that if the dough rises while baking and exceeds the volume of 43.2 eggs, one is not obligated to be "},{"text":"mafrish challah. ","italic":true},{"text":"The "},{"text":"Rama ","italic":true},{"text":"adds that the same applies if the dough rises before it is baked. The "},{"text":"ט״ז ","bold":true},{"text":"(טז) explains that since the requirement of "},{"text":"hafrashas challah ","italic":true},{"text":"takes effect when the dough is first made, that is when the amount of flour is calculated."},{"text":"[45]","footnote":45},{"text":" "}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":1,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[45],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":199,"xmlIndex":207,"text":"\tThe S”A adds in סעיף יד, based on the ירושלמי (ג-א), that it is forbidden to deliberately make a smaller batch to avoid being mafrish challah. However, if you make a smaller batch because you are not hosting any guests or for some other legitimate reason, it is permitted. ","spans":[{"text":"\tThe S”A adds in "},{"text":"סעיף יד","bold":true},{"text":","},{"text":" ","bold":true},{"text":"based on the ירושלמי (ג-א), that it is forbidden to deliberately make a smaller batch to avoid being "},{"text":"mafrish challah. ","italic":true},{"text":"However, if you make a smaller batch because you are not hosting any guests or for some other legitimate reason, it is permitted. "}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":1,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}],"footnotes":[{"id":45,"blocks":[{"kind":"paragraph","paragraph":0,"xmlIndex":0,"text":" Bez”H, we will learn later in the zman whether the best time to be mafrish is before the dough rises or afterward.","spans":[{"text":" Bez”H, we will learn later in the "},{"text":"zman","italic":true},{"text":" whether the best time to be "},{"text":"mafrish ","italic":true},{"text":"is"},{"text":" ","italic":true},{"text":"before the dough rises or afterward."}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}]}]},"sort_order":45,"official_shiur_number":5,"estimated_minutes":5,"difficulty":"practical","tags":["challah-part-1","original-difficulty:practical","source:56"],"source_file_name":"Notes for Hafrashas Challah (part 1).docx","source_start_page":null,"source_end_page":null,"is_selectable":true},{"program_id":"winter-5787","chunk_code":"HC1-Q1","source_type":"qa","siman":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","workbook_title":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","section_key":"A","section_title":"Introduction to the Mitzvah of Challah","chunk_title":"Is Hafrashas Challah Biblical or Rabbinic?","chunk_summary":"S”A: biblical in root, today biblical only in EY when Jewish people live there; chu”l is rabbinic so it will not be forgotten.","content_markdown":"1. Is the *mitzvah* of *hafrashas challah* biblical or rabbinic?\n\nThe S”A in **סעיף א** *passkins* that the *mitzvah* of *hafrashas challah* is biblical, but in **סעיף ב** he clarifies that it is only biblical in *Eretz Yisrael* when all of the Jewish people are living there. In **סעיף ג**, he adds that in *chutz la’aretz,* the *mitzvah* was rabbinically enacted so the *mitzvah* of *challah* will not be forgotten. ","content_document":{"version":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"question","paragraph":2,"xmlIndex":2,"text":"Is the mitzvah of hafrashas challah biblical or rabbinic?  ","spans":[{"text":"Is the "},{"text":"mitzvah ","italic":true},{"text":"of "},{"text":"hafrashas challah ","italic":true},{"text":"biblical or rabbinic?  "}],"marker":"1.","level":0,"indent":36.0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0,"hanging":18.0},{"kind":"answer","paragraph":3,"xmlIndex":3,"text":"The S”A in סעיף א passkins that the mitzvah of hafrashas challah is biblical, but in סעיף ב he clarifies that it is only biblical in Eretz Yisrael when all of the Jewish people are living there. In סעיף ג, he adds that in chutz la’aretz, the mitzvah was rabbinically enacted so the mitzvah of challah will not be forgotten. ","spans":[{"text":"The S”A in ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"סעיף א ","bold":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"passkins ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"that the ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"mitzvah ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"of ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"hafrashas challah ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"is biblical, but in ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"סעיף ב ","bold":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"he clarifies that it is only biblical in ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"Eretz Yisrael ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"when all of the Jewish people are living there. In ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"סעיף ג","bold":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":", he adds that in ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"chutz la’aretz, ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"the ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"mitzvah ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"was rabbinically enacted so the ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"mitzvah ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"of ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"challah ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"will not be forgotten. ","color":"#C00000"}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":36.0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}],"footnotes":[]},"sort_order":46,"official_shiur_number":null,"estimated_minutes":null,"difficulty":"core","tags":["challah-part-1","original-difficulty:easy"],"source_file_name":"Q&A's for Hafrashas Challah (part 1).docx","source_start_page":null,"source_end_page":null,"is_selectable":true},{"program_id":"winter-5787","chunk_code":"HC1-Q2","source_type":"qa","siman":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","workbook_title":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","section_key":"B","section_title":"Status of Hafrashas Challah Today","chunk_title":"Majority of Jews in Israel Today","chunk_summary":"Ritva/Be’er Basadeh vs Chazon Ish whether majority living in EY makes challah biblical today.","content_markdown":"2. According to the Israel Central Bureau of Statistics, about 46% of Jews live in Israel. However, of the roughly 6 million Jews living in the US, 37% are Reform, many of whom are converts (or whose mothers converted) and are not *halachically* Jewish. Thus, it is possible that today, the majority of Jews already live in Israel. Does this affect the *mitzvah* of *hafrashas challah*?\n\nThe **ריטב״א** (5) *passkins* that if even just the majority of Jews live in Israel and not all of them, the *mitzvah* of *hafrashas challah* is biblical. The **באר בשדה** (6) proves that even when the Jewish people initially entered *Eretz Yisrael,* it was only the majority that lived there, but not everyone, since there were still Jews from the *shevatim* of גד, ראובן and מנשה who remained on the other side of the Jordan river, and yet, the *mitzvah* of *hafrashas challah* was still biblical. The **חזון איש** (7) argues that in order for *challah* to be biblically obligatory, the majority of Jews might need to enter Israel by direct instruction from Hashem, either through a Navi or king. Today, since the majority of Jews enter without the direct instruction of a king or Navi, the *mitzvah* might remain rabbinic. ","content_document":{"version":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"question","paragraph":4,"xmlIndex":4,"text":"According to the Israel Central Bureau of Statistics, about 46% of Jews live in Israel. However, of the roughly 6 million Jews living in the US, 37% are Reform, many of whom are converts (or whose mothers converted) and are not halachically Jewish. Thus, it is possible that today, the majority of Jews already live in Israel. Does this affect the mitzvah of hafrashas challah? ","spans":[{"text":"According to the Israel Central Bureau of Statistics, about 46% of Jews live in Israel. However, of the roughly 6 million Jews living in the US, 37% are Reform, many of whom are converts (or whose mothers converted) and are not "},{"text":"halachically ","italic":true},{"text":"Jewish. Thus, it is possible that today, the majority of Jews already live in Israel. Does this affect the "},{"text":"mitzvah ","italic":true},{"text":"of "},{"text":"hafrashas challah","italic":true},{"text":"? "}],"marker":"2.","level":0,"indent":36.0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0,"hanging":18.0},{"kind":"answer","paragraph":5,"xmlIndex":5,"text":"The ריטב״א (5) passkins that if even just the majority of Jews live in Israel and not all of them, the mitzvah of hafrashas challah is biblical. The באר בשדה (6) proves that even when the Jewish people initially entered Eretz Yisrael, it was only the majority that lived there, but not everyone, since there were still Jews from the shevatim of גד, ראובן and מנשה who remained on the other side of the Jordan river, and yet, the mitzvah of hafrashas challah was still biblical. The חזון איש (7) argues that in order for challah to be biblically obligatory, the majority of Jews might need to enter Israel by direct instruction from Hashem, either through a Navi or king. Today, since the majority of Jews enter without the direct instruction of a king or Navi, the mitzvah might remain rabbinic. ","spans":[{"text":"The ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"ריטב״א ","bold":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"(5) ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"passkins ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"that if even just the majority of Jews live in Israel and not all of them, the ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"mitzvah ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"of ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"hafrashas challah ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"is biblical. The ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"באר בשדה","bold":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":" (6) proves that even when the Jewish people initially entered ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"Eretz Yisrael, ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"it was only the majority that lived there, but not everyone, since there were still Jews from the ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"shevatim ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"of גד, ראובן and מנשה who remained on the other side of the Jordan river, and yet, the ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"mitzvah ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"of ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"hafrashas challah ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"was still biblical. The ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"חזון איש","bold":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":" (7) argues that in order for ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"challah ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"to be biblically obligatory, the majority of Jews might need to enter Israel by direct instruction from Hashem, either through a Navi or king. Today, since the majority of Jews enter without the direct instruction of a king or Navi, the ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"mitzvah ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"might remain rabbinic. ","color":"#C00000"}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":36.0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}],"footnotes":[]},"sort_order":47,"official_shiur_number":null,"estimated_minutes":null,"difficulty":"core","tags":["challah-part-1","original-difficulty:medium","source:5","source:6","source:7"],"source_file_name":"Q&A's for Hafrashas Challah (part 1).docx","source_start_page":null,"source_end_page":null,"is_selectable":true},{"program_id":"winter-5787","chunk_code":"HC1-Q3","source_type":"qa","siman":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","workbook_title":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","section_key":"B","section_title":"Status of Hafrashas Challah Today","chunk_title":"Majority Jews: Shemittah and Terumah","chunk_summary":"Shemittah tied to yovel/shevatim; terumah may become biblical with majority, with Rav Chaim Kanievsky’s ten tribes caveat.","content_markdown":"3. If the majority of Jews live in Israel, what will be the status of the upcoming *shemittah*? Will there be any room to rely on *heter mechira*? Is the *mitzvah* of *terumah* affected today?\n\nRegarding *shemittah,* the **רמב״ם** (4) *passkins* that *shemittah* will only be a biblical obligation when *yovel* is biblically required. *Yovel* is only biblically required when the majority of the Jewish people enter the land of Israel and settle in their ancestral portion based on their *shevet.* Thus, only when *Mashiach* comes, reveals the *shevet* of each individual, and settles him in the appropriate area, will *shemittah* become biblical.\n\nRegarding *terumah,* the **ראב״ד** (8) *passkins* that the obligation is biblical even without a majority of Jews living in the land. In contrast, the **רמב״ם** (8) equates it to *challah* and *passkins* that it will only become biblical when the majority of Jews live in Israel. The **חזון איש** (9) writes that as opposed to *hafrashas challah,* even the רמב״ם agrees that regarding *terumah,* as long as the majority of Jews enter the land of Israel, even without direction from a King or Navi, the *mitzvah* becomes biblical. However, **Rav Chaim Kanievsky** (10) suggests that since there are 10 lost tribes, even if all the Jews come to Israel today, the majority of Jews (i.e., the 10 lost tribes) are still in *chutz la’aretz,* and thus, *terumah* will remain rabbinic until *Mashiach* comes*.* ","content_document":{"version":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"question","paragraph":6,"xmlIndex":6,"text":"If the majority of Jews live in Israel, what will be the status of the upcoming shemittah? Will there be any room to rely on heter mechira? Is the mitzvah of terumah affected today?","spans":[{"text":"If the majority of Jews live in Israel, what will be the status of the upcoming "},{"text":"shemittah","italic":true},{"text":"? Will there be any room to rely on "},{"text":"heter mechira","italic":true},{"text":"? Is the "},{"text":"mitzvah ","italic":true},{"text":"of "},{"text":"terumah ","italic":true},{"text":"affected today?"}],"marker":"3.","level":0,"indent":36.0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0,"hanging":18.0},{"kind":"answer","paragraph":7,"xmlIndex":7,"text":"Regarding shemittah, the רמב״ם (4) passkins that shemittah will only be a biblical obligation when yovel is biblically required. Yovel is only biblically required when the majority of the Jewish people enter the land of Israel and settle in their ancestral portion based on their shevet. Thus, only when Mashiach comes, reveals the shevet of each individual, and settles him in the appropriate area, will shemittah become biblical.","spans":[{"text":"Regarding ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"shemittah, ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"the ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"רמב״ם","bold":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":" (4) ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"passkins ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"that ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"shemittah ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"will only be a biblical obligation when ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"yovel ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"is","color":"#C00000"},{"text":" ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"biblically required. ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"Yovel ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"is only biblically required when the majority of the Jewish people enter the land of Israel and settle in their ancestral portion based on their ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"shevet. ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"Thus, only when ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"Mashiach ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"comes, reveals the ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"shevet ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"of each individual, and settles him in the appropriate area, will ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"shemittah ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"become biblical.","color":"#C00000"}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":36.0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"answer","paragraph":8,"xmlIndex":8,"text":"Regarding terumah, the ראב״ד (8) passkins that the obligation is biblical even without a majority of Jews living in the land. In contrast, the רמב״ם (8) equates it to challah and passkins that it will only become biblical when the majority of Jews live in Israel. The חזון איש (9) writes that as opposed to hafrashas challah, even the רמב״ם agrees that regarding terumah, as long as the majority of Jews enter the land of Israel, even without direction from a King or Navi, the mitzvah becomes biblical. However, Rav Chaim Kanievsky (10) suggests that since there are 10 lost tribes, even if all the Jews come to Israel today, the majority of Jews (i.e., the 10 lost tribes) are still in chutz la’aretz, and thus, terumah will remain rabbinic until Mashiach comes. ","spans":[{"text":"Regarding ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"terumah, ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"the ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"ראב״ד ","bold":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"(8) ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"passkins ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"that the obligation is biblical even without a majority of Jews living in the land. In contrast, the ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"רמב״ם ","bold":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"(8) equates it to ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"challah ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"and ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"passkins ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"that it will only become biblical when the majority of Jews live in Israel. The ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"חזון איש","bold":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":" (9) writes","color":"#C00000"},{"text":" ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"that as opposed to ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"hafrashas challah,","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":" even the רמב״ם agrees that regarding ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"terumah, ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"as long as the majority of Jews enter the land of Israel, even without direction from a King or Navi, the ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"mitzvah ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"becomes biblical. However, ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"Rav Chaim Kanievsky ","bold":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"(10) suggests that since there are 10 lost tribes, even if all the Jews come to Israel today, the majority of Jews (i.e., the 10 lost tribes) are still in ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"chutz la’aretz, ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"and thus, ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"terumah ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"will remain rabbinic until ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"Mashiach ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"comes","color":"#C00000"},{"text":". ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":36.0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}],"footnotes":[]},"sort_order":48,"official_shiur_number":null,"estimated_minutes":null,"difficulty":"core","tags":["challah-part-1","original-difficulty:medium","source:4","source:8","source:9","source:10"],"source_file_name":"Q&A's for Hafrashas Challah (part 1).docx","source_start_page":null,"source_end_page":null,"is_selectable":true},{"program_id":"winter-5787","chunk_code":"HC1-Q4","source_type":"qa","siman":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","workbook_title":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","section_key":"C","section_title":"Differences Between Challah of Eretz Yisrael and Chutz La’aretz","chunk_title":"Forgot Challah on Shabbos","chunk_summary":"Chu”l: eat and leave over; EY: do not eat, borrow; weekday correction in EY by separating from remaining bread.","content_markdown":"4. True story: Your wife made the most delicious challah for Shabbos, but forgot to be *mafrish challah –* are you allowed to eat the challah?\n\nThe S”A (11) *passkins* that in *chutz la’aretz,* you are allowed to eat the challah and leave over a piece to be *mafrish* from after Shabbos. However, in *Eretz Yisrael,* you are not allowed to eat it and you must borrow from a neighbor. ","content_document":{"version":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"question","paragraph":9,"xmlIndex":9,"text":"True story: Your wife made the most delicious challah for Shabbos, but forgot to be mafrish challah – are you allowed to eat the challah? ","spans":[{"text":"True story: Your wife made the most delicious challah for Shabbos, but forgot to be "},{"text":"mafrish challah – ","italic":true},{"text":"are you allowed to eat the challah? "}],"marker":"4.","level":0,"indent":36.0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0,"hanging":18.0},{"kind":"answer","paragraph":10,"xmlIndex":10,"text":"The S”A (11) passkins that in chutz la’aretz, you are allowed to eat the challah and leave over a piece to be mafrish from after Shabbos. However, in Eretz Yisrael, you are not allowed to eat it and you must borrow from a neighbor. ","spans":[{"text":"The S”A (11) ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"passkins ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"that in ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"chutz la’aretz, ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"you are allowed to eat the challah and leave over a piece to be ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"mafrish ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"from after Shabbos. However, in ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"Eretz Yisrael, ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"you are not allowed to eat it and you must borrow from a neighbor. ","color":"#C00000"}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":36.0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}],"footnotes":[]},"sort_order":49,"official_shiur_number":null,"estimated_minutes":null,"difficulty":"core","tags":["challah-part-1","original-difficulty:easy","source:11"],"source_file_name":"Q&A's for Hafrashas Challah (part 1).docx","source_start_page":null,"source_end_page":null,"is_selectable":true},{"program_id":"winter-5787","chunk_code":"HC1-Q5","source_type":"qa","siman":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","workbook_title":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","section_key":"C","section_title":"Differences Between Challah of Eretz Yisrael and Chutz La’aretz","chunk_title":"Gifted Challah and Forgot","chunk_summary":"If some challah is with you in chu”l, leave over; in EY issue of Shabbos and not min hamukaf, with Baruch Omer leniency.","content_markdown":"5. The Rav of your community and his *rebbetzin* just had a baby. Your amazing wife decided to make them her delicious challah for Shabbos together with the challah she made for the family but forgot to be *mafrish challah.* The problem is that you and your wife are away for Shabbos, have no way of telling them, and only remembered on Shabbos night. What do you do? Does it help if you also have some of the challah with you?\n\nIf you brought some of the same challah with you from which *challah* was not taken and you live in *chutz la’aretz,* you should leave over some of it and after Shabbos be *mafrish* from the remaining amount, both on what you ate and what was given to the Rav and the *rebbetzin*. If you live in *Eretz Yisrael,* there are two issues. First, it is forbidden to be *mafrish* from one location to the next, and second, it is forbidden to be *mafrish* on Shabbos. Yet, the **ברוך אומר** (14) *passkins* that one should still be *mafrish,* since *be’dieved* being *mafrish* from one location to the next is valid, and it is preferable for you to transgress two small prohibitions rather than having the Rav (or even a friend) transgress a larger prohibition. Though, see notes for more details where we also quote some who argue. ","content_document":{"version":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"question","paragraph":11,"xmlIndex":11,"text":"The Rav of your community and his rebbetzin just had a baby. Your amazing wife decided to make them her delicious challah for Shabbos together with the challah she made for the family but forgot to be mafrish challah. The problem is that you and your wife are away for Shabbos, have no way of telling them, and only remembered on Shabbos night. What do you do? Does it help if you also have some of the challah with you?  ","spans":[{"text":"The Rav of your community and his "},{"text":"rebbetzin ","italic":true},{"text":"just had a baby. Your amazing wife decided to make them her delicious challah for Shabbos together with the challah she made for the family but forgot to be "},{"text":"mafrish challah. ","italic":true},{"text":"The problem is that you and your wife are away for Shabbos, have no way of telling them, and only remembered on Shabbos night. What do you do? Does it help if you also have some of the challah with you?  "}],"marker":"5.","level":0,"indent":36.0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0,"hanging":18.0},{"kind":"answer","paragraph":12,"xmlIndex":12,"text":"If you brought some of the same challah with you from which challah was not taken and you live in chutz la’aretz, you should leave over some of it and after Shabbos be mafrish from the remaining amount, both on what you ate and what was given to the Rav and the rebbetzin. If you live in Eretz Yisrael, there are two issues. First, it is forbidden to be mafrish from one location to the next, and second, it is forbidden to be mafrish on Shabbos. Yet, the ברוך אומר (14) passkins that one should still be mafrish, since be’dieved being mafrish from one location to the next is valid, and it is preferable for you to transgress two small prohibitions rather than having the Rav (or even a friend) transgress a larger prohibition. Though, see notes for more details where we also quote some who argue. ","spans":[{"text":"If you brought some of the same challah with you from which ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"challah ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"was not taken and you live in ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"chutz la’aretz, ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"you should leave over some of it and after Shabbos be ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"mafrish ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"from the remaining amount, both on what you ate and what was given to the Rav and the ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"rebbetzin","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":". If you live in ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"Eretz Yisrael, ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"there are two issues. First, it is forbidden to be ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"mafrish ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"from one location to the next, and second, it is forbidden to be ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"mafrish ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"on Shabbos. Yet, the ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"ברוך אומר ","bold":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"(14) ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"passkins ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"that one should still be ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"mafrish, ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"since ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"be’dieved ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"being ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"mafrish ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"from one location to the next is valid, and it is preferable for you to transgress two small prohibitions rather than having the Rav (or even a friend) transgress a larger prohibition. Though, see notes for more details where we also quote some who argue. ","color":"#C00000"}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":36.0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}],"footnotes":[]},"sort_order":50,"official_shiur_number":null,"estimated_minutes":null,"difficulty":"advanced","tags":["challah-part-1","original-difficulty:hard","source:14"],"source_file_name":"Q&A's for Hafrashas Challah (part 1).docx","source_start_page":null,"source_end_page":null,"is_selectable":true},{"program_id":"winter-5787","chunk_code":"HC1-Q6","source_type":"qa","siman":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","workbook_title":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","section_key":"C","section_title":"Differences Between Challah of Eretz Yisrael and Chutz La’aretz","chunk_title":"Forgot Matzah on Pesach Night","chunk_summary":"Chu”l: leave pieces; EY: RSZA, katan option, or Rav’s pre-Yom Tov hafrasha.","content_markdown":"6. You are baking *matzah* with your *frum* friend and tell him, “I buy the *matzos* from which *challah* was not separated so my wife can make the *bracha* later.” He tells you, “That’s a ‘*frum* rookie’ mistake; you will forget to be *mafrish.*” He was right - What do you do when you remember on Pesach night? How is this case different than the *challah* on Shabbos?\n\nIf you live in *chu”l* and do not know which *matzah* came from which batch of dough, the **משנ״ב** (17) *passkins* that you should leave over a bit from each *matzah* and on *chol ha’moed* place all the pieces in a bowl and be *mafrish* from all of them. \n\nIf you live in *EY,* there are three possible solutions: 1) **RSZA** (18) *passkins* that if there is no other way to get *matzah,* you are allowed to be *mafrish* in order to enable you to fulfill the biblical *mitzvah* of eating *matzah.* He adds that when being *mafrish* in *Eretz Yisrael* on Yom Tov, it is best to make the declaration mentally without verbalizing it and without reciting a *bracha* (but the physical separation still needs to be performed)*.*  2) The **חלת לחם**  (20) writes that you can give the *matzah* to a 12-year-old boy or 11-year-old girl who will be *mafrish challah.* This is permitted because at that age the *hafrasha* is valid, and according to the רשב״א, a *katan* is allowed to violate a rabbinic prohibition for his own sake. 3) **RSZA** (3) suggests that the  Rav of every community should be *mafrish challah* right before Pesach and have in mind that it should cover all those who forgot. See notes for more details. ","content_document":{"version":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"question","paragraph":13,"xmlIndex":13,"text":"You are baking matzah with your frum friend and tell him, “I buy the matzos from which challah was not separated so my wife can make the bracha later.” He tells you, “That’s a ‘frum rookie’ mistake; you will forget to be mafrish.” He was right - What do you do when you remember on Pesach night? How is this case different than the challah on Shabbos? ","spans":[{"text":"You are baking "},{"text":"matzah ","italic":true},{"text":"with your "},{"text":"frum ","italic":true},{"text":"friend and tell him, “I buy the "},{"text":"matzos","italic":true},{"text":" from which "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"was not separated so my wife can make the "},{"text":"bracha ","italic":true},{"text":"later.” He tells you, “That’s a ‘"},{"text":"frum ","italic":true},{"text":"rookie’ mistake; you will forget to be "},{"text":"mafrish.","italic":true},{"text":"” He was right - What do you do when you remember on Pesach night? How is this case different than the "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"on Shabbos? "}],"marker":"6.","level":0,"indent":36.0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0,"hanging":18.0},{"kind":"answer","paragraph":14,"xmlIndex":14,"text":"If you live in chu”l and do not know which matzah came from which batch of dough, the משנ״ב (17) passkins that you should leave over a bit from each matzah and on chol ha’moed place all the pieces in a bowl and be mafrish from all of them. ","spans":[{"text":"If you live in ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"chu”l ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"and do not know which ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"matzah ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"came from which batch of dough, the ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"משנ״ב","bold":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":" (17) ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"passkins ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"that you should leave over a bit from each ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"matzah ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"and on ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"chol ha’moed ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"place all the pieces in a bowl and be ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"mafrish","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":" from all of them. ","color":"#C00000"}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":36.0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0},{"kind":"answer","paragraph":15,"xmlIndex":15,"text":"If you live in EY, there are three possible solutions: 1) RSZA (18) passkins that if there is no other way to get matzah, you are allowed to be mafrish in order to enable you to fulfill the biblical mitzvah of eating matzah. He adds that when being mafrish in Eretz Yisrael on Yom Tov, it is best to make the declaration mentally without verbalizing it and without reciting a bracha (but the physical separation still needs to be performed).  2) The חלת לחם  (20) writes that you can give the matzah to a 12-year-old boy or 11-year-old girl who will be mafrish challah. This is permitted because at that age the hafrasha is valid, and according to the רשב״א, a katan is allowed to violate a rabbinic prohibition for his own sake. 3) RSZA (3) suggests that the  Rav of every community should be mafrish challah right before Pesach and have in mind that it should cover all those who forgot. See notes for more details. ","spans":[{"text":"If you live in ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"EY,","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":" there are three possible solutions: 1)","color":"#C00000"},{"text":" ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"RSZA ","bold":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"(18) ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"passkins ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"that if there is no other way to get ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"matzah, ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"you are allowed to be ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"mafrish ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"in order to enable you to fulfill the biblical ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"mitzvah ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"of eating ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"matzah.","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":" He adds that when being ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"mafrish ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"in ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"Eretz Yisrael ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"on Yom Tov, it is best to make the declaration mentally without verbalizing it and without reciting a ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"bracha ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"(but the physical separation still needs to be performed)","color":"#C00000"},{"text":".","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":" ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":" ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"2) The ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"חלת לחם","bold":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"  (20) writes that you can give the ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"matzah ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"to a 12-year-old boy or 11-year-old girl who will be ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"mafrish challah. ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"This is permitted because at that age the ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"hafrasha ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"is valid, and according to the רשב״א, a ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"katan ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"is allowed to violate a rabbinic prohibition for his own sake. 3) ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"RSZA ","bold":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"(3) suggests that the  Rav of every community should be ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"mafrish challah ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"right before Pesach and have in mind that it should cover all those who forgot. See notes for more details. ","color":"#C00000"}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":36.0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}],"footnotes":[]},"sort_order":51,"official_shiur_number":null,"estimated_minutes":null,"difficulty":"advanced","tags":["challah-part-1","original-difficulty:hard","source:3","source:17","source:18","source:20"],"source_file_name":"Q&A's for Hafrashas Challah (part 1).docx","source_start_page":null,"source_end_page":null,"is_selectable":true},{"program_id":"winter-5787","chunk_code":"HC1-Q7","source_type":"qa","siman":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","workbook_title":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","section_key":"C","section_title":"Differences Between Challah of Eretz Yisrael and Chutz La’aretz","chunk_title":"Israeli Matzah Sent to Spain","chunk_summary":"Obligation follows where dough was made according to Rambam; Cheshav Ha’Efod/Bach may treat as chu”l after export.","content_markdown":"7. True story: An Israeli Pesach program provider ordered 500 boxes of *matzah* in Israel to bring on their Pesach program in Spain. On *leil ha’seder,* the *mashgiach* remembered that he forgot to be *mafrish challah* – what should he do now?\n\nThe **רמב״ם**  *passkins* that the obligation of *hafrashas challah* follows the location in which the flour was made into dough. Since the above flour was made into dough in EY, you would have to use one of the options listed in the previous question. However, the**חשב האפוד**  (23) quoting the ב״ח, understands the רמב״ם differently and *passkins* that if bread is taken out of EY, it gets the status of *chu”l*, where you are allowed to eat from each *matzah* and leave over some, as suggested by the**משנ״ב**  (17). However, the **משפטי ארץ** (פ״ט-א) *passkins* like the כסף משנה and משנה למלך’s understanding of the רמב״ם above, and one would not be allowed to be *mafrish.*","content_document":{"version":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"question","paragraph":16,"xmlIndex":16,"text":"True story: An Israeli Pesach program provider ordered 500 boxes of matzah in Israel to bring on their Pesach program in Spain. On leil ha’seder, the mashgiach remembered that he forgot to be mafrish challah – what should he do now? ","spans":[{"text":"True story: An Israeli Pesach program provider ordered 500 boxes of "},{"text":"matzah ","italic":true},{"text":"in Israel to bring on their Pesach program in Spain. On "},{"text":"leil ha’seder, ","italic":true},{"text":"the "},{"text":"mashgiach ","italic":true},{"text":"remembered that he forgot to be "},{"text":"mafrish challah ","italic":true},{"text":"– what should he do now? "}],"marker":"7.","level":0,"indent":36.0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0,"hanging":18.0},{"kind":"answer","paragraph":17,"xmlIndex":17,"text":"The רמב״ם  passkins that the obligation of hafrashas challah follows the location in which the flour was made into dough. Since the above flour was made into dough in EY, you would have to use one of the options listed in the previous question. However, theחשב האפוד  (23) quoting the ב״ח, understands the רמב״ם differently and passkins that if bread is taken out of EY, it gets the status of chu”l, where you are allowed to eat from each matzah and leave over some, as suggested by theמשנ״ב  (17). However, the משפטי ארץ (פ״ט-א) passkins like the כסף משנה and משנה למלך’s understanding of the רמב״ם above, and one would not be allowed to be mafrish.","spans":[{"text":"The ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"רמב״ם  ","bold":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"passkins ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"that the obligation of ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"hafrashas challah ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"follows the location in which the flour was made into dough. Since the above flour was made into dough in EY, you would have to use one of the options listed in the previous question. However, the","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"חשב האפוד","bold":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"  (23) quoting the ב״ח, understands the רמב״ם differently and ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"passkins ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"that if bread is taken out of EY, it gets the status of ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"chu”l","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":", where you are allowed to eat from each ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"matzah ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"and leave over some, as suggested by the","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"משנ״ב","bold":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"  (17). However, the ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"משפטי ארץ ","bold":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"(פ״ט-א) ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"passkins ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"like the כסף משנה and משנה למלך’s understanding of the רמב״ם above, and one would not be allowed to be ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"mafrish.","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":36.0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}],"footnotes":[]},"sort_order":52,"official_shiur_number":null,"estimated_minutes":null,"difficulty":"advanced","tags":["challah-part-1","original-difficulty:hard","source:17","source:23"],"source_file_name":"Q&A's for Hafrashas Challah (part 1).docx","source_start_page":null,"source_end_page":null,"is_selectable":true},{"program_id":"winter-5787","chunk_code":"HC1-Q8","source_type":"qa","siman":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","workbook_title":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","section_key":"D","section_title":"How Much to be Mafrish?","chunk_title":"How Much to Separate?","chunk_summary":"Today any amount works; custom is kezayis.","content_markdown":"8. Mazel tov! Your daughter just became *Bat-Mitzvah* and it is her first time being *mafrish challah.* She asks, “You go to SCP; how much do I need to be *mafrish*?” What do you answer?\n\nAlthough the S”A and *Rama* have a dispute on the matter, today both *Ashkenazim* and *Sephardim* (30) *passkin* that any amount may be separated and burned, but the custom is to separate a *kezayis.* ","content_document":{"version":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"question","paragraph":18,"xmlIndex":18,"text":"Mazel tov! Your daughter just became Bat-Mitzvah and it is her first time being mafrish challah. She asks, “You go to SCP; how much do I need to be mafrish?” What do you answer?  ","spans":[{"text":"Mazel tov! Your daughter just became "},{"text":"Bat-Mitzvah ","italic":true},{"text":"and it is her first time being "},{"text":"mafrish challah. ","italic":true},{"text":"She asks, “You go to SCP; how much do I need to be "},{"text":"mafrish","italic":true},{"text":"?” What do you answer?  "}],"marker":"8.","level":0,"indent":36.0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0,"hanging":18.0},{"kind":"answer","paragraph":19,"xmlIndex":19,"text":"Although the S”A and Rama have a dispute on the matter, today both Ashkenazim and Sephardim (30) passkin that any amount may be separated and burned, but the custom is to separate a kezayis. ","spans":[{"text":"Although the S”A and ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"Rama ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"have a dispute on the matter, today both ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"Ashkenazim ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"and ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"Sephardim ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"(30) ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"passkin ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"that any amount may be separated and burned, but the custom is to separate a ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"kezayis. ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":36.0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}],"footnotes":[]},"sort_order":53,"official_shiur_number":null,"estimated_minutes":null,"difficulty":"core","tags":["challah-part-1","original-difficulty:easy","source:30"],"source_file_name":"Q&A's for Hafrashas Challah (part 1).docx","source_start_page":null,"source_end_page":null,"is_selectable":true},{"program_id":"winter-5787","chunk_code":"HC1-Q9","source_type":"qa","siman":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","workbook_title":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","section_key":"D","section_title":"How Much to be Mafrish?","chunk_title":"Can a Kohen Eat Challah Today?","chunk_summary":"S”A permits certain kohen in chu”l, but minhag is to separate and burn.","content_markdown":"9. Is there ever a situation where a *kohen* is allowed to accept and eat the *challah* today?\n\nThe S”A (29) *passkins* that a *kohen* who is not *tamei* with *tumah* that emanates from his body is allowed to receive and eat the *challah* only in *chu”l.* The *Rama* writes that when doing so, a special *bracha* is recited, but as mentioned in the previous question, the custom today is to separate and burn it. ","content_document":{"version":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"question","paragraph":20,"xmlIndex":20,"text":"Is there ever a situation where a kohen is allowed to accept and eat the challah today? ","spans":[{"text":"Is there ever a situation where a "},{"text":"kohen ","italic":true},{"text":"is allowed to accept and eat the "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"today? "}],"marker":"9.","level":0,"indent":36.0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0,"hanging":18.0},{"kind":"answer","paragraph":21,"xmlIndex":21,"text":"The S”A (29) passkins that a kohen who is not tamei with tumah that emanates from his body is allowed to receive and eat the challah only in chu”l. The Rama writes that when doing so, a special bracha is recited, but as mentioned in the previous question, the custom today is to separate and burn it. ","spans":[{"text":"The S”A (29) ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"passkins ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"that a ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"kohen ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"who is not ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"tamei ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"with ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"tumah ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"that emanates from his body is allowed to receive and eat the ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"challah ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"only in ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"chu”l. ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"The ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"Rama ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"writes that when doing so, a special ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"bracha ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"is recited, but as mentioned in the previous question, the custom today is to separate and burn it. ","color":"#C00000"}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":36.0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}],"footnotes":[]},"sort_order":54,"official_shiur_number":null,"estimated_minutes":null,"difficulty":"core","tags":["challah-part-1","original-difficulty:medium","source:29"],"source_file_name":"Q&A's for Hafrashas Challah (part 1).docx","source_start_page":null,"source_end_page":null,"is_selectable":true},{"program_id":"winter-5787","chunk_code":"HC1-Q10","source_type":"qa","siman":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","workbook_title":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","section_key":"E","section_title":"What To Do with the Separated Challah?","chunk_title":"What to Do with the Separated Dough?","chunk_summary":"Burn; if impossible, less than kezayis wrapped/disposed according to some.","content_markdown":"10. After your daughter was *mafrish challah* for the first time, she asks you, “What do I do with this dough now?” What do you answer?\n\nThe S”A and *Rama passkin* that the *challah* should be burned. If this is not possible, **Rav Ovadya** (35) *passkins* that you should be *mafrish* less than a *kezayis* (since the obligation of burning *challah* only takes effect if the *challah* is a *kezayis* or greater), place it in a plastic bag, and throw it out. The **מנחת יצחק** and **שש״כ** write that if there is no way to burn the *challah,* it may be thrown out.","content_document":{"version":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"question","paragraph":22,"xmlIndex":22,"text":"After your daughter was mafrish challah for the first time, she asks you, “What do I do with this dough now?” What do you answer? ","spans":[{"text":"After your daughter was "},{"text":"mafrish challah ","italic":true},{"text":"for the first time, she asks you, “What do I do with this dough now?” What do you answer? "}],"marker":"10.","level":0,"indent":36.0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0,"hanging":18.0},{"kind":"answer","paragraph":23,"xmlIndex":23,"text":"The S”A and Rama passkin that the challah should be burned. If this is not possible, Rav Ovadya (35) passkins that you should be mafrish less than a kezayis (since the obligation of burning challah only takes effect if the challah is a kezayis or greater), place it in a plastic bag, and throw it out. The מנחת יצחק and שש״כ write that if there is no way to burn the challah, it may be thrown out.","spans":[{"text":"The S”A and ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"Rama passkin ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"that the ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"challah ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"should be burned. If this is not possible, ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"Rav Ovadya ","bold":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"(35) ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"passkins ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"that you should be ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"mafrish ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"less than a ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"kezayis ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"(since the obligation of burning ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"challah ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"only takes effect if the ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"challah ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"is a ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"kezayis ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"or greater), place it in a plastic bag, and throw it out. The ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"מנחת יצחק","bold":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":" and ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"שש״כ","bold":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":" write that if there is no way to burn the ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"challah, ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"it may be thrown out.","color":"#C00000"}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":36.0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}],"footnotes":[]},"sort_order":55,"official_shiur_number":null,"estimated_minutes":null,"difficulty":"core","tags":["challah-part-1","original-difficulty:easy","source:35"],"source_file_name":"Q&A's for Hafrashas Challah (part 1).docx","source_start_page":null,"source_end_page":null,"is_selectable":true},{"program_id":"winter-5787","chunk_code":"HC1-Q11","source_type":"qa","siman":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","workbook_title":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","section_key":"E","section_title":"What To Do with the Separated Challah?","chunk_title":"Foil Ripped While Burning in Oven","chunk_summary":"Other food likely permitted according to Chayei Halevi; rack touching challah needs kashering; some are stricter.","content_markdown":"11. True story: You wrap the *challah* with aluminum foil and burn it in your oven, but when you take it out, you notice that the dough rose and the foil ripped. Are you allowed to eat the other food that was baked in the oven at the same time? What about the rack it was on?\n\nYou are allowed to eat the other food that was baked in the oven since there is no issue ofריחא  or זיעה. Let’s explain. There is no issue of ריחא, since in a *be’dieved* situation (where the foods were already cooked together), *reicha* is not taken into consideration (see S”A יו\"ד סימן ק\"ח). There is also no issue of זיעה, since the dough is not a liquid and does not emit זיעה. Based on this, the **חיי הלוי**  (33) *passkins* that the other food in the oven is permitted. However, the rack which came in direct contact with the *challah* needs to be *kashered.* Some *acharonim,* such as the **הפרשת חלה כהלכתה** (עמ׳ קה), argue on Rav Moshe and *passkin* that even bread emits steam and all the food in the oven, as well as the oven itself, would be rendered not-kosher, if the foil rips.","content_document":{"version":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"question","paragraph":24,"xmlIndex":24,"text":"True story: You wrap the challah with aluminum foil and burn it in your oven, but when you take it out, you notice that the dough rose and the foil ripped. Are you allowed to eat the other food that was baked in the oven at the same time? What about the rack it was on? ","spans":[{"text":"True story: You wrap the "},{"text":"challah ","italic":true},{"text":"with aluminum foil and burn it in your oven, but when you take it out, you notice that the dough rose and the foil ripped. Are you allowed to eat the other food that was baked in the oven at the same time? What about the rack it was on? "}],"marker":"11.","level":0,"indent":36.0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0,"hanging":18.0},{"kind":"answer","paragraph":25,"xmlIndex":25,"text":"You are allowed to eat the other food that was baked in the oven since there is no issue ofריחא  or זיעה. Let’s explain. There is no issue of ריחא, since in a be’dieved situation (where the foods were already cooked together), reicha is not taken into consideration (see S”A יו\"ד סימן ק\"ח). There is also no issue of זיעה, since the dough is not a liquid and does not emit זיעה. Based on this, the חיי הלוי  (33) passkins that the other food in the oven is permitted. However, the rack which came in direct contact with the challah needs to be kashered. Some acharonim, such as the הפרשת חלה כהלכתה (עמ׳ קה), argue on Rav Moshe and passkin that even bread emits steam and all the food in the oven, as well as the oven itself, would be rendered not-kosher, if the foil rips.","spans":[{"text":"You are allowed to eat the other food that was baked in the oven since there is no issue ofריחא  or זיעה. Let’s explain. There is no issue of ריחא, since in a ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"be’dieved ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"situation (where the foods were already cooked together), ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"reicha ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"is not taken into consideration (see S”A יו\"ד סימן ק\"ח). There is also no issue of זיעה, since the dough is not a liquid and does not emit זיעה. Based on this, the ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"חיי הלוי ","bold":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":" (33) ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"passkins ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"that the other food in the oven is permitted. However, the rack which came in direct contact with the ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"challah ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"needs to be ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"kashered. ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"Some ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"acharonim, ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"such as the ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"הפרשת חלה כהלכתה ","bold":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"(עמ׳ קה),","color":"#C00000"},{"text":" ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"argue on Rav Moshe and ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"passkin ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"that even bread emits steam and all the food in the oven, as well as the oven itself, would be rendered not-kosher, if the foil rips.","color":"#C00000"}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":36.0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}],"footnotes":[]},"sort_order":56,"official_shiur_number":null,"estimated_minutes":null,"difficulty":"core","tags":["challah-part-1","original-difficulty:medium","source:33"],"source_file_name":"Q&A's for Hafrashas Challah (part 1).docx","source_start_page":null,"source_end_page":null,"is_selectable":true},{"program_id":"winter-5787","chunk_code":"HC1-Q12","source_type":"qa","siman":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","workbook_title":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","section_key":"E","section_title":"What To Do with the Separated Challah?","chunk_title":"Burning Challah in Biur Chametz Fire","chunk_summary":"Concern of benefiting from challah flames; reasons to permit include already-hot fire and mitzvos lav lehenos nitnu.","content_markdown":"12. You throw your *hafrashas challah matzah* into the *biur chametz* fire when your *frum* friend yells “Hey! You are ruining everything for us! We can’t burn our *chametz* here anymore!” What is he talking about?\n\nThe **חלקת בנימין** (34) asks that if one throws *challah* into the *biur chametz,* others should be forbidden from throwing their *chametz* in because they are benefitting from the extra flames created by the burning *challah*. He answers that it should be permitted for two reasons: 1) Usually the fire is big and hot enough without the added dough, and thus, you are not benefitting from the extra flames the *challah* creates. 2) Even if you are benefitting from the extra flames caused by the burning *challah*, since מצוות לאו ליהנות ניתנו – *mitzvos* are not given for benefitting from, using the flames to fulfill the *mitzvah* of burning *chametz* is not a benefit which is prohibited. ","content_document":{"version":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"question","paragraph":26,"xmlIndex":26,"text":"You throw your hafrashas challah matzah into the biur chametz fire when your frum friend yells “Hey! You are ruining everything for us! We can’t burn our chametz here anymore!” What is he talking about? ","spans":[{"text":"You throw your "},{"text":"hafrashas challah matzah ","italic":true},{"text":"into the "},{"text":"biur chametz ","italic":true},{"text":"fire when your "},{"text":"frum ","italic":true},{"text":"friend yells “Hey! You are ruining everything for us! We can’t burn our "},{"text":"chametz ","italic":true},{"text":"here anymore!” What is he talking about? "}],"marker":"12.","level":0,"indent":36.0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0,"hanging":18.0},{"kind":"answer","paragraph":27,"xmlIndex":27,"text":"The חלקת בנימין (34) asks that if one throws challah into the biur chametz, others should be forbidden from throwing their chametz in because they are benefitting from the extra flames created by the burning challah. He answers that it should be permitted for two reasons: 1) Usually the fire is big and hot enough without the added dough, and thus, you are not benefitting from the extra flames the challah creates. 2) Even if you are benefitting from the extra flames caused by the burning challah, since מצוות לאו ליהנות ניתנו – mitzvos are not given for benefitting from, using the flames to fulfill the mitzvah of burning chametz is not a benefit which is prohibited. ","spans":[{"text":"The ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"חלקת בנימין ","bold":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"(34) asks that if one throws ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"challah ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"into the ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"biur chametz, ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"others should be forbidden from throwing their ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"chametz ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"in because they are benefitting from the extra flames created by the burning ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"challah","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":". He answers that it should be permitted for two reasons: 1) Usually the fire is big and hot enough without the added dough, and thus, you are not benefitting from the extra flames the ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"challah ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"creates. 2) Even if you are benefitting from the extra flames caused by the burning ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"challah","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":", since מצוות לאו ליהנות ניתנו – ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"mitzvos ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"are not given for benefitting from, using the flames to fulfill the ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"mitzvah ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"of burning ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"chametz ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"is not a benefit which is prohibited. ","color":"#C00000"}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":36.0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}],"footnotes":[]},"sort_order":57,"official_shiur_number":null,"estimated_minutes":null,"difficulty":"core","tags":["challah-part-1","original-difficulty:medium","source:34"],"source_file_name":"Q&A's for Hafrashas Challah (part 1).docx","source_start_page":null,"source_end_page":null,"is_selectable":true},{"program_id":"winter-5787","chunk_code":"HC1-Q13","source_type":"qa","siman":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","workbook_title":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","section_key":"E","section_title":"What To Do with the Separated Challah?","chunk_title":"Fork Used for Burning Challah","chunk_summary":"Fork inserted into challah can become treif and likely needs libun.","content_markdown":"13. Your wife asks you to take the *hafrashas challah* out of the oven, so you stick a fork in it. You then hear your wife yell, “You are *treifing-*up my utensils!” Is the fork really not kosher? How would you *kasher* it?\n\nThe**אמרי יעקב**  (38) *passkins* that the fork is *treif* and most likely needs *libun.*","content_document":{"version":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"question","paragraph":28,"xmlIndex":28,"text":"Your wife asks you to take the hafrashas challah out of the oven, so you stick a fork in it. You then hear your wife yell, “You are treifing-up my utensils!” Is the fork really not kosher? How would you kasher it? ","spans":[{"text":"Your wife asks you to take the "},{"text":"hafrashas challah ","italic":true},{"text":"out of the oven, so you stick a fork in it. You then hear your wife yell, “You are "},{"text":"treifing-","italic":true},{"text":"up my utensils!” Is the fork really not kosher? How would you "},{"text":"kasher ","italic":true},{"text":"it? "}],"marker":"13.","level":0,"indent":36.0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0,"hanging":18.0},{"kind":"answer","paragraph":29,"xmlIndex":29,"text":"Theאמרי יעקב  (38) passkins that the fork is treif and most likely needs libun.","spans":[{"text":"The","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"אמרי יעקב  ","bold":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"(38) ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"passkins ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"that the fork is ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"treif ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"and most likely needs ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"libun.","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":36.0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}],"footnotes":[]},"sort_order":58,"official_shiur_number":null,"estimated_minutes":null,"difficulty":"core","tags":["challah-part-1","original-difficulty:medium","source:38"],"source_file_name":"Q&A's for Hafrashas Challah (part 1).docx","source_start_page":null,"source_end_page":null,"is_selectable":true},{"program_id":"winter-5787","chunk_code":"HC1-Q14","source_type":"qa","siman":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","workbook_title":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","section_key":"F","section_title":"Amount of Dough Needed to be Mafrish","chunk_title":"Minimum Batch Size","chunk_summary":"Dough must be one-tenth of an eifah, 43.2 eggs.","content_markdown":"14. How large does the batch of dough need to be in order to be obligated in *hafrashas challah*?\n\nThe **גמרא עירובין** (41) teaches that a batch of dough must be at least 1/10 of an *eifah* to be obligated in *challah,* which equals 43.2 eggs.","content_document":{"version":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"question","paragraph":30,"xmlIndex":30,"text":"How large does the batch of dough need to be in order to be obligated in hafrashas challah? ","spans":[{"text":"How large does the batch of dough need to be in order to be obligated in "},{"text":"hafrashas challah","italic":true},{"text":"? "}],"marker":"14.","level":0,"indent":36.0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0,"hanging":18.0},{"kind":"answer","paragraph":31,"xmlIndex":31,"text":"The גמרא עירובין (41) teaches that a batch of dough must be at least 1/10 of an eifah to be obligated in challah, which equals 43.2 eggs.","spans":[{"text":"The ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"גמרא עירובין ","bold":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"(41) teaches that a batch of dough must be at least 1/10 of an ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"eifah ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"to be obligated in ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"challah, ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"which equals 43.2 eggs.","color":"#C00000"}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":36.0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}],"footnotes":[]},"sort_order":59,"official_shiur_number":null,"estimated_minutes":null,"difficulty":"core","tags":["challah-part-1","original-difficulty:easy","source:41"],"source_file_name":"Q&A's for Hafrashas Challah (part 1).docx","source_start_page":null,"source_end_page":null,"is_selectable":true},{"program_id":"winter-5787","chunk_code":"HC1-Q15","source_type":"qa","siman":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","workbook_title":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","section_key":"F","section_title":"Amount of Dough Needed to be Mafrish","chunk_title":"Why Chazon Ish Shiurim Are Large","chunk_summary":"Tzlach/Chazon Ish resolve discrepancy by saying eggs shrank; Rav Chaim Naeh reconciles differently.","content_markdown":"15. The חזון איש once promised that no missiles will ever fall in Bnei Brak. A few years ago, a fragment from a missile fell in the city. They quickly ran with it to Rav Chaim Kanievsky and asked “Look a missile fell! What happened to the promise of the חזון איש?” Rav Chaim looked at it and said, “It’s so small, it’s not the *shiur* of the חזון איש.” Why are the sizes of the חזון איש so large?\n\nThe **גמרא פסחים** (42) teaches that the volume of a *revi’is* (1/4 of a לוג) is 2 fingerbreadths by 2 fingerbreadths by 2 and 7/10 fingerbreadths, which comes out to a volume of **10.8 cubic fingerbreadths** (2x2x2.7). When using this calculation (where a fingerbreadth is 2.5 cm), the size of 1/10 of an *eifah* equals 4.86 liters. However, when using the average volume of an egg (52 cc), the volume of 1/10 of an *eifah* equals 2.25 liters! Therefore, the **צל״ח** (44) and the**חזון איש**  (45) conclude that the size of our eggs have shrunk almost in half. If the eggs of Chazal were double the size of eggs today, then the volumes mentioned in the two גמרות would be aligned. The צל״ח concludes that since a *kezayis* is half the size of an egg of Chazal, today’s *kezayis* is the size of our eggs. See, however, the notes for the reconciliation of רב חיים נאה.","content_document":{"version":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"question","paragraph":32,"xmlIndex":32,"text":"The חזון איש once promised that no missiles will ever fall in Bnei Brak. A few years ago, a fragment from a missile fell in the city. They quickly ran with it to Rav Chaim Kanievsky and asked “Look a missile fell! What happened to the promise of the חזון איש?” Rav Chaim looked at it and said, “It’s so small, it’s not the shiur of the חזון איש.” Why are the sizes of the חזון איש so large? ","spans":[{"text":"The חזון איש once promised that no missiles will ever fall in Bnei Brak. A few years ago, a fragment from a missile fell in the city. They quickly ran with it to Rav Chaim Kanievsky and asked “Look a missile fell! What happened to the promise of the חזון איש?” Rav Chaim looked at it and said, “It’s so small, it’s not the "},{"text":"shiur ","italic":true},{"text":"of the חזון איש.” Why are the sizes of the חזון איש so large? "}],"marker":"15.","level":0,"indent":36.0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0,"hanging":18.0},{"kind":"answer","paragraph":33,"xmlIndex":33,"text":"The גמרא פסחים (42) teaches that the volume of a revi’is (1/4 of a לוג) is 2 fingerbreadths by 2 fingerbreadths by 2 and 7/10 fingerbreadths, which comes out to a volume of 10.8 cubic fingerbreadths (2x2x2.7). When using this calculation (where a fingerbreadth is 2.5 cm), the size of 1/10 of an eifah equals 4.86 liters. However, when using the average volume of an egg (52 cc), the volume of 1/10 of an eifah equals 2.25 liters! Therefore, the צל״ח (44) and theחזון איש  (45) conclude that the size of our eggs have shrunk almost in half. If the eggs of Chazal were double the size of eggs today, then the volumes mentioned in the two גמרות would be aligned. The צל״ח concludes that since a kezayis is half the size of an egg of Chazal, today’s kezayis is the size of our eggs. See, however, the notes for the reconciliation of רב חיים נאה.","spans":[{"text":"The ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"גמרא פסחים ","bold":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"(42) teaches that the volume of a ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"revi’is ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"(1/4 of a לוג) is 2 fingerbreadths by 2 fingerbreadths by 2 and 7/10 fingerbreadths, which comes out to a volume of ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"10.8 cubic fingerbreadths","bold":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":" (2x2x2.7). When using this calculation (where a fingerbreadth is 2.5 cm), the size of 1/10 of an ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"eifah ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"equals 4.86 liters. However, when using the average volume of an egg (52 cc), the volume of 1/10 of an ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"eifah ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"equals 2.25 liters! Therefore, the ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"צל״ח ","bold":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"(44) and the","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"חזון איש","bold":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"  (45) conclude that the size of our eggs have shrunk almost in half. If the eggs of Chazal were double the size of eggs today, then the volumes mentioned in the two גמרות would be aligned. The צל״ח concludes that since a ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"kezayis ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"is half the size of an egg of Chazal, today’s ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"kezayis ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"is the size of our eggs. See, however, the notes for the reconciliation of רב חיים נאה.","color":"#C00000"}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":36.0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}],"footnotes":[]},"sort_order":60,"official_shiur_number":null,"estimated_minutes":null,"difficulty":"core","tags":["challah-part-1","original-difficulty:medium","source:42","source:44","source:45"],"source_file_name":"Q&A's for Hafrashas Challah (part 1).docx","source_start_page":null,"source_end_page":null,"is_selectable":true},{"program_id":"winter-5787","chunk_code":"HC1-Q16","source_type":"qa","siman":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","workbook_title":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","section_key":"F","section_title":"Amount of Dough Needed to be Mafrish","chunk_title":"How Much Matzah on Seder Night?","chunk_summary":"Biur Halacha says use larger shiur for biblical mitzvos; Yerushalayim custom followed Rav Chaim Naeh.","content_markdown":"16. It’s the *seder* night and you are about to eat *matzah*. Your son asks you, “Do we *passkin* like the חזון איש? How much *matzah* should I eat?” What do you answer?\n\nThe **ביאור הלכה** (47) *passkins* that for biblical *mitzvos,* one should follow the calculation of the צל״ח. Therefore, for the first *kezayis* of *matzah,* one should use the larger *shiur,* but one is not required to use that measurement for the *afikomen,* which is rabbinic. The custom in Yerushalayim, however, was always to follow the measurements of רב חיים נאה.","content_document":{"version":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"question","paragraph":34,"xmlIndex":34,"text":"It’s the seder night and you are about to eat matzah. Your son asks you, “Do we passkin like the חזון איש? How much matzah should I eat?” What do you answer? ","spans":[{"text":"It’s the "},{"text":"seder ","italic":true},{"text":"night and you are about to eat "},{"text":"matzah","italic":true},{"text":"."},{"text":" ","italic":true},{"text":"Your son asks you, “Do we "},{"text":"passkin ","italic":true},{"text":"like the חזון איש? How much "},{"text":"matzah ","italic":true},{"text":"should I eat?” What do you answer? "}],"marker":"16.","level":0,"indent":36.0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0,"hanging":18.0},{"kind":"answer","paragraph":35,"xmlIndex":35,"text":"The ביאור הלכה (47) passkins that for biblical mitzvos, one should follow the calculation of the צל״ח. Therefore, for the first kezayis of matzah, one should use the larger shiur, but one is not required to use that measurement for the afikomen, which is rabbinic. The custom in Yerushalayim, however, was always to follow the measurements of רב חיים נאה.","spans":[{"text":"The ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"ביאור הלכה","bold":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":" (47) ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"passkins ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"that for biblical ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"mitzvos, ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"one should follow the calculation of the צל״ח. Therefore, for the first ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"kezayis ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"of ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"matzah, ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"one should use the larger ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"shiur, ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"but one is not required to use that measurement for the ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"afikomen, ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"which is rabbinic. The custom in Yerushalayim, however, was always to follow the measurements of רב חיים נאה.","color":"#C00000"}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":36.0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}],"footnotes":[]},"sort_order":61,"official_shiur_number":null,"estimated_minutes":null,"difficulty":"core","tags":["challah-part-1","original-difficulty:medium","source:47"],"source_file_name":"Q&A's for Hafrashas Challah (part 1).docx","source_start_page":null,"source_end_page":null,"is_selectable":true},{"program_id":"winter-5787","chunk_code":"HC1-Q17","source_type":"qa","siman":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","workbook_title":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","section_key":"F","section_title":"Amount of Dough Needed to be Mafrish","chunk_title":"Added More Flour After Hafrasha","chunk_summary":"Separate again without bracha because of safek whether original hafrasha worked or new obligation began.","content_markdown":"17. True story: Your wife made a batch of dough with 1.5 kg of flour and was *mafrish challah,* when your neighbors called, asking if they could come with their family for Shabbos lunch. She agrees and quickly adds another kg of flour, more water, and mixes everything together. Is she obligated to be *mafrish* again?\n\nShe should be *mafrish* without a *bracha*. Let’s explain. If 1.2 kg is really the amount needed for *hafrashas challah,* then the first batch of dough was already obligated and exempted by the *hafrasha*, while the additional flour added now (which is less than 1.2 kg) is not enough to require a new *hafrasha.* However, if 1.66 kg is the amount needed to be *mafrish*, then the *hafrasha* you made on the 1.2 kg is not valid, and when you now add more flour and exceed the 1.66 kg threshold, you are obligated to be *mafrish* for the first time, which should ideally be with a *bracha.* Therefore, it is best to avoid this situation, but when it is necessary to do so, you should be *mafrish* again without a *bracha.*","content_document":{"version":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"question","paragraph":36,"xmlIndex":36,"text":"True story: Your wife made a batch of dough with 1.5 kg of flour and was mafrish challah, when your neighbors called, asking if they could come with their family for Shabbos lunch. She agrees and quickly adds another kg of flour, more water, and mixes everything together. Is she obligated to be mafrish again? ","spans":[{"text":"True story: Your wife made a batch of dough"},{"text":" ","italic":true},{"text":"with 1.5 kg of flour and was "},{"text":"mafrish challah, ","italic":true},{"text":"when your neighbors called, asking if they could come with their family for Shabbos lunch. She agrees and quickly adds another kg of flour, more water, and mixes everything together. Is she obligated to be "},{"text":"mafrish ","italic":true},{"text":"again? "}],"marker":"17.","level":0,"indent":36.0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0,"hanging":18.0},{"kind":"answer","paragraph":37,"xmlIndex":37,"text":"She should be mafrish without a bracha. Let’s explain. If 1.2 kg is really the amount needed for hafrashas challah, then the first batch of dough was already obligated and exempted by the hafrasha, while the additional flour added now (which is less than 1.2 kg) is not enough to require a new hafrasha. However, if 1.66 kg is the amount needed to be mafrish, then the hafrasha you made on the 1.2 kg is not valid, and when you now add more flour and exceed the 1.66 kg threshold, you are obligated to be mafrish for the first time, which should ideally be with a bracha. Therefore, it is best to avoid this situation, but when it is necessary to do so, you should be mafrish again without a bracha.","spans":[{"text":"She should be ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"mafrish ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"without a ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"bracha","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":". Let’s explain. If 1.2 kg is really the amount needed for ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"hafrashas challah, ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"then the first batch of dough was already obligated and exempted by the ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"hafrasha","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":", while the additional flour added now (which is less than 1.2 kg) is not enough to require a new ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"hafrasha. ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"However, if 1.66 kg is the amount needed to be ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"mafrish","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":", then the ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"hafrasha ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"you made on the 1.2 kg is not valid, and when you now add more flour and exceed the 1.66 kg threshold, you are obligated to be ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"mafrish ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"for the first time, which should ideally be with a ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"bracha. ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"Therefore, it is best to avoid this situation, but when it is necessary to do so, you should be ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"mafrish ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"again without a ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"bracha.","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":36.0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}],"footnotes":[]},"sort_order":62,"official_shiur_number":null,"estimated_minutes":null,"difficulty":"core","tags":["challah-part-1","original-difficulty:medium"],"source_file_name":"Q&A's for Hafrashas Challah (part 1).docx","source_start_page":null,"source_end_page":null,"is_selectable":true},{"program_id":"winter-5787","chunk_code":"HC1-Q18","source_type":"qa","siman":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","workbook_title":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","section_key":"F","section_title":"Amount of Dough Needed to be Mafrish","chunk_title":"Frozen Flour Needs More Weight","chunk_summary":"Frozen flour absorbs moisture and may weigh more than its real flour volume.","content_markdown":"18. You perfectly weigh the exact amount of flour needed to recite the *bracha* for *hafrashas challah,* when your daughter in seminary says, “I heard that if you take the flour out of the freezer, you need to add more flour!” What is she talking about?\n\nThe**לבוש מרדכי**  (51) writes that since frozen flour often absorbs moisture when frozen, it weighs more. Thus, if you use exactly 1.66 kg of flour, you are really using less than the volume of 43.2 eggs. Therefore, whenever using frozen flour, you should add a bit more flour to ensure that you have the required amount of flour that is equivalent to the volume of 43.2 eggs. ","content_document":{"version":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"question","paragraph":38,"xmlIndex":38,"text":"You perfectly weigh the exact amount of flour needed to recite the bracha for hafrashas challah, when your daughter in seminary says, “I heard that if you take the flour out of the freezer, you need to add more flour!” What is she talking about?","spans":[{"text":"You perfectly weigh the exact amount of flour needed to recite the "},{"text":"bracha","italic":true},{"text":" for "},{"text":"hafrashas challah, ","italic":true},{"text":"when your daughter in seminary says, “I heard that if you take the flour out of the freezer, you need to add more flour!” What is she talking about?"}],"marker":"18.","level":0,"indent":36.0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0,"hanging":18.0},{"kind":"answer","paragraph":39,"xmlIndex":39,"text":"Theלבוש מרדכי  (51) writes that since frozen flour often absorbs moisture when frozen, it weighs more. Thus, if you use exactly 1.66 kg of flour, you are really using less than the volume of 43.2 eggs. Therefore, whenever using frozen flour, you should add a bit more flour to ensure that you have the required amount of flour that is equivalent to the volume of 43.2 eggs. ","spans":[{"text":"The","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"לבוש מרדכי","bold":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"  (51) writes that since frozen flour often absorbs moisture when frozen, it weighs more. Thus, if you use exactly 1.66 kg of flour, you are really using less than the volume of 43.2 eggs. Therefore, whenever using frozen flour, you should add a bit more flour to ensure that you have the required amount of flour that is equivalent to the volume of 43.2 eggs. ","color":"#C00000"}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":36.0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}],"footnotes":[]},"sort_order":63,"official_shiur_number":null,"estimated_minutes":null,"difficulty":"core","tags":["challah-part-1","original-difficulty:easy","source:51"],"source_file_name":"Q&A's for Hafrashas Challah (part 1).docx","source_start_page":null,"source_end_page":null,"is_selectable":true},{"program_id":"winter-5787","chunk_code":"HC1-Q19","source_type":"qa","siman":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","workbook_title":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","section_key":"F","section_title":"Amount of Dough Needed to be Mafrish","chunk_title":"Whole Wheat Needs More Flour","chunk_summary":"Bran added back after milling may not count toward the 1.66 kg flour amount; may need about 1.9 kg.","content_markdown":"19. Your challah last Shabbos was so great that you decide this week to try making whole wheat challah. This time, to be safe, you weigh 1.8 kg of flour, but when you tell your *frum* friend what you are doing, he yells “Hey! You need more flour than that! It’s whole wheat!” What is he talking about?\n\nThe **S”A** (52) *passkins* that if you remove the bran from the flour and add it back later, the bran cannot be included in the calculation of 1.66 kg. Today, most mills selling whole wheat flour indeed remove the bran and then add it again later. Therefore, when using whole wheat flour to bake bread, you will need 1.9 kg of flour, of which there is 1.66 kg of flour obligated in *hafrasha.* See notes for more details. ","content_document":{"version":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"question","paragraph":40,"xmlIndex":40,"text":"Your challah last Shabbos was so great that you decide this week to try making whole wheat challah. This time, to be safe, you weigh 1.8 kg of flour, but when you tell your frum friend what you are doing, he yells “Hey! You need more flour than that! It’s whole wheat!” What is he talking about? ","spans":[{"text":"Your challah last Shabbos was so great that you decide this week to try making whole wheat challah. This time, to be safe, you weigh 1.8 kg of flour, but when"},{"text":" ","italic":true},{"text":"you tell your "},{"text":"frum ","italic":true},{"text":"friend what you are doing, he yells “Hey! You need more flour than that! It’s whole wheat!” What is he talking about? "}],"marker":"19.","level":0,"indent":36.0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0,"hanging":18.0},{"kind":"answer","paragraph":41,"xmlIndex":41,"text":"The S”A (52) passkins that if you remove the bran from the flour and add it back later, the bran cannot be included in the calculation of 1.66 kg. Today, most mills selling whole wheat flour indeed remove the bran and then add it again later. Therefore, when using whole wheat flour to bake bread, you will need 1.9 kg of flour, of which there is 1.66 kg of flour obligated in hafrasha. See notes for more details. ","spans":[{"text":"The ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"S”A","bold":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":" (52) ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"passkins ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"that if you remove the bran from the flour and add it back later, the bran cannot be included in the calculation of 1.66 kg. Today, most mills selling whole wheat flour indeed remove the bran and then add it again later. Therefore, when using whole wheat flour to bake bread, you will need 1.9 kg of flour, of which there is 1.66 kg of flour obligated in ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"hafrasha. ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"See notes for more details. ","color":"#C00000"}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":36.0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}],"footnotes":[]},"sort_order":64,"official_shiur_number":null,"estimated_minutes":null,"difficulty":"core","tags":["challah-part-1","original-difficulty:medium","source:52"],"source_file_name":"Q&A's for Hafrashas Challah (part 1).docx","source_start_page":null,"source_end_page":null,"is_selectable":true},{"program_id":"winter-5787","chunk_code":"HC1-Q20","source_type":"qa","siman":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","workbook_title":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","section_key":"G","section_title":"Practical Applications","chunk_title":"Gluten-Free Wheat Flour","chunk_summary":"Some say separate without bracha; Rav Rubin requires with bracha in certain Jewish-produced wheat-starch cases.","content_markdown":"20. Are you obligated to be *mafrish challah* on gluten-free wheat flour?\n\nThe **אמרי יעקב** (54) *passkins* that since gluten-free wheat flour cannot become *chametz,* it should really be exempt from *hafrasha,* but since some gluten always enters into the flour, you should be *mafrish* without a *bracha.* **Rav Rubin** (55) *passkins* that if the flour is made by Jews, you are *mafrish* with a *bracha* (assuming you have 1.66 kg of actual wheat flour), but if it is made by non-Jews, you should be *mafrish* without a *bracha.* See notes for details.  ","content_document":{"version":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"question","paragraph":42,"xmlIndex":42,"text":"Are you obligated to be mafrish challah on gluten-free wheat flour? ","spans":[{"text":"Are you obligated to be "},{"text":"mafrish challah ","italic":true},{"text":"on gluten-free wheat flour? "}],"marker":"20.","level":0,"indent":36.0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0,"hanging":18.0},{"kind":"answer","paragraph":43,"xmlIndex":43,"text":"The אמרי יעקב (54) passkins that since gluten-free wheat flour cannot become chametz, it should really be exempt from hafrasha, but since some gluten always enters into the flour, you should be mafrish without a bracha. Rav Rubin (55) passkins that if the flour is made by Jews, you are mafrish with a bracha (assuming you have 1.66 kg of actual wheat flour), but if it is made by non-Jews, you should be mafrish without a bracha. See notes for details.  ","spans":[{"text":"The ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"אמרי יעקב ","bold":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"(54) ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"passkins ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"that since gluten-free wheat flour cannot become ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"chametz, ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"it should really be exempt from ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"hafrasha, ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"but since some gluten always enters into the flour, you should be ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"mafrish ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"without a ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"bracha. ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"Rav Rubin ","bold":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"(55) ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"passkins ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"that if the flour is made by Jews, you are ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"mafrish ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"with a ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"bracha ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"(assuming you have 1.66 kg of actual wheat flour), but if it is made by non-Jews, you should be ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"mafrish ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"without a ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"bracha. ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"See notes for details. ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":" ","bold":true,"italic":true,"color":"#C00000"}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":36.0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}],"footnotes":[]},"sort_order":65,"official_shiur_number":null,"estimated_minutes":null,"difficulty":"advanced","tags":["challah-part-1","original-difficulty:hard","source:54","source:55"],"source_file_name":"Q&A's for Hafrashas Challah (part 1).docx","source_start_page":null,"source_end_page":null,"is_selectable":true},{"program_id":"winter-5787","chunk_code":"HC1-Q21","source_type":"qa","siman":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","workbook_title":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","section_key":"G","section_title":"Practical Applications","chunk_title":"Dough Rose Above Shiur","chunk_summary":"No new obligation if original flour volume was below shiur and it later rose above 43.2 eggs.","content_markdown":"21. You are already a mini-master in the *halachos* of *hafrashas challah,* so your wife asks you: “Since we are not having guests this week, I made challah with 1 kg of flour, but the yeast went crazy and the dough rose to a size much greater than 43.2 eggs. Do I need to be *mafrish* now?” What do you answer?\n\nNo. The S”A (56) *passkins* that if the dough was made with a volume of less than 43.2 eggs and then it rose to more than 43.2 eggs, it is still exempt from *hafrashas challah.* ","content_document":{"version":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"question","paragraph":44,"xmlIndex":44,"text":"You are already a mini-master in the halachos of hafrashas challah, so your wife asks you: “Since we are not having guests this week, I made challah with 1 kg of flour, but the yeast went crazy and the dough rose to a size much greater than 43.2 eggs. Do I need to be mafrish now?” What do you answer? ","spans":[{"text":"You are already a mini-master in the "},{"text":"halachos ","italic":true},{"text":"of "},{"text":"hafrashas challah, ","italic":true},{"text":"so your wife asks you: “Since we are not having guests this week, I made challah with 1 kg of flour, but the yeast went crazy and the dough rose to a size much greater than 43.2 eggs. Do I need to be "},{"text":"mafrish ","italic":true},{"text":"now?” What do you answer? "}],"marker":"21.","level":0,"indent":36.0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0,"hanging":18.0},{"kind":"answer","paragraph":45,"xmlIndex":46,"text":"No. The S”A (56) passkins that if the dough was made with a volume of less than 43.2 eggs and then it rose to more than 43.2 eggs, it is still exempt from hafrashas challah. ","spans":[{"text":"No. The S”A (56) ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"passkins ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"},{"text":"that if the dough was made with a volume of less than 43.2 eggs and then it rose to more than 43.2 eggs, it is still exempt from ","color":"#C00000"},{"text":"hafrashas challah. ","italic":true,"color":"#C00000"}],"marker":"","level":0,"indent":36.0,"firstLine":0,"leadingTabs":0,"alignment":"left","rtl":false,"footnoteIds":[],"defaultTabWidth":36.0}],"footnotes":[]},"sort_order":66,"official_shiur_number":null,"estimated_minutes":null,"difficulty":"core","tags":["challah-part-1","original-difficulty:easy","source:56"],"source_file_name":"Q&A's for Hafrashas Challah (part 1).docx","source_start_page":null,"source_end_page":null,"is_selectable":true},{"program_id":"winter-5787","chunk_code":"HC1-S1","source_type":"source","siman":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","workbook_title":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","section_key":"A","section_title":"Introduction to the Mitzvah of Challah","chunk_title":"Source 1","chunk_summary":"Related notes: HC1-A2 - Torah Source and Tevel Status","content_markdown":"![Official Hafrashas Challah source 1, PDF page 1](storage://official-learning-materials/sha256/4ef5851f6b5fc577312a0d302e5f4ff1073c1f185a5fb3e36c751b8c923266e0.jpg)","content_document":{"version":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"image","paragraph":1,"alt":"Official Hafrashas Challah source 1, PDF page 1","width":1224,"height":1584,"displayWidth":816,"storagePath":"sha256/4ef5851f6b5fc577312a0d302e5f4ff1073c1f185a5fb3e36c751b8c923266e0.jpg"}],"footnotes":[]},"sort_order":67,"official_shiur_number":1,"estimated_minutes":null,"difficulty":"core","tags":["challah-part-1","original-difficulty:core","source:1"],"source_file_name":"Marei_Mekomos_Hafrashas_Challah_Sources_1-57_Split.pdf","source_start_page":1,"source_end_page":1,"is_selectable":true},{"program_id":"winter-5787","chunk_code":"HC1-S2","source_type":"source","siman":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","workbook_title":"Hafrashas Challah Part 1","section_key":"A","section_title":"Introduction to the Mitzvah of Challah","chunk_title":"Source 2","chunk_summary":"Related notes: HC1-A4 - Gemara Kesubos: Biblical or Rabbinic Today; HC1-B1 - Current Demographics and the Question Today","content_markdown":"![Official Hafrashas Challah source 2, PDF page 2](storage://official-learning-materials/sha256/3b77f1f86d93a29e510fcfad4bc87422d6a9c9